INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1609,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12421977','Updates in the Management of Malignant Ovarian Germ Cell Tumors.','Yang J, Peng P, Xiang Y, Yang J.','2025','chemosensitive dysgerminoma nongestational dysgerminoma statics teenage dysgerminoma nongestational dysgerminomas seminomas dysgerminoma dysgenesis nondysgerminoma embryologic megabase 12p arrested meiosis prophase mitosis meiosis isochromosome 12p 12p dysgerminoma nonepithelial nonovarian dysgerminoma igcccg igcccg igcccg triages dysgerminoma nondysgerminoma categorizes igcccg omentectomy omentectomy skipped omentectomy omentum omentum noniatrogenic omentum spillage cystectomy adnexectomy skipped remnants dysgerminomas dysgerminomas dysgerminoma dysgerminoma agct1531 nct03067181 dysgerminoma einhorn superseded vac d15 d21 dactinomycin micrograms pvb d16 bep d15 mogct bec d15 dysgerminoma peb jeb mesna mesna veip mesna vac dactinomycin pvb bep mogct bec etopside peb jeb veip igcccg dysgerminoma nongestational dysgerminomas omits dysgerminomas nondysgerminoma nonseminomatous agct1531comparing dysgerminoma chemosensitive remissions teratomatous enlarging retroperitoneum megabase neuroepithelial restructured ewsr1 dysgerminoma nondysgerminoma nondysgerminoma pumch 2022yfc2704202 u114000 e249 stoltzfus tchelebi trifiletti lehrer e2027738 veneris physician_gls trabert desantis samimi runowicz oldenburg berney bokemeyer climent daugaard gietema euracan coquard morice oaknin pautier iv1 mdy001 solheim gershenson trope rokkones weedon fekjaer hel timmermans altena kruitwagen rfpm slangen sonke solheim kærn trope rokkones nesland giambartolomei korde canep kraggerud hoei alagaratnam skotheim abeler rajpert meyts skold glimelius joim oosterhuis looijenga nrc1568 cutcutache ramgopal ramnarayanan seminomas nieuwenhuysen busschaert neven moerman liontos zong woldu amatruda bagrodia mou dysgerminoma idrees ulbright 12p murali hockings liberale meisel sudarsan geist knezevic bromberg funt mangili sigismondi cormio candiani scarfone mogts mito mdw563 mahdi swensen fehmi semaan billmire vinocur rescorla schlatter imudia fehmi lockley murali pashankar gell shamash billmire moraru mitranovici chiorean coros moraru oala diagnostics13091516 einhorn donohue diamminedichloroplatinum einhorn loehrer nejm198706043162302 krailo amatruda arul billmire dysgerminoma stoneham e49 e49 rashdan einhorn ammakkanavar matei abonour einhorn oechsle kollmannsberger honecker oing bokemeyer wyvekens sholl vasudevaraja s41379 ulbright loehrer einhorn magers einhorn hamza pisters czerniak s13048 patni e54 e54 wesseling aldape brat capper cree cimpact cimpact ulbright pumch toprak hovestadt dtw capper ohgaki wiestler zong bitar drakaki modpat kvammen myklebust solberg klepp matei monahan gershenson murbraech solheim aulie aakhus cisplatinum ehf2 skalleberg solheim smastuen osnes gundersen pom dahm kahler staf floter radestad wallberg thomakos malakasis machairiotis zarogoulidis rodolakis jca piatek szymusik sobiczewski michalski kowalska ołtarzewski cancers15164170 donnez dolmans nejmra1614676 tretarre molinie woronoff bossard bessaoud marrer slayton shingleton creasman blessing dactinomycin sikic turbow ballon pvb blessing hanjani papadopoulou andreopoulou papadimitriou pavlidis aravantinos hellenic gyno kauderer lentz aghajanian dysgerminoma billmire rescorla pinkerton broadbent horwich mcelwain meller jeb gleeson knezevic bromberg sheinfeld daugaard skoneczna aass santis dumez bep gtcsg mdq575 loehrer gonin weathers einhorn','Recent advances in malignant ovarian germ cell tumor management emphasize risk-stratified therapy, enabling fertility preservation, chemotherapy de-escalation, and a reduction of long-term toxicity. Abstract Malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are rare malignancies primarily affecting adolescents and young adults. Management must balance curative intent with fertility preservation. This review highlights recent advances in malignant ovarian germ cell tumor management, focusing on risk-stratified approaches, surgical techniques, somatic transformation, and long-term surveillance. The bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen remains the first-line therapy. Fertility-sparing surgery, including unilateral salpingo-oophorectomy with minimal staging, is recommended for adolescent and young adult patients. Chemotherapy is not recommended for those with surgically resected immature teratomas. In advanced-stage malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, adjuvant chemotherapy may be considered for frail patients with chemosensitive tumors. Given the risk of late complications, long-term surveillance and lifelong care are essential for survivors. Future directions emphasize molecular profiling to guide personalized treatment. Multidisciplinary care, precision surgery, and tailored systemic therapies continue to improve survival while preserving quality of life. Collaborative trials are crucial for standardizing salvage approaches and mitigating long-term toxicities, ensuring optimal outcomes for this young, survivorship-focused population. Malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are rare, affecting predominantly adolescents and young adults younger than age 40 years. 1 , 2 The main histopathologic types of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors include immature teratoma, yolk sac tumor, dysgerminoma, embryonal carcinoma, nongestational choriocarcinoma, and mixed germ cell tumors. Malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are usually diagnosed with stage I disease involving one adnexa, with bilateral tumors being relatively rare. The majority of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are sensitive to chemotherapy; thus, it is usually possible for young female patients to preserve their fertility. In the late 1970s, cisplatin-based combination chemotherapy significantly increased the cure rate of malignant germ cell tumors, with the 5-year survival rate of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor reaching as high as 95%, 3 allowing these young patients with cancer to survive for a long time. Subsequently, researchers gradually recognized the side effects of chemotherapy and their effects on long-term survival. In the current National Comprehensive Cancer Network (NCCN) guidelines, postoperative adjuvant chemotherapy is not recommended for patients with surgically resected stage I grade 1 immature teratoma or stage I dysgerminoma. 4 Data from the SEER (Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results) database have shown that the 40-year cumulative mortality rate of testicular germ cell tumor survivors could be as high as 23.3%, significantly higher than that of the same age group, of which 12% of deaths were directly caused by non–cancer-related factors (SEER Cancer Statics Review: https://seer.cancer.gov/csr/ ). Therefore, under the premise of ensuring the effectiveness of cancer treatment, stratifying patients by risk to reduce chemotherapy side effects and long-term effects is crucial in the contemporary management of adolescent and young adult patients with cancer. The patient population of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor is unique, with a wide age range spanning from childhood to young adulthood. The diagnosis and treatment process involves pediatrics, gynecology, oncology, and reproductive physicians. Communication among health care professionals from different fields is often challenging. Moreover, there are differences in treatment concepts among physicians from different fields regarding this disease. This review summarizes the recent advances in the management of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor and highlights the differences in treating children and young adults. The current understanding of molecular biology is discussed, as well as clinical efforts in risk stratification and results from contemporary trials in malignant ovarian germ cell tumor. INCIDENCE AND CAUSE Malignant ovarian germ cell tumor accounts for approximately 1–3% of malignant ovarian tumors, with an annual incidence rate of 3.7 per 1,000,000 worldwide. 2 , 5 The incidence rate of ovarian germ cell tumors is 1/10th that of testicular germ cell tumors. 6 Malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are more common in young female individuals with a peak incidence in the late teenage and young adult years, accounting for 80% of all adolescent and young adult ovarian malignancies. 7 According to the SEER data and cancer registries in European countries, dysgerminoma is the most common malignant ovarian germ cell tumor, accounting for 30–35% of all tumors, followed by immature teratoma (17–30%), yolk sac tumor (15–20%), and mixed germ cell tumor (10–15%). 5 , 8 – 10 Nongestational choriocarcinoma is extremely rare with limited data reported. The cause of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors is unknown, but it is likely related to both genetic and environmental factors. 11 , 12 Malignant ovarian germ cell tumors share a similar origin and developmental features with testicular germ cell tumors. Dysgerminomas, akin to seminomas in men, are considered to originate from pluripotent cells. 13 One recognized cause of dysgerminoma is gonadal dysgenesis with an XY karyotype. The other nondysgerminoma subtypes encompass some degree of differentiation mimicking the embryologic development pathway. 12 , 14 Genetic studies suggest that malignant ovarian germ cell tumors have a low mutation rate ranging from 0.05 to 1.0 mutations per megabase 15 – 17 but remarkable aneuploidy such as gains in chromosome arm 12p. 16 This is consistent with their embryonal origin in development. Oocytes are arrested in meiosis prophase I until puberty for ovulation. It is hypothesized that the increased activity of mitosis and meiosis in puberty may lead to dysregulation and chromosomal abnormalities. Testicular germ cell tumors have presented with ubiquitous gain of isochromosome 12p. 18 In malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, gains in chromosome 12p have been reported in a large proportion of patients with dysgerminoma (50–70%) 3 , 19 and yolk sac tumor (41%), 20 but they are seldom detected in immature teratomas. 16 This theory partly explains why the affected age group of germ cell tumors usually those at the onset of puberty. STAGING AND RISK STRATIFICATION Currently, there are no independent staging criteria for malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. As a kind of nonepithelial ovarian cancer, it follows the staging system of the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics for ovarian epithelial cancer. 7 The main factors affecting prognosis include age, histopathologic type, stage, and extent of surgical resection. The European guidelines for nonovarian epithelial tumors propose that age older than 45 years is a risk factor for malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. 7 The MaGIC (Malignant Germ Cell International Consortium) conducted a more detailed analysis of germ cell tumors in children and adolescents and concluded that onset at or after 11 years is a risk factor for poor prognosis. 21 In terms of pathology, dysgerminoma has the best prognosis, and mixed germ cell tumors have a poorer prognosis. Immature teratomas with malignant transformation have an extremely poor prognosis. 21 A large retrospective report noted that those histopathologic types of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor significantly affect tumor recurrence and progression, with yolk sac tumor and mixed germ cell tumor being associated with a more unfavorable prognosis. 22 Because of the high heterogeneity of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor and the varying prognoses, a stratified treatment strategy based on tumor risk is of great significance. This approach aims to reduce overtreatment with chemotherapy in patients at low risk while ensuring adequate treatment for patients at high risk to minimize the risk of recurrence. It is crucial for preserving organ function in young female patients and improving overall tumor prognosis. The prognostic stratification of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor is primarily adapted from the IGCCCG (International Germ Cell Cancer Collaborative Group) classification for testicular germ cell tumors as modified IGCCCG. 23 The modified IGCCCG classification triages patients to dysgerminoma and nondysgerminoma and categorizes female genital germ cell tumors into three risk groups. Data from the Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center showed that the 3-year progression-free survival rates for groups at low, intermediate, and high risk were 88%, 78%, and 31%, respectively; the 3-year overall survival rates were 91%, 80%, and 50%, respectively. 24 Subsequent validation with larger sample size of 271 patients by Peking Union Medical College Hospital from China has also demonstrated that this stratification can effectively differentiate patient outcomes. 25 The modified IGCCCG classification has been widely recognized and adopted by most medical organizations. The current recommendations in the NCCN guidelines suggest three cycles of adjuvant chemotherapy for patients at low risk and four cycles for patients at intermediate and high risk. 4 MANAGEMENT OF EARLY-STAGE MALIGNANT OVARIAN GERM CELL TUMOR About 60–70% of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are diagnosed at an early stage, usually confined to one ovary. 3 The overall principle of malignant ovarian germ cell tumor treatment is to surgically remove all lesions while preserving fertility whenever possible and to administer timely and standardized postoperative chemotherapy. Surgery Historically, staging surgery for malignant ovarian germ cell tumors followed the surgical staging criteria for epithelial ovarian carcinoma. Growing evidence has suggested that comprehensive staging procedures such as omentectomy and lymph node dissection may not be necessary for early-stage malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. The Multicenter Italian Trials in Ovarian Cancer trial 26 and a large-scale database study 27 indicate that patients who underwent incomplete staging (without omentectomy or lymph node dissection) had slightly higher recurrence rates but no difference in overall survival compared with those who underwent complete staging surgery. Cross-sectional studies also reveal a clinical trend toward avoiding extensive surgical staging in young patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. In a large pediatric intergroup cohort study, 97% of patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors did not undergo lymph node dissection, 59% skipped contralateral ovarian biopsy, and 36% omitted omentectomy. The 6-year overall survival rate remained high (93–98%). Pathologic examinations further showed that lymph nodes and omentum with no gross abnormalities during surgery also lacked microscopic tumor involvement. 28 However, this study focused exclusively on children and adolescents, and its applicability to adult populations remains uncertain because of evolving surgical practices. The current ovarian cancer protocols for comprehensive surgical staging, which involve the removal of macroscopically normal structures, have shown no obvious evidence of benefit in terms of survival for patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors while potentially adding to morbidity. Some studies suggest limited utility of lymph node dissection in early-stage malignant ovarian germ cell tumors 27 ; others report lymph node dissection positivity rates up to 18–28% in advanced cases, 29 with nodal involvement correlating with survival outcomes. 30 Thus, lymph node dissection may still play a role in select patients at high risk. Thus, current clinical guidelines and consensus recommend comprehensive inspection of the peritoneal surfaces, omentum, and lymph nodes, as well as the contralateral ovary, for patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors undergoing initial staging surgery. 3 , 7 The MaGIC group advocates that the minimum requirement for staging of all patients with ovarian immature teratoma, whether pediatric or adult, should include 1) removal of the primary tumor, without violation of the tumor capsule if intact at the time of surgery (carefully document spontaneous, noniatrogenic rupture); 2) inspection and biopsy of any abnormal-appearing peritoneal surfaces, lymph nodes, omentum, and contralateral ovary; and 3) peritoneal fluid cytology. 31 Traditional open laparotomy is advised to ensure complete resection of the affected adnexa and to minimize tumor rupture, which risks abdominal implantation. Limited evidence exists on the effects of minimally invasive surgery (eg, laparoscopy) on malignant ovarian germ cell tumor outcomes, but its higher risk of intraoperative tumor spillage makes it generally unsuitable for suspected malignant ovarian germ cell tumor cases. If laparoscopic surgery is performed, it should be conducted by experienced gynecologic oncologists adhering to oncologic principles: The affected adnexa should be resected intact and extracted within a specimen bag. 3 Malignant Ovarian Germ Cell Tumors Diagnosed After Incomplete Staging For patients who underwent focal excision or cystectomy with pathologic diagnosis of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, there is controversy regarding whether to perform a second surgery for adnexectomy or staging. For patients who had focal excision or may have residual lesions, a second surgery can help remove all lesions. However, a comparison of survival outcomes showed that there was no significant difference between patients who underwent staging procedures and those that did not (event free survival 83% vs 84%). 30 The necessity of a second surgery remains to be explored. The current NCCN guidelines recommend that for patients whose pathology confirms malignant ovarian germ cell tumors after incomplete staging, a comprehensive decision should be based on the pathologic type, serum tumor markers, and presence of residual lesions and metastasis on imaging. For patients with seminoma or grade 1 immature teratoma, if both imaging and tumor marker tests are negative, follow-up may be considered. If the pathology is of other types and both postoperative imaging and tumor markers are negative, comprehensive staging can be skipped, and chemotherapy can be initiated directly. For patients with suspicious tumor remnants on imaging or positive tumor markers, comprehensive staging followed by standardized chemotherapy is recommended. 14 For patients with high-risk pathologic types such as yolk sac tumor or embryonal carcinoma, a second surgery should be performed as soon as possible to complete comprehensive surgical pathologic staging. However, in clinical practice, because a second surgery may not be feasible in a timely manner, administration of one course of neoadjuvant chemotherapy can be considered, followed by the second surgery. Postoperative Management Ovarian dysgerminomas have a favorable prognosis after complete resection. Dysgerminomas are highly sensitive to platinum-based chemotherapy; thus, the prognosis of recurrent disease is similar to that of primary disease. Adjuvant chemotherapy in immature teratoma is different between children and adult patients. There is a consensus that for adult patients with stage I and low-risk disease (stage I dysgerminoma or stage I grade 1 immature teratoma), postoperative adjuvant therapy can be omitted in favor of regular follow-up surveillance. 3 , 4 Pediatric and adolescent patients with stage IA or IB dysgerminoma; stage IA, grade 1 immature teratoma; stage IA embryonal carcinomas; or stage IA yolk sac tumors may consider observation or chemotherapy as treatment options. 4 The MaGIC consensus recommends surgery alone for ovarian immature teratoma in both pediatric and adult patients 31 because risk–benefit evaluation supports active surveillance over postoperative chemotherapy for stage I immature teratoma. 32 For adults with early-stage immature teratoma, a randomized controlled trial (COG-AGCT1531 [Active Surveillance, Bleomycin, Etoposide, Carboplatin or Cisplatin in Treating Pediatric and Adult Patients with Germ Cell Tumors], NCT03067181 ) is currently underway to evaluate outcomes with chemotherapy compared with active surveillance (Table 1 ). Research from Peking Union Medical College Hospital on the prognosis of patients with immature teratoma under active surveillance demonstrated that recurrence rates for stage I grade 2–3 teratoma under surveillance were comparable with those in patients who received chemotherapy. 33 Table 1. Active Surveillance Program in the Management of Ovarian Immature Teratoma Time Period Examination Pelvic Ultrasonography Tumor Markers Chest X-Ray C/A/P CT 1st year Monthly Every 2 mo Every 2 wk (1st 6 mo), then monthly Every 2 mo 1 mo * 3 mo † 12 mo 2nd year Every 2 mo Every 4 mo Every 2 mo Every 4 mo 3rd year Every 3 mo Every 6 mo Every 3 mo Every 6 mo 4th year Every 4 mo Every 4 mo Every 8 mo 5th–10th year Every 6 mo Every 6 mo Annually Open in a new tab C/A/P, chest/abdominal/pelvic; CT, computed tomography. Suggested surveillance program by European Society for Medical Oncology guideline. 7 * If not carried out preoperatively. † If clear, second-look laparoscopy if inadequate staging or immature teratoma. For other patients with early-stage malignant ovarian germ cell tumors except stage I dysgerminoma or immature teratoma, postoperative adjuvant chemotherapy is recommended. The pharmacologic treatment for malignant germ cell tumors involves primarily platinum-based multidrug combination chemotherapy. 3 , 7 In 1974, Dr. Lawrence Einhorn pioneered the use of a cisplatin-based combination regimen—cisplatin/vinblastine/bleomycin—for treating advanced testicular germ cell tumors in male patients. More than 70% of patients achieved tumor remission, marking the first time a cure for malignant germ cell tumors became possible. 34 In the 1980s, Dr. Stephen Williams replaced vinblastine with etoposide, establishing the bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen, which demonstrated comparable efficacy with fewer and more manageable side effects. 35 Since then, the bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen has remained the first-line chemotherapy protocol for malignant germ cell tumors. Over the past 40 years, aside from dose adjustments, this regimen has not been superseded by other treatments. The chemotherapy regimens commonly used for malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are listed in Table 2 . Table 2. Agents and Dosing of Chemotherapy Regimens Used for Malignant Ovarian Germ Cell Tumors Regimen Agents and Dosing Notes VAC 68 28-d cycle, 2–3 cycles Vincristine 1.5 mg/m 2 D1, D8, D15, D21 Dactinomycin 300 micrograms/m 2 D1–5 Cyclophosphamide 150 mg/m 2 IV D1–5 PVB 69 21-d cycle, 3–4 cycles Cisplatin 20 mg/m 2 D1–5 Vinblastine 0.15 mg/kg D1, D2 Bleomycin 30 units IV D2, D9, D16 BEP 70 21-d cycle, 3–4 cycles Cisplatin 20 mg/m 2 D1–5 Etoposide 100 mg/m 2 D1–5 Bleomycin 30 units IV D1, D8, D15 Adult MOGCT standard BEC 71 21-d cycle, 3–4 cycles Carboplatin 600 mg/m 2 (approximate AUC 7.9)—1,200 mg maximum D1 Etoposide 100 mg/m 2 D1–5 Bleomycin 15 units/m 2 (maximum 30 units) IV D1, D8, D15 CE 72 28-d cycle, 3 cycles Etoposide 120 mg/m 2 D1–3 Carboplatin 400 mg/m 2 (approximate AUC 5–6) D1 Adult ovarian dysgerminoma alternative PEb 73 21-d cycle, 3–4 cycles Cisplatin 20 mg/m 2 D1–5 Etoposide 100 mg/m 2 D1–5 Bleomycin 15 units/m 2 (maximum 30 units) D1 (once) Pediatric regimen JEb 74 21-d cycle, 4 cycles Carboplatin 600 mg/m 2 (approximate AUC 7.9)—1,200 mg maximum Etoposide 120 mg/m 2 D1–3 Bleomycin 15 units/m 2 (maximum 30 units) D1 (once) Pediatric alternative TIP 75 21-d cycle Paclitaxel 250 mg/m 2 D1 Ifosfamide 1,500 mg/m 2 D2–5 (with mesna rescue) Cisplatin 25 mg/m 2 D2–5 Refractory or recurrent disease VIP 76 21-d cycle Etoposide 100 mg/m 2 D1–5 Ifosfamide 1,200 mg/m 2 D1–5 (with mesna rescue) Cisplatin 20 mg/m 2 D1–5 Refractory or recurrent disease VeIP 77 21-d cycle Vinblastine 0.11 mg/kg D1, D2 Ifosfamide 1,200 mg/m 2 D1–5 (with mesna rescue) Cisplatin 20 mg/m 2 D1–5 Refractory or recurrent disease Open in a new tab VAC, vincristine/dactinomycin/cyclophosphamide; D, day; IV, intravenously; PVB, cisplatin/vinblastine/bleomycin; BEP, bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin; MOGCT, malignant ovarian germ cell tumors; BEC, bleomycin, etopside, carboplatin; AUC, area under the curve; CE, etoposide/carboplatin; PEb, cisplatin/etoposide/bleomycin; JEb, carboplatin/etoposide/bleomycin; TIP, paclitaxel/ifosfamide/cisplatin; VIP, etoposide/ifosfamide/cisplatin; VeIP, vinblastine/ifosfamide/cisplatin. According to the modified IGCCCG stratification, patients with stage II–IV dysgerminoma or immature teratoma, any-stage embryonal tumor or any stage yolk sac tumor, or nongestational choriocarcinoma are recommended to undergo chemotherapy with bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin. For patients with intermediate- and low-risk disease, a three-cycle bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen is administered; for patients at high risk, a four-cycle bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen is recommended. 4 Given that dysgerminomas are highly sensitive to platinum-based chemotherapy, an alternative regimen known, carboplatin/etoposide, which uses carboplatin as a substitute for cisplatin and omits bleomycin, has been demonstrated to be effective with reduced toxicity. A pooled analysis of data from three pediatric and three adult trials within the MaGIC data set further supports the inclusion of carboplatin in treatment regimens for dysgerminomas. 36 Although bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin remains the standard regimen, cumulative toxicities increase with cycle number, including bleomycin-induced pulmonary fibrosis, cisplatin-related nephrotoxicity, and etoposide-associated secondary malignancies. There are many attempts to use carboplatin as a substitute for cisplatin in nondysgerminoma patients. In children, carboplatin does not appear to be inferior to cisplatin. 37 However, because of the inferior outcomes with carboplatin in nonseminomatous testicular cancer in the 1990s, prospective trials such as GOG (Gynecologic Oncologist Group)-116 were closed early, and the study in this entity has been halted. 37 MaGIC has pooled 25 years of clinical trial data on pediatric and adolescent germ cell tumors and suggested that the difference between these results and the prior disappointing experience with carboplatin may be attributable to the pediatric dose being significantly higher and dosed on a more frequent basis. 37 A meta-analysis suggested that the efficacy of carboplatin is dose dependent, with high-dose carboplatin achieving better therapeutic effects and more tolerable toxicity profiles than cisplatin. 38 In 2017, COG-AGCT1531comparing the cisplatin and carboplatin regimens to treat germ cell tumor was opened. MANAGEMENT OF ADVANCED OR REFRACTORY AND RECURRENT MALIGNANT OVARIAN GERM CELL TUMORS Advanced Malignant Ovarian Germ Cell Tumors For adolescent and young adult patients with advanced-stage disease, fertility-sparing surgery may still be considered if one ovary appears normal. 7 The goal of surgery is optimal tumor cytoreduction, but initiating chemotherapy promptly after surgery is equally critical. 3 Neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by interval cytoreductive surgery is considered for rapidly progressing tumors, high tumor burden, or frail patients with pleural effusions, ascites, hypoalbuminemia, unstable cardiopulmonary status, or anesthesia intolerance. Direct cytoreductive surgery in frail patients may lead to severe complications. A bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin regimen (or cisplatin/etoposide dual therapy) may first be administered as neoadjuvant chemotherapy, followed by timely cytoreduction after stabilizing the patient''s general condition. Clinical studies demonstrate comparable survival outcomes between neoadjuvant chemotherapy with interval cytoreduction and primary cytoreduction but with significantly reduced surgical morbidity, particularly severe grade 4–5 adverse events. 39 However, for immature teratomas, which are relatively chemotherapy-resistant, primary cytoreductive surgery should be prioritized. Therefore, treatment decisions for patients with advanced malignant ovarian germ cell tumors require individualized assessment based on patient status, tumor type, and disease burden. For patients with advanced-stage malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, except for those with dysgerminoma, most fall into groups at intermediate or high risk and should receive three or four courses of bleomycin/etoposide/cisplatin chemotherapy postoperatively. Clinically, it is standard practice to administer two additional courses of consolidation chemotherapy after serum tumor markers have normalized. Patients with a high risk of recurrence, particularly those whose tumor markers have not normalized after two courses of postoperative chemotherapy, are classified as having refractory malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. Refractory or Recurrent Disease There are no standard recommendations for patients with refractory or recurrent disease. 13 Patients with persistent disease or relapse after primary chemotherapy are considered to have a less favorable prognosis. 7 Germ cell tumors are highly chemosensitive even at recurrence. Conventional-dose chemotherapy or high-dose chemotherapy is an acceptable option for first-salvage chemotherapy in testicular germ cell tumors. 6 For patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, the treatment of recurrent disease should still aim for cure, incorporating surgery, conventional-dose chemotherapy, and high-dose chemotherapy combined with hematopoietic stem cell transplantation. 40 For patients who have surgically resectable residual tumors, cytoreductive surgery should be performed with the goal of achieving complete resection. Paclitaxel/ifosfamide/cisplatin is recommended as a second-line treatment in patients with refractory or recurrent disease. 13 Other conventional-dose chemotherapy regimens include etoposide/ifosfamide/cisplatin, and vinblastine/ifosfamide/cisplatin. 41 Limited case reports of high-dose chemotherapy for recurrent ovarian germ cell tumor germ cell tumor suggested durable and prolonged remissions. 42 However, this is not widely used in malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, and the role of high-dose chemotherapy and stem cell transplantation is still unclear. 42 Because of the rarity and lack of consensus in recurrent or refractory malignant ovarian germ cell tumors, centralization of care in larger centers and collaboration of international networks may help improve clinical outcomes. SOMATIC TRANSFORMATION Although the majority of patients can be cured by surgery and platinum-based chemotherapy, 10% of patients will develop chemoresistance and are refractory to aggressive treatment. 43 , 44 A possible reason for the resistance is the somatic transformation of teratomatous components resembling either benign or malignant overgrowth. 45 Somatic transformation in germ cell tumors refers to the malignant evolution of components into non–germ cell malignancies such as sarcomas, carcinomas, neuroectodermal tumors, and, rarely, myeloid neoplasms. 46 – 48 This phenomenon complicates prognosis and treatment, particularly in recurrent or chemotherapy-resistant cases. Here we review current evidence on key subtypes, including growing teratoma syndrome, squamous cell carcinoma, and embryonic-type neuroectodermal tumors. Growing Teratoma Syndrome Growing teratoma syndrome is characterized by the paradoxical enlargement of teratoma components during or after chemotherapy, despite normalized tumor markers. It occurs in 7–10% of ovarian germ cell tumors and is driven by differentiation of malignant germ cells into mature somatic tissues rather than tumor proliferation. 49 Chemotherapy is hypothesized as a trigger for immature teratoma component converting to mature teratoma. 50 Growing teratoma syndrome often manifests as enlarging masses with cystic or solid components, typically in the pelvis or retroperitoneum. In ovarian cases, recurrence may involve peritoneal or distant metastases. 50 Complete surgical resection is critical because residual disease can lead to further malignant transformation. This highlights the importance of minimizing the use of chemotherapy for immature teratomas and instead focusing on vigilant imaging surveillance to monitor for disease recurrence. Squamous Cell Carcinoma Malignant transformation of mature teratomas is rare, occurring in less than 2% of cases. 51 However, squamous cell carcinoma is the most common type of malignancy, accounting for approximately 80% of all malignant transformations. 51 , 52 Long-standing teratomas, postmenopausal status, and large tumor size (larger than 10 cm) are associated with squamous cell carcinoma transformation. 51 Elevated markers such as squamous cell carcinoma antigen or CA 125 may signal malignancy, although specificity is low. Molecular testing of ovarian squamous cell carcinoma exhibited a median tumor mutation burden of 10.00 mutations per megabase (range 7.28–46.86), as well as PIK3CA mutations. 52 Radical surgery is preferred. However, because of the chemoresistance, squamous cell carcinoma transformation carries a poor prognosis. The recently reported tumor mutation burden level suggested that patients with squamous cell carcinoma derived from germ cell tumor may benefit from immunotherapy. 52 Embryonic-Type Neuroectodermal Tumors Tumors consisting predominantly of more primitive malignant neuroepithelial components are classified as primitive neuroectodermal tumor. The term “primitive neuroectodermal tumor” encompasses a diverse group of malignant neoplasms characterized by small round blue cells with neural differentiation that can arise from the central nervous system (CNS), bone and soft tissue, testis, the gynecologic tract, and other sites. 53 , 54 Ovarian primitive neuroectodermal tumors are extremely rare, with only about 20 cases reported in English literature. 55 With the increasing understanding of the heterogeneity and molecular biology of these tumor types, 56 the generalized term primitive neuroectodermal tumor is too vague and inaccurate to meet the need of modern pathologic diagnosis. The 2016 World Health Organization CNS classification has restructured the tumor categories on the basis of several molecular signatures, including the complete removal of the term primitive neuroectodermal tumor from the nomenclature and the use of embryonal tumors with further genetic classification. 57 The previous term primitive neuroectodermal tumor, used as a synonym for Ewing sarcoma, was eliminated from the 2013 edition of the World Health Organization Classification of Tumors of Soft Tissue and Bone to avoid confusion with similarly named lesions in the CNS and gynecologic tract, which typically do not exhibit the characteristic EWSR1 rearrangement. 57 Therefore, a new term, “embryonic-type neuroectodermal tumor,” is in demand to reduce confusion with Ewing sarcoma. 54 The molecular features of this tumor arising from ovaries remain largely unknown, increasing the difficulties in pathologic diagnosis. Incorporating both histologic and molecular information has facilitated pathologic diagnosis in CNS tumors and enabled a clear communication of tumor characteristics. 58 Aggressive resection combined with sarcoma-type regimens (eg, doxorubicin/ifosfamide) is recommended, although evidence remains anecdotal. 55 SURVEILLANCE AND SURVIVAL Surveillance of malignant ovarian germ cell tumors requires a tailored approach integrating biomarkers, imaging, and risk stratification. However, late complications such as chemotherapy toxicity and secondary cancers remain challenges. Late Complications Germ cell tumors are highly curable, resulting in a growing population of long-term survivors. However, curative treatments carry significant risks of late complications that profoundly affect survivorship. In testicular germ cell tumors, survivors face elevated rates of cardiovascular disease, hypertension, heart failure, metabolic syndrome, neuropathy, tinnitus, and secondary malignancies compared with the general population. 59 Although these risks are well documented in testicular germ cell tumors, patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors remain understudied. 60 Emerging evidence suggests that ovarian germ cell tumor survivors experience higher rates of hypertension 61 and increased risks of tinnitus 60 and hearing impairment. 62 Notably, secondary malignancies—a major concern in testicular germ cell tumor survivors—show less definitive association with ovarian germ cell tumors. A population-based cohort study found no secondary cancers in patients with ovarian germ cell tumor treated with surgery alone, whereas 12% of chemotherapy-treated patients and 18% of those receiving radiotherapy or chemotherapy developed secondary malignancies within 10 years. 8 Reducing unnecessary adjuvant cancer treatment is important in adolescent and young adult patients. Fertility also is frequently compromised. Retrospective cohort studies on malignant ovarian germ cell tumors indicate that 50–92.7% of patients undergoing fertility-sparing surgery retain regular menstruation, with posttreatment pregnancy success rates ranging from 23.2% to 45.3%. 63 – 65 Clinical fertility preservation strategies for female patients with cancer primarily include three approaches: oocyte or embryo cryopreservation, ovarian tissue freezing, and the use of gonadotropin-releasing hormone agonists during chemotherapy. 66 However, it is unclear what the effect of these methods are in fertility protection and obstetric outcomes in patients with malignant ovarian germ cell tumors. These late complications emphasis the importance of minimizing unnecessary adjuvant therapies such as chemotherapy and radiotherapy in adolescent and young adult patients with germ cell tumors. Tailoring treatment intensity to disease risk and prioritizing fertility preservation are critical to reducing long-term morbidity while maintaining high cure rates. Survival Survival outcomes for malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are generally favorable. The 5-year cause-specific survival rate in 2,541 malignant ovarian germ cell tumors identified in SEER 1978–2020 was 94% (97% in patients with dysgerminoma and 92% in those with nondysgerminoma). 8 However, population-based studies across different countries reveal variability in prognosis: In France, the 5-year net survival among patients diagnosed with ovarian germ cell tumors in 1989–2010 was 82%. 67 In the Netherlands, the 5-year overall survival rate for patients with germ cell tumor diagnosed in 1989–2015 was 73–88%. 9 The 20-year ovarian cancer–specific survival rate in Norwegian patients diagnosed 1980–2009 was 88% 10 A Chinese ovarian germ cell tumor registry data suggested that the 3-year disease free survival and overall survival rates were 82.3% and 94.1%. 25 Nondysgerminoma histology, advanced stage, and age older than 40 years are associated with inferior survival rates. 25 CONCLUSIONS Although malignant ovarian germ cell tumors are associated with excellent survival rates, the growing population of long-term cancer survivors underscores the urgent need to address lifelong health challenges. Late complications, chemotherapy-induced organ toxicity, and fertility impairment significantly affect quality of life and survivorship outcomes. Risk stratification is critical to optimizing care, enabling tailored de-escalation of adjuvant therapies in patients at low risk while preserving curative intent. Moving forward, multidisciplinary survivorship programs, coupled with biomarker-driven risk assessment, are essential to balancing cure with long-term well-being in this young patient population. Footnotes This study was supported by the National High Level Hospital Clinical Research Funding (2022-PUMCH-B-083), National Key Research and Development Program of China (2022YFC2704202), and National Key Clinical Specialty Construction Project (U114000). Financi