INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1618,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12459728','Retrospective Analysis of the Application Effect of Music Therapy Combined with Psychotherapy on the Labor Process and Delivery Outcome of Primiparas.','Yuan C, Huang C, Chen J, Zou P, Wang L, Shen F.','2025','primiparous primiparous nanchang jingdezhen panas cbsei c32 caq panas panas cbsei c32 caq essages primiparas primiparas primiparas ntroduction parturient multiparous primiparous primiparous multiparous primiparous primiparous primiparas primiparous primiparas abdelaziz primiparous inexperience nppm nppm melodic reframing calming primiparous primiparous aterials ethods primiparous primiparas primiparas primiparas jingdezhen jd2yy202504 primiparous doula pregestational parturients parturient animated parturients divert parturients lamaze drawings paintings calming parturient praised stamina primiparous melody calming mozart soundscapes birdsong synchronization primiparas soothing headphones pinghu detach serene scenes beaches parturient musical primipara forcibly upbeat rhythmically marching parturient songs parturient musical soothing calming lullabies parturient uplifting celebratory primipara puerperal panas panas caq cbsei c32 caq cbsei c32 esults primiparas primiparas predelivery puerperal parturient puerperal predelivery panas panas panas panas prepartum caq cbsei c32 cbsei c32 caq cbsei c32 caq caq cbsei c32 cbsei c32 caq cbsei c32 iscussion sjomark catalgol primiparas primiparas uncoordinated parturient parturient parturients primiparas parturients jahromi primiparas caq cbsei c32 divert parturients melody endorphins calming parturient parturient strangeness unease prelabor soothing uncoordinated parturients divert parturient calming cheerful pleasurable multichannel primiparous multiparous primipara baselines endorphin onclusion primiparas chaojun panhong jingdezhen jd2yy202504 eferences kananikandeh shokravi mirghafourvand jahanfar salutogenesis nop2 primiparas multiparas ijnss primiparas multiparas horsch garthus niegel ayers s1116 primipara feli heydarpour yazdanbakhsh heydarpour primiparous s12888 abdelaziz alshammari elsharkawy oraby ramadan tokophobia nori kassim helmi jcm12237203 zuarez erez zafran carmeli garmi salim s1246 ng235 birthi c2023 ng235 primiparas kjwhn huskisson sinicization sjomark svanberg catalgol ceber turfan alizadeh dibazari abdolalipour mirghafourvand primiparous panminerva s0031 boz akgun duman psychoeducation 0167482x jahromi rahmanian kiani taghipour e70051 hsr2 dhakal chalise bharati primigravid counc jnhrc v21i1 buglione saccone heiderscheit galbally gravina mutwalli himmerich ijerph20237120','Abstract Objective: This study analyzed the influence of music therapy combined with psychotherapy on the labor process and delivery outcomes in primiparous women. Methods: A retrospective analysis was conducted on the clinical data of 120 primiparous women who delivered at the People’s Hospital of Nanchang City and the First People’s Hospital of Jingdezhen between June 2021 and April 2024. Participants were divided into three groups: the combined group (receiving both music therapy and psychotherapy), the psychotherapy group, and the conventional care group. The study compared perinatal outcomes, psychological status [assessed using the Positive and Negative Affect Scale (PANAS)], childbirth self-efficacy [measured by the Childbirth Self-Efficacy Inventory (CBSEI-C32)], and fear of childbirth [evaluated using the Childbirth Attitudes Questionnaire (CAQ)] among the three groups. Results: The durations of the first and second stages of labor were significantly shorter in the combined group than in the psychotherapy and conventional care groups ( P < 0.05). At 24 hours postpartum, the combined group exhibited higher positive affect scores and lower negative affect scores on the PANAS compared to the other two groups ( P < 0.05). At 24 hours postpartum, the combined group showed higher positive emotional scores and lower negative emotional score on the PANAS ( P < 0.05) compared to the other groups. Additionally, the combined group demonstrated significantly higher outcome expectancy and self-efficacy scores on the CBSEI-C32, and lower CAQ scores, indicating reduced fear of childbirth ( P < 0.05). Additionally, the combined group reported lower pain levels, as measured by the Visual Analog Scale at 3 cm cervical dilation and at full cervical dilation ( P < 0.05). Neonates in the combined group had higher Apgar scores at 5 minutes post-delivery, and their mothers experienced reduced postpartum hemorrhage ( P < 0.05). Conclusion: The integration of music therapy with psychotherapy during labor is effective in alleviating labor pain, enhancing psychological well-being, reducing fear and psychological stress associated with childbirth, improving delivery outcomes, and minimizing adverse maternal and neonatal events. Keywords: music therapy, psychotherapy, delivery K EY M ESSAGES (1) Music therapy combined with psychotherapy can speed up the first and second stages of labor in primiparas and reduce the occurrence of adverse delivery outcomes for mothers and infants. (2) Music therapy combined with psychotherapy is more conducive to alleviating negative emotions of primiparas, alleviating psychological stress, and reducing labor pain. (3) Music therapy combined with psychotherapy can improve delivery efficiency in primiparas and reduce their fear of delivery. I NTRODUCTION Childbirth fear is a psychological stress response characterized by tension, anxiety, and apprehension experienced by pregnant and parturient women regarding the process and potential risks of childbirth. Compared with multiparous women, primiparous women tend to exhibit a greater degree of childbirth-related fear due to their lack of childbirth experience.[ 1 , 2 ] Previous studies have identified fear of childbirth as a potential predictor of labor pain intensity, with primiparous women demonstrating heightened sensitivity to labor pain compared to multiparous women. In addition to contributing to pain perception, negative emotions—such as childbirth fear—can impair uterine contractility, prolong the duration of labor, and increase the likelihood of conversion to cesarean delivery. These psychological stressors may also elevate the risk of perinatal depression and postpartum post-traumatic stress disorder, thereby adversely affecting the overall quality of life in primiparous women.[ 3 , 4 ] A cross-sectional survey investigating the childbirth experiences of primiparous women and their association with future fertility intentions found that negative childbirth experiences can reduce the willingness to have additional children. This trend may have long-term implications for the demographic composition and population growth of a country or region.[ 5 ] These findings underscore the critical importance of implementing targeted nursing interventions during the perinatal period to mitigate childbirth fear and foster positive birth experiences in primiparas. The World Health Organization, in its 2018 guidelines on improving the childbirth experience, emphasized the need for enhanced perinatal care—particularly for primiparous women—through improved intrapartum care.[ 6 ] However, conventional perinatal care remains predominantly focused on physiological monitoring and labor management, often neglecting the psychological and emotional needs of primiparas at different stages of labor. This gap in care limits the effectiveness of traditional approaches in addressing negative emotional responses, such as the fear of childbirth. Psychotherapy is a nursing model based on established psychological theories—such as cognitive behavioral theory, mindfulness-based psychology, and humanistic psychology—which aims to improve patients’ psychological well-being through structured communication strategies and systematic psychological support techniques.[ 7 ] A randomized controlled trial conducted by Abdelaziz et al .[ 8 ] involving pregnant women in Egypt showed that psychotherapy could considerably reduce the fear of childbirth and enhanced maternal self-efficacy. Notably, the pain associated with natural childbirth represents a profound physiological stressor, particularly for primiparous women, who often exhibit maladaptive psychological responses due to inexperience. Such responses may compromise emotional regulation during labor, increase physical strain, and ultimately hinder the progress of labor. Therefore, in addition to addressing psychological distress, effective pain management during the perinatal period plays a crucial role in facilitating favorable delivery outcomes. Pharmacological analgesia remains a widely used and effective approach; however, its associated limitations and potential risks to both mother and neonate have prompted a clinical search for safe and noninvasive alternatives.[ 9 , 10 ] In line with this, the 2023 intrapartum care guidelines issued by the United Kingdom’s National Institute for Health and Care Excellence endorsed the clinical value of nonpharmacological pain management (NPPM) and recommended its prioritization in labor analgesia.[ 11 ] Among various NPPM strategies, music therapy is commonly employed in clinical practice. Through melodic structure and rhythmic patterns, music can induce physiological relaxation and modulate individuals’ subjective perception of pain through mechanisms often described as “emotional–cognitive restructuring.” A study by An et al .[ 12 ] affirmed that music therapy during labor effectively reduces pain intensity and enhances the overall childbirth experience. Conceptually, the cognitive reframing provided by psychological therapy and the physiologically calming effects of music therapy may exert a synergistic effect. Their integration holds the potential to yield multidimensional benefits, encompassing cognitive, emotional, and physiological domains of maternal care. However, the application of combined psychotherapy and music therapy interventions in primiparous women remains in its early stages, with limited systematic evidence available to evaluate their combined effectiveness and underlying mechanisms. Against this backdrop, the present study focuses on primiparous women as a specific population of interest, aiming to rigorously assess the clinical impact of integrating music therapy with psychotherapy on labor progression and delivery outcomes. The findings are intended to inform the development of a more targeted, psychologically and physiologically integrated perinatal care model for this vulnerable patient group. M ATERIALS AND M ETHODS General Information From June 2021 to April 2024, a total of 128 primiparous women were initially recruited for the study. Following the application of inclusion and exclusion criteria, 120 participants were finally included in the final analysis. Exclusions consisted of one case involving premature rupture of membranes prior to the onset of labor, two cases that required conversion to cesarean section, and five cases with incomplete clinical data. Informed consent was obtained from all participants prior to their inclusion in the study. Participant grouping was based on the chronological implementation of updates to the hospital’s nursing protocols. Specifically, 40 primiparas who received the conventional nursing plan between June 2021 and May 2022 were assigned to the conventional group. A total of 42 primiparas who received the conventional nursing plan supplemented with psychological therapy between June 2022 and May 2023 were assigned to the psychotherapy group. Finally, 38 primiparas who received the conventional nursing plan combined with psychological therapy and music therapy between June 2023 and April 2024 were assigned to the combined group. The Medical Ethics Review of the First People’s Hospital of Jingdezhen (Jd2yy202504) approved the study. The study was conducted in strict accordance with the ethical principles outlined in the World Medical Association’s Declaration of Helsinki.[ 13 ] Inclusion and Exclusion Criteria Participants were included in the study if they met the following criteria: normal childbearing age (20–35 years); being primiparous; carrying a singleton, full-term pregnancy (gestational age of 37–42 weeks at delivery); willingness to undergo vaginal delivery with prenatal evaluations confirming suitability for natural childbirth; no clinical indications for cesarean section; normal cognitive function with the ability to understand, communicate effectively, and cooperate with questionnaire-based assessments; fetal heart monitoring and maternal pelvic measurements confirmed to be within normal levels prior to labor; and complete clinical data. Exclusion criteria included the use of epidural anesthesia or other pharmacological methods of labor analgesia, or the presence of a doula during childbirth. High-risk pregnancies, premature rupture of membranes prior to the onset of labor, and a history of pelvic floor dysfunction or uterine curettage also led to exclusion. Women with serious pre-existing medical conditions—such as autoimmune diseases, pregestational diabetes, chronic nephritis, moderate to severe anemia, congenital heart disease, thyroid dysfunction, chronic hypertension, thrombocytopenia, moderate to severe pneumonia, asthma, epilepsy, myasthenia gravis, or malignant tumors—were excluded. Additional exclusion criteria included a history of anxiety or depression, the presence of other objective factors that could interfere with the natural labor process, and any intrapartum conversion to cesarean section. Nursing Methods Conventional Group Participants in the conventional care group received routine perinatal nursing interventions. Prior to entering the delivery room, parturients were given verbal instructions regarding key aspects of the labor process and informed of matters requiring attention during childbirth. Upon admission to the delivery room, they were encouraged to actively cooperate with the attending midwife or nursing staff. Throughout labor, the nursing team closely monitored each parturient’s vital signs and provided appropriate comfort measures and verbal encouragement to support emotional stability and physical endurance during delivery. Psychotherapy Group Perinatal care (identical to that provided to the conventional group) was combined with psychotherapy. The psychotherapy measures were as follows: (1) during the latent phase of the first stage of labor, individualized instruction was provided based on the mother’s personality traits and educational background. Using natural childbirth process models and animated demonstrations, medical staff explained the principles, stages, and advantages of natural childbirth in accessible language, along with potential risks and corresponding countermeasures. Spouses were encouraged to accompany the parturients, share recent events to help divert attention, and support the mother’s efforts to eat, drink, and rest to conserve energy. (2) During the active phase of the first stage of labor, parturients were taught abdominal bearing-down techniques and Lamaze breathing through video demonstrations. (3) During the second stage of labor, psychological support was provided through environmental, linguistic, behavioral, and empathetic interventions. Environment: Children’s drawings and landscape paintings were displayed in the delivery room, and lighting was adjusted to promote a calming atmosphere. Language: Authoritative and encouraging verbal strategies were employed, with ideal index values (e.g., fetal descent) emphasized instead of actual metrics to maintain optimism. Behavior: Caregivers used eye contact, facial expressions, and physical contact to enhance the parturient’s comfort and self-esteem. Verbal contracts, such as “Remember to follow my breathing instructions during contractions,” were established, and positive reinforcement was provided for good performance. Empathy: Caregivers shared their own delivery experiences, praised the mother’s efforts, and provided guidance on energy management and physical stamina during delivery. Combined Group Participants in the combined group received comprehensive perinatal care that integrated the routine nursing measures provided to the conventional group and the structured psychotherapy interventions administered to the psychotherapy group, along with a music therapy program. The music therapy intervention was tailored to each stage of labor to address the psychological and physiological needs of primiparous women throughout the childbirth process. The first stage of labor is characterized by cervical dilation from 0 to 10 cm, along with increasing contractions, pain, and anxiety. During the incubation period of the first stage of labor (cervical dilation of 0–3 cm), specific music with a soft melody and slow rhythm was used to create a calming environment and foster a sense of security. Examples included light instrumental music, classical compositions (e.g., by Mozart or Bach), and natural soundscapes such as birdsong or ocean waves. In addition, personalized music preferences were considered to enhance emotional comfort. The music was played continuously until the end of the latent phase, and volume level was adjusted according to individual comfort. In the active phase of the first stage of labor (cervical dilation 3–7 cm), a combination of music-guided imagery and music synchronization was implemented. Primiparas listened to soothing music through headphones—such as piano pieces and traditional Chinese compositions, including Autumn Moon on the Spring River and Autumn Moon on Pinghu —with guidance to concentrate on the music, mentally detach from contraction pain, and regulate their breathing rhythm. Visualization prompts encouraged participants to imagine serene scenes, such as beaches and forests. The music was played continuously. This method continued until the end of the active phase. During the transition period of the first stage (cervical dilation 7–10 cm), music with a slightly faster rhythm was introduced to maintain positive emotions, and family members were encouraged to accompany the parturient and share the musical experience to provide emotional support. The music was played until the end of the transition phase of the first stage. In the second stage of labor, the primipara’s cervix is completely expanded and the fetus needs to be forcibly delivered. Upbeat, rhythmically clear music, such as athletic marching music or the parturient’s preferred popular songs, was played at a moderate tempo to foster concentration and strengthen the parturient’s sense of control. This musical rhythm was used to guide breathing and pushing efforts. During rest intervals between contractions, soothing music was played to facilitate brief periods of relaxation. The intervention continued until the completion of the second stage of labor. Finally, in the third stage of labor, when the fetus and placenta are delivered and physical exhaustion is paired with emotional relief, soft and calming music, such as lullabies or gentle classical pieces, was played to relax the parturient both physically and emotionally. During mother–infant bonding and skin-to-skin contact, uplifting and celebratory music was played to mark the birth of the newborn and support the primipara in experiencing the emotional fulfillment of first-time motherhood. Music was played continuously until the end of the third stage of labor. Observation Indicators (1) Perinatal conditions: The three groups were compared in terms of the duration of the first, second, and third stages of labor, volume of blood loss within 2 hours after delivery, Apgar scores at 5 minutes post-birth, maternal pain levels, and maternal complications.[ 14 ] The Apgar score, which assesses neonatal physical condition, includes five parameters: skin color, heart rate, response to stimulation, muscle tone, and respiration. Each item was scored 0 to 2 points, and the total score ranged from 0 to 10. A higher score indicates better neonatal health. Maternal pain was assessed using the Visual Analog Scale (VAS), which ranges from 0 (no pain) to 10 (most severe pain).[ 15 ] Pain levels were measured at the onset of labor, at 3 cm cervical dilation, and upon complete cervical dilation. Maternal complications assessed included postpartum hemorrhage (defined as ≥500 mL of blood loss within 24 hours postpartum), puerperal infection, and premature rupture of membranes. (2) Mental state: The Chinese version of the Positive and Negative Affect Scale (PANAS) was used to evaluate the maternal emotional state 24 hours before and after childbirth. The PANAS includes two subscales: negative affect (10 items, including items 2, 4, 6, 7, 8, 11, 13, 15, 18, and 20) and positive affect (10 items, including items 1, 3, 5, 9, 10, 12, 14, 16, 17, and 19).[ 16 ] This tool provided a structured evaluation of emotional changes across the perinatal period. (3) Fear of delivery and delivery efficiency: Psychological readiness and confidence related to childbirth were assessed using the Childbirth Attitudes Questionnaire (CAQ) and the Chinese version of the Childbirth Self-Efficacy Inventory (CBSEI-C32), which were administered before and 24 hours after delivery.[ 17 , 18 ] The CAQ contains 16 items covering four dimensions: self-control (four items), fetal health concerns (five items), medical care concerns (three items), and fear of labor pain and injury (four items). Each item is scored from 1 (never afraid) to 4 (severely afraid), and the total score ranged from 16 to 64. Higher scores indicate greater fear of childbirth. The CBSEI-C32 includes two dimensions: outcome expectancy (16 items) and self-efficacy expectancy (16 items). Each item was rated on a 10-point Likert scale. For outcome expectancy, scores ranged from 1 (completely unhelpful) to 10 (extremely helpful), and for self-efficacy expectancy, scores ranged from 1 (completely negative) to 10 (very positive), resulting in a total score of 32 to 320. Higher scores reflect stronger childbirth self-efficacy. Statistical Methods Data organization and table creation were performed using Microsoft Excel (Version 2206, Microsoft Corporation, Redmond, WA, USA), while statistical analyses were conducted using SPSS software (Version 27.0, IBM Corporation, USA). Measurement data conforming to a normal distribution were expressed as mean ± standard deviation (x̄± s ). The Shapiro–Wilk test was used to assess the normality of the data, and results confirmed that the data were normally distributed ( P > 0.05). Homogeneity of variance was tested using Levene’s test. When the assumption of homogeneity was met ( P > 0.05), independent sample t -tests were used for between-group comparisons, and paired sample t -tests were used for within-group comparisons. In cases of unequal variances, a corrected t -test ( t ′ test) was applied. Independent t -tests were used for comparisons between two groups at the same time point. Categorical (count) data were expressed as frequency and percentage [ n (%)], and the χ 2 test was used for analysis. When the minimum expected cell frequency was less than 5, a continuity-corrected χ 2 test was applied. If the sample size was less than 40 or the theoretical frequency ( T ) was less than 1, Fisher’s exact test was used. For comparisons involving more than two groups, one-way analysis of variance was performed, followed by the least significant difference (LSD) t -test for pairwise comparisons. A P value of less than 0.05 was considered statistically significant. R ESULTS Three Groups of General Information The general data of the three groups of primiparas were well balanced ( P > 0.05; Table 1 ). Table 1. Comparison of General Information of Primiparas in Three Groups. Index Conventional Group ( n = 40) Psychotherapy Group ( n = 42) Combine