47 points, respectively) were significantly lower than those in the psychological therapy group (4.37 ± 0.46 and 7.18 ± 0.55 points) and the conventional group (4.69 ± 0.54 and 7.53 ± 0.62 points), with statistically significant differences ( P < 0.05). The psychological therapy group also had significantly lower VAS scores at these dilation stages compared to the conventional group ( P < 0.05). No statistically significant difference in VAS scores at the onset of labor was found among the three groups (combined group: 3.81 ± 0.32; psychological therapy group: 3.85 ± 0.34; conventional group: 3.88 ± 0.37; P > 0.05). The 5-minute Apgar score of newborns in the combined group (9.62 ± 0.17) was significantly higher than that in both the conventional (8.81 ± 0.36) and psychological therapy groups (8.89 ± 0.38; P < 0.05). The volume of postpartum bleeding at 2 hours in the combined group (140.63 ± 10.52 mL) was significantly lower than that in both the conventional group (169.31 ± 14.26 mL) and the psychological therapy group (166.59 ± 13.64 mL), with statistically significant differences ( P < 0.05). No significant differences were observed in the 5-minute Apgar scores and 2-hour postpartum bleeding volumes between the conventional group and the psychological therapy group ( P > 0.05). Additionally, there were no statistically significant differences in the incidence of puerperal complications among the three groups ( P > 0.05). For detailed data, refer to Tables 2 and 3 . Table 2. Comparison of Perinatal Conditions among the Three Groups (x̄± s ). Group Time of Labor (min) Hemorrhage 2 h after Delivery (mL) Apgar Score of Newborn 5 min after Birth (Points) VAS Score of Parturient at Different Time Points (Points) First Stage of Labor Second Stage of Labor Third Stage of Labor Onset of Labor Cervical Dilation of 3 cm Cervical Dilation Complete Conventional group ( n = 40) 545.98 ± 33.75 62.57 ± 4.82 12.55 ± 3.27 169.31 ± 14.26 8.81 ± 0.36 3.88 ± 0.37 4.69 ± 0.54 7.53 ± 