y and music therapy more effectively improves the maternal emotional state during the early postpartum period. For detailed data, refer to Table 4 . Table 4. Comparison of Dimensions of Positive and Negative Affect Scale between Three Groups Before and After Delivery(x̄± s , Point). Group Positive Emotional Score of PANAS Negative Emotional Score of PANAS Antenatal Period 24 h Postpartum Antenatal Period 24 h Postpartum Conventional group ( n = 40) 23.71 ± 3.56 33.78 ± 3.75* 39.36 ± 3.68 24.82 ± 2.76* Psychotherapy group ( n = 42) 24.23 ± 3.52 38.33 ± 4.14* ,† 38.51 ± 3.73 21.69 ± 2.42* ,† Combined group ( n = 38) 23.86 ± 3.49 42.53 ± 4.68* ,†,‡ 38.83 ± 3.65 18.21 ± 2.03* ,†,‡ F 0.237 42.402 0.553 72.240 P 0.789 <0.001 0.577 <0.001 Open in a new tab Compared with the group before intervention, * P < 0.05; Compared with the conventional group, † P < 0.05; Compared with psychotherapy group, ‡ P < 0.05; PANAS = Positive and Negative Affect Scale Fear of Delivery and Delivery Efficiency Compared with the prepartum period, CAQ scores of all three groups significantly decreased at 24 hours postpartum, indicating a reduction in fear of childbirth. Concurrently, both the outcome expectation and self-efficacy expectation scores on the CBSEI-C32 significantly increased, reflecting improved confidence in childbirth ( P < 0.05). At 24 hours postpartum, the combined group exhibited significantly higher CBSEI-C32 outcome expectation and self-efficacy expectation scores (119.78 ± 12.47 and 114.28 ± 10.26 points, respectively) compared to the conventional group (106.33 ± 8.63 and 98.53 ± 7.25 points, respectively) and the psychotherapy group (112.57 ± 10.42 and 105.62 ± 8.73 points, respectively). Additionally, the CAQ score in the combined group (21.25 ± 2.36 points) was significantly lower than those in the conventional group (28.75 ± 3.54 points) and the psychotherapy group (25.68 ± 3.09 points), indicating a greater reduction in fear of delivery ( P < 0.05). Furthermore, the psychotherapy group also demonstrated significantly higher CBSEI-C32 scores and a lower CAQ score compared to the conventional group ( P < 0.05). These results suggest that the combination of psychotherapy and music therapy yields superior outcomes in enhancing maternal self-efficacy and reducing childbirth-related fear. Refer to Table 5 for detailed data. Table 5. Comparison of Childbirth Attitudes Questionnaire Scores and Childbirth Self-Efficacy Inventory Scales in the Three Groups Before and After Delivery (x̄± s , Point). Group CAQ Score Expected Score of the CBSEI-C32 Scale Results Self-efficacy Expectation Score of the CBSEI-C32 Scale Antenatal Period 24 h Postpartum Antenatal Period 24 h Postpartum Antenatal Period 24 h Postpartum Conventional group ( n = 40) 40.25 ± 4.36 28.75 ± 3.54* 82.87 ± 4.51 106.33 ± 8.63* 85.51 ± 5.22 98.53 ± 7.25* Psychotherapy group ( n = 42) 39.48 ± 4.25 25.68 ± 3.09* ,† 83.35 ± 4.62 112.57 ± 10.42* ,† 84.78 ± 4.86 105.62 ± 8.73* ,† Combined group ( n = 38) 40.19 ± 4.28 21.25 ± 2.36* ,†,‡ 82.31 ± 4.23 119.78 ± 12.47* ,†,‡ 84.02 ± 4.79 114.28 ± 10.26* ,†,‡ F 0.408 59.533 0.542 15.743 0.879 16.528 P 0.666 <0.001 0.583 <0.001 0.418 <0.001 Open in a new tab CAQ = Childbirth Attitudes Questionnaire, CBSEI-C32 = Childbirth Self-Efficacy Inventory; Compared with the group before intervention, * P < 0.05; Compared with the conventional group, † P < 0.05; Compared with psychotherapy group, ‡ P < 0.05. D ISCUSSION The results of this study show that compared with the conventional and psychotherapy groups, the combined group had shorter first and second stages of labor and higher 5-minute Apgar scores. Furthermore, they also demonstrated higher positive emotion scores, lower negative emotion scores, and a lower incidence of postpartum hemorrhage within 2 hours. These findings are consistent with the conclusions of Sjömark et al .[ 19 ] and Çatalgöl et al .,[ 20 ] indicating that music therapy combined with psychotherapy can shorten the first and second stages of labor for primiparas and improve psychological well-being and obstetric outcomes. The observed benefits may be attributed to the effect of emotional states. Excessive fear of childbirth or emotional tension in primiparas can activate the sympathetic nervous system, leading to elevated levels of stress hormones, such as catecholamines, resulting in uncoordinated or weakened contractions and increased postpartum bleeding. In psychological therapy, personalized health education implemented during the first stage of labor help first-time mothers to establish a correct understanding of the natural childbirth process, correct misinformation about natural childbirth, and acquire practical skills and coping strategies. In addition, such interventions can provide information about possible risks during the childbirth process and corresponding coping measures, thereby reducing anxiety, negative emotions, and psychological stress, particularly childbirth-related anxiety caused by a lack of childbirth knowledge and concerns about the health and safety of the fetus during natural delivery. This in turn fosters a stronger sense of self-efficacy and control. Partner support, through shared stories and emotional presence, can activate the mother’s brain reward circuit through the social support system; release pleasure hormones, such as oxytocin; enhance maternal confidence; and improve childbirth efficiency.[ 21 , 22 ] During the second stage of labor, positive psychological cues can be provided to the parturient from various aspects, such as the environment, language, behavior, and empathy. Physical contact and eye contact can convey care, and establishing a simple verbal agreement can help a parturient establish a sense of order. Additionally, immediate feedback can reinforce positive behavior and form a “positive behavior–reward” cycle. Motivational psychological intervention can enhance parturients’ confidence and belief in natural childbirth, assist primiparas in adjusting their psychological state and achieve a positive transformation toward adaptive responses. These mechanisms help parturients maintain a continuously high level of excitement during the delivery process, enhance childbirth efficiency and perinatal compliance, and actively cooperate with the delivery process, thereby minimizing the duration of the first and second stages of labor.[ 23 ] Jahromi et al .[ 24 ] applied music therapy to full-term pregnant women in Iran; their results indicated that music therapy can improve maternal and infant outcomes by reducing anxiety and pain perception. Gautam et al .[ 25 ] conducted a study on the effect of music therapy on labor pain intensity in primiparas at tertiary hospitals, demonstrating that music therapy was helpful in reducing pain sensation during the first stage of labor (latent and active phases). These findings are consistent with those of the present study: Compared with the conventional and psychotherapy groups after the intervention, the combined group had lower CAQ scores and lower VAS scores at both 3 cm cervical dilation and full dilation and higher expectation scores and self-efficacy expectation scores in the CBSEI-C32 results. The use of music therapy during the perinatal period can not only divert the attention of parturients from labor pain to the melody, but also promote the release of endorphins and stimulate dopamine secretion in the brain, achieving a nonpharmacological calming effect. During the latent phase of the first stage of labor, playing a parturient’s preferred music can activate positive memory associations and form a “conditioned relaxation response,” which helps to eliminate the parturient’s sense of strangeness and unease toward the hospital and relieve prelabor tension. During the active phase of the first stage of labor, playing soothing music (such as piano music or light music) can stimulate the limbic system through the auditory nerve, reduce the secretion of stress hormones such as adrenaline and cortisol, regulate breathing and heart rate, relieve uterine muscle tension, reduce pain caused by uncoordinated contractions due to anxiety and fear, and help parturients enter a relaxed state, thereby alleviating fatigue and restoring physical strength. “Music visualization” can divert the parturient’s attention away from contraction pain through visualized intentions, thereby reducing cognitive processing of pain stimuli and lowering the subjective perception of pain.[ 26 ] Playing moderately paced classical music or the sound of ocean waves during the latent phase of the first stage of labor can evoke calming emotional memories and reduce pain sensitivity exacerbated by fear. During the second stage of labor, playing cheerful music synchronized with the rhythm of breathing and heart rate can help regulate the autonomic nervous system, activate the descending inhibitory pathway in the spinal dorsal horn, reduce the transmission of pain signals to the cerebral cortex, and increase the secretion of pleasurable hormones, such as dopamine and oxytocin, thereby enhancing the efficiency of uterine contractions and preventing prolonged labor from causing persistent pain. Moreover, encouraging music can convey a sense of “power” and “achievement,” thereby enhancing a mother’s sense of control over the delivery process. Varying the tempo from slow to medium can stimulate the mother’s physical and mental energy, boost confidence in natural childbirth, improve the efficiency of labor, reduce negative expectations related to pain, break the vicious cycle of “fear–tension–prolonged labor,” and establish a positive feedback loop of “confidence–relaxation–efficient contractions.”[ 27 ] During the third stage of labor, playing warm and family-friendly music can help highlight the joy of a new mother and enhance the pleasant feeling of the bond between mother and baby, maintaining a positive mood. Through the negative correlation between positive emotions and pain, the overall delivery experience can be significantly improved. The combination of music and psychological therapies can produce a comprehensive analgesic effect through a multichannel mechanism involving psychological adjustment, behavioral coordination, environmental support, and endocrine regulation, improving the delivery experience of first-time mothers and enhancing the quality of delivery. This study has several limitations. First, as a retrospective analysis, it relied on previously recorded medical data, which may affect the reliability and comparability of certain variables across participants. Second, standardization in the implementation of the intervention was lacking. Specifically, variations in the type of music selected (as preferences differed among participants), as well as the timing and the frequency of music therapy sessions introduced clinical heterogeneity, potentially affecting the consistency and reproducibility of the intervention effects. Third, the study population was limited to primiparous women delivering at a single hospital, and the sample size was relatively small. These factors may have introduced sample bias related to regional, institutional, or demographic characteristics, thereby limiting the generalizability of the results to broader populations, including multiparous women, those with obstetric complications, or women with multiple pregnancies. Fourth, participant grouping was based on different time periods, which may have introduced time-related confounding factors. Notably, the composition of the primipara population over the study period had been changed by shifts in national fertility policies or the hospital’s service orientation, and these changes may have influenced baseline characteristics (e.g., increased proportion of advanced maternal age), further impacting the external validity of the findings. Moreover, individual differences in pain tolerance and subjective interpretation of pain scores (e.g., VAS) may have influenced the precision of pain-related outcomes. To strengthen the evidence base, future research ought to utilize prospective, randomized controlled designs with rigorously matched baselines and larger, more diverse cohorts. The development of standardized operating procedures for psychological and music interventions is recommended to ensure consistency in the intervention type, duration, and intensity. Incorporating objective physiological indicators of pain, such as serum β-endorphin levels, may enhance the accuracy of pain assessments and reduce bias associated with self-reported pain scales. C ONCLUSION Music therapy combined with psychological therapy has demonstrated the ability to significantly shorten the first and second stages of labor in primiparas, alleviate labor pain, improve maternal psychological well-being, reduce psychological stress and fear of childbirth, enhance labor efficiency, and lower the incidence of adverse maternal and neonatal outcomes. Given its nonpharmacological nature, comprehensive benefits, and ease of implementation, this integrative approach holds substantial clinical value and is worthy of broader application and promotion in obstetric practice. Availability of data and materials The original contributions presented in the study are included in the article. Further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding authors. Author contributions Chaojun Yuan, Fei Shen, Chong Huang: Conceptualization, methodology, writing—original draft, writing—review experimental studies, editing, project administration, data collection, data analysis, and statistical analysis. Jia Chen, Panhong Zhou, Li Wang: Data curation, experimental studies, literature search, writing—review, formal analysis, editing, and supervision. Ethical approval and consent to participate The Medical Ethics Review of the First People’s Hospital of Jingdezhen (Jd2yy202504) approved the study. Informed consent was obtained from all participants. Conflict 