INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1625,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12454337','Editorial: Update on the diagnosis and surgical treatment of gynecological tumors.','Cianci S, Capozzi VA, Restaino S.','2025','ncrnas cianci adnex restaino polypoid adenomyoma grammatical rockall rangarajan kubik huch nature10166 stelloo nout osse jurgenliemk jobsen lutgens perelli mattei scambia cavaliere restaino arcieri mariuzzi orsaria tulisso thoburn afsari danilova aar3247 cavaliere perelli zaami piergentili mattei vizzielli healthcare9080965 piedmonte spirtos eisenkop schlaerth mannel lap2 melamed margul nejmoa1804923 bentivegna gouy maulard pautier mdw311','Editorial on the Research Topic Update on the diagnosis and surgical treatment of gynecological tumors Gynecological tumors, including malignancies of the ovaries, uterus, cervix, vulva, and vagina, account for a significant portion of cancer-related morbidity and mortality among women worldwide ( 1 ). Recent advances in diagnostic techniques and surgical interventions have significantly impacted clinical practice by contributing to earlier detection, better risk stratification, and more personalized therapeutic strategies. Advances in imaging technology have improved the accuracy of preoperative tumor characterization. These tools facilitate precise staging of gynecologic malignancies ( 1 , 2 ). Simultaneously, molecular diagnostics and next-generation sequencing have become integral to the management of ovarian and endometrial cancers. Identification of pathogenic variants such as BRCA1/2, TP53, and mismatch repair (MMR) gene deficiencies allows for more precise classification and targeted treatment planning ( 3 – 5 ). Emerging diagnostic modalities, such as liquid biopsies, offer a non-invasive way to detect circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) and other tumor-derived markers, providing a promising approach for monitoring disease recurrence and therapeutic response in real time ( 6 , 7 ). Other promising technologies are available in the form of non-coding RNAs (ncRNAs) in gynecologic malignancies, particularly endometrial cancer ( 7 , 8 ). While they are still undergoing validation for widespread clinical use, early studies show considerable potential in gynecologic oncology. Surgical treatment remains the primary modality for most gynecologic cancers. Over the past decade, a paradigm shift toward minimally invasive surgery (MIS) has occurred, with laparoscopic and robotic-assisted techniques being increasingly preferred due to their favorable perioperative outcomes. Studies have demonstrated equivalent oncologic safety for selected early-stage endometrial and cervical cancers, along with reduced morbidity and shorter recovery times ( 6 – 10 ; Cianci et al. ). Robotic-assisted surgery, in particular, has gained prominence due to its enhanced visualization, dexterity, and ergonomics. While the upfront costs remain a limiting factor, this technology continues to expand, particularly in high-volume centers. For complex cases, such as radical hysterectomy or staging for high-grade endometrial carcinoma, robotics offers technical advantages that may translate into improved functional outcomes, especially when nerve-sparing procedures are employed ( 9 , 11 ). The management of gynecologic cancers is inherently multidisciplinary. Tumor boards that integrate surgical oncologists, medical oncologists, pathologists, radiologists, and reproductive specialists have become essential for guiding individualized treatment plans. Concurrently, artificial intelligence is beginning to influence diagnostic imaging and risk prediction models, although clinical integration is still in its early stages. The diagnosis and surgical treatment of gynecological tumors continue to evolve, driven by technological innovation, molecular medicine, and a growing emphasis on patient-centered care. It was a pleasure for us to serve as Guest Editors of the Frontiers in Surgery Research Topic entitled “ Update on the Diagnosis and Surgical Treatment of Gynecological Tumors .” Here, we explored the latest innovations and state-of-the-art advancements in the diagnosis and surgical management of gynecological cancers. A series of articles authored by experts in the field of gynecologic oncology has been presented. We believe that the topics addressed in this Research Topic will be of interest to a broad audience, ranging from academic researchers to clinicians working in gynecologic oncology. Han et al. , in “ A newly modified nerve-sparing radical hysterectomy technique with analysis of short-term oncologic, surgical, and functional outcomes ,” proposed a modified surgical approach for the treatment of cervical cancer. The second article, by Huang et al. , “ Early Recurrence After Surgery in FIGO 2023 Stage I–III Endometrial Cancer: Characteristics and Risk Factors ,” focused on the management of patients undergoing surgery for endometrial cancer. In the third article, “ Robot-Assisted Müllerian Compartment Resection for Cervical Cancer ,” Li et al. presented compelling data on the use of robotic surgery for the treatment of cervical cancer. The fourth article by Rei et al. , “ Ultrasound in Endometrial Cancer: Evaluating the Impact of Pre-Surgical Staging ,” assessed the role of ultrasound in the pre-operative management of patients with endometrial cancer. The fifth article, by Chen et al. , entitled “ Comparison of the Value of the GI-RADS and ADNEX Models in the Diagnosis of Adnexal Tumours by Junior Physicians ,” examined the diagnostic accuracy of these models when used by less experienced clinicians. In the narrative review “ Surgical Management of Anastomotic Leakage Related to Ovarian Cancer Surgery ,” Restaino et al. offered an overview of current strategies for managing this complex surgical complication. The seventh article, by Su et al. , “ Effect of Different Treatment Modalities on the Prognosis of Patients with Stage IIIC Cervical Cancer ,” investigated therapeutic options for patients with advanced-stage cervical cancer. Finally, the eighth article by Wang et al. , entitled “ Clinicopathological Characteristics and Fertility-Preserving Treatment of Atypical Polypoid Adenomyoma ,” presented new evidence regarding this rare uterine lesion and its conservative management. We would like to express our sincere thanks to all the authors for their valuable contributions. These articles provide significant insights and enrich the scientific community''s understanding of gynecological oncology. Author contributions SC: Conceptualization, Investigation, Methodology, Supervision, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. VC: Conceptualization, Investigation, Methodology, Supervision, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. SR: Conceptualization, Methodology, Supervision, Validation, Visualization, Writing – original draft, Writing – review & editing. Conflict of interest The author declares that the research was conducted in the absence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest. The author(s) declared that they were an editorial board member of Frontiers, at the time of submission. This had no impact on the peer review process and the final decision. Generative AI statement The author(s) declare that Generative AI was used in the creation of this manuscript. AI was used for grammatical language correction. Any alternative text (alt text) provided alongside figures in this article has been generated by Frontiers with the support of artificial intelligence and reasonable efforts have been made to ensure accuracy, including review by the authors wherever possible. If you identify any issues, please contact us. Publisher''s note All claims expressed in this article are solely those of the authors and do not necessarily represent those of their affiliated organizations, or those of the publisher, the editors and the reviewers. Any product that may be evaluated in this article, or claim that may be made by its manufacturer, is not guaranteed or endorsed by the publisher. References 1. American Cancer Society. Cancer Facts & Figures 2025. Atlanta: American Cancer Society; (2025). [ Google Scholar ] 2. Sala E, Rockall A, Rangarajan D, Kubik-Huch RA. The role of dynamic contrast-enhanced and diffusion weighted magnetic resonance imaging in the female pelvis. Eur J Radiol. (2010) 76(3):367–85. 10.1016/j.ejrad.2010.01.026 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Cancer Genome Atlas Research Network. Integrated genomic analyses of ovarian carcinoma. Nature. (2011) 474(7353):609–15. 10.1038/nature10166 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Stelloo E, Nout RA, Osse EM, Jürgenliemk-Schulz IJ, Jobsen JJ, Lutgens LC, et al. Improved risk assessment by integrating molecular and clinicopathological factors in early-stage endometrial cancer. Ann Oncol. (2016) 27(4):709–16. 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-15-2878 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Perelli F, Mattei A, Scambia G, Cavaliere AF. Editorial: methods in gynecological oncology. Front Oncol. (2023) 13:1167088. 10.3389/fonc.2023.1167088 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Restaino S, Poli A, Arcieri M, Mariuzzi L, Orsaria M, Tulisso A, et al. Molecular classification of endometrial carcinoma on endometrial biopsy: an early prognostic value to guide personalized treatment. Int J Gynecol Cancer. (2024) 34(8):1211–6. 10.1136/ijgc-2024-005478 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Cohen JD, Li L, Wang Y, Thoburn C, Afsari B, Danilova L, et al. Detection and localization of surgically resectable cancers with a multi-analyte blood test. Science. (2018) 359(6378):926–30. 10.1126/science.aar3247 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Cavaliere AF, Perelli F, Zaami S, Piergentili R, Mattei A, Vizzielli G, et al. Towards personalized medicine: non-coding RNAs and endometrial cancer. Healthcare (Basel). (2021) 9(8):965. 10.3390/healthcare9080965 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Walker JL, Piedmonte MR, Spirtos NM, Eisenkop SM, Schlaerth JB, Mannel RS, et al. Recurrence and survival after random assignment to laparoscopy versus laparotomy for comprehensive surgical staging of uterine cancer: gynecologic oncology group LAP2 study. J Clin Oncol. (2012) 30(7):695–700. 10.1200/JCO.2011.38.8645 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. Melamed A, Margul DJ, Chen L, Keating NL, Del Carmen MG, Yang J, et al. Survival after minimally invasive radical hysterectomy for early-stage cervical cancer. N Engl J Med. (2018) 379(20):1905–14. 10.1056/NEJMoa1804923 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Bentivegna E, Gouy S, Maulard A, Pautier P, Leary A, Colombo N, et al. Fertility-sparing surgery in epithelial ovarian cancer: a systematic review of oncological issues. Ann Oncol. (2016) 27(11):1994–2004. 10.1093/annonc/mdw311 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',59,'2025-10-13 06:35:34','2025-10-13 06:35:34');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1626,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12454873','Gender disparity and workforce trends in German neurosurgery: A longitudinal national analysis.','Corr F, Zeitlberger AM, Hostettler IC, Firtinidou A, Schulz E, Neidert MC, Bozinov O, Stienen MN, Ryang YM, Motov S; EANS Diversity in Neurosurgery Committee.','2025','quota gbe gesundheitsberichterstattung bundes gma lulla riedel dgnc memberships dgnc wfns apaydin emekli hakvoort grasreiner quotas efe midwestern pugazenthi renfrow gesundheitsberichterstattung bundes gbe gbe gebiets facharztbezeichnung consolidates gma gma gma gma gbe retirees gma gbe midpoints kelleher homoscedasticity helitzer piecewise neurosurgeon neurosurgeon materially forecasted forecasted lulla gravitate gedrose buddeberg radunz pugazenthi hesitations pugazenthi anxieties stienen stienen kabangu ambition radunz zeitlberger odonkor kabangu onumah peterman rosati preventza backhus eans dgnc midpoints gesundheitsberichterstattung bundes peul apaydin emekli e304 e313 buddeberg stamm klaghofer smw efe aliyeva beyaztas swiatek esene abdulrauf ravindra behrens nistor jns21225 gedrose wonneberger robra stosch grasreiner dahmen settmacher hakvoort conzen dilger gutzmann losse tauber chechko hollig neubiorev helitzer newbill cardinali morahan magrane mdw069 kelleher jcdd10020042 almgren skidmore raval blow neuopn kabangu yekzaman minchew birney heskett jns231152 s00701 lulla behmer focus20902 jella cwalina parr woodrow jns212968 odonkor pahwa rincon torroella bonsrah yenokyan dada onumah wikstrom cioletti peterman macinnis stauffer preventza backhus jtd pugazenthi strahle jns23286 radunz pustu mazilescu benko renfrow germano abosch neuros nyx251 riedel hennigs dobberkau riedel bugaj nikendei rosati koniaris molena blitzer tahboub jtd cerasiello semonche sotayo cerqueira merces magella ruivo rautenfeld aras yuce schlick khorfan amortegui stienen gautschi schaller netuka demetriades ringel jns16788 stienen freyschlag schaller meling s00701 bussche wonneberger birck robra berufliche facharztlichen velalakan s10143 gangi inauen senn markun bmcmededuc utm_source zeitlberger baticam sprenger tizi schaller stienen gesundheitsberichterstattung bundes','Abstract Introduction Neurosurgery remains one of the most gender-imbalanced specialties worldwide, with women continuing to hold only a minority of positions, especially senior levels despite their growing presence in the medical field. Research question This study aimed to quantify gender-specific workforce trends in German neurosurgery from 1991 to 2023 and to project the future proportion of female neurosurgeons. Material and methods A retrospective longitudinal analysis was performed using official national registry data from the German Medical Association and the Federal Health Reporting System. Trends in gender distribution, sectoral employment, and board certification were analyzed. Linear and segmented regression models were used to forecast future representation. Results The quota of female neurosurgeons increased from 6.2 % in 1991 to 21.9 % in 2023. Women were more often employed in inpatient care (23.5 %). Projections based on historical workforce data estimate female representation to reach 25.9 % by the year 2030. The female share of the neurosurgical workforce is estimated to reach the 30 percent critical mass threshold by the year 2039 (95 %CI: 2037.5–2040.7). Compared to other specialties, neurosurgery along with orthopedic surgery remains among the most gender-imbalanced fields. Discussion and conclusion Despite a steady increase in female representation in neurosurgery in Germany, substantial disparities remain across all age groups and employment sectors. At the current pace, only one-quarter of the neurosurgical workforce will be female by 2030, with gender parity unlikely to happen before 2079. Strategic interventions, including mentorship and family-friendly policies, appear to be essential to accelerate equity and retention within the field. Keywords: Diversity, Gender equity, Gender equality, Neurosurgery, Female leadership, Gender disparity Highlights • Female representation in German neurosurgery increased from 6.2 % to 21.9 % (1991–2023). • Female representation was the highest in inpatient care (23.5 %). • Female representation projected to reach the 30 % critical mass by 2039 and gender parity by 2079. • Neurosurgery ranked fourth lowest in female representation among all surgical specialties in Germany in 2023. Abbreviations AIC Akaike Information Criterion BIC Bayesian Information Criterion CI Confidence Interval GBE Gesundheitsberichterstattung des Bundes (Federal Health Reporting System) GMA German Medical Association (Bundesärztekammer) SD Standard Deviation STROBE Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology 1. Introduction Neurosurgery remains one of the most gender-imbalanced medical specialties globally ( Lulla et al., 2021 ). In Germany, where women represent over 60 % of medical students ( Riedel et al., 2022 ), this demographic shift has not translated into the proportional representation in neurosurgery ( Forster et al., 2022 ). As of 2024, around 35 % of neurosurgeons in training in Germany are female, whereas only 6.3 % of German department heads are female, with European rates even lower at 3 % ( Forster et al., 2022 ). More than 90 % of leadership positions are held by men ( Forster et al., 2022 ; Weiss et al., 2023 ). This disparity extends to academic and institutional settings, where female neurosurgeons remain underrepresented at conferences and in senior academic roles ( Silva et al., 2019 ). The German national neurosurgical society (DGNC) also struggles to increase the number of female memberships and representation during their annual meetings. In 2018, only 12 % of DGNC members were female. During the annual congress, female speakers accounted for around 30 %, whereas the rate of female keynote or plenary speakers was only 4 % between 2015 and 2018, while only 11 % of scientific sessions were chaired by a woman ( Lawson, 2020 ). With regard to leadership positions in neurosurgical journals and societies worldwide, only 13.7 % of editorial board members of the ten top-ranked neurosurgical journals and only 4 % of World Federation of Neurosurgical Societies (WFNS)-affiliated countries had female presidents or delegates ( Apaydin and Emekli, 2024 ). The percentage of female authors in neurosurgical journals amounts to 19 %, however, the percentage of first or last authorships held by women is less than 10 % ( Hakvoort et al., 2023 ). While increasing numbers of female medical graduates express interest in surgical careers ( Grasreiner et al., 2018 ), barriers to career progression persist. Surveys indicate that men are significantly less likely to acknowledge gender-based inequities within neurosurgery, and that there is limited support for policies such as gender quotas or structured mentorship programs ( Efe et al., 2022 ). In a survey among medical students of a midwestern medical school in the US maternity needs were more of concern among female than male medical students ( Pugazenthi et al., 2023 ). International trends reveal that many female neurosurgery graduates transition into private practice or non-academic roles within a decade of finishing training, raising concerns about retention and long-term career advancement within the field ( Renfrow et al., 2018 ). The present study aimed to conduct the first nationwide, longitudinal analysis of gender representation and workforce trends in neurosurgery in Germany. By integrating official physician registry data and federal health statistics, we aimed to quantify temporal trends in gender distribution, sectoral employment patterns, age-related workforce composition, board certification rates, and projected future developments of female workforce representation. 2. Material and methods 2.1. Study design This retrospective, longitudinal cohort study evaluated gender-specific and structural workforce trends in neurosurgery across Germany over 33 years (1991–2023). Utilized data were publicly available, derived from official national registries (see 2.2). The study adhered to the ethical principles outlined in the Declaration of Helsinki. The primary aim was to characterize temporal trends in the gender representation of board-certified neurosurgeons, shifts in sectoral employment, and changes in age distribution of female workers in neurosurgery. The secondary aim was to compare gender representation in neurosurgery with that in other medical specialties and to project the future proportion of female neurosurgeons. This study was exempt from institutional review board approval as it utilized exclusively publicly available data without any patient identifiers. We report in accordance with the Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology (STROBE) statement for cohort studies ( Appendix 1 ). 2.2. Setting The study was conducted using data from the Federal Health Reporting System (Gesundheitsberichterstattung des Bundes, GBE), which served as the primary source for workforce trend analysis. The GBE dataset „ Ärztinnen und Ärzte mit Gebiets-und Facharztbezeichnung, BÄK “ consolidates specialty-specific physician workforce data collected by the German Medical Association (Bundesärztekammer, GMA). Analyses included actively practicing physicians across all federal states of Germany between 1991 and 2023. Additional data on annual neurosurgical board certifications were obtained directly from the GMA''s official dataset to support trend modeling and validation ( Supplementary Table 1 ). 2.3. Participants All board-certified neurosurgeons registered with the GMA during the study period were eligible for inclusion. Analyses focused primarily on actively practicing physicians. Comparisons across disciplines were restricted to actively practicing physicians to ensure sectoral consistency. For annual board certification trends, data were restricted to the period from 2004 onwards due to data completeness. 2.4. Variables The following core variables were extracted and analyzed: specialty classification, type of professional activity (inpatient, outpatient, private practice, public institutions, other), geographic region, age category, and annual board certification status. Employment sectors were defined according to official classifications from the GMA and GBE. Actively practicing physicians included all individuals engaged in medical work. Inpatient care was defined as hospital-based clinical work (i.e., physicians working in hospital settings, typically providing care to admitted patients). Outpatient care encompassed both self-employed physicians in private practice and employed physicians in ambulatory settings. Physicians employed in governmental bodies or public institutions were categorized separately. Employment outside conventional healthcare settings, such as research, academia, or industry, was classified as "other". Inactive physicians were defined as registered but non-practicing individuals, including retirees and those in non-clinical occupations. Age was categorized into six predefined intervals: ≤34 years, 35–39 years, 40–49 years, 50–59 years, 60–65 years, and >65 years. Gender (male/female) was the principal variable of interest for assessing workforce composition. 2.5. Data sources, measurement, and quantitative variables Primary data were sourced from the centralized online reporting system of the GMA, which aggregates mandatory membership data from 17 state medical chambers. This dataset provides complete annual records on specialty certification, workforce movement, and employment sector transitions. Additional epidemiological data were obtained from the GBE ( Supplementary Table 1 ). Age was provided in predefined discrete intervals by the data sources and analyzed accordingly. Due to the availability of only categorical age data, midpoints were assigned to each predefined age group for use in continuous statistical modeling ( Hunter and Kelleher, 2023 ; Huang et al., 2016 ): <35 years = 30 years, 35–39 years = 37 years, 40–49 years = 44.5 years, 50–59 years = 54.5 years, 60–65 years = 62.5 years, and >65 years = 70 years. Gender representation was calculated annually as both absolute numbers and proportions of the total workforce. 2.6. Statistical methods Statistical analyses were conducted in R (version 4.4.0; R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria) utilizing open-source packages. Normality was assessed using the Shapiro–Wilk test. Continuous variables were summarized as mean ± standard deviation (SD). Female representation was quantified annually as absolute counts and proportions. Sector-specific and discipline-specific shares were described using mean, SD, minimum, and maximum values. Temporal trends were analyzed using simple linear regression, with calendar year as the independent variable and female share as the outcome. Model assumptions, including linearity and homoscedasticity, were verified by visual inspection of residual plots. Forecasts were generated to estimate the projected female representation by 2030, the year when the workforce is expected to reach 30 percent female representation, a threshold commonly cited in the literature as the critical mass required for underrepresented groups to exert meaningful influence ( Helitzer et al., 2017 ), as well as the year when gender parity at 50 percent might be achieved, based on linear extrapolation of historical trends. Structural changes were assessed by segmented (piecewise) regression, with break points estimated via maximum likelihood. Multivariable linear regression was performed with female share (%) as the dependent variable and mid-age, calendar year, and employment sector as independent variables. Robustness was evaluated through sensitivity analyses, by excluding the "Other" sector, incorporating interaction terms, and restricting analyses to actively practicing sectors. All tests were two-sided, and p-values <0.05 were considered statistically significant. 3. Results 3.1. Female representation in the German neurosurgical workforce Germany''s neurosurgical workforce comprised 3,169 individuals in 2023, of whom 2,713 (85.6 %) were actively practicing. Women represented 21.2 % of the total workforce (n = 673) and 21.9 % of actively practicing neurosurgeons (n = 595). In inpatient care, there were 436 female neurosurgeons (23.5 %) compared to 1,417 males. Outpatient settings included 111 women (15.3 %) and 616 men, while private practice comprised 61 women (13.2 %) and 399 men. Public institutions employed 19 women (33.3 %) and 38 men, and "other" roles accounted for 29 women (36.3 %) and 51 men. 3.2. Trends in annual neurosurgery board certifications Between 2004 and 2023, a total of 2,317 neurosurgery board certifications were awarded in Germany (116.8 ± 22.9 per year), including 1,715 men (85.8 ± 14.2 per year) and 602 women (30.1 ± 10.6 per year) ( Fig. 1 A). The median annual female proportion was 25.6 % (range 13–48 %), with a mean of 25.2 % ± 5.7 ( Fig. 1 B). Linear regression analysis demonstrated a significant upward trend in female representation (β = 0.539, R 2 = 0.31, 95 % CI: 0.141–0.937, p = 0.0107), corresponding to an average increase of 0.54 percentage points per year. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Trends in Neurosurgical Board Certifications and Female Representation in Germany (2004–2023). (A) Annual number of neurosurgical board certifications stratified by gender (female, male, total) from 2004 to 2023. (B) Annual proportion of female neurosurgeons among newly board-certified individuals during the same period. A dashed black line indicates the linear trend, highlighting a steady increase in female representation over time. 3.3. Comparative female representation across medical disciplines From 2006 to 2023, the proportion of female neurosurgeons in Germany increased from 6.2 % to 21.9 %, representing an absolute gain of 15.7 percentage points ( Fig. 2 ). The mean female representation over this period was 13.8 % ± 5.1 %. Among medical specialties, neurosurgery ranked fourth lowest in female representation. General surgery exhibited a slightly higher mean (16.4 % ± 5.4) and a similar trend of increase, while pediatric surgery consistently maintained the highest female proportion (mean 30.8 %, final year 41.6 %). Cardiac (mean 9.6, final year 18.6 %) and orthopedic surgery (mean 12.9, final year 19.1 %), despite substantial relative increases, remained below neurosurgery. Maxillofacial surgery followed a similar trend, with a lower overall mean (9.97 % ± 3.32 %) ( Supplementary Table 2 ). Among all medical specialties, the highest mean proportions of female physicians were observed in human genetics (59.6 %), child and adolescent psychiatry (57.9 %), and pediatrics (53.7 %), whereas neurosurgery ranked among the six lowest specialties in terms of female representation ( Supplementary Table 3 ). As of 2023, the medical specialties with the highest representation of female physicians included gynecology and obstetrics (73.1 %), child and adolescent psychiatry and psychotherapy (69.9 %), human genetics (64.6 %), pediatrics (63.8 %), and dermatology (60.7 %). Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Trends in Female Representation Across Surgical and Medical Specialties in Germany (1991–2023). Stacked area plot showing changes in the proportion of female physicians across major surgical specialties from 1991 to 2023. 3.4. Sectoral distribution of female neurosurgeons Among actively practicing female neurosurgeons, the largest numbers were employed in inpatient care, followed by outpatient settings and private practice ( Fig. 3 ). By 2023, public institutions employed only 19 women and "other" sectors 29 women. Linear regression confirmed statistically significant increases in female neurosurgeon numbers across all sectors (all p < 0.001), with the largest growth in inpatient care (+12.5 neurosurgeons/year; 95 % CI: 11.4–13.7), followed by outpatient care (+4.25/year; 95 % CI: 3.88–4.63) and private practice (+2.44/year; 95 % CI: 2.23–2.64). Overall, female neurosurgeon absolute numbers increased by 18.0 per year (95 % CI: 16.4–19.5), and inactive women by 2.22 per year (95 % CI: 2.08–2.35). In proportional terms, the highest annual increase in female share occurred in outpatient care (+1.82 percentage points/year; 95 % CI: 1.55–2.08), followed by "other" sectors (+0.66/year) and public institutions (+0.51/year). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Trends in Female Neurosurgeons Across Employment Sectors in Germany (1991–2023). Number of actively practicing female neurosurgeons by employment sector. 3.5. Multivariable regression analysis of female representation Multivariable regression identified age and calendar year as key predictors of female representation among actively practicing neurosurgeons. Across age groups, each additional year in the age group midpoint was associated with a 0.60 percentage point decrease in female representation (β = −0.60, p < 0.001), while each calendar year predicted a 0.59 percentage point increase (β = 0.59, p < 0.001). Compared to inpatient care, female proportions were significantly lower in outpatient care (β = −8.06, p < 0.001), private practice (β = −9.77, p < 0.001), and public institutions (β = −3.68, p = 0.004). The "Other" sector was not significant (p = 0.38). Sensitivity analyses confirmed the robustness of these findings ( Supplementary Table 4 ). Excluding the "Other" sector or restricting analyses to actively practicing sectors did not materially alter effect estimates. Adding interaction terms slightly improved model fit (adjusted R 2 = 0.459), but age remained a strong negative predictor (β range: 0.55 to −0.60, all p < 0.001) and calendar year a strong positive predictor (β range: +0.51 to +0.59, all p < 0.001) across all models. 3.6. Projected trends in female representation among neurosurgeons A time series analysis of female representation among neurosurgeons in Germany from 1991 to 2023 revealed a significant upward trend, with an annual increase of 0.51 percentage points (β = 0.508, R 2 = 0.995, 95 % CI: 0.495–0.521, p < 0.001). By 2030, projections estimate that female neurosurgeons will constitute 25.9 percent of the workforce (95 % CI: 25.1 %–26.6 %) ( Fig. 4 , Supplementary Figure 1 ; Supplementary Table 5 + 6 ). The female share of the neurosurgical workforce is estimated to reach the 30 percent critical mass threshold by the year 2039 (95 % CI: 2037.5–2040.7). At the current rate of increase (β = 0.50 percentage points/year), linear extrapolation suggests that gender parity in neurosurgery will not be achieved before the year 2079 (95 % CI: 2076.0–2081.0). Segmented regression identified a breakpoint in 1998 (95 % CI: 1990.5–2007.0), indicating a slight acceleration in growth. The annual increase shifted from 0.45 percentage points before 1999 (95 % CI: 0.35–0.55) to 0.52 percentage points thereafter (95 % CI: 0.32–0.72), although this difference was not statistically significant (p = 0.18) ( Supplementary Table 7 ). Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Forecasted trends in female representation among neurosurgeons in Germany (1991–2040). Proportion of women in the German neurosurgical workforce observed from 1991 to 2023 (black line) with fitted linear regression model (red line) and extrapolated forecasts to 20340 (blue line). The light blue shaded area represents the 95 % prediction interval for the forecasted trend. A dotted horizontal line marks the 30 % female representation benchmark. 4. Discussion Despite a rising proportion of female medical graduates in Germany, neurosurgery remains one of the most gender-imbalanced medical specialties ( Lulla et al., 2021 ). Prior studies have identified significant disparities in leadership roles, academic participation, and board certification rates among female neurosurgeons ( Forster et al., 2022 ; Weiss et al., 2023 ; Silva et al., 2019 ). However, comprehensive, longitudinal analyses quantifying gender-specific workforce dynamics across sectors in German neurosurgery have been lacking. This study addresses this critical gap by analyzing national registry data over three decades and projecting future trends. 4.1. Key findings Our findings demonstrate that the proportion of female neurosurgeons in Germany increased from 6.2 % in 1991 to 21.9 % in 2023. In absolute terms, the number of actively practicing female neurosurgeons rose more than 17-fold, from 34 to 595 active female neurosurgeons over this period. Yet, neurosurgery remains among the six lowest-ranking specialties in terms of female representation, substantially lagging behind fields such as gynecology, pediatrics and psychiatry, which now exhibit near or actual gender parity. Women were most strongly represented in inpatient care (23.5 %), with lower shares in outpatient settings (15.3 %) and private practice (13.2 %). By 2030, women are projected to comprise 25.9 percent of the neurosurgical workforce. The 30 percent critical mass threshold is expected by 2039. Still, gender parity remains distant, with 50 percent representation unlikely before 2079. 4.2. Workforce growth and sectoral shifts While the majority of female neurosurgeons in Germany remain employed in inpatient care (23.5 % in 2023), the most rapid relative increase in female representation has occurred in outpatient sectors. This trend aligns with international patterns observed across various surgical disciplines, where women increasingly gravitate toward ambulatory or private practice settings ( Gedrose et al., 2012 ; van den et al., 2014 ; Buddeberg et al., 2010 ; Fitzgerald et al., 2013 ). A German national survey reported that 26.4 % of female surgeons preferred future work in outpatient clinics or private practice ( Radunz et al., 2020 ). Similarly, medical graduates have consistently expressed stronger preferences for ambulatory over inpatient care, citing autonomy and family compatibility as key factors ( Weiss et al., 2024 ). Therefore, it might not be surprising that in a survey among medical school students only 6.1 % considered neurosurgery as their top choice( Pugazenthi et al., 2023 ). Among female medical students maternity needs were considered main hesitations to enter neurosurgery, among residents it was perceived happiness in the field independent of gender ( Pugazenthi et al., 2023 ). An international survey from Brazil found that nearly two-thirds of female vascular surgeons spent the majority of their working time in outpatient rather than inpatient settings ( Silva et al., 2021 ). Lifestyle factors, such as preferences for flexible scheduling, fewer on-call duties, and better work–life balance, combined with systemic challenges in hospital-based settings, may be contributing to the growing attractiveness and perceived viability of outpatient care as an alternative. Survey data among European neurosurgical trainees revealed that female residents were significantly more likely to report future career anxieties and less likely to pursue academic careers ( Stienen et al., 2016 ). Moreover, female trainees have been shown to perform fewer cranial, spinal, and adult procedures than their male counterparts during residency, suggesting disparities in operative exposure at formative training stages ( Stienen et al., 2020 ). 4.3. Structural barriers and sectoral inequity Multivariable analysis demonstrated that gender disparities in neurosurgery are associated with intersecting structural and career-stage factors. Female representation was significantly lower in outpatient care, private practice, and public institutions compared to inpatient settings, with age and calendar year also predicting variance. Despite increasing female entry into neurosurgery, women chaired only nine of 143 neurosurgical departments in Germany (as of 2021, 6.3 %), while the rest of the departments (93.7 %) remained under male leadership ( Forster et al., 2022 ). This distribution has been consistent across recent national surveys, and historically, only 22 women have ever held a department chair position ( Weiss et al., 2023 ). In U.S. neurosurgery, a one percent rise in attrition among female trainees correlates with a nearly three percent drop in overall representation ( Kabangu et al., 2024 ). Notably, parenthood does not appear to suppress leadership ambition among female surgeons ( Radunz et al., 2020 ), although persistent structural barriers, including discrimination and reduced satisfaction, continue to be reported ( Zeitlberger et al., 2022 ). However, retention and progression remain closely linked to the availability of mentorship ( Odonkor et al., 2025 ). 4.4. Projected trends and pace of change Our projections estimate that the 30 % critical mass threshold in the German neurosurgical workforce will be reached by 2039. Although this reflects measurable progress, the overall pace of change remains slow, particularly when compared to non-surgical disciplines where gender parity has already been achieved or surpassed. In Germany, female representation has increased by approximately 0.51 percentage points per year since 1991, closely mirroring U.S. trends where neurosurgery residency cohorts grow by 0.65 points annually, projecting parity only beyond 2060 ( Kabangu et al., 2024 ). Broader workforce projections raise further concern. Our linear extrapolation suggests that gender parity in German neurosurgery may not be reached until 2079, while estimates from the United States indicate that equal representation in practice may not be achieved until well into the 22nd century with a calculated timeline of 156 years to achieve gender parity ( Mulligan et al., 2023 ). While this study focused on quantifying disparities and projecting future trends, the projected timeline to gender parity highlights the need to consider targeted structural measures. Interventions such as mentorship ( Silver et al., 2024 ), leadership development, and institutional support for work–life integration have shown crucial to supporting career progression and workforce stability ( Onumah et al., 2021 ). Embedding such measures within neurosurgical training and career pathways may help counteract attrition and support long-term retention, particularly during key transitional stages. 4.5. Comparative insights Despite over three decades of gradual progress, neurosurgery continues to rank among the most gender-disparate medical specialties. While some surgical fields made tangible progress, most notably pediatric surgery and obstetrics and gynecology, where women now constitute a majority, others continue to resist change. Neurosurgery stands alongside orthopedics, cardiothoracic surgery, and oral and maxillofacial surgery as a persistent outlier. In the United States, women make up just 8.4 % of practicing neurosurgeons (as of 2017) ( Johnson et al., 2022 ), 7.4 % of orthopedic surgeons (as of 2022) ( Peterman et al., 2022 ), and between 5.2 % and 7 % of cardiac and thoracic surgeons ( Rosati et al., 2016 ; Preventza and Backhus, 2021 ). Therefore, persistent underrepresentation in neurosurgery may not be solely attributable to a lack of interest or pipeline limitations, but may also reflect enduring structural factors that influence retention and advancement within the field. These include the near-total absence of institutionalized parental leave accommodations specific to surgical training, as well as the limited availability of structured mentoring and sponsorship programs. While international initiatives such as AO Spine''s global mentoring program represent a positive shift, Germany lacks a dedicated, nationwide mentoring or sponsorship program specifically tailored to neurosurgery. Although broader surgical organizations - such as the EANS Diversity in Neurosurgery Committee and the DGNC Women in Neurosurgery Commission - provide valuable support, the lack of structured, targeted initiatives within neurosurgery during formative career stages likely contributes to the continued attrition of women in neurosurgery. 4.6. Strengths and limitations A key strength of this study is its use of a comprehensive nationwide dataset, providing a detailed, longitudinal perspective on gender representation among board-certified neurosurgeons in Germany. The reliance on official registry data ensures strong methodological validity and reproducibility. The inclusion of multiple employment sectors and age groups adds valuable depth to the analysis across different career stages. However, several limitations must be considered. The findings are specific to the German healthcare system and may not fully reflect gender dynamics in other international contexts. Extrapolation to countries with different institutional or cultural environments should be made with caution. Furthermore, while the study focused on gender, other intersecting factors such as race, ethnicity, socioeconomic status, or disability were not addressed. Important qualitative factors, including perceptions of workplace culture, access to mentorship, and challenges balancing career and family life, were beyond the scope of the analysis. The dataset lacked granularity regarding neurosurgical subspecialties, part-time employment, marital status, and academic appointment rates. These factors could substantially influence career progression and retention and were not captured. Moreover, the cross-sectional nature of the data precluded tracking individual career trajectories, limiting the ability to differentiate between cohort effects and workforce attrition. Longitudinal individual-level studies would help address this gap. Due to the sole availability of age-grouped data, midpoints were assigned to approximate continuous age in regression models. Although this is a widely accepted method in medical research, it introduces minor approximation bias. The relatively narrow age intervals likely reduce this effect, but associations between age and female representation should be interpreted with appropriate caution. Finally, the projections assume linear growth in female representation, which may not account for the influence of future policy initiatives or societal shifts. More dynamic forecasting models could improve future projections. 5. Conclusion This nationwide study provides the first comprehensive longitudinal analysis of gender representation in neurosurgery in Germany, documenting significant increases in female participation over the past three decades. Despite these gains, neurosurgery remains one of the most gender-imbalanced specialties, with women continuing to be substantially underrepresented. At the current trajectory, women are projected to reach the 30 % critical mass threshold by 2039, whereas full gender parity is unlikely to be achieved before 2079. These findings emphasize the urgent need for sustained efforts to promote gender equity and improve career progression for women in neurosurgery. Future research should prioritize identifying the specific barriers that women encounter within the field and developing targeted interventions to accelerate progress towards gender parity in neurosurgery, including mentorship programs, family-friendly policies, and leadership training initiatives. Data availability statement All data used in this study are publicly available from the German Medical Association (Bundesärztekammer) and the Federal Health Reporting System (Gesundheitsberichterstattung des Bundes). Funding This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Conflict of interest The authors declare no competing interests. Acknowledgements None. Handling Editor: Dr W Peul Footnotes Appendix A Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.bas.2025.105596 . Appendices. Table A1. STROBE Statement—Checklist of items that should be included in reports of cohort studies. To be completed once the manuscript is finalized. Item No Recommendation Page No Title and abstract 1 ( a ) Indicate the study''s design with a commonly used term in the title or the abstract 1 ( b ) Provide in the abstract an informative and balanced summary of what was done and what was found Introduction Background/rationale 2 Explain the scientific background and rationale for the investigation being reported 7 Objectives 3 State specific objectives, including any prespecified hypotheses 7 Methods Study design 4 Present key elements of study design early in the paper 8 Setting 5 Describe the setting, locations, and relevant dates, including periods of recruitment, exposure, follow-up, and data collection 8 Participants 6 ( a ) Give the eligibility criteria, and the sources and methods of selection of participants. Describe methods of follow-up 8 ( b ) For matched studies, give matching criteria and number of exposed and unexposed Variables 7 Clearly define all outcomes, exposures, predictors, potential confounders, and effect modifiers. Give diagnostic criteria, if applicable 8–9 Data sources/measurement 8∗ For each variable of interest, give sources of data and details of methods of assessment (measurement). Describe comparability of assessment methods if there is more than one group 9 Bias 9 Describe any efforts to address potential sources of bias 9 Study size 10 Explain how the study size was arrived at 9 Quantitative variables 11 Explain how quantitative variables were handled in the analyses. If applicable, describe which groupings were chosen and why 9 Statistical methods 12 ( a ) Describe all statistical methods, including those used to control for confounding 9 ( b ) Describe any methods used to examine subgroups and interactions ( c ) Explain how missing data were addressed ( d ) If applicable, explain how loss to follow-up was addressed ( e ) Describe any sensitivity analyses Results Participants 13∗ (a) Report numbers of individuals at each stage of study—eg numbers potentially eligible, examined for eligibility, confirmed eligible, included in the study, completing follow-up, and analyzed 10–14 (b) Give reasons for non-participation at each stage (c) Consider use of a flow diagram Descriptive data 14∗ (a) Give characteristics of study participants (eg demographic, clinical, social) and information on exposures and potential confounders 10–14 (b) Indicate number of participants with missing data for each variable of interest (c) Summarise follow-up time (eg, average and total amount) Outcome data 15∗ Report numbers of outcome events or summary measures over time 10–14 Main results 16 (a) Give unadjusted estimates and, if applicable, confounder-adjusted estimates and their precision (eg, 95 % confidence interval). Make clear which confounders were adjusted for and why they were included 10–14 (b) Report category boundaries when continuous variables were categorized 10–14 (c) If relevant, consider translating estimates of relative risk into absolute risk for a meaningful time period 10–14 Other analyses 17 Report other analyses done—eg analyses of subgroups and interactions, and sensitivity analyses 10–14 Discussion Key results 18 Summarise key results with reference to study objectives 15–16 Limitations 19 Discuss limitations of the study, taking into account sources of potential bias or imprecision. Discuss both direction and magnitude of any potential bias 17 Interpretation 20 Give a cautious overall interpretation of results considering objectives, limitations, multiplicity of analyses, results from similar studies, and other relevant evidence 15–16 Generalisability 21 Discuss the generalisability (external validity) of the study results 15–16 Other information Funding 22 Give the source of funding and the role of the funders for the present study and, if applicable, for the original study on which the present article is based 6 Open in a new tab Appendix A. Supplementary data The following is the Supplementary data to this article: Multimedia component 1 mmc1.docx (160.3KB, docx) References Apaydin A.S., Emekli I. Gender inequality in leadership positions in neurology and neurosurgery journals and societies. World Neurosurg. 2024;183:e304–e313. doi: 10.1016/j.wneu.2023.12.085. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Buddeberg-Fischer B., Stamm M., Klaghofer R. Career paths in physicians'' postgraduate training - an eight-year follow-up study. Swiss Med. Wkly. 2010;140 doi: 10.4414/smw.2010.13056. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Efe I.E., Aliyeva I., Beyaztas D., Swiatek V.M., Esene I.N., Abdulrauf S.I. Gender differences in perceptions and attitudes of medical students toward neurosurgery: a German nationwide survey. World Neurosurg. 2022;163:96–103.e2. doi: 10.1016/j.wneu.2022.03.134. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Fitzgerald J.E.F., Tang S.-W., Ravindra P., Maxwell-Armstrong C.A. Gender-related perceptions of careers in surgery among new medical graduates: results of a cross-sectional study. Am. J. Surg. 2013;206:112–119. doi: 10.1016/j.amjsurg.2012.04.009. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Forster M.-T., Behrens M., Lawson McLean A.C., Nistor-Gallo D.I., Weiss M., Maurer S. Gender disparity in German neurosurgery. J. Neurosurg. 2022;136:1141–1146. doi: 10.3171/2021.3.JNS21225. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Gedrose B., Wonneberger C., Junger J., Robra B.P., Schmidt A., Stosch C., et al. Do female medical graduates have different views on professional work and workload compared to Do female medical graduates have different views on professional work and workload compared to their male colleagues? Results of a multicenter postal survey in Germany. Dtsch Med Wochenschr. 2012;137(23):1242–1247. doi: 10.1055/s-0032-1304872. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Grasreiner D., Dahmen U., Settmacher U. Specialty preferences and influencing 