 C, Maekawa T, Takemura MY, Irie R, Wada A, Suzuki M, Aoki S (2019) 3D quantitative synthetic MRI-derived cortical thickness and subcortical brain volumes: scan-rescan repeatability and comparison with conventional T(1) -weighted images. J Magn Reson Imag JMRI 50(6):1834–1842. 10.1002/jmri.26744 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials 11547_2025_2042_MOESM1_ESM.tif (35.8MB, tif) Supplementary Fig 1. Representative pathological images of lymph node metastasis (LNM), positive lymphovascular space invasion (LVSI), deep cervical stromal invasion (DSI) and tumor maximum diameter (TMD). A LNM: the lymph node was invaded by malignant epithelial cells (white arrows; HE staining, ×100) showed, B LVSI: arcinoma cells were shown in tumor microvessels (white arrows; HE staining, ×100), C DSI: tumor invasion greater than one-third of the cervical wall (white arrows; HE staining, ×100), and D TMD: the longest diameter of mass was more than 4cm (white arrows; HE staining, ×100) (TIF 36631 KB) Supplementary file2 (DOCX 13 KB) (13.2KB, docx)',53767,'2025-10-13 06:36:14','2025-10-13 06:36:14');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1629,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12456061','The Potential for Providing Treatment on an Outpatient Rather Than Inpatient Basis: A Nationwide Analysis of Hospital Discharge Data in Germany for the Year 2022.','Pioch C, Busse R, Mansky T, Nimptsch U.','2025','diverted 115b militating 115f microdata pccl militating 115b pioch 115b 115b a2a ecf presupposes ecf presupposes pccl iqi pccl pccl iqi ecf a2a g09 g09 g09z g24c g24b i20 k56 i20f y95 ecf iqi pioch xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx xxx barmer aftercare approximating pccl tending grahame databrowser hlth_rs_bds ae3016b9 worz schattauer gutachten gutachten_2018 gutachten_2018 bundesgesundheitsministerium dateien 3_downloads krankenhausreform 3te_stellungnahme_regierungskommission_grundlegende_reform_kh verguetung_6_dez_2022_mit_tab anhang classen gefaßchirurgie igesgroup myzms e1621 e10211 e27603 e27841 e27842 e27844 attr_objs27932 iges_aop_gutachten_032022_ger destatis umwelt krankenhaeuser _inhalt forschungsfoerderung endbericht_ambpo_korrigiert_15022024 repschlager epgsf nimptsch depositonce swart bifg versorgungskompass ambulantisierungspotential roßler gwa2023 gkv spitzenverband krankenversicherung ambulant_stationaere_versorgung hybrid_drg_115f hybrid_drg paasch heidelb heidelb rhoen ambul kh_ databrowser hlth_rs_bds worz auslandische klauber robra schellschmitt krankenhausreport schwerpunkt versorgungszentren schattauer sachverstandigenrat begutachtung bedarfsgerechte steuerung gesundheitsversorgung gutachten gutachten gutachten_2018 gutachten_2018 regierungskommission bedarfsgerechte krankenhausversorgung dritte regierungskommission bedarfsgerechte krankenhausversorgung grundlegende krankenhausvergutung bundesgesundheitsministerium dateien 3_downloads krankenhausreform 3te_stellungnahme_regierungskommission_grundlegende_reform_kh verguetung_6_dez_2022_mit_tab anhang classen ambulantisierung tellerrand hinausgedacht chancen knappen mensch umwelt energie gefaßchirurgie mansky schiffhorst igesgroup myzms e1621 e10211 e27603 e27841 e27842 e27844 attr_objs27932 iges_aop_gutachten_032022_ger gutachten 115b gutachten kassenarztliche bundesvereinigung gkv spitzenverband krankenhausgesellschaft statistisches destatis destatis umwelt krankenhaeuser _inhalt statistisches grunddaten krankenhauser statistischer evas nummer pioch nimptsch mansky ambulantisierungspotenzial akutkrankenhausern zentralinstitut kassenarztliche forschungsfoerderung endbericht_ambpo_korrigiert_15022024 forschungsdatenzentren statistischen amter bundes berechnungen repschlager roßler sievers wende epgsf barmer gesundheitssystemforschung bifg erganzende auswertungen vorschlag operieren neuburger kraindler nimptsch mansky iqi bundesreferenzwerte auswertungsjahr universitatsverlag swart gothe geyer sekundardatenanalyse barmer gesundheitssystemforschung bifg bifg versorgungskompass ambulantisierungspotential bifg versorgungskompass ambulantisierungspotential roßler repschlager wende ambulantisierungen erweiterung vertrags ambulantes operieren stationsersetzende gutachtens repschlager osterkamp bifg gkv spitzenverband gkv spitzenverband krankenversicherung ambulant_stationaere_versorgung hybrid_drg_115f hybrid_drg gkv spitzenverband sektorengleiche vergutung 115f paasch schildberg vorgaben zielvorstellungen motive haltungen denken operationsprofil viszeralchirurgie heidelb s00104 reissfelder bussen machbarkeit strukturvoraussetzungen ambulantisierung proktologie heidelb s00104 spinner kaiss hagemeier katholing rhoen ambul kh_ neustadt saale rhon stationsersetzenden','Abstract Background In an expert analysis by the IGES Institute, it was examined which inpatient hospital treatments could also be carried out on an outpatient basis. A method of determining the feasibility of performing any particular treatment in the outpatient setting based on routine documentation was proposed in the report. A new version of the German catalogue of operations that can be performed on an outpatient basis and other outpatient procedures (the AOP catalogue) was issued accordingly. In addition, sector-blind (hybrid DRG) reimbursement of selected treatments was introduced, i.e., the amount of the reimbursement is now the same regardless of whether the treatment is provided on an inpatient or outpatient basis. Methods The present analysis is based on German nationwide hospital discharge data from 2022. It was calculated how many inpatient treatments could have been carried out on an outpatient basis according to the criteria of the IGES report and the AOP catalogue, and how many cases would fall under hybrid DRGs. Results Of 14.8 million inpatient treatment cases (excluding deliveries and neonates), 7.2% were identified that might have been provided on an outpatient basis according to both models, and a total of 17.3% and 21.8%, respectively, according to the IGES model or the AOP catalogue. The estimated number of potential hybrid DRG cases was 153 000, or 1.0%. Conclusion Both the IGES model and the AOP catalogue identified a relevant potential for providing treatment on an outpatient rather than inpatient basis. However, the overlap between the two approaches is small. Further development of the AOP catalogue appears necessary for the more accurate identification of treatment cases that can be provided in the outpatient setting. The extent to which the new AOP catalogue and hybrid DRG remuneration can promote the provision of outpatient rather than inpatient care will need to be determined in future analyses. Hospital care in Germany is characterized by the large number of hospital beds and acute inpatient hospital treatment. In 2019 there were six hospital beds ( 1 ) and 252 inpatient treatment cases per 1000 population in Germany ( 2 ), which is significantly above the European and OECD average of four hospital beds and 146 treatment cases ( 1 , 2 ). In view of the statutory prioritization of outpatient care (Section 39 of the German Social Code V), staff shortages, and insufficient investment cost funding in the hospital sector, there are calls for more inpatient services to be diverted to the outpatient setting ( 3 – 6 ). In 2020, in accordance with Section 115b German Social Code V—Hospital-Based Outpatient Surgery, the contract parties (National Association of Statutory Health Insurance Funds, German Hospital Federation, National Association of Statutory Health Insurance Physicians) commissioned an expert analysis to provide recommendations for expanding the catalogue of operations which can be performed on an outpatient basis and other procedures to replace inpatient care (AOP catalogue). This analysis was drawn up by the IGES Institute and has identified numerous treatment services so far provided on an inpatient basis which could, however, also be feasible without the patient having to stay in hospital overnight (potential for providing treatment on an outpatient rather than inpatient basis). Examples of this include diagnostic endoscopy of the upper gastrointestinal tract, which in 2019 was documented around 1.3 million times under inpatient services, and conservative treatments of cancer, such as radiotherapy, nuclear medicine therapy, and chemotherapy which were performed around 450 000 times on an inpatient basis ( 7 ). The report also developed a process by which the feasibility of outpatient provision on a case-related basis was reviewed using routine documentation. This so-called context factor analysis uses documented diagnoses and procedures to assess whether there are any reasons militating against outpatient treatment. This then formed the basis for expanding the AOP catalogue and supplementing it with the context factor analysis. This came into effect in 2023 and was again expanded in 2024, yet without fully implementing the recommendations proposed in the IGES report. For example, not all exclusion criteria recommended for context factor analysis were adopted. At the same time, hybrid DRGs (hybrid diagnosis-related groups) were introduced together with Section 115f of German Social Code V—Special Sector-Specific Reimbursement. This means that, as of 2024, selected treatment services will be reimbursed at the same level, regardless of whether they are provided on an outpatient or inpatient basis. The remuneration amount for a hybrid DRG is calculated based on the relative proportion of outpatient or inpatient services provided and adjusted over time. The aim of these measures is to encourage the outpatient provision of services that were previously provided unnecessarily on an inpatient basis. In 2022, hospitals in Germany provided a total of 1.9 million operations in the outpatient setting, including procedures replacing inpatient care, while 16.8 million cases were treated on an inpatient basis ( 8 ). The present analysis is based on nationwide hospital discharge data in Germany for the year 2022 to estimate the proportion of treatment cases which can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting according to the criteria of the IGES report and compare it with the proportion based on the 2024 AOP catalogue. This analysis draws on the previous analysis covering the years 2017 to 2021 ( 9 ). In addition, the case figures of hybrid DRGs according to the criteria of the 2024 DRG catalogue are also estimated. The aim is to inform clinicians about the potential for providing treatment on an outpatient rather than inpatient basis and to create a data set that could help draw up further measures to allow treatment on an outpatient so far provided on an inpatient basis. Methods Data The microdata of the DRG statistics for 2022 were provided by the Research Data Center of the Federal Statistical Office and evaluated using controlled remote data processing ( 10 ). The data cover all acute inpatient cases treated in hospitals involved in the DRG reimbursement system and include for each treatment case its demographic data, reason for admission and discharge, specialist department(s) providing the treatment, primary and secondary diagnoses (International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems, 10th Revision, German modification of the ICD-10), and procedures (Operation and Procedure Codes, German procedure classification [OPS]). Treatment cases relating to deliveries or those involving neonates were excluded ( 11 ). This is based on the understanding that outpatient care of a neonate would only be possible if the mother’s delivery were also conducted in the outpatient setting. However, the available data do not allow a link to be created between the treatment cases of mother and neonate. Potential for providing treatment on an outpatient basis using the IGES model Using the IGES model ( 7 ), treatments that can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting were first identified from defined inclusion lists (Lists A1–A4) containing selected diagnoses, procedures, and DRG codes ( Box ). In a second step of the context factor analysis, cases were again excluded which are not considered feasible in the outpatient setting based on certain diagnoses, procedures, DRG case groups, mechanical ventilation, or other factors (Lists K1–K3, K5–K8, and the ICD observation status list). Lists K4 (Complexity arising from combinations of mild interventions) and K9 (Inpatient treatment indicated based on social circumstances) were not used due to the lack of sufficient implementation. Instead, the Hospital Frailty Risk Score developed by Gilbert et al. was applied for the context factor “frailty” (K8) ( 12 ). The Patient Clinical Complexity Level (PCCL; K7: Complex comorbidity warrants inpatient treatment), which is not included in the DRG statistics, was determined by applying the complication index of the German Inpatient Quality Indicators ( 13 ) and when “death” was the reason for discharge ( eTable 1 ). Box. Criteria for providing outpatient services. In its expert report, the IGES Institute has identified services which have so far been provided on an inpatient basis, but which could also be offered in an outpatient setting. Certain criteria are first analyzed using so-called context factors and must be fulfilled before a treatment can be provided in an outpatient setting. Outpatient provision of treatment is only assumed feasible if it is safe for the individual case, i.e., there are no context factors such as co-morbidities or the complexity of the procedure militating against outpatient provision ( 7 ). The AOP catalogue (catalogue of operations that can be performed on an outpatient basis and other outpatient procedures replacing inpatient care and inpatient treatments) conclusively lists services which hospitals can provide on an outpatient basis funded by the statutory health insurance and billed on the basis of the Uniform Rating Scale. The agreement is in accordance with Section 115b German Social Code Part V. The IGES expert report is intended to serve as a basis for expanding the AOP catalogue. Apart from the expansion, as from the year 2023, general circumstances were also agreed which, if present, may necessitate the inpatient provision of services (context factors). eTable 1. Inclusion and exclusion criteria for identifying treatment cases which can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting and (estimated) hybrid DRGs based on Pioch et al. ( 9 ). IGES model ( 6 ) 2024 AOP catalogue 2024 hybrid DRGs Inclusion list Description Inclusion list Description Inclusion list Description A1: Cases 115b (AOP) (5355 OPS codes) Cases with OPS code taken from the 2019 AOP catalogue and cases with OPS codes which were recommended for inclusion in the AOP catalogue. A1: Cases 115b (AOP) (3312 OPS codes) Cases with OPS code taken from the 2024 AOP catalogue. Hybrid DRG start catalogue (244 OPS codes) and corresponding DRG (12 DRGs) Cases with OPS code according to Annex 1 of the Hybrid DRG directive and corresponding (non-hybrid) DRGs. A2a: med. DRG (103 DRGs) Cases with medical DRG which may be considered for outpatient treatment. A3: DRG cancer (14 DRGs) Cases with medical DRG for neoplasms without complicating DRG treatment context.* 1 A4: DRG radiotherapy (31 DRGs) Cases with DRG involving primary service radiation therapy without complicating DRG treatment context.* 1 Exclusion list Description Exclusion list Description Exclusion list Description Deliveries Cases with inpatient delivery as reason for admission. Deliveries Cases with inpatient delivery as reason for admission. Deliveries Cases with inpatient delivery as reason for admission. Neonates Cases with birth or age up to 28 days as reason for admission. Neonates Cases with birth or age up to 28 days as reason for admission. Neonates Cases with birth or age up to 28 days as reason for admission. K1: DRG not feasible on an outpatient basis (242 DRGs) Cases with DRG which is not feasible on an outpatient basis due to the DRG benefit specifications. K2: Inpatient treatment indicated according to OPS code (19 440 OPS codes) Cases with OPS code which can only be performed on an inpatient basis due to the type of OPS service (incl. OPS codes ending with .x or .y). K2* 3 : Inpatient treatment indicated according to OPS code (5561 OPS codes) Cases with OPS code which can only be performed on an inpatient basis due to the type of OPS service (incl. OPS codes ending with .x or .y). ECF-V3: Complex procedure (5541 OPS codes) Cases with OPS code which can only be performed on an inpatient basis due to the type of OPS service (incl. OPS codes ending with .x or .y). K3: Ventilation >0 hours Cases for which ventilation is required. K3: Ventilation > 0 hours Cases for which ventilation is required. Ventilation >0 hours Cases for which ventilation is required. K4: High level of complexity arising from combinations of mild interventions Not defined so not included. K5: Postoperative reliability of care not ensured (154 ICD codes) Cases with ICD codes where problems with postoperative compliance and/or reliability of care are expected. K6: Diagnosis warrants inpatient treatment (1296 ICD -codes) Cases with ICD codes that require a particular acuity of patient care and therefore justify an inpatient stay. K6* 3 : Inpatient treatment indicated, or diagnosis warrants additional measures (1473 ICD codes) Cases with ICD codes that require a particular acuity of patient care and therefore justify an inpatient stay, and cases whose ICD code presupposes additional perioperative measures or post-operative care requirements. ECF-V1: Complex diagnoses (1433 ICD codes) Cases with ICD codes which justify an inpatient stay. K6-S: Inpatient treatment or diagnosis warrants additional measures (739 ICD codes) Cases whose ICD code presupposes additional perioperative measures or post-operative care requirements. K7: Complex comorbidity warrants inpatient treatment (PCCL ≥ 4) Cases with complex morbidity. Operationalization possible only to a certain degree via G-IQI complication index or “death” as reason for discharge. PCCL ≥ 3 Cases with PCCL ≥ 3. Operationalization possible only to a certain degree via G-IQI complication index or “death” as reason for discharge. K8: Inpatient treatment indicated due to frailty (care level, frailty, disability) Cases with OPS code for care level 4 or 5 or Hospital Frailty Index ≥ 5 (frail).* 2 The degree of disability was not taken into account due to lack of information. Functional impairment (7 ICD codes) Cases with ICD for severe/very severe motor/cognitive functional impairment according to the Barthel Index, FIM or MMSE. Care level (OPS codes) Cases with OPS for care level 4 or 5. K9: Inpatient treatment indicated due to social circumstances Not taken into account because not reflected in data. Medical or social reasons for care at home not being ensured. Not taken into account because not reflected in data. ICD Cancer stage As a supplement to lists A3 and A4. Not taken into account because not reflected in data. ECF-V2: Malignant neoplasms (523 ICD codes) Cases with ICD codes justifying an inpatient stay. ICD Observation status (311 ICD codes) Cases with ICD codes for which exclusion of a serious acute disorder may be assumed from the med. DRG codes (A2a). The codes were considered exclusion criteria regardless of List A2. Lower age limit Inpatient treatment may be justified until the child reaches the age of 1. Lower age limit and care level 2 to 5 (OPS codes) Inpatient treatment is justified from the beginning of the 2nd year of life until completion of the 12th year of life and care level is 2 to 5. Lower age limit and congenital heart defect (69 ICD codes + 6 OPS codes) Inpatient treatment is justified for certain procedures involving congenital heart defects until completion of the 12th year of life. Bilateral procedure (36 OPS codes with body site “B”) Cases with OPS codes and body site “B” in which the bilateral procedure can justify inpatient treatment. Ocular comorbidities (48 OPS codes) Cases with OPS codes for which ocular comorbidities (not defined by ICD) justify inpatient treatment. The listed OPS codes were taken into account regardless of any ocular comorbidities as exclusion criterion. G09-K8 and G09-K9 (OPS codes) Bilateral procedure, certain hernia repairs, inguinal and femoral, or certain hernia repairs umbilical hernia (II) in cases with DRG codes G09Z, G24C, G24B. I20-K56 (OPS codes) Complex procedure involving the foot (in cases with DRG code I20F). Hospital stay ≥ 2 days Cases with a hospital stay of two or more days. Open in a new tab * 1 In the case of conservative or radiotherapy treatments, the DRG treatment context must be taken into account for outpatient treatment. This includes the general condition of the patient and the reason for treatment. These are not reflected in DRG statistics data set. * 2 The Frailty Index comprises 109 ICD codes of which the W codes (“falls”) and the code Y95 (“nosocomial infection”) cannot be taken into account because they are not included in the ICD-10-GM. * 3 The exclusion criteria used in the AOP catalogue are based on the IGES model. ECF, exclusion context factor; AOP catalogue, catalogue of outpatient operations and procedures replacing inpatient care; DRG, Diagnosis Related Group; FIM, Functional Independence Measure; G-IQI, German Inpatient Quality Indicators; Hybrid DRGs, hybrid diagnosis related groups; ICD, International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems; MMSE, Mini Mental Status Examination; OPS, German procedure classification AOP catalogue Using the AOP catalogue, treatments that can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting were identified from the catalogue’s inclusion procedures (OPS codes). In a second step, the exclusion criteria of the AOP catalogue were applied for context factor analysis and cases not feasible for the outpatient setting were subsequently excluded. The context factors of the AOP catalogue contain proportionate codes drawn from the IGES lists K2, K3, K6, and K8. Unlike the IGES model, lower age limits are used as context factors in the 2024 AOP catalogue: This applies to children up to the age of one year and children aged two to 12 with a care level of 2 to 5 or who have a heart defect. Furthermore, OPS codes for bilateral eye procedures and eye operations for certain ocular comorbidities are also defined as exclusion criteria ( eTable 1 ). Hybrid DRGs Hybrid DRG cases were identified in approximation, based on the corresponding (non-hybrid) DRGs and applying hybrid DRG-specific inclusion and exclusion criteria (including, amongst others, certain diagnoses, procedures, length of stay, ventilation) in accordance with the 2024 G-DRG definition manual. These three approaches are listed in eTable 1 for comparison. Analysis Treatment cases identified as being potentially available in the outpatient setting according to the IGES model and the AOP catalogue were presented as proportions of the total number of inpatient treatment cases and stratified according to age groups, specialist department groups, reason for admission, and hospital stay categories. The proportion of hybrid DRG cases was also presented. SAS Version 9.3 was used for analysis, with due consideration of Good Practice in Secondary Data Analysis ( 14 ). Results Of 14.8 million inpatient cases during the year 2022 (excluding deliveries and neonates), 10.4 million (70.3%) were initially included that could potentially have been treated in the outpatient setting according to the IGES model. Of these, 7.8 million (75.4%) were then excluded by context factor analysis, so that 2.6 million (17.3% of all inpatient treatment cases) were ultimately identified that could potentially have been provided in the outpatient setting. Initially, 4.6 million cases were included (31.3%) based on the AOP catalogue, of which 1.4 million (30.5%) were subsequently excluded by context analysis. Hence, 3.2 million cases (21.8% of all treatment cases) were identified that could have potentially been treated in the outpatient setting ( eFigure ). At around 153 000 cases, a proportion of 1.0% of all treatment cases were identified as potential hybrid DRGs ( Table , eTable 2 ). Based on the IGES model, the highest proportions of treatment cases potentially suitable for outpatient treatment were observed among children and adolescents up to the age of 19 (31.3%), followed by adults of working age (24.3%). The highest proportion based on the AOP catalogue was accounted for by adults of working age (26.7%), followed by the age group 65 to 84 years (21.5%). The specialist departments radiotherapy, urology, ears, nose and throat medicine, and ophthalmology demonstrated the highest potential for providing treatment on an outpatient rather than inpatient basis according to the IGES model (29.1 to 33.9%). Based on the AOP catalogue, the highest potential was found in urology, gynecology, and ears, nose and throat medicine (35.4 to 44.0%), and in ophthalmology and dentistry and oral medicine (around 30% each). The identified hybrid DRGs were almost entirely accounted for by the specialist departments of gynecology, surgery, and urology ( Table , eTable 2 ). eFigure. Open in a new tab Inclusions and exclusions for identifying treatment cases which can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting according to the IGES model and the AOP catalogue, data year 2022 based on Pioch et al. ( 9 ) * 1 The number of cases in the individual lists may exceed the total number of cases as one single case can be assigned to several lists. * 2 The exclusion criteria used in the AOP catalogue are based on the IGES model. AOP catalogue, catalogue of outpatient operations and procedures replacing inpatient care; DRG, Diagnosis Related Group; ICD, International Statistical Classification of Diseases and Related Health Problems; OPS, German procedure classification Table. Proportion of treatment cases which can potentially be provided in the outpatient setting according to the IGES model and the AOP catalogue as well as the proportion of hybrid DRGs, data year 2022. IGES model 2024 AOP catalogue 2024 hybrid DRGs Proportion of potential outpatient cases % Proportion of potential inpatient cases % Proportion of hybrid DRGs % All inpatient treatment cases* 1 17.3 21.8 1.0 Age groups Up to age 19 years (excluding neonates) 31.3 12.7 0.7 20 to 64 years 24.3 26.7 1.7 65 to 84 years 11.3 21.5 0.7 Age 85 years or older 4.8 11.6 0.2 Specialist department groups Internal medicine and predominantly non-surgical specialties 9.0 18.3 0.0 Neurology 11.9 15.2 0.0 Pediatric and adolescent medicine 27.1 6.0 0.3 Neonatal and perinatal medicine 26.1 5.0 0.2 Gynecology (and obstetrics) 25.7 38.7 3.7 Surgery and predominantly surgical specialties 23.3 24.3 2.2 Neurosurgery 9.5 5.3 XXX Urology 30.3 44.0 4.4 Ear, nose and throat medicine 33.9 35.4 XXX Ophthalmology 29.1 30.5 0.0 Nuclear medicine 1.9 3.7 0.0 Radiotherapy 29.8 8.7 0.0 De