AHA.115.001593 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 12. Komura D, Ishikawa S.. Machine learning approaches for pathologic diagnosis. Virchows Arch. 2019;475(2):131–138. doi: 10.1007/s00428-019-02594-w [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 13. Handelman GS, Kok HK, Chandra RV, et al. eDoctor: machine learning and the future of medicine. J Intern Med. 2018;284(6):603–619. doi: 10.1111/joim.12822 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Khayyat A, Esmaeil Pour MA, Mousavi S, et al. Primary malignant melanoma of the genitourinary system: a systemic review and report of eight cases. Cureus. 2022;14(11):e30444. doi: 10.7759/cureus.30444[Mismatch] ** Provides a recent systemic review and new cases to expand insight on rare GU melanomas. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. DePalo DK, Elleson KM, Carr MJ, et al. Genitourinary melanoma: an overview for the clinician. Asian J Urol. 2022;9(4):407–422. doi: 10.1016/j.ajur.2022.01.003 ** Offers clinical-focused, up-to-date guidance on diagnosis and management of GU melanomas. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Heinzelmann-Schwarz AV, Nixdorf S, Valadan M, et al. A clinicopathological review of 33 patients with vulvar melanoma identifies c-KIT as a prognostic marker. Int J Mol Med. 2014;33(4):784–794. doi: 10.3892/ijmm.2014.1659 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Nagarajan P, Curry JL, Ning J, et al. Tumor thickness and mitotic rate robustly predict melanoma-specific survival in patients with primary vulvar melanoma: a retrospective review of 100 cases. Clin Cancer Res. 2017;23(8):2093–2104. doi: 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-16-2126 * Large study identifying strong prognostic markers specific to vulvar melanoma. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Dobrică EC, Vâjâitu C, Condrat CE, et al. Vulvar and vaginal melanomas—the darker shades of gynecological cancers. Biomedicines. 2021;9(7):758. doi: 10.3390/biomedicines9070758 * Recent review emphasizing diagnostic challenges and evolving treatment strategies. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Cuccia F, D’Alessandro S, Blasi L, et al. The role of radiotherapy in the management of vaginal melanoma: a literature review with a focus on the potential synergistic role of immunotherapy. JPM. 2023;13(7):1142. doi: 10.3390/jpm13071142 ** A recent and detailed review emphasizing emerging treatment combinations, particularly radiotherapy and immunotherapy. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 20. Skovsted S, Nielsen K, Blaakær J.. Melanomas of the vulva and vagina. Dan Med J. 2017;64(2):A5346. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Frumovitz M, Etchepareborda M, Sun CC, et al. Primary malignant melanoma of the vagina. Obstet Gynecol. 2010;116(6):1358–1365. doi: 10.1097/AOG.0b013e3181fb8045 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Wang D, Xu T, Zhu H, et al. Primary malignant melanomas of the female lower genital tract: clinicopathological characteristics and management. Am J Cancer Res. 2020;10(12):4017–4037. [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Janco JMT, Markovic SN, Weaver AL, et al. Vulvar and vaginal melanoma: case series and review of current management options including neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Gynecol Oncol. 2013;129(3):533–537. doi: 10.1016/j.ygyno.2013.02.028 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Lian B, Si L, Cui C, et al. Phase II randomized trial comparing high-dose IFN-α2b with temozolomide plus cisplatin as systemic adjuvant therapy for resected mucosal melanoma. Clin Cancer Res. 2013;19(16):4488–4498. doi: 10.1158/1078-0432.CCR-13-0739[Mismatch] * One of the few RCTs addressing systemic adjuvant therapy for mucosal melanoma. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Hsu HC, Tsai SY, Wu SL, et al. Longitudinal perceptions of the side effects of chemotherapy in patients with gynecological cancer. Support Care Cancer. 2017;25(11):3457–3464. doi: 10.1007/s00520-017-3768-7 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Schreiber RD, Old LJ, Smyth MJ.. Cancer immunoediting: integrating immunity’s roles in cancer suppression and promotion. Science. 2011;331(6024):1565–1570. doi: 10.1126/science.1203486 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Hodi FS, O’Day SJ, McDermott DF, et al. Improved survival with ipilimumab in patients with metastatic melanoma. N Engl J Med. 2010;363(8):711–723. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1003466 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Topalian SL, Hodi FS, Brahmer JR, et al. Safety, activity, and immune correlates of anti-PD-1 antibody in cancer. N Engl J Med. 2012;366(26):2443–2454. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1200690 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Wolchok JD, Kluger H, Callahan MK, et al. Nivolumab plus ipilimumab in advanced melanoma. N Engl J Med. 2013;369(2):122–133. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa1302369 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Chen DS, Mellman I.. Elements of cancer immunity and the cancer-immune set point. Nature. 2017;541(7637):321–330. doi: 10.1038/nature21349 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplemental Material IFSO_A_2540747_SM2974.docx (2.3MB, docx) Supplemental Material IFSO_A_2540747_SM2973.docx (2.5MB, docx) Supplemental Material IFSO_A_2540747_SM2969.docx (17.8KB, docx)',25,'2025-10-13 06:42:28','2025-10-13 06:42:28');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1652,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12451213','Denmark''s sharp rise in the annual prevalence of gestational diabetes: Rethinking screening and prevention.','Baygi F, Vinter CA, Søndergaard J.','2025','gestationel macrosomic glucosuria glucosuria loses firts vinter copilot jeppesen maindal ovesen scheuer mathiesen s00592 damm houshmand oeregaard kelstrup lauenborg mathiesen clausen gestationel behandling obstetrisk kontrol opfølgning sundhed sundhed sundhedsfaglig laegehaandbogen obstetrik tilstande sygdomme risikofaktorer svangerskabet gestationel scheuer who2013 s00592 hapo nejmoa0707943 sharifi iadpsg vaarasmaki pouta elliot kwp020 dalmacy akinduro e2254765','Denmark has historically reported relatively low gestationel diabetes mellitus (GDM) rates (3%–4% of pregnancies). 1 However, recent data show a concerning rise. 2 A national cohort study of over 287 000 births between 2013 and 2017 showed an average 7% annual increase in GDM prevalence, reaching 4.2% nationally by 2017, with some regions approaching 6.2%. 2 This upward trend is alarming and warrants immediate evaluation, as Denmark''s previously low GDM rates may soon align with higher rates observed in other countries. This shift carries serious implications, including increased risks of macrosomia, childhood obesity, and the future development of type 2 diabetes. 3 This increase has occurred despite unchanged screening criteria, indicating a shift in maternal health risk profile. Among the modifiable risk factors contributing to this trend, rising maternal age and pre‐pregnancy body mass index (BMI) are well established. 3 For instance, women aged 35 to 49 have nearly double the GDM prevalence of those aged 25 to 34. 1 Furthermore, women of non‐Western origin face a significantly higher risk (about 1.7 times greater) compared to native Danish women. 1 These demographic shifts contribute to a growing burden on maternal health services, as they are associated with higher GDM risk and often require more individualized screening, care coordination, and follow‐up. Denmark employs a risk‐factor‐based screening approach, in which only women with predefined criteria receive an oral glucose tolerance test. These criteria include pre‐pregnancy BMI ≥ 27 kg/m 2 , previous GDM, first‐degree relatives with diabetes, polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS), twins or multiple pregnancies, and previous delivery of a macrosomic infant (≥4500 g), and glucosuria at any stage of pregnancy. 4 If glucosuria is detected, an OGTT is prompted unless a normal test was performed within the past 4 weeks. 4 Additionally, notably, maternal age and ethnicity are not part of these predefined criteria. While this model is resource‐conserving, it may fail to identify a significant number of GDM cases, resulting in a substantial gap in detection. This is supported by recent Danish data showing that if WHO 2013 diagnostic thresholds were applied, the estimated GDM prevalence would rise from 2.2% to 21.5%, identifying many previously undiagnosed women at elevated risk of adverse outcomes. 5 As more women meet at least one existing risk factor—such as elevated pre‐pregnancy BMI—the current approach loses its intended selectivity and may not function effectively as a targeted screening strategy. Moreover, employing diagnostic thresholds that are less stringent than those recommended by WHO 2013 means that many milder cases go undetected. 5 , 6 Evidence indicates that even mild hyperglycemia can increase adverse outcomes, and that treatment can improve both maternal and neonatal health. 6 This calls into question the continued appropriateness of the current screening policy. Universal screening for GDM is currently recommended for 24 to 28 weeks of gestation. 7 Revising screening criteria such as lowering BMI or age threshold may enhance case detection by identifying women who would otherwise remain undiagnosed. 5 While such changes would require additional resources, they also highlight how diagnostic definitions directly shape reported prevalence and clinical action. The rising prevalence of GDM and existing detection gaps present both clinical and policy challenges. Undiagnosed or late‐diagnosed GDM increases the risk of pregnancy complications, such as large‐for‐gestational‐age infants and shoulder dystocia. 8 GDM in general has long‐term metabolic consequences: Mothers face an elevated risk of T2D, 3 while offspring are more likely to develop obesity, metabolic syndrome, and impaired glucose tolerance. 9 This underscores the need for continued postnatal monitoring and support. Although national guidelines to improve GDM detection are currently pending approval, both their timely implementation and prevention measures must be prioritized. Pre‐conception care should target modifiable risk factors and engage high‐risk women early. Women with GDM require structured follow‐up after delivery; however, adherence remains low. 10 Integrating postpartum glucose testing and long‐term monitoring into primary care could improve outcomes and reduce long‐term complications. Future analyses using Danish data to estimate population‐attributable risk could further guide these efforts by quantifying the broader impact of GDM and improving identification of high‐risk individuals. We believe that Denmark''s rise in GDM prevalence is no longer negligible. Previously distinguished by its low rates, the country is now moving closer to the European average. A more integrated, data‐driven approach covering preconception to postpartum care is essential—using registry data and risk profile to guide prevention, screening, and follow‐up. Timely prevention and screening can mitigate the long‐term consequences for women and future generations. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS FB conceptualized the editorial, conducted the literature review, and wrote the firts draft. JS, and Vinter C.A provided critical review. All authors approved the final version for publication. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT None declared. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This manuscript was revised with the assistance of Microsoft Copilot to improve clarity and language. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study. REFERENCES 1. Jeppesen C, Maindal HT, Kristensen JK, Ovesen PG, Witte DR. National study of the prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus among Danish women from 2004 to 2012. Scand J Public Health. 2017;45(8):811‐817. doi: 10.1177/1403494817736943 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Scheuer CM, Andersen MH, Mathiesen ER, et al. Regional divergence and time trends in the prevalence of gestational diabetes mellitus: a national Danish cohort study. Acta Diabetol. 2023;60(3):379‐386. doi: 10.1007/s00592-022-02013-8 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Damm P, Houshmand‐Oeregaard A, Kelstrup L, Lauenborg J, Mathiesen ER, Clausen TD. Gestational diabetes mellitus and long‐term consequences for mother and offspring: a view from Denmark. Diabetologia. 2016;59(7):1396‐1399. doi: 10.1007/s00125-016-3985-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Gestationel Diabetes Mellitus (GDM) . Behandling, obstetrisk kontrol og postpartum opfølgning. Sundhed.dk; 2023. https://www.sundhed.dk/sundhedsfaglig/laegehaandbogen/obstetrik/tilstande‐og‐sygdomme/risikofaktorer‐i‐svangerskabet/gestationel‐diabetes‐gdm/ [ Google Scholar ] 5. Scheuer CM, Jensen DM, McIntyre HD, et al. Applying WHO2013 diagnostic criteria for gestational diabetes mellitus reveals currently untreated women at increased risk. Acta Diabetol. 2023;60(12):1663‐1673. doi: 10.1007/s00592-023-02148-2 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. HAPO Study Cooperative Research Group , Metzger BE, Lowe LP, et al. Hyperglycemia and adverse pregnancy outcomes. N Engl J Med. 2008;358(19):1991‐2002. doi: 10.1056/NEJMoa0707943 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. US Preventive Services Task Force , Davidson KW, Barry MJ, et al. Screening for gestational diabetes: US preventive services task force recommendation statement. JAMA. 2021;326(6):531‐538. doi: 10.1001/jama.2021.11922 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Shah BR, Sharifi F. Perinatal outcomes for untreated women with gestational diabetes by IADPSG criteria: a population‐based study. BJOG. 2020;127(1):116‐122. doi: 10.1111/1471-0528.15964 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Vääräsmäki M, Pouta A, Elliot P, et al. Adolescent manifestations of metabolic syndrome among children born to women with gestational diabetes in a general‐population birth cohort. Am J Epidemiol. 2009;169(10):1209‐1215. doi: 10.1093/aje/kwp020 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 10. D''Amico R, Dalmacy D, Akinduro JA, et al. Patterns of postpartum primary care follow‐up and diabetes‐related care after diagnosis of gestational diabetes. JAMA Netw Open. 2023;6(2):e2254765. doi: 10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2022.54765 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data sharing not applicable to this article as no datasets were generated or analysed during the current study.',28,'2025-10-13 06:42:40','2025-10-13 06:42:40');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1653,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12452526','Addendum.','Pauly N, Sievert KD.','2025','wahner steepest tesaro eickler kds grieger eisemann hammersen katalinic waldmann m2024 wahner hubner meisel s15010 rieck tfm siedler impfquoten kinderschutzimpfungen impfsurveillance epid','We sincerely appreciate this excellent review article ( 1 ) and would like to highlight an important aspect that was not addressed: the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on vaccination behavior. As reported by Wähner et al. ( 2 ), the introduction of cost reimbursement for HPV vaccination in boys initially resulted in monthly vaccination rates comparable to, or even exceeding, those of girls. By the end of 2021, the HPV vaccination rate for 15-year-old boys was 44.4% for the first dose and 26.4% for the complete series. However, the COVID-19 pandemic had a profound negative impact on HPV vaccine uptake. In 2020, the number of administered doses dropped significantly, with the steepest declines occurring during the first lockdown. By 2021, monthly vaccine uptake had fallen by up to 71% in boys and 60% in girls compared to 2019. This decline can be attributed to multiple factors, including reduced healthcare visits, prioritization of COVID-19 vaccinations, and a rise in vaccine hesitancy. By the end of 2021, only 54% of girls and 26.4% of boys had completed the full HPV vaccination series ( 3 ), falling significantly short of the WHO’s target of 90%. Given these trends, an even further decline in coverage in subsequent years is likely. These concerning findings underscore the urgent need for further research on HPV vaccination uptake post-pandemic to identify and address emerging gaps. Targeted public health campaigns will be crucial to mitigating this decline and improving vaccination rates. Achieving the WHO’s goal of reducing HPV-related cancers through vaccination necessitates a significant and sustained increase in vaccine uptake, presenting a critical opportunity for cancer prevention. Footnotes Conflict of interest statement NP received lecture honoraria from GSK, Tesaro, AstraZeneca, and Eickler. KDS declares that no conflict of interest exists. References 1. Grieger P, Eisemann N, Hammersen F, Rudolph C, Katalinic A, Waldmann A. Initial evidence of a possible effect of HPV vaccination on cancer incidence in Germany—focus on cervical cancer. Dtsch Arztebl Int. 2024;121:415–421. doi: 10.3238/arztebl.m2024.0062. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Wähner C, Hübner J, Meisel D, et al. Uptake of HPV vaccination among boys after the introduction of gender-neutral HPV vaccination in Germany before and during the COVID-19 pandemic. Infection. 2023;51:1293–1304. doi: 10.1007/s15010-023-01978-0. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Rieck TFM, Siedler A. Impfquoten von Kinderschutzimpfungen in Deutschland - aktuelle Ergebnisse aus der RKI-Impfsurveillance. Epid Bull. 2022;48:3–25. [ Google Scholar ]',4,'2025-10-13 06:42:54','2025-10-13 06:42:54');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1654,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12451191','Implementation of extensive cytoreductive surgery protocol in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer: Real-world data on surgical treatment distribution and survival of the whole patient cohort; a single center experience.','Norppa N, Staff S, Helminen M, Auranen A, Saarelainen S.','2025','tampere tampere tampere tampere c48 c48 c56 c56 c56 c56 c56 c56 c57 c57 c80 r19 r18 peritonectomy splenectomy cardiophrenic survivals aletti cardiophrenic ca12 carcinosis ca12 carcinosis taxan ca12 taxane midway dismal operability shalowitz wallen tripling falconer norppa helminen auranen saarelainen tampere r19582 aogs tampereen yliopisto tampereen ammattikorkeakoulu finelib norppa helminen auranen saarelainen aogs ervik gco gco aletti dowdy gostout hodeib bristow eisenhauer zivanovic eisenhauer rustum sonoda bristow hammadi falconer joneborg krawiec palsdottir bottai salehi norppa helminen auranen saarelainen aletti dowdy podratz cliby fagotti ferrandina vizzielli nct01461850 trope amant kehoe nankivell chorus onda jcog0602 shawky choudhary coleridge cd005343 savvatis cummins shalowitz giuntoli harlan trimble thrall symons flum aogs','Abstract Introduction The aim was to assess the effect of the implementation of maximal surgical cytoreduction on the treatment decisions and overall survival of the entire patient cohort with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. Material and Methods We collected retrospectively all newly diagnosed patients with FIGO stage IIIB–IVB epithelial ovarian cancer between 2013 and 2019 in Tampere University Hospital, Finland. Altogether, 333 patients were divided into two groups based on the date of diagnosis: Group 1 ( n = 162) diagnosed before March 2016 and Group 2 ( n = 171) after 1st of March 2016, when a systematic change in surgical approach toward maximal surgical cytoreduction was implemented in our institution. Results No statistically significant differences were found in the proportions of patients having surgery or treated non‐surgically between the two time periods: 76.5% of patients in Group 1 and 71.9% in Group 2 underwent surgical treatment, while the others were treated with chemotherapy only or referred directly to palliative care ( p = 0.38). In addition, there was no statistically significant difference in overall survival in patients who received any treatment (surgery and chemotherapy or chemotherapy only) between groups: the median overall survival was 32.8 months (95% CI 25.1–40.5) in Group 1 and 37.3 months (31.5–43.1) in Group 2, p = 0.214. Conclusions The change in surgical approach toward maximal surgical cytoreduction in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer did not significantly change the magnitude of patients who received only chemotherapy or underwent surgical treatment in our center. While the shift in surgical paradigm may provide survival benefits for carefully selected surgically treated patients, it did not result in a statistically significant improvement in overall survival for the entire patient cohort. Keywords: cytoreductive surgery, epithelial ovarian carcinoma, gynecologic surgery, ovarian cancer, surgical oncology The change in surgical approach toward maximal surgical cytoreduction in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer does not significantly seem to change the magnitude of patients receiving only chemotherapy or undergoing surgical treatment. While the shift in surgical paradigm may provide survival benefits for carefully selected surgically treated patients, it does not seem to result in statistically significant improvement in overall survival for the entire patient cohort. Abbreviations NACT neoadjuvant chemotherapy PARP poly (ADP‐ribose) polymerase OS overall survival Key message. This single‐center study provides reliable real‐world data on the impact of the implementation of a structured surgical protocol on treatment decisions and overall survival of the whole patient cohort diagnosed with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. 1. INTRODUCTION Epithelial ovarian cancer is the 8th most common cancer among women, with 320 000 new cases worldwide annually. It presents the worst survival of gynecologic malignancies due to diagnoses made at the advanced stage in the majority of cases. 1 The standard treatment of care in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer includes surgical treatment aiming at resection of all visible tumors from the abdominal cavity, followed by adjuvant chemotherapy. Complete cytoreduction has been shown to associate with the highest survival rates in several studies. 2 , 3 , 4 , 5 , 6 In the last two decades, extensive surgical procedures, also called ultra‐radical surgery, have been introduced into the surgical treatment of ovarian cancer. Studies supporting extensive cytoreductive surgery in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer have usually included carefully selected patients who are fit enough to undergo massive surgical treatment and mostly represent FIGO (The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) stage III. Also, patient selection for surgical treatment might be influenced by patients'' socioeconomic or insurance status. 7 , 8 Patients who are not, for any reason, eligible to undergo surgical treatment are treated with chemotherapy only or sometimes referred straight to palliative care. Prevalence and survival outcomes of these non‐surgically managed patients have been rarely presented in studies regarding the advantages of the surgical paradigm change toward extensive cytoreductive surgery. 9 Tampere University Hospital is the second largest tertiary referral center in Finland, serving a catchment area of 0.9 million people, where all patients with gynecological cancer are referred. In early 2016, our hospital adopted extensive cytoreductive surgery as the standard approach for treating advanced epithelial ovarian cancer, marking a significant change in our surgical paradigm. The adoption of the new surgical protocol also included the thorough assessment of nutritional and performance status of all newly diagnosed ovarian cancer patients in order to target possible rehabilitation measures. We have previously shown that this change led to significantly improved overall survival (OS) among surgically treated patients. 10 In the present study, we aimed to further evaluate the impact of this new surgical protocol on treatment decisions regarding all newly diagnosed patients with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. More specifically, we wanted to focus on the proportion of patients deemed ineligible for surgical treatment during the two time periods. The secondary objective was to evaluate the survival of non‐surgically treated patients, as well as the OS of the whole patient cohort in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer after the surgical paradigm change toward maximal surgical effort. 2. MATERIAL AND METHODS 2.1. Data collection This retrospective, registry‐based study included all patients diagnosed with advanced ovarian cancer in Tampere University Hospital between March 2013 and March 2019, including 333 patients in total. The Tampere University Hospital patient database was queried for cases of patients with a diagnosis of ovarian cancer, abdominal tumor of unclear origin, or ascites (identified by International Classification of Diseases (ICD‐10) codes C48.19&, C48.29&, C56.00&, C56.71&, C56.72%, C56.73&, C56.74&, C56.98&, C57.8&, C57.9&, C80.90& R19, R18) who visited our emergency room, gynecological ward, or outpatient clinic between March 2013 and March 2019. Inclusion criteria for this analysis were FIGO 2014 stage IIIB–IVB ovarian, Fallopian tube, or primary peritoneal epithelial cancer. The diagnosis was based on tumor histology or cytology from ascites or pleural effusion if the histological sample was not available. Patients without a histological or cytological diagnosis or concomitant other malignant disease were excluded. 2.2. Study groups Patients were categorized into two groups according to the date of diagnosis. Group 1 included patients diagnosed between March 2013 and February 2016. (Figure 1 ). All patients who were considered possible candidates for surgical treatment underwent standard surgery with the intent to reach maximal cytoreduction. Patients whose disease involved the upper abdomen were, in most cases, considered unresectable. There was no standardized protocol in patient selection during these years. Group 2 consisted of patients treated from the 1st of March 2016 to the end of March 2019. In our institution, the change in surgical approach toward maximal surgical effort was initiated in March 2016; hence, this was decided to be the division point between the two groups. The change was preceded by internal educational sessions of the gynecologic oncology team and surgical training in a European accredited ovarian cancer surgical center in 2015. Gastrointestinal and thoracic surgeons were educated about the change in the surgical approach. After March 1st, 2016, extensive cytoreductive procedures, including peritonectomy, diaphragm resection, splenectomy, distal pancreatectomy, liver resection, cholecystectomy, and multiple bowel resections, were performed when considered applicable. All the bowel resections were performed by gastrointestinal surgeons and hepatic resections by hepatic surgeons, while all the other procedures were performed by gynecologic oncologists. Thoracic surgeons were involved in the diaphragmatic and cardiophrenic lymph node resections in the beginning. FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Distribution of patients according to treatment strategy in both study groups. After this time point, the patient selection for extensive surgery was structured with the criteria of American Society of Anesthesiologists classification ≤2, age under 75 years, and preoperative albumin level over 30 g/L. If the albumin level was suboptimal at the time of diagnosis, a preoperative nutritional support was initiated to reach the required level of 30 g/L. If these criteria were not met, patients were referred for neoadjuvant chemotherapy (NACT) followed by possible interval cytoreduction, and possible rehabilitation measures were initiated. 10 If cytoreductive surgery was assumed to be ineligible for any reason at the time of diagnosis, the planned treatment was chemotherapy or best supportive care. All patients were treated by trained gynecologic oncologists. 2.3. Outcome measures The main outcome measures were the OS of the two patient cohorts, including both surgically and medically‐only treated patients, and the proportion of non‐surgically treated patients. OS of non‐surgically treated patients was defined as the time interval from the diagnosis to death or last follow‐up visit, whichever occurred first. The survival data of surgically treated patients presented in our previous study was also updated. 10 The rationale not to perform surgery, either by primary or interval setting, was collected. Given medical treatment, including chemotherapy, bevacizumab, and poly (ADP‐ribose) polymerase (PARP) inhibitor, was documented. 2.4. Statistical methods Categorical variables were compared with Fisher''s exact test. Continuous variables were compared using the Mann‐Whitney U‐test. Univariate survival analyses were performed using the Kaplan–Meier method, and differences in survivals were analyzed with the log‐rank test. A post hoc power analysis was performed to estimate the probability of detecting a statistically significant difference given the observed effect size and sample size, which yielded a statistical power of 14.2%, indicating limited ability to detect group differences. All statistical tests used were two‐sided, and p ‐value <0.05 was considered significant. The analyses were performed with IBM SPSS Statistics version 29 (IBM Corp., Armonk, N.Y., USA). 3. RESULTS The total number of 333 patients with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer was diagnosed during the study period from March 2013 to March 2019, 162 patients in Group 1 and 171 patients in Group 2. No statistically significant differences were found in the proportion of patients having surgery or treated non‐surgically between groups. In Group 1, 76.5% of patients underwent surgical treatment, while 23.5% were treated non‐surgically. The corresponding numbers in Group 2 were 71.9% and 28.1% ( p = 0.381). The characteristics of surgically treated patients in both study groups were detailed in a study published previously. 10 The key findings from the analysis of these patients were as follows: The rate of primary cytoreductive surgeries increased from 58% to 69%, but the difference was not statistically significant. The complexity of surgeries was retrospectively categorized by surgical complexity score by Aletti. 11 The median surgical complexity score was 2 and 7 in Groups 1 and 2, respectively. The proportion of low complexity surgeries decreased from 70% to 18%, while the proportion of high surgical complexity scores increased from 4% to 46% ( p < 0.001). Bowel, peritoneal, diaphragm, liver, and cardiophrenic lymph node resections were more often performed in the second study group. The proportion of complete cytoreduction increased from 17% to 53%, while the suboptimal cytoreduction rate decreased from 63% to 14%. (Table 1 ). Additionally, the rate of minor versus major complications among patients who had any complications was similar between the two study groups. The reduced use of bevacizumab in the second study group was mostly attributable to the higher rate of complete cytoreduction achieved in stage III patients. TABLE 1. Characteristics of all patients. Group 1 ( n = 162) Group 2 ( n = 171) p ‐value Age (years) 69 (24–89) 69 (31–90) FIGO stage 0.023 IIIB 6 (3.7%) 17 (9.9%) IIIC 88 (54.3%) 80 (46.8%) IVA 28 (17.3%) 19 (11.1%) IVB 40 (24.7%) 55 (32.2%) Treatment 0.381 Surgery 124 (76.5%) 123 (71.9%) PCS 72 (58.1%) 85 (69.1%) ICS 52 (41.9%) 38 (30.9%) Proportion of NACT patients who underwent ICS 52/60 (86.7%) 38/49 (77.6%) Chemotherapy after surgery 113 (91.1%) 117 (95.1%) No surgery 38 (23.5%) 48 (28.1%) Chemotherapy only 29 (76.3%) 36 (75.0%) Palliative care 9 (23.7%) 12 (25.0%) Surgical complexity score <0.001 Low ≤3 87 (70.2%) 22 (17.9%) Intermediate 4–7 32 (25.8%) 45 (36.6%) High ≥8 5 (4.0%) 56 (45.5%) Cytoreduction <0.001 Complete (no macroscopic residual tumor) 21 (16.9%) 65 (52.9%) Optimal (≤1 cm) 25 (20.2%) 41 (33.3%) Suboptimal (>1 cm) 78 (62.9%) 17 (13.8%) Clavien‐Dindo complication grade 0.298 I–IIIA 73 (58.9%) 89 (72.4%) IIIB–V 9 (7.3%) 18 (14.6%) Bevacizumab 84 (51.9%) 69 (40.4%) 0.028 PARP inhibitor 8 (4.9%) 24 (14.0%) 0.008 Open in a new tab Note : Data are presented as n (%) or median (range). Abbreviations: FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; ICS, interval cytoreductive surgery; NACT, neoadjuvant chemotherapy; PARP, poly ADP ribose polymerase; PCS, primary cytoreductive surgery. The characteristics of non‐surgically treated patients are presented in Table 2 . These patients were similar with respect to median age, body mass index, American Society of Anesthesiologists classification, FIGO stage, level of CA12‐5 or serum albumin, or presence of ascites at diagnosis. The rationale for not planning surgical treatment at the time of diagnosis is presented in Table 2 . The most common reason to withhold surgery in both groups was extensive carcinosis that was interpreted as unresectable with the current surgical approach, affecting 63% of patients in Group 1 and 56% in Group 2. After the implementation of extensive cytoreductive surgery and more structured patient selection, factors such as age, poor health status, and low albumin level increasingly influenced treatment decisions compared to previous years; however, the differences between groups were not statistically significant. TABLE 2. Characteristics of non‐surgically treated patients. Group 1 ( n = 38) Group 2 ( n = 48) p ‐value Age (years) 76 (43–89) 75 (45–90) 0.734 BMI (kg/m 2 ) 26 (19–52) 27 (18–47) 0.855 ASA class 0.267 I–II 20 (52.6%) 32 (66.7%) III–IV 18 (47.4%) 16 (33.3%) FIGO stage 0.124 III 11 (28.9%) 22 (45.8%) IV 27 (71.1%) 26 (54.2%) CA12‐5 (U/mL) 511 (10–23 000) 668 (27–4930) 0.639 Ascites 28 (73.7%) 41 (85.4%) 0.187 Serum albumin (g/L) 30 (14–52) 26 (16–43) 0.304 Histology <0.001 Cytological only 8 (21.1%) 0 (0.0%) Histological sample 30 (78.9%) 48 (100.0%) High grade serous 29 (96.7%) 46 (95.8%) Other 1 (3.3%) 2 (4.17%) Diagnostic laparoscopy 10 (26.3%) 23 (47.9%) 0.047 Main reason not to perform surgery 0.640 Age 8 (21.1%) 13 (27.1%) Poor health status 2 (5.3%) 5 (10.4%) Carcinosis/cancer dissemination 24 (63.2%) 27 (56.3%) Low albumin 1 (2.6%) 2 (4.2%) Patient''s own desire 3 (7.9%) 1 (2.1%) NACT started aiming for possible further surgery 8 (21.1%) 11 (22.9%) 1.00 Reasons not to perform ICS 0.385 SD/PD in CT scan 4 (50.0%) 8 (72.7%) Poor health status 3 (37.5%) 3 (27.3%) Patient''s own wish 1 (12.5%) 0 (0.0%) Treatment 1.000 Chemotherapy 29 (76.3%) 36 (75.0%) Taxan‐carboplatin 13 (44.8%) 20 (55.6%) Single carboplatin 16 (55.2%) 16 (44.4%) No chemotherapy 9 (23.7%) 12 (25.0%) Bevacizumab 9 (23.7%) 10 (20.8%) 0.798 PARP inhibitor 1 (2.6%) 4 (8.5%) 0.374 Open in a new tab Note : Data are presented as n (%) or median (range). Abbreviations: ASA, American Society of Anesthesiologists; BMI, body mass index; CA12‐5, cancer antigen 12–5; CT, computed tomography; FIGO, International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; NACT, neoadjuvant chemotherapy; PARP, poly ADP ribose polymerase; PD, progressive disease; SD, stable disease. There was no significant difference between the groups in the proportion of non‐surgically treated patients who received chemotherapy, 76.3% (29/38) in Group 1 and 75.0% (36/48) in Group 2. Combination chemotherapy with taxane and carboplatin was the most used treatment modality in both groups. Furthermore, the use of bevacizumab or PARP‐inhibitors did not differ between the study groups. The proportion of non‐surgically treated patients referred directly to palliative care was quite similar, 23.7% and 25.0% in Group 1 and Group 2, respectively ( p = 1.000) (Table 2 ). At the start of chemotherapy treatment, 21% of non‐surgically treated patients in Group 1 and 22% of patients in Group 2 were considered possible candidates for further interval cytoreductive surgery but were deemed ineligible for surgical treatment primarily due to stable or progressive disease in the body CT scan after three cycles of chemotherapy (Table 2 ). Most patients whose treatment started with NACT underwent interval cytoreductive surgery after three cycles of chemotherapy, but there was a slight decreasing trend during the second period: 86.7% in Group 1 and 77.6% in Group 2. All the other non‐surgically treated patients were referred directly to the chemotherapy treatment only or to palliative care. The median follow‐up time for the entire patient cohort was 31 months (0.7–127) in Group 1 and 34 months (0.5–90) in Group 2. The OS rates were similar in both study groups. The median OS in patients who received surgical or medical treatment was 32.8 months (95% CI 25.1–40.5) in Group 1 and 37.3 months (31.5–43.1) in Group 2, p = 0.21 (Figure 2 ). The median OS for patients who received only chemotherapy was 23.9 months (95% CI 15.7–32.0) in Group 1 and 16.8 months (95% CI 9.0–24.6) in Group 2, p = 0.42 (Figure 3 ). When including patients who did not receive any cancer‐specific treatment (9 in Group 1 and 12 in Group 2), the median OS was 29.7 months (95% CI 22.2–37.3) in Group 1 and 34.3 months (95% CI 27.5–41.0) in Group 2, p = 0.26 (Figure S1 ). The estimated OS for patients with palliative care only was 1.3 months (95% CI 0.8–1.8) in Group 1 and 1.7 months (95% CI 0.8–2.7) in Group 2, p = 0.08. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Overall survival in patients having surgical or medical treatment. FIGURE 3. Open in a new tab Overall survival in patients with chemotherapy only. The survival rates of surgically treated patients in the same time period have been presented in our previously published study. 10 The median OS for these patients increased 21 months, from 33.5 months (95% CI 26.2–40.8) in Group 1 to 54.5 months (95% CI 35.2–73.8) in Group 2, p = 0.028. However, the survival benefit seemed to be limited to stage III patients, who represented 69% and 59% of the surgically treated patients in Groups 1 and 2, respectively. 4. DISCUSSION This retrospective analysis showed that approximately 75% of patients with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer underwent surgical treatment, while one‐fourth of the patients were deemed ineligible for surgery during the 6‐year study period. The shift toward a surgical paradigm emphasizing maximal cytoreductive effort, introduced midway through the study, did not significantly alter the proportion of patients directed to surgical versus non‐surgical treatment. Furthermore, scheduled interval cytoreductive surgeries were often canceled due to stricter radiological response requirements following neoadjuvant chemotherapy under the structured surgical protocol. Overall, the prognosis for patients deemed unsuitable for surgery remained dismal and showed no improvement over the study period. While the shift toward maximal surgical effort has demonstrable benefits for carefully selected patients undergoing surgery, this approach does not translate into improved outcomes for the entire patient cohort. As the complexity and quality requirements of cytoreductive surgery have expanded, one might anticipate a decline in the proportion of patients offered surgical treatment compared to the era of standard surgical approaches. Our small patient data showed a statistically non‐significant 4.9 percentage unit difference in treatment decisions between groups. As indicated by a post hoc power analysis, the study was underpowered to detect significant differences between groups, which should be considered when interpreting the findings. Our post hoc calculation suggested that a total of 2924 patients (1462 per group) would be required to detect the observed difference (76.5% vs. 71.9% of surgically treated patients) with adequate statistical power, which is probably not feasible in a single‐center, or even multicenter studies regarding treatment decisions in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. Also, our finding may still be clinically reassuring, as it demonstrates that the survival benefits observed with more extensive surgery in our institution were not achieved by reducing the proportion of operated patients but rather by selectively applying extensive surgery to appropriate candidates. In both study groups, the primary reason for withholding surgical treatment was the presence of extensive peritoneal carcinomatosis or widespread cancer dissemination that was deemed non‐resectable according to prevailing operability criteria. Nonetheless, the shift in the surgical paradigm appeared to coincide with more individualized decision‐making, as patient‐related factors, such as nutritional and performance status, increasingly influenced the choice of treatment modality. Furthermore, most patients initially considered potential candidates for interval cytoreductive surgery after NACT were ultimately referred to surgery after three cycles of chemotherapy in both study periods. However, a slight decline in this proportion was observed during the later study period, likely reflecting stricter adherence to radiological response criteria after NACT. Recent randomized controlled trials investigating the outcomes of primary cytoreductive surgery compared to NACT followed by interval cytoreductive surgery have found no significant difference in survival between these two approaches. 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 Similarly, our retrospective analysis of the same patient cohort revealed no statistically significant difference in OS or PFS between primary cytoreductive surgery and interval cytoreductive surgery in surgically treated patients. 10 Patients deemed ineligible for surgical treatment have been underrepresented in studies evaluating the benefits of surgery in advanced ovarian cancer. The study by Hall et al. from the UK revealed that 22.4% of the patients received chemotherapy only, representing a similar rate compared to this present analysis. 17 Additionally, the impact of the implementation of extensive cytoreductive surgery on non‐surgically treated patients remains poorly documented. 18 Shalowitz et al. reported that 12% of 130 664 patients with FIGO stage III‐IV received chemotherapy alone, with a median survival of 12 months. 19 However, they estimated that only 75% of epithelial ovarian cancer cases were captured in the analyzed database. Similarly, Wallen et al. reported that 48% of stage III and 29% of stage IV patients received appropriate surgical and chemotherapy treatment, though the study lacked information on the quality of the cytoreductive surgery and fewer than 80% of patients had a gynecologic oncologist consultation. 20 Neither study addressed the reasons for declining surgical treatment. It has been shown that inadequate or absent health insurance serves as a significant barrier to appropriate treatment, making these patients less likely to undergo surgery. 7 , 19 , 21 This is notably different from the situation in the Nordic countries, where healthcare is publicly funded, and treatment decisions are made solely based on medical considerations. In the cohort including patients treated with surgery and chemotherapy or chemotherapy alone, OS showed an increasing survival trend of 5 months after the shift to extensive cytoreductive surgery; however, this difference did not reach statistical significance. Our previous study from the same time period demonstrated an increased rate of complete cytoreduction in surgically treated patients from 17% to 53%, which resulted in a significant 21‐month survival improvement among surgically treated patients. 10 However, this survival benefit was observed only in FIGO stage III patients representing 43% of the entire cohort in the second study group. Despite a tripling of the complete cytoreduction rate after the surgical paradigm shift, no additional survival benefit was identified when medically treated patients and surgically treated FIGO stage IV patients were included in the analysis. When evaluating the impact of implementing maximal surgical effort on patient selection and OS, similar findings have been reported from Sweden. 9 Falconer et al. documented a statistically significant rise in the proportion of non‐surgically treated patients, increasing from 24% to 33%, in contrast to the findings in our study cohort, which, however, must be interpreted with caution due to the lack of statistical power. Consistent with our results, their study demonstrated a nearly doubled rate of complete cytoreduction. However, despite these advancements, no statistically significant improvement in OS of the whole patient cohort was observed after the introduction of extensive cytoreductive surgery at their institution. Studies have established that carefully selected patients do obtain survival benefits from expanding surgical expertise, but it appears unlikely that focusing solely on this group would provide additional survival advantages for the entire population of advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. Currently, up to one‐third of women with the disease are not applicable for surgical treatment, and to improve the survival of the entire cohort, greater effort should be made to reduce this proportion of patients. Future research should focus on tumor biology, disease prevention, the development of new medical treatments, and methods for identifying patients at earlier stages who are more likely to benefit from surgical intervention. This study has several limitations. As a retrospective analysis, treatment decisions are assessed afterwards, which may lead to a simplified interpretation of the patient selection process. This may be due to the absence of comprehensive documentation of all clinical and contextual factors influencing the decision‐making process at the time of treatment. Additionally, the limited sample size of the present cohort lacks sufficient statistical power needed to demonstrate significant differences in treatment decisions between groups. As a result, only descriptive analyses could be presented from our cohort in this regard, highlighting the need for larger, prospective studies on this topic. Further data on treatment costs and especially patient‐reported outcomes addressing quality of life are necessary to fully assess the effectiveness of surgical treatment for advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. The strength of this study is that in Finland all patients with gynecological cancer are treated within the public healthcare system, the treatment is centralized in five university hospitals with comprehensive and accurate patient databases, ensuring that the patient cohorts reflect the entire population of women affected by the disease. Additionally, patient compliance is high, leading to basically no loss to follow‐up. Furthermore, Finland''s publicly financed healthcare system, with minimal client fees that are not based on the actual cost of services, ensures that even the most complex healthcare and surgical treatments are accessible to all, regardless of wealth or insurance status. This eliminates any influence of a patient''s financial capacity on treatment decisions, allowing for a more equitable and unbiased selection of patients. Consequently, our data represent real‐world evidence and provide an accurate reflection of the outcomes for all women diagnosed with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. 5. CONCLUSION This retrospective analysis provides valuable real‐world evidence regarding treatment trends in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer during the era of extensive cytoreductive surgery. While surgical treatment benefits a carefully selected patient population, to improve the survival of the entire patient cohort, more effort should be made toward early diagnosis and advancements in the medical management of patients with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS N Norppa: Formal analysis, methodology, visualization, and writing—original draft. S Staff: Conceptualization, methodology, validation, and writing—review and editing. M Helminen: Formal analysis, methodology, and writing—review and editing. A Auranen: Validation and writing—review and editing. S Saarelainen: Conceptualization, methodology, validation, writing—review and editing, project administration, and supervision. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT No conflict of interest. ETHICS STATEMENT This study was approved by Tampere University Hospital ( R19582 ) on November 6, 2020. No ethics committee approval is needed for retrospective studies in Finland. Supporting information Figure S1. Overall survival in all patients diagnosed with advanced epithelial ovarian cancer. AOGS-104-2006-s001.docx (1.6MB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENT Open access publishing facilitated by Tampereen yliopisto ja Tampereen ammattikorkeakoulu, as part of the Wiley ‐ FinELib agreement. Norppa N, Staff S, Helminen M, Auranen A, Saarelainen S. Implementation of extensive cytoreductive surgery protocol in advanced epithelial ovarian cancer: Real‐world data on surgical treatment distribution and survival of the whole patient cohort; a single center experience. Acta Obstet Gynecol Scand. 2025;104:2006‐2013. doi: 10.1111/aogs.70017 REFERENCES 1. Ferlay J, Ervik M, Lam F, et al. Global Cancer Observatory: Cancer Today. 2024. https://gco.iarc.who.int/today Accessed April 11, 2024. https://gco.iarc.who.int/media/globocan/factsheets/cancers/25‐ovary‐fact‐sheet.pdf 2. Aletti GD, Dowdy SC, Gostout BS, et al. Aggressive surgical effort and improved survival in advanced‐stage ovarian cancer LEVEL OF EVIDENCE: 