schau nct03703271 nct03703271 primal postmolar nct03703271 recure nct04756713 nct04756713 aaronson aaronson ahmedzai bullinger cull duez langebrekke hudelist obstetricia gynecologica abrao abrao tiezzi marana candido clagnan dactinomycin ahmadzadeh ahmadzadeh najafian lalvand mastroyannis sonalkar aminimoghaddam aminimoghaddam chaichian kashian mohazzab pourali ayatollahi ayatollahi yekta afsari nonmetastatic urmia bagshawe bagshawe uberti emh fajardo viggiano grillo brinton brinton bracken tulsky sarafian bonomi menefee nbk568791 ctep protocoldevelopment electronic_applications ctcae_v5_quick_reference_5x7 haes haes jcjm knippenberg fce neijt bradburn delli delli carpini verdecchia giannella gardella myomectomy dersimonian dersimonian eiriksson eiriksson sebastianelli comeau postmolar ishizuka tomoda postmolar gradepro gdt gradepro gdt gradepro growdon growdon wolfberg feltmate chinchilla postmolar borchert tyrey creasman handcock isstd gtd actinomycin hemida hemida toson doorn hemida hemida deek arafa toson postmolar cumpston obstetrica gynaecologica japonica kamijo kamijo oide anan taito kataoka e45726 kanazawa kanazawa sasagawa obstetricia gynecologica lawrie lawrie alazzam tidy cd007102 cd007102 ketcham lorigan lorigan xxvi lurain lurain lurain lurain mah mah lavecchia pokoradi a422 meheus meheus atun ilbawi vaccarella lortet tieulent saracci nbk566198 physician_gls govt gtd ngan ngan benedet montruccoli pecorelli ngan ngan golfier sekharan filiaci schink mannel behbakht nonmetastatic torri parsa parsa pelasseyed gynakologische rundschau pezeshki pezeshki silcocks everard rcog epdf schlaerth schlaerth skoetz cumpston qualls prairie rayburn soper soper terrin strohl strohl lurain tchelebi tchelebi potters boffa tidy tidy trommel trommel massuger lfag verheijen rhm fcgj cmg vandewal vandewal delbecque rompuy marbaix delvenne obstetricia gynecologica neoplasias yarandi yarandi shojaei izadi boonyapipat boonyapipat nanthamongkolkul saeaib liabsuetrakul cd014755 cd014755','Abstract Background The primary treatment approach for addressing low‐risk nonmetastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN) in women desiring fertility preservation involves chemotherapy. An alternative option for treatment is fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, either alone or in combination with adjuvant chemotherapy. The hypothesised advantages of choosing fertility‐sparing surgery in cases of LR‐NMGTN include potential avoidance of adverse effects associated with chemotherapy, potential reduction in the number of chemotherapy cycles required to achieve complete remission, and potential reduction in time to remission. Objectives To measure the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, with or without adjuvant chemotherapy, compared to primary chemotherapy alone, for the treatment of women with low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN). Search methods We searched CENTRAL, MEDLINE, Embase, Web of Science, ClinicalTrials.gov and WHO ICTRP on 31 January 2024. We also searched abstracts of scientific meetings and reference lists of included studies. Selection criteria We included all randomised controlled trials (RCTs) comparing fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, with or without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy, versus primary chemotherapy as standard care for the treatment of women with LR‐NMGTN. Data collection and analysis We employed standard Cochrane methodological procedures. We used the GRADE approach to assess the certainty of evidence for each outcome, if available. We focused on the following outcomes: treatment success rate, relapse, disease‐specific mortality, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, quality of life, and any adverse events. Main results We included two RCTs, with a total of 151 participants contributing data to our analyses. Both studies used uterine curettage as the fertility‐sparing surgical intervention. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone One RCT involving 62 participants with varying hCG (human chorionic gonadotrophin) levels evaluated this comparison. Most of our outcomes of interest were not measured in this study. The relative risk of experiencing any adverse event could not be estimated as chemotherapy adverse effects were not reported. The study reported that there were no surgical complications. Chemotherapy was administered to 50% of participants in the intervention group after curettage because their hCG levels increased. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone One RCT involving 89 participants with hCG levels < 5000 IU/L evaluated this comparison. We judged the risk of bias in the study to be high. The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on treatment success rate (RR 1.03, 95% CI 0.86 to1.23; 86 participants), relapse (RR 0.5, 95% CI 0.05 to 5.31; 86 participants), pregnancy rate (RR 0.86, 95% CI 0.31 to 2.34; 86 participants), and rate of adverse events (RR 1.15, 95% CI 0.63 to 2.13; 86 participants), all very low certainty evidence. The relative risks of disease‐specific mortality and death due to treatment could not be estimated as there were no deaths in either group. There were no results for quality of life as this outcome was not reported. Authors'' conclusions Uterine curettage is the only fertility‐sparing surgical intervention for LR‐NMGTN that has been evaluated in a randomised controlled trial. The evidence is very uncertain about the benefits and harms of uterine curettage, with or without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy, compared to primary chemotherapy alone. The two available studies are small with a high risk of bias, and future research may find substantially different results for all reported outcomes. Larger RCTs, with appropriate clinical outcome measures, would be required to determine the benefits or harms of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions for this population. Keywords: Female; Humans; Pregnancy; Chemotherapy, Adjuvant; Fertility Preservation; Fertility Preservation/methods; Gestational Trophoblastic Disease; Gestational Trophoblastic Disease/drug therapy; Gestational Trophoblastic Disease/mortality; Gestational Trophoblastic Disease/surgery; Neoplasm Recurrence, Local; Neoplasm Recurrence, Local/prevention & control; Pregnancy Rate; Quality of Life; Randomized Controlled Trials as Topic Plain language summary What are the benefits and harms of ''fertility‐sparing'' surgery to treat low‐risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia that has not spread to other parts of the body? Key messages • We are very uncertain about the benefits and harms of surgery that does not affect fertility (''fertility‐sparing'' surgical interventions), with or without chemotherapy, for low‐risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia that has not spread to other parts of the body (''non‐metastatic''). • Larger, well‐designed studies would be needed to determine the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgery for women with low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia. What is gestational trophoblastic neoplasia? Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is a rare cancer that can develop in the womb after pregnancy, especially a molar pregnancy (where there is a problem with the fertilised egg that means the baby and placenta do not develop in the way they should). It can cause abnormal bleeding or high hormone levels. Treatment usually involves chemotherapy or surgery, or both, depending on the severity of the cancer. Chemotherapy is the standard treatment for patients with low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN), but this can affect a woman''s ability to become pregnant. ''Fertility‐sparing'' surgical interventions may be used as an alternative in LR‐NMGTN patients who want to preserve fertility. Receiving fertility‐sparing surgery in the LR‐NMGTN may avoid side effects from chemotherapy treatment. What did we want to find out? We wanted to know how fertility‐sparing surgical interventions affect the treatment success rate, time to remission, relapse, death from disease, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, quality of life, number of cycles of chemotherapy, any adverse events, and emergency removal of the womb. What did we do? We searched for studies that investigated the effectiveness of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention compared to chemotherapy (comparison 1), or the combination of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention and chemotherapy compared to chemotherapy alone (comparison 2), and whether surgical intervention caused any unwanted effects in women with LR‐NMGTN. We compared and summarised the results of the studies and rated our confidence in the certainty of evidence. What did we find? We found two studies that involved 62 people for comparison 1 and 89 people for comparison 2. The fertility‐sparing surgical intervention used in both studies was uterine curettage, in which abnormal cells are scraped from the lining of the womb using a spoon‐like instrument. Main results For comparison 1, the one study that evaluated fertility‐sparing surgical intervention versus chemotherapy did not report the outcomes of interest: treatment success rate, relapse, death from disease, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, or quality of life, or any adverse events (i.e. unwanted or harmful effects). Comparison 2 was evaluated in one study. We are very uncertain if uterine curettage followed by chemotherapy has an effect on treatment success rate, relapse, death from disease, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, or any adverse events, when compared to chemotherapy alone. Quality of life was not reported in the study. What are the limitations of the evidence? We are very uncertain about the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention in LR‐NMGTN. We are not confident in the evidence for three main reasons. Firstly, the design of the included studies puts them at high risk of bias. Secondly, there was only one study for each comparison, and they each had a small number of participants. Finally, there were gaps in the information about the outcomes we think are most important for evaluating fertility‐sparing surgery with or without chemotherapy compared to chemotherapy alone for LR‐NMGTN. How up to date is this evidence? We searched for evidence up to 31 January 2024. Summary of findings Summary of findings 1. Summary of findings table ‐ Uterine curettage without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy compared to primary chemotherapy for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia. Uterine curettage without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy compared to primary chemotherapy for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Patient or population: low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Setting: outpatient/inpatient Intervention: uterine curettage without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy Comparison: primary chemotherapy Outcomes Anticipated absolute effects * (95% CI) Relative effect (95% CI) № of participants (studies) Certainty of the evidence (GRADE) Comments Risk with primary chemotherapy Risk with uterine curettage without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy Treatment success rate ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ The study reported that chemotherapy was administered to 50% of intervention group participants after curettage because the hCG levels increased. Relapse ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Disease‐specific mortality ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Death due to treatment ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Pregnancy rate ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Quality of life ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Any adverse events ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ This composite outcome could not be reported as adverse events of chemotherapy were not documented. The study did note that there were no surgical complications. New outcome 0 per 1000 0 per 1000 (0 to 0) Not estimable ( studies) ‐ * The risk in the intervention group (and its 95% confidence interval) is based on the assumed risk in the comparison group and the relative effect of the intervention (and its 95% CI). CI: confidence interval; RR: risk ratio GRADE Working Group grades of evidence High certainty: we are very confident that the true effect lies close to that of the estimate of the effect. Moderate certainty: we are moderately confident in the effect estimate: the true effect is likely to be close to the estimate of the effect, but there is a possibility that it is substantially different. Low certainty: our confidence in the effect estimate is limited: the true effect may be substantially different from the estimate of the effect. Very low certainty: we have very little confidence in the effect estimate: the true effect is likely to be substantially different from the estimate of effect. See interactive version of this table: https://gdt.gradepro.org/presentations/#/isof/isof_question_revman_web_442988636975989490 . Open in a new tab Summary of findings 2. Summary of findings table ‐ Uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy compared to primary chemotherapy for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia. Uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy compared to primary chemotherapy for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Patient or population: low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Setting: outpatient/inpatient Intervention: uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy Comparison: primary chemotherapy Outcomes Anticipated absolute effects * (95% CI) Relative effect (95% CI) № of participants (studies) Certainty of the evidence (GRADE) Comments Risk with primary chemotherapy Risk with uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy Treatment success rate follow‐up: mean 23 months 84 per 100 86 per 100 (72 to 100) RR 1.03 (0.86 to 1.23) 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low a The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on treatment success rate. Relapse follow‐up: mean 23 months 5 per 100 2 per 100 (0 to 25) RR 0.50 (0.05 to 5.31) 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low a The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on relapse. Disease‐specific mortality follow‐up: mean 23 months 0 per 100 0 per 100 (0 to 0) Not estimable 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low b The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on disease specific‐mortality. There were no events reported in either group. Death due to treatment follow‐up: mean 23 months 0 per 100 0 per 100 (0 to 0) Not estimable 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low b The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on death due to treatment. There were no events reported in either group. Pregnancy rate follow‐up: mean 23 months 16 per 100 14 per 100 (5 to 38) RR 0.86 (0.31 to 2.34) 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low a The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on pregnancy rate. Quality of life ‐ not reported ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ ‐ Not reported Any adverse events follow‐up: mean 23 months 30 per 100 35 per 100 (19 to 64) RR 1.15 (0.63 to 2.13) 86 (1 RCT) ⊕⊝⊝⊝ Very low a, c The evidence is very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on any adverse events from chemotherapy. The study noted that there were no surgical complications. * The risk in the intervention group (and its 95% confidence interval) is based on the assumed risk in the comparison group and the relative effect of the intervention (and its 95% CI). CI: confidence interval; RR: risk ratio GRADE Working Group grades of evidence High certainty: we are very confident that the true effect lies close to that of the estimate of the effect. Moderate certainty: we are moderately confident in the effect estimate: the true effect is likely to be close to the estimate of the effect, but there is a possibility that it is substantially different. Low certainty: our confidence in the effect estimate is limited: the true effect may be substantially different from the estimate of the effect. Very low certainty: we have very little confidence in the effect estimate: the true effect is likely to be substantially different from the estimate of effect. See interactive version of this table: https://gdt.gradepro.org/presentations/#/isof/isof_question_revman_web_443094140937292068 . Open in a new tab a Downgraded 3 levels for imprecision: small number of participants and confidence intervals that include the possibility of either benefit or harm b Downgraded 3 levels for imprecision: small number of participants and no events c Downgraded 1 level for risk of bias: no outcome assessor blinding Background Description of the condition Gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) is a group of rare malignant diseases related to pregnancy, where a tumour develops from abnormal trophoblastic cells. Normally, trophoblastic cells attach an embryo to the uterus to form the placenta after fertilisation. GTN most often occurs following molar pregnancy, also known as hydatidiform mole, which is a pregnancy where the fertilised egg does not develop as it should. A complete molar pregnancy (also known as complete hydatidiform mole) is one in which abnormal cells grow in the womb after conception and there is no foetus; a partial molar pregnancy (or partial hydatidiform mole) is where there seems to be a foetus, but it cannot fully develop or survive. GTN can also, more rarely, develop after other types of pregnancy, for example, an ectopic pregnancy or full‐term birth ( Handcock 2022 ). GTN has several subtypes, including invasive mole, choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT) and epithelioid trophoblastic tumour (ETT). The common subtypes are invasive mole and choriocarcinoma. The incidence of GTN varies across geographic regions. Estimates from studies suggest higher rates of GTN in Southeast Asian countries and Japan ( Strohl 2014 ). The incidence of choriocarcinoma, a type of GTN, in Europe and North America has been reported as 1 in 25,000 to 40,000 pregnancies, but as high as 9.2 and 3.3 in 40,000 pregnancies in Southeast Asia and Japan, respectively ( Brinton 1986 ; Lurain 2010 ; Smith 2003 ). The reported incidence of GTN following a complete hydatidiform mole varies from 15.7% to 24.6% ( Albright 2020 ; Braga 2014 ), while its reported incidence following a partial hydatidiform mole is approximately 2.9% to 7.6% ( Albright 2020 ; Braga 2014 ; Goto 1993 ). The diagnosis of GTN is based on signs and symptoms, as well as elevation in the level of human chorionic gonadotrophin (hCG), which is a hormone produced primarily by placental cells. The diagnostic criteria for GTN are shown in Table 3 ( Ngan 2003 ; Soper 2021 ). GTN occurring after a molar pregnancy, known as post‐molar GTN, is diagnosed using the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) 2000 criteria ( FIGO Oncology Committee 2002 ; Ngan 2003 ). Post‐molar GTN typically presents with unusual vaginal bleeding or high levels of hCG even after the pregnancy has ended. Non‐molar pregnancy‐related GTN may present with abnormal vaginal bleeding, or non‐gynaecological symptoms, and elevated hCG levels. 1. Diagnostic criteria for GTN. I. hCG criteria after molar evacuation a. Sustained hCG level plateau ± 10% of 4 values over a 3‐week duration b. Sustained hCG level rise greater than 10% of 3 values over a 2‐week duration c. Persistence of detectable hCG more than 6 months after molar evacuation II. Presence of metastatic disease III. Histologic diagnosis of GTN a. Invasive mole b. Gestational choriocarcinoma c. Placental site trophoblastic disease d. Epithelioid trophoblastic disease Open in a new tab GTN: gestational trophoblastic neoplasia; hCG: human chorionic gonadotropin Due to the chemosensitive nature of GTN, the main treatment is typically chemotherapy, depending on the severity of the disease ( Ngan 2003 ; Soper 2021 ). There are various classification systems used to judge the severity ( Table 4 ; Table 5 ; Appendix 1 ). The most commonly used is the risk scoring system of the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) 2000 ( FIGO Oncology Committee 2002 ), which incorporates the World Health Organization (WHO) risk score (ultra‐high risk (score > 13)), high risk (score 7 to 12), and low risk (score up to 6) and the anatomical distribution of disease (non‐metastasis and metastasis). Ultra‐high‐risk and high‐risk GTN are treated with multiple‐agent chemotherapy. 2. FIGO staging and classification for GTN. Stage I Disease confined to the uterus Stage II GTN extends outside of the uterus, but is limited to the genital structures (adnexae, vagina, broad ligament) Stage III GTN extends to the lungs with or without known genital tract involvement Stage IV All other metastatic sites Open in a new tab From Ngan 2021 FIGO: International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; GTN: gestational trophoblastic neoplasia; hCG: human chorionic gonadotropin; 3. WHO scoring system based on prognostic factors modified as FIGO score. Scores 0 1 2 4 Age (years) < 40 ≥ 40 _ _ Antecedent pregnancy Mole Abortion Term _ Interval months from index pregnancy < 4 4 to 6 7 to 12 > 12 Pretreatment serum hCG (IU/L) < 10 3 10 3 to 10 4 10 4 to 10 5 > 10 5 Largest tumour size (including uterus) < 3 3 to 4 cm ≥ 5 cm _ Site of metastases Lung Spleen, kidney Gastrointestinal Liver, brain Number of metastases _ 1 to 4 5 to 8 > 8 Previous failed chemotherapy _ _ Single drug ≥ 2 drugs Open in a new tab To stage and allot a risk factor score, a patient''s diagnosis is allocated to a stage as represented by a Roman numeral I, II, III, or IV. This is then separated by a colon from the sum of all the actual risk factor scores expressed in Arabic numerals, e.g. stage II:4, stage IV: 9. This stage and score will be allotted for each patient ( Ngan 2021 ). A score ≤ 6 indicates low risk; 6 to 12 indicates high risk; > 13 indicates ultra high risk. hCG: human chorionic gonadotropin; IU/L: international units per litre; WHO: World Health Organization Low‐risk, non‐metastatic GTN (LR‐NMGTN) is classified when GTN is in FIGO stage I (confined to the uterus) with a WHO score < 6 ( Table 3 ; Table 4 ) ( Ngan 2021 ); this highly curable condition accounts for 81% of all GTN ( Wang 2009 ). A single‐agent chemotherapy regimen, using either methotrexate or dactinomycin, is usually recommended after diagnosis. The commonly used regimens include pulsed methotrexate, 5‐day or 8‐day methotrexate, pulsed dactinomycin, or 5‐day dactinomycin ( Goldstein 2015 ; Lurain 2011 ). Chemotherapy is given until the hCG levels return to the normal range (generally considered to be below 5 mIU/mL) ( Elias 2019 ), and it is continued for 6 to 8 weeks to reduce the risk of relapse. Women with LR‐NMGTN whose hCG levels do not return to normal, and instead plateau or rise, or those who develop metastasis, are switched to second‐line chemotherapy or a hysterectomy (i.e. a surgical operation to completely remove the womb) ( Tidy 2021 ). Although chemotherapy can induce remission, more than half of those receiving chemotherapy for LR‐NMGTN will experience side effects (''adverse events'') such as fatigue, nausea and vomiting, diarrhoea, bone marrow suppression, alopecia, stomatitis/mucositis, neuropathy, and hepatic (relating to the liver) or renal (relating to the kidneys) dysfunction ( Abrão 2008 ; Hao 2021 ; Lawrie 2016 ). In a recent meta‐analysis comparing two chemotherapy drugs, Hao 2021 reported that a high proportion of participants experienced fatigue (54.5%), anaemia (low iron) (29.1%), and nausea (19.9%) in the methotrexate group, and fatigue (62.7%), nausea (41.2%), anaemia (35.7%), and alopecia (hair loss) (29.7%) in the actinomycin group ( Hao 2021 ). An alternative to chemotherapy is a hysterectomy, which can be offered to women who do not wish to retain their fertility, or where there is a co‐existing uterine or pelvic pathology that needs surgical management. However, most women diagnosed with GTN are of reproductive age and may want to have children, so hysterectomy is not their preferred choice. A treatment option for these women is a ''fertility‐sparing surgical intervention'', which is conservative compared to hysterectomy as it does not remove the entire womb. Description of the intervention Treatment options for LR‐NMGTN include chemotherapy, fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, and hysterectomy. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention is conservative surgery, which removes only the lining, or some part of the womb affected by the tumour. Elements of surgical intervention are listed below; these can be performed alone or in combination. Uterine curettage isa procedure to remove tissue from inside the uterus using an electric suction device, or manual vacuum aspirator, or by using a spoon‐like instrument, with or without the use of intraoperative ultrasound or a hysteroscopy to locate the residual tumour before removing the tissue. Hysteroscopy is a procedure where a doctor uses a thin, flexible tube to look inside the uterus. This procedure can be performed under sedation, or with a short‐acting general anaesthesia or regional anaesthesia(a type of pain relief that keeps you awake but pain‐free). Uterine curettage is generally a safe and routine procedure, but it has some risks, such as uterine perforation, severe bleeding, and postoperative intrauterine adhesions (Asherman''s syndrome), which can cause infertility ( Cooper 2023 ). Directed resection under hysteroscopy is a procedure to remove a tumour by direction visualisation through the telescope and using a diathermy‐resecting loop (a surgical tool with a heated wire loop that cuts and seals tissue). The procedure allows visualisation that can help identify abnormal tissue in a specific area but requires expert surgeons and costly equipment ( Lorigan 1996 ; Osborne 2016 ). Potential complications of hysteroscopy include uterine perforation, injury of nearby organs, and heavy bleeding ( Aas‐Eng 2017 ). The procedure can be done under sedation, or with local, regional or general anaesthesia. Myometrial resection is a procedure to remove some part of the womb affected by a tumour followed by reconstruction with suturing (stiches) ( Kanazawa 1988 ). This procedure generally requires regional or general anaesthesia. This procedure may also cause heavy bleeding, and if bleeding becomes uncontrolled, a hysterectomy may need to be performed. Another concern with this intervention is an increase in the risk of the tumour spreading ( Wang 2017 ). Caesarean section may be recommended in any subsequent pregnancy to avoid the risk of uterine rupture during labour ( Delli 2023 ). Postoperative serial hCG levels are used to monitor remission status and assess the need for further chemotherapy. Women whose hCG levels return to the normal range are considered surgically cured, and further treatment is not necessary. For those women whose postoperative hCG levels do not reach normal levels, or whose hCG levels plateau or rise, based on the FIGO 2000 criteria ( FIGO Oncology Committee 2002 ; Ngan 2003 ), further chemotherapy or hysterectomy is recommended. With respect to safety, studies have found that surgery can lead to complications, though their occurrence is not frequent ( Osborne 2016 ; Van Trommel 2005 ; Yarandi 2014 ). A phase II Gynecologic Oncology Group trial reported that amongst 60 women undergoing curettage, one experienced uterine perforation and another had uterine haemorrhage ( Osborne 2016 ). One cohort study from Iran reported 1 woman out of 12 undergoing curettage experienced uterine perforation ( Yarandi 2014 ), and a retrospective Dutch survey reported that 4 out of 85 women undergoing curettage experienced uterine perforation or haemorrhage, although these were managed conservatively ( Van Trommel 2005 ). Intraoperative ultrasound guidance and the use of uterotronics (medicine that helps the uterus contract) have been used to reduce the risk of complications ( RCOG 2020 ; Soper 2021 ). How the intervention might work The curative effect of fertility‐sparing surgery can be explained by the tumour debulking effect on the intrauterine pathology, which may reduce the need for subsequent chemotherapy treatment. The management strategies for the use of fertility‐sparing surgery in LR‐NMGTN include surgery alone or surgery in combination with adjuvant chemotherapy. The potential benefits of fertility‐sparing surgery in LR‐NMGTN include the following. Avoiding chemotherapy treatment and potential adverse effects: the therapeutic effect of fertility‐sparing surgery was first investigated in retrospective studies, which found that 9.4% to 60% of women did not need further chemotherapy ( Lorigan 1996 ; Pezeshki 2004 ; Savage 2005 ; Schlaerth 1990 ; Van Trommel 2005 ). A more recent retrospective study reported that 83% of women with low‐risk GTN were cured by a second curettage ( Yarandi 2014 ). Additionally, a multicentre prospective study reported that 40% of women with LR‐NMGTN were cured after a second curettage ( Osborne 2016 ), and in a randomised controlled trial, 50% of women with LR‐NMGTN were cured after curettage ( Ayatollahi 2017 ). A recent systematic review showed that a second uterine curettage might result in a slight reduction in adverse events, based on low certainty of evidence ( Kamijo 2023 ). Reducing the number of chemotherapy cycles required to achieve complete remission: one study reported that uterine curettage or evacuation was associated with fewer chemotherapy cycles being required for disease remission compared with the number of cycles required by women who did not receive the surgical procedure ( Hemida 2011 ). Another study indicated the median number of cycles of subsequent chemotherapy in women who received uterine curettage was lower than in a control group (5 versus 6 cycles) ( Van Trommel 2005 ). However, a recent RCT showed no difference in the mean number of chemotherapy courses required to reach hCG normalisation after a second curettage compared to no curettage ( Hemida 2019 ). Shortening the time to attain complete response: an RCT reported that the mean number of weeks to achieve negative hCG was significantly lower in the curettage plus chemotherapy group compared to the chemotherapy alone group (3.2 weeks versus 9.2 weeks) ( Ayatollahi 2017 ). Potential reduction in emergency surgeries due to genital bleeding ( Kamijo 2023 ). Several factors have been investigated as predictors of the benefits of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention. Studies have identified that 1500 to 5000 mIU/mL is the optimal hCG level cut‐off point for predicting the need for chemotherapy after such interventions ( Lorigan 1996 ; Pezeshki 2004 ; Savage 2005 ). Another study found that a FIGO score of 5 or 6 and an age greater than 39 years or less than 19 years are risk factors for the failure of a second curettage to prevent the need for chemotherapy. However, this study did not find any association between successful uterine curettage and factors such as hCG level, size, or depth of an intrauterine tumour ( Osborne 2016 ). Why it is important to do this review The standard treatment strategies in LR‐NMGTN have some shortcomings. Chemotherapy treatment may cause adverse effects, may not be accessible in some regions, or women may not be able to comply with the required follow‐up ( Meheus F 2019 ; Tchelebi 2022 ). Hysterectomy has the potential risks of anaesthesia and surgery, and results in loss of fertility. To avoid these disadvantages, a fertility‐sparing surgical intervention is potentially a better alternative. Additionally, fertility‐sparing surgery may result in a reduction in chemotherapy side effects and a decrease in emergency surgeries due to genital bleeding ( Kamijo 2023 ). Current recommendations from cancer treatment guidelines for the use of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions in post‐molar GTN vary. Some recommend a fertility‐sparing surgical intervention as first‐line management for women with a newly diagnosed post‐molar GTN with FIGO risk score of 0 to 4, and chemotherapy is given only if postoperative hCG levels are rising or plateau ( New Zealand Gynecologic Cancer Group 2018 ). Others suggest that fertility‐sparing surgical interventions is an option for managing post‐molar GTN with a FIGO risk score of 0 to 4 ( National Comprehensive Cancer Network 2024 ), or when the hCG level is lower than 1500 mIU/mL ( Pezeshki 2004 ) or 5000 mIU/mL ( Savage 2005 ). Still others do not recommend a fertility‐sparing surgical intervention alone or in combination with chemotherapy for the treatment of women with LR‐NMGTN ( Eiriksson 2021 ; RCOG 2020 ; Tidy 2021 ). We, therefore, aim to identify, summarise, and evaluate the evidence from randomised controlled trials for the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions in the treatment of LR‐NMGTN. Objectives To measure the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, with or without adjuvant chemotherapy, compared to primary chemotherapy alone, for the treatment of women with low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN). Methods Criteria for considering studies for this review Types of studies We included all randomised controlled trials (RCTs) comparing fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, with or without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy, versus primary chemotherapy as standard care for the treatment of women with low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN). Types of participants We included studies involving women diagnosed as low‐risk or non‐metastatic GTN using any scoring systems (including FIGO ( FIGO Oncology Committee 2002 ), Bagshawe ( Bagshawe 1976 ), National Institutes of Health (NIH) classification ( Hammond 1973 ), or Japanese Choriocarcinoma Risk Score ( Japan Society of Obstetrics and Gynaecology 1982 )) ( Appendix 1 ). We excluded studies including women with recurrent GTN and women with placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT) or epithelioid trophoblastic tumour (ETT) histologic subtype. Types of interventions We considered any type of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention (i.e. uterine curettage, directed resection by hysteroscopy, myometrial resection), with or without adjuvant chemotherapy, compared with primary treatment with any chemotherapy regimen as standard care on a head‐to‐head comparison basis. We considered the following two comparisons: fertility‐sparing surgical interventions without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone; fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone. The regimens of chemotherapy for primary chemotherapy or subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy for low‐risk GTN ( Lawrie 2016 ; Soper 2021 ) are shown in Appendix 2 . Types of outcome measures Primary outcomes Treatment success rate (complete remission or cure), defined as achieving normalised hCG levels for at least three consecutive weeks ( Berkowitz 2021 ) Time to remission, defined as time from randomisation, or the initiation of treatment, or diagnosis to remission (months) Relapse, defined as the re‐elevation of hCG levels after achieving complete remission, measured as an event rate or as time‐to‐event (progression‐free survival, PFS), i.e. time from complete remission to the diagnosis of relapse Disease‐specific mortality, defined as events or time‐to‐event (disease‐specific survival or overall survival (OS) Death due to treatment Secondary outcomes Pregnancy rate Quality of life, measured on a validated scale, such as the European Organisation for the Research and Treatment of Cancer Quality of Life Questionnaire Core 30 Items (EORTC QLQ‐C30) ( Aaronson 1993 ), the Functional Assessment of Cancer Therapy‐General (FACT‐G) ( Cella 1993 ), or the Rotterdam Symptom Checklist (RSCL) ( De Haes 1990 ) Number of chemotherapy cycles to remission Any adverse events, defined as any negative outcomes resulting from surgical intervention or chemotherapy according to grades of toxicity in the Common Terminology Criteria for Adverse Events (CTCAE) system ( CTCAE 2017 ) (we considered ''any adverse events'' or ''adverse events of severity grade 3 or 4'' or both, as appropriate): adverse events from surgical interventions, such as uterine perforation, haemorrhage, infections, severe pain, vasovagal reaction, or other patient‐reported outcome measures; adverse events from chemotherapy such as haematological (anaemia, neutropenia, abnormal liver function), gastrointestinal (pain, nausea, vomiting), genitourinary (vaginal bleeding, cystitis), skin (mucositis or pain or inflammation of the mucous membrane such as mouth (stomatitis), alopecia, allergy), or neurological (peripheral and central) Emergency hysterectomy in fertility‐sparing treatments Search methods for identification of studies Electronic searches The Cochrane Information Specialist helped to design the search strategies, which are presented in Appendix 3 . We searched the following databases, with no language or date restrictions. Cochrane Central Register of Controlled Trials (CENTRAL; Issue 1 of 12, 31 January 2024) MEDLINE (1946 to 31 January 2024) Embase (1980 to 31 January 2024) Web of Science (1980 to 31 January 2024) Searching other resources We also searched trials from the reference lists of the relevant papers, trials registries (World Health Organization (WHO) International Clinical Trials Registry Platform (ICTRP) and US National Institutes of Health Ongoing Trials Register clinicaltrials.gov) on 31 January 2024. We also searched relevant conference proceedings from the International Gynecologic Cancer Society (IGCS), American Society of Clinical Oncology (ASCO), European Society for Medical Oncology (ESMO), European Society for Radiotherapy and Oncology (ESTRO), European Society of Gynaecological Oncology (ESGO), and Asian Society of Gynecologic Oncology (ASGO), on 31 January 2024. We contacted the authors of relevant trials to request additional information if the details of the studies were not clear. Data collection and analysis The methods section of this review follows the guidance outlined in the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions ( Higgins 2021 ). Two review authors conducted the process of independent study selection, data extraction, and assessment of the risk of bias. Selection of studies Two review authors (Sathana Boonyapipat (SB) and Kulisara Nanthamongkolkul (KN)) independently selected potential articles on the basis of title and abstract screening from the search results. We removed duplicates and excluded studies that clearly did not meet the inclusion criteria. If a decision could not be made from this preliminary scan, we sourced the full texts, and SB and KN independently determined whether each identified article met the review eligibility criteria. We resolved differences of opinion by discussion between the two review authors and by involving a third review author (Nungrutai Saeaib (NS)). Data extraction and management SB and KN independently extracted data using a pre‐designed data extraction form, which included the following information from each study. Design: description of randomisation method, blinding, number of study centres, study duration, and number of withdrawals from the study Participants: number, age, FIGO stage, risk score, presence of myometrial invasion Intervention: type of surgical intervention (uterine curettage/direct resection under hysteroscopy/myometrial resection), details of intervention (outpatient/office, general/regional/spinal anaesthesia or intravenous sedation), pain scores, patient‐reported outcomes measures (PROMs), the regimen of chemotherapy used, dose, route of administration, and schedule Outcomes: for dichotomous outcomes (e.g. treatment success rate, adverse events, need for additional chemotherapy), we extracted the number of women in each treatment arm who experienced the outcome of interest, in order to estimate a risk ratio (RR); for continuous outcomes (e.g. number of chemotherapy cycles to remission), we extracted the arithmetic mean and standard deviation (SD) of the outcome of interest in each treatment arm. If reported, we planned to extract median and range data; for time‐to‐event data (time from diagnosis to remission), we extracted the log of the hazard ratio (logHR) and its standard error from the trial reports. If these were not reported, we planned to estimate the logHR and its standard error using the methods of Parmar 1998 . Where data were insufficient or missing from a trial, we contacted the trial authors for more information. We resolved differences between the review authors (SB and KN) by discussion or by referral to a third review author (NS). Assessment of risk of bias in included studies Review authors SB and KN independently assessed the risk of bias in the included studies using version 2 of the Cochrane risk of bias tool (RoB 2) ( Higgins 2021 ; Sterne 2019), which considers bias according to the following domains. Bias from the randomisation process Bias due to deviations from the intended interventions Bias due to missing outcome data Bias in measurement of the outcome Bias in selection of the reported results Overall bias We assessed the risk of bias for all reported outcomes. We used RoB 2 signalling questions and classified each domain as ''low risk of bias'', ''some concerns'' or ''high risk of bias''. We reached our overall judgement following the algorithm in the RoB2 tool, as recommended in Chapter 8 of The Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions ( Higgins 2021 ). The overall risk of bias in the result was the least favourable assessment across the domains of bias. We were interested in the effect of assignment to the intervention at baseline, regardless of whether the interventions were received as intended (i.e. the ''intention‐to‐treat'' effect). We used the RoB 2 Excel tool for risk of bias assessments (available on the riskofbias.info website) and we stored data for presentation in an online repository in Figshare. We resolved differences of opinion through discussion between the two review authors (SB and KN) or by involving the fourth review author (Tippawan Liabsuetrakul (TL)). Measures of treatment effect For dichotomous outcomes, we presented results as the summary RR with 95% confidence intervals (CI). For continuous outcomes, we presented results as the mean difference (MD) between treatment arms with the associated standard deviation (SD) with 95% CI. For time‐to‐event data, we planned to present results as the summary HR with 95% CI. Unit of analysis issues The unit of analysis was the trial participant. Dealing with missing data We attempted to extract data on the outcomes only among participants who were assessed at the endpoint. If data were missing, we contacted trial authors to request data. We did not impute missing outcome data for any of the outcomes. Assessment of heterogeneity When possible, we planned to assess heterogeneity between studies by visual inspection of forest plots and by measuring the statistical variation between combined studies using the I 2 statistic ( Deeks 2001 ; Higgins 2021 ). We planned to determine the potential reasons for any substantial heterogeneity (I 2 > 50%) using subgroup analysis and sensitivity analysis. We planned to use the random‐effects model for all pooled effect estimates. Assessment of reporting biases We planned to use a comparison‐adjusted funnel plot and Egger''s test to assess potential publication bias if we identified at least 10 studies with the same comparison and outcome to increase the chance of detecting ''real'' asymmetry ( Sterne 2011 ). Data synthesis We planned to perform meta‐analyses of included studies using Review Manager 2020 software if two or more included studies were clinically and methodologically similar. We planned to use the random‐effects model for all meta‐analyses ( DerSimonian 1986 ). For dichotomous outcomes, we planned to calculate the RR for each study and pool these data. For continuous outcomes, we planned to calculate the MD between the treatment arms at the end of follow‐up and pool these data if outcomes were measured similarly between trials. If the outcome measurements were different across studies, we planned to analyse them using the standardised MD. For time‐to‐event data, we planned to calculate pooled HR from similar trials. Subgroup analysis and investigation of heterogeneity We planned to conduct the following subgroup analyses if there was substantial hetereogeneity in the meta‐analyses. We selected these based on findings from previous trials ( Lorigan 1996 ; Pezeshki 2004 ; Savage 2005 , Osborne 2016 ). Pretreatment hCG level ( < 1500 mIU/mL versus > 1500 mIU/mL, or < 5000 mIU/mL versus > 5000 mIU/mL) Presence or absence of myometrial invasion WHO risk score (0 to 4 versus 5 to 6) Different chemotherapy regimens between intervention and control groups Sensitivity analysis We planned to conduct sensitivity analysis to examine the effects of the risk of bias on the meta‐analyses. Summary of findings and assessment of the certainty of the evidence We used GRADEpro GDT software to create the summary of findings table ( GRADEpro GDT 2021 ), which contains a summary of the intervention effect and certainty of the evidence considered five aspects, namely study limitations, consistency of the effect, imprecision, indirectness, and publication bias, for each outcome. We downgraded the evidence from high certainty by one level for serious limitations or by two levels for very serious limitations, according to the guidance in the Cochrane Handbook for Systematic Reviews of Interventions ( Schünemann 2020 ). Two review authors (SB and KN) independently assessed the certainty of the evidence. We resolved any disagreements by discussion or by involving a third review author (TL). We created a summary of findings table for each comparison and the following outcomes: treatment success rate; relapse; disease‐specific mortality; death due to treatment; pregnancy rate; quality of life; any adverse events. Results Description of studies Results of the search Our searches identified a total of 2966 references. After removing 19 duplicates, we screened 2947 records based on title and abstract. We excluded 2941 based on title and abstract, and assessed the full text of the remaining six. We included two studies (two references), excluded two studies, and two studies are ongoing. The numbers in the study selection process are detailed in a PRISMA flow diagram ‐ see Figure 1 . 1. Open in a new tab Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Included studies We identified only two studies for inclusion in this review: Ayatollahi 2017 and Hemida 2019 . We contacted the authors of the two studies to request additional information regarding surgical techniques, adverse events from interventions, and treatment outcomes of study participants who were excluded before the study analyses. Only Hemida 2019 responded. Hemida 2019 provided a small proportion of the missing information, i.e. they stated that there were no deaths during or after the study period. Participants Comparison 1: fertility‐sparing surgical interventions without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone The Ayatollahi 2017 study, conducted in Iran, included 62 women diagnosed with post‐molar LR‐NMGTN classified by FIGO criteria, with varying hCG levels. The median hCG levels were 10,400 mIU/mL, and the median FIGO scores were 2.5 for the chemotherapy group and 3 for the surgical intervention group. The study excluded recurrent GTN, and patients whose disease had transformed into choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT), and epithelioid tumour. Comparison 2: fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone The Hemida 2019 study, conducted in Egypt, involved 89 women diagnosed with post‐molar LR‐NMGTN (WHO score < 7) and an hCG level of less than 5000 mIU/ml. The study excluded patients with metastasis, a history of chemotherapy, previous uterine perforation, PSTT, and life‐threatening bleeding. The median hCG levels were 3274 + 1813 mIU/mL for the surgical intervention with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy group and 3000 + 1974 mIU/mL for the primary chemotherapy group. Mean uterine lesion volumes were 171 mm 3 and 139 mm 3 for surgical intervention with the subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy group and the primary chemotherapy group, respectively. Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions and anaesthesia Both studies utilised uterine curettage as a surgical intervention, specifically employing a "second uterine curettage. The operative procedure details were briefly mentioned in both studies, Ayatollahi 2017 mentioned cervical ripening before curettage. In the case of Hemida 2019 , uterine curettage was performed under short‐acting anaesthesia or sedation, and antibiotic prophylaxis was administered. Additionally, cervical dilatation was performed before curettage using a manual vacuum aspirator under ultrasound guidance. Chemotherapy In Ayatollahi 2017 , chemotherapy was administered if the hCG levels did not decrease as expected following surgical intervention. On the other hand, in Hemida 2019 , all participants received chemotherapy as part of comparison 2. Both studies employed methotrexate (MTX) as the chemotherapy agent, but they had different regimens. Ayatollahi 2017 used a weekly intramuscular administration of 50 mg/m 2 , while Hemida 2019 employed a biweekly eight‐day methotrexate with folinic acid regimen of 50 or 75 mg MTX on days 1, 3, 5, and 7, alternating with folinic acid on days 2, 4, 6, and 8. The first cycle was initiated within 24 hours of the curettage. Hemida 2019 included two consolidation chemotherapy cycles, while Ayatollahi 2017 did not specify the number of consolidation chemotherapy cycles. Follow‐up For follow‐up assessments, the duration ranged from 6 months for participants receiving uterine curettage to 1 year for participants receiving primary chemotherapy in comparison 1 ( Ayatollahi 2017 ). In comparison 2, the mean duration ( + SD) reported was 23 (± 1.4) months ( Hemida 2019 ). Both studies used serum hCG levels to determine remission status, with normal hCG levels ranging from 2.4 mIU/mL ( Ayatollahi 2017 ) to 7 mIU/mL ( Hemida 2019 ). For comparison 1, six of the 62 randomised participants dropped out after randomisation due to being diagnosed with high‐risk GTN, three from each group. Additionally, three participants who developed choriocarcinoma were not included in the ﬁnal analysis, one from the uterine curettage with adjuvant chemotherapy group and two from the primary chemotherapy group. There was one loss to follow‐up in the curettage arm. A total of 10 participants (16.1%) were not included in the study''s analyses ( Ayatollahi 2017 ). For comparison 2, three of the 89 randomised participants (3.4%) dropped out after randomisation. Of these, two were from the uterine curettage group and one was from the chemotherapy group. ( Hemida 2019 ) Excluded studies We excluded two studies as they used an ineligible study design ( Lewis 1966 ; Parsa 1979 ). Ongoing studies Two relevant studies that we identified were still at the protocol stage and had not yet reported results on the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention in LR‐NMGTN. An open‐label RCT carried out in China to compare hysteroscopic repeat curettage to chemotherapy as the first‐line treatment in low‐risk, post‐molar GTN ( NCT03703271 ). The trial register reports the estimated completion date of the study as 31 December 2023. A multicentre randomised trial in Brazil with two treatment arms: immediate treatment with single‐agent chemotherapy or second uterine curettage in women with low‐risk, non‐metastatic GTN ( ReCure ). The estimated completion date is December 2025. Risk of bias in included studies We performed risk of bias assessment using the RoB2 tool for all outcomes that are included in the summary of findings tables and risk of bias tables. We provide detailed results of the risk of bias assessment for each outcome as supplemental data in an online repository. Comparison 1: fertility‐sparing surgical interventions without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Number of chemotherapy cycles to achieve remission Ayatollahi 2017 measured only one of our outcomes of interest: number of chemotherapy cycles to achieve remission. We judged Ayatollahi 2017 as having a high risk of bias overall due to the high risk of bias in missing outcome data and measurement of the outcome ( Table 9 ). Risk of bias for analysis 1.1 Number of chemotherapy cycles to remission. Study Bias Randomisation process Deviations from intended interventions Missing outcome data Measurement of the outcome Selection of the reported results Overall Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Ayatollahi 2017 Low risk of bias Sealed opaque envelops with random number were used. A