g surgical interventions without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 1: Number of chemotherapy cycles to remission Any adverse events We could not report on this outcome. Although there were no surgical complications in the study, adverse events from chemotherapy were not reported in the results, even though they were listed in the methods section. Emergency hysterectomy in fertility‐sparing treatments The study did not report on emergency hysterectomies. Comparison 2: fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Only one RCT evaluated this comparison ( Hemida 2019 ). See Table 2 . Primary outcomes Treatment success rate The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on the treatment success rate compared to the primary chemotherapy group (RR 1.03, 95% CI 0.86 to 1.23; 86 participants; very low‐certainty evidence; Analysis 2.1 ). 2.1. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 1: Treatment success rate Time to remission Hemida 2019 did not report time to remission. Relapse The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on relapse before one year compared to the primary chemotherapy group (RR 0.50, 95% CI 0.05 to 5.31; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.2 ). 2.2. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 2: Relapse Disease‐specific mortality Hemida 2019 reported no disease‐specific deaths in either group (86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.3 ). 2.3. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 3: Disease‐specific mortality Death due to treatment The study reported no deaths due to treatment in either group (86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.4 ) 2.4. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 4: Death due to treatment Secondary outcomes Pregnancy rate The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on pregnancy rate compared to the primary chemotherapy group (RR 0.86, 95% CI 0.31 to 2.34; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence, Analysis 2.5 ). 2.5. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 5: Pregnancy rate Quality of life The study did not report the quality of life of the participants. Number of chemotherapy cycles to remission The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on the number of chemotherapy cycles to remission compared to the primary chemotherapy group (MD ‐0.60, 95% CI ‐1.55 to 0.35; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence, Analysis 2.6 ). 2.6. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 6: Number of chemotherapy cycles to remission Any adverse events The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on any adverse events (RR 1.15, 95% CI 0.63 to 2.13; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.7 ). All reported adverse events were from chemotherapy and were predominantly grade 1 or 2. 2.7. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 7: Any adverse events The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on adverse events (grade 3 to 4 from chemotherapy) (RR 0.20, 95% CI 0.01 to 4.05; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.8 ). 2.8. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 8: Adverse events (grade 3 or 4) There were no surgical complications recorded in the intervention group. Emergency hysterectomy in fertility‐sparing treatments None of the participants in the uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy group required emergency hysterectomy, but one participant in the primary chemotherapy group required emergency hysterectomy (RR 0.33, 95% CI 0.01 to 7.96; 86 participants; very low certainty evidence; Analysis 2.9 ). 2.9. Analysis. Open in a new tab Comparison 2: Fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy, Outcome 9: Emergency hysterectomy Discussion Summary of main results In this review, we included two RCTs that enroled 138 participants to evaluate the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention for LR‐NMGTN. The RCTs evaluated different comparisons, so we were unable to combine results; therefore, each result is based on findings from one small study. All participants included in both studies presented with post‐molar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN), without choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumour (PSTT), or epithelioid trophoblastic tumour. The fertility‐sparing intervention technique used in both studies was uterine curettage. Comparison 1. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Only one study evaluated comparison 1 ( Ayatollahi 2017 ). There were no reported outcome data for treatment success rate, time to remission, relapse, disease‐specific mortality, death due to treatment, pregnancy, quality of life, adverse events from chemotherapy, and emergency hysterectomy. Half of the participants in the uterine curettage group received adjuvant chemotherapy because their hCG levels increased. Evidence from this subgroup of participants suggests that fewer chemotherapy cycles may be needed to achieve remission with uterine curettage plus adjuvant chemotherapy compared to primary chemotherapy, but the evidence is very uncertain. This outcome was considered to have a high risk of bias due to the small sample size, missing outcome data, and issues with outcome measurement. At present, the benefits and harms of uterine curettage without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy are uncertain because of a lack of reporting on specific outcomes of interest. Comparison 2. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Only one study evaluated comparison 2, with ''some concerns'' about risk of bias for all outcomes, and ''high risk'' of bias for the ''any adverse events'' outcome ( Hemida 2019 ). The evidence was very uncertain about the effect of uterine curettage with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy on treatment success rate, relapse, disease‐specific mortality, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, number of chemotherapy cycles to remission, any adverse events, and emergency hysterectomy. The study did not report outcome data about quality of life. We judged the results to provide only very low certainty evidence, due to the risk of bias from incomplete blinding and the fact that the findings were drawn from only one study with a small sample size. Overall completeness and applicability of evidence Due to the rarity of the disease and current treatment guidelines recommending primary chemotherapy for the initial management of LR‐NMGTN, we found only two RCTs of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions for LR‐NMGTN, with only one study per comparison for analysis. Both studies used interventions resembling real‐world practice. The fertility‐sparing surgical intervention technique investigated in the studies was uterine curettage, which can be readily implemented in most settings. Hemida 2019 described ultrasound‐guided manual vacuum aspiration, while Ayatollahi 2017 did not specify the use of ultrasound guidance or the surgical technique used for the curettage. The chemotherapeutic regimens used in the studies were weekly intramuscular methotrexate (comparison 1) and a biweekly eight‐day regimen of methotrexate with folinic acid (comparison 2), which appear to be feasible and are widely adopted. However, both studies were conducted in the Middle East (Iran and Egypt), so generalising these results to other ethnicities and healthcare contexts may require further research. The results in routine implementation may also differ depending on surgical techniques, experiences of the surgeons, chemotherapeutic approaches (including consolidation treatment), and hCG levels. Due to having only two included studies, there was a lack of data for subgroup analysis, leaving us unable to determine if there are specific factors that may influence the effects of the intervention. The included studies lacked data on many important outcomes: treatment success rate, time to remission, relapse, disease‐specific mortality, death from treatment, pregnancy outcomes, quality of life, adverse events, and need for emergency hysterectomy in comparison 1, and time to remission, and quality of life in comparison 2. Moreover, the included studies only reported outcomes at short‐term follow‐up, which means it is not possible to assess long‐term events, such as relapse, quality of life, and pregnancy outcomes. Follow‐up duration was at least 12 months for comparison 1, and 23 months for comparison 2. However, longer follow‐up is needed to evaluate the effects of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention for LR‐NMGTN. Of note, although the included studies reported no surgical complications, it remains uncertain whether the absence of complications was real, or due to missing data, small numbers of participants, or selective reporting bias. We lack information on adverse events from chemotherapy (comparison 1) because the study did not report any details on them. As our findings were based on studies at high risk of bias with small sample sizes, which did not report our outcomes of interest or reported them poorly, there was insufficient evidence, in terms of either benefits or harms, to support the use of uterine curettage, with or without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy, over chemotherapy alone. Healthcare providers should discuss the risks and benefits of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions with patients with LR‐NMGTN. Large well‐designed multicentre trials would be needed to identify the effects of fertility‐sparing surgical intervention and to identify the subgroups who may benefit from this treatment strategy. Comparison 1. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention without adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone We assessed the certainty of the evidence for only one outcome, ''the number of chemotherapy cycles to remission,'' as other outcomes of interest were either not reported or incompletely reported (any adverse events). We downgraded by two levels due to a high risk of bias resulting from significant missing data and lack of blinding. We also downgraded by one level for imprecision due to the small number of participants. As a result, we judged the certainty of evidence for this outcome as very low. Comparison 2. Fertility‐sparing surgical intervention with subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone We judged the certainty of the evidence to be very low for all outcomes. We downgraded by three levels for imprecision for the outcomes ''disease‐specific mortality'' and ''death from treatment'' as there were no events and a small number of participants, and for the remaining outcomes, as there were few participants and wide confidence intervals. We further downgraded by one level for the "any adverse events" outcome, due to risk of bias from the lack of blinding of outcome assessors. Potential biases in the review process To minimise bias, we included only RCTs that adjusted for baseline differences between treatment groups. However, only two studies were eligible. It is possible that we may not have detected all outcomes of interest. For example, we may not have identified all relevant studies due to an incomplete search, have had gaps in the data collection process, or made incorrect assumptions about the classification of interventions, outcomes, or subgroups. We attempted to prevent bias in the review by making an effort to obtain missing data from the investigators; however, data from several outcomes remain unavailable for analysis, which limits the conclusions we can draw in the review. Additionally, we are unable to assess reporting bias due to the limited number of included studies. If this bias exists, it might lead to an overestimation or underestimation of the effects of uterine curettage. We are also concerned about the possibility that some of the included cases of hydatidiform mole with persistent molar tissue were classified as gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN) in the included studies. Neither study mentioned excluding participants who had retained molar tissue. If these cases were inadvertently included, their prognosis might be better than that of those with true GTN. However, we cannot confirm this without clear identification in the original studies. Systematic reviews A recent systematic review by Kamijo 2023 , which encompassed both randomised and non‐randomised trials, assessed the efficacy and safety of a surgery‐first approach (using uterine curettage or hysterectomy) compared to a chemotherapy‐first approach in low‐risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (GTN). This review included 10 studies involving 2192 participants, of which eight examined uterine curettage and two examined hysterectomy. This review found that uterine curettage may result in little to no difference in remission, a slight reduction in adverse events, and uncertainty regarding the number of chemotherapy cycles. The review authors concluded that uterine curettage was comparable to the chemotherapy‐first approach as the first‐line treatment option, based on evidence of low to very low certainty. These results included the two randomised trials included in our review. Another systematic review has investigated the rate of chemotherapy avoidance and the number of chemotherapy sessions (if needed) in patients with post‐molar GTN receiving uterine curettage compared to those receiving primary chemotherapy ( Zhao 2023 ; Zhao 2024 ). The results of this review were derived from analyses of five studies, including both RCTs and non‐randomised retrospective studies. The authors concluded that the second curettage reduced the need for chemotherapy, and reduced the number of chemotherapy cycles by two cycles. Pooled analyses were performed by subgrouping studies based on their quality. In Zhao''s review, the quality of the included studies was assessed as high, although the certainty of the evidence was not clearly stated. Mah 2022 examined the efficacy of uterine curettage in patients with low‐risk GTN. This review included two randomised trials and four cohort studies, with data analysed according to the study type, whether cohort or trial. Based on two randomised trials, it was found that uterine curettage may decrease the mean difference in the number of chemotherapy cycles by two cycles compared to the chemotherapy group. This reduction did not result in an increased necessity for multi‐agent chemotherapy. The analysis of the four cohort studies found that women undergoing uterine curettage had a reduced need for chemotherapy. The certainty of the evidence is very low due to potential biases, including risks of confounding, selection bias, missing data, and inconsistency in the results. The review suggests that uterine treatment might enhance the rate of successful treatment without the need for chemotherapy, and may reduce the number of chemotherapy cycles necessary without making it more likely that multi‐agent chemotherapy will be needed. The systematic reviews conducted by Kamijo 2023 and Mah 2022 were derived from the meta‐analysis of two RCTs, both of which were also included in this review, specifically, Ayatollahi 2017 and Hemida 2019 . We did not perform a meta‐analysis of the trials as we were concerned that adjuvant chemotherapy might be given for different purposes. Instead, we analysed the results separately by group. Our review focuses on all types of fertility‐sparing surgical interventions and examines a broader range of outcomes than the other systematic reviews listed in this section. Non‐randomised studies Comparison 1: fertility‐sparing surgical intervention without adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Our results were consistent with the findings of Osborne 2016 , a phase II prospective trial. This study demonstrated that using a uterine curettage alone as the initial treatment for LR‐NMGTN, regardless of hCG levels, resulted in a successful cure for 24 individuals (40%) of participants, without notable complications. Surgical failure was noted in 59% of participants, with a higher occurrence amongst women aged 19 or younger and those aged 40 or older, or WHO score 5 to 6. In contrast to the results of the observational retrospective studies, the treatment success rate of uterine curettage alone varied from 9.4% to 83% ( Ahmadzadeh 2020 ; Aminimoghaddam 2023 ; Pezeshki 2004 ; Van Trommel 2005 ; Yarandi 2014 ; Vandewal 2021 ), and reduced the number of chemotherapy cycles by 1 cycle (P = 0.036) ( Aminimoghaddam 2023 ). Patients who were likely to benefit from uterine curettage alone were those whose hCG levels ranged from lower than 1500 to 5000 mIU/mL ( Pezeshki 2004 ; Vandewal 2021 ). The inconsistency in these results could be attributed to variations in participant demographics, surgical techniques, surgical experience levels, and differences in chemotherapy regimens, including consolidation approaches. Comparison 2: fertility‐sparing surgical intervention with adjuvant chemotherapy versus primary chemotherapy alone Our findings were in line with Growdon 2009 , which found that uterine curettage did not affect whether a participant required additional chemotherapy doses or alternate types of chemotherapy, but they contrasted with Hemida 2019 , which showed that uterine curettage in low‐risk GTN was significantly correlated with a lower number of chemotherapy cycles. Authors'' conclusions Implications for practice. Uterine curettage is the only fertility‐sparing surgical intervention for low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplasia (LR‐NMGTN) that has been evaluated in a randomised controlled trial. The evidence, based on two small trials, is very uncertain about the benefits and harms of uterine curettage, with or without subsequent adjuvant chemotherapy, compared to primary chemotherapy, in women with LR‐NMGTN. Additionally, several important clinical outcomes were not measured. Therefore, current evidence neither supports nor refutes the use of surgical uterine evacuation as an initial management strategy for women with LR‐NMGTN. Due to the very low certainty of evidence, further research is needed to establish the benefits and harms of fertility‐sparing surgical alternatives to hysterectomy for women with LR‐NMGTN. Women should be provided with clear and balanced information about uterine curettage, including the uncertainties surrounding the available evidence, to ensure that they understand the potential benefits and harms of different options before making a decision about treatment. Implications for research. A large‐scale trial is necessary to thoroughly evaluate the benefits and harms associated with fertility‐sparing surgical interventions, both with and without adjuvant chemotherapy, in comparison to primary chemotherapy. This head‐to‐head trial should be well‐designed and use a long follow‐up period (e.g. 5 years) for assessment of patient‐important outcomes, including quality of life, survival, pregnancy outcomes, and cost, in relation to different types of surgical interventions. A multicentre trial may be required to attain the optimal information size. Additionally, subgroups of LR‐NMGTN patients who are likely to benefit from the intervention based on hCG level should be identified. Women with retained molar tissue after the initial uterine evacuation should not be included in the trial. History Protocol first published: Issue 10, 2022 Risk of bias Risk of bias for analysis 2.8 Adverse events (grade 3 or 4). Study Bias Randomisation process Deviations from intended interventions Missing outcome data Measurement of the outcome Selection of the reported results Overall Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Authors'' judgement Support for judgement Hemida 2019 Low risk of bias Randomization was assigned via online process and the use of biased‐coin minimization procedure with consealed allocation. No baseline different between groups. Some concerns Blinding of participants and surgeon were impossible. There were deviations that might relate to trial context. Appropriate analysis. Low risk of bias Outcome data were from nealry all participants. High risk of bias No data about assessor''s blinding. Knowledge of intervention is likely to influence the outcome. Low risk of bias Trial analysed in accordance with a prespecified plan. Result was not selected from multiple analyses. High risk of bias There was