verse events 1 Risk Ratio (M‐H, Fixed, 95% CI) Totals not selected 2.8 Adverse events (grade 3 or 4) 1 Risk Ratio (M‐H, Fixed, 95% CI) Totals not selected 2.9 Emergency hysterectomy 1 Risk Ratio (M‐H, Fixed, 95% CI) Totals not selected Open in a new tab Characteristics of studies Characteristics of included studies [ordered by study ID] Ayatollahi 2017 . Study characteristics Methods Study design: RCT Study grouping : parallel Study start date: 2014 Study end date: 2015 Participants Country: Iran Setting: Motahhari Teaching Hospital in the province of West Azerbaijan Inclusion criteria: persistent low‐risk GTD according to FIGO/WHO staging and risk score is defined by one of the following criteria: 10% decline in B‐hCG levels in four consecutive measurements over a 3‐week period; 20% rise in B‐hCG levels in three consecutive measurements over a 2‐week period; elevated B‐hCG level for 6 months, and risk score less than 6 according to WHO. Exclusion criteria: patients with evidence of metastasis and recurrent GTN who had previously been treated with chemotherapy; patients whose disease had transformed into choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic tumour, or epithelioid tumour (histologically diagnosed at curettage procedure). The study population included complete and partial mole. Total randomised: 62 Exclusions and loss to follow‐up: 10/62 (16.1%): choriocarcinoma (2 participants in the primary chemotherapy arm and 1 in the uterine curettage arm), developing high‐risk post‐molar GTN (3 participants in each group), and 1 participant lost to follow‐up in the intervention arm Analysed: 52 participants, 26 in each group Interventions Intervention: uterine curettage Description: cervical preparation with prostaglandin was performed in order to reach cervical ripening before the second curettage. Re‐curettage was performed by one gynaecologist, and one pathologist examined all samples to report the type of tumour. Follow‐up: follow‐up sessions for the intervention arm were scheduled 24 hours and 1 week after re‐curettage. These sessions continued until the B‐hCG levels normalised for three consecutive weeks. Afterward, participants were monitored monthly for six months to ensure that B‐hCG levels remained normal. For participants with increasing B‐hCG after 1 week, chemotherapy was initiated. Comparison: usual care (chemotherapy) Description: participants received chemotherapy medications as usual care. Weekly low‐dose methotrexate (50mg/m 2 , intramuscular) was used to treat control participants as the first‐line treatment in this study. Follow‐up : B‐hCG levels were measured weekly until B‐hCG became normal for 3 consecutive weeks, then monthly follow‐up for at least 1 year was continued by B‐hCG measurement. Outcomes Primary outcome: number of chemotherapy cycles Secondary outcome: number of weeks of therapy required to obtain a complete response in each arm Identification Sponsorship source: none Author name: Haleh Ayatollah Institution: Department of Gynecology and Obstetrics, Urmia University of Medical Sciences, Shahid Motahhari Hospital Email: hayatollahi@yahoo.com Address: Kashani Street, Urmia 571478334, Iran Notes No grants or support reported We contacted the authors but have not received a response. Open in a new tab Hemida 2019 . Study characteristics Methods Study design: RCT Study grouping: parallel Study start date: 12 March 2012 Study end date: not stated Participants Country: Egypt Setting: Mansoura University, Mansoura, Egypt Inclusion criteria: women aged 18 years or older with post‐molar GTN, having a score less than 7, a serum B‐hCG level of 5000 mIU/mL or less, and fit for standard treatment with methotrexate Exclusion criteria: previous uterine perforation and life‐threatening bleeding Group differences: B‐hCG level < 1500 vs 1500 to 5000 mIU/ml, presence or absence of vaginal bleeding Total randomised: 89 participants Exclusions and loss to follow‐up : 3 participants (3.4%) were not included in the trial''s analysis: one chose hysterectomy in another hospital; the second refused subsequent treatment when methotrexate failed, and was lost to follow‐up; and the third ultimately did not fulfil the diagnostic criteria of post‐molar GTN. Analysed: 86 participants, 43 in each group Interventions Intervention: second uterine curettage with adjuvant chemotherapy Surgical procedure: uterine curettage was performed under short‐acting general anaesthesia or sedation, and antibiotic prophylaxis. After cervical dilatation, the uterine cavity was evacuated using manual vacuum aspiration under ultrasound guidance (Ipas MVAEasyGrip Cannulae). The tissue obtained was sent for histopathologic analysis. Adjuvant chemotherapy : the first methotrexate was given within 24 hours of the curettage. All participants received standard methotrexate treatment with folinic acid support, the same as that of the chemotherapy group. Follow‐up: monthly testing of serum hCG levels for a year after normalisation of B‐hCG Comparison: chemotherapy Description: all participants received standard methotrexate treatment with folinic acid support, both given intramuscularly in a biweekly 8‐day methotrexate‐with‐folinic‐acid regimen. Methotrexate was administered at a dose of 50 or 75 mg (the latter for women exceeding 75 kg) on days 1, 3, 5, and 7, alternating with folinic acid at a dose of 12.5 mg on days 2, 4, 6, and 8. After normalisation of serum B‐hCG, that is, B‐hCG level less than 7 international units/L, two consolidation courses of methotrexate were given. Follow‐up: monthly testing of serum B‐hCG levels for a year after normalisation of hCG Outcomes Primary outcome: mean number of chemotherapy courses needed to achieve hCG normalisation Secondary outcomes: length of chemotherapy cycles, complications of methotrexate, treatment outcomes, histology, and follow‐up. Treatment outcomes included "successful treatment", which was defined as normalisation of serum hCG level by methotrexate only, no emergency surgery, and no relapse within one year. Identification Sponsorship source: the authors did not report any potential conflicts of interest. Author name: Helena C van Doorn Institution: Department of Gynecologic Oncology, Erasmus MC Cancer Center, University Medical Center Rotterdam Email: h.vandoorn@erasmusmc.nl Address: Room Na15‐09, PO Box 2040, 3000 CA Rotterdam, the Netherlands Trial references registration in Dutch Trial Registry: NTR3390 Notes The study was carried out in Egypt, but randomisation was managed by the research centre in the Netherlands. The trial author reported that there were no disease‐specific deaths or deaths due to treatment during the study period (unpublished data). Funding for language editing service was provided by one of the authors (Helena C van Doorn). Open in a new tab B‐hCG: beta‐human chorionic gonadotropin; FIGO: International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; GTD: gestational trophoblastic disease; GTN: gestational trophoblastic neoplasia; mg/m 2 : milligrams per square metre; mIU/mL: milli‐international units per millilitre; RCT: randomised controlled trial; WHO: World Health Organization Characteristics of excluded studies [ordered by study ID] Study Reason for exclusion Lewis 1966 Ineligible study design Parsa 1979 Ineligible study design Open in a new tab Characteristics of ongoing studies [ordered by study ID] NCT03703271 . Study name A prospective randomized multicenter clinical control study of hysteroscopic repeat curettage as the primal management of low‐risk postmolar gestational trophoblastic neoplasia Methods Study design: RCT (open label) Setting: multicentre Country: China Planned enrolment: 214 Study duration: 1 March 2019 to 31 December 2023 (final data collection date for primary outcome measure) Recruitment status: recruiting Participants Inclusion criteria Low‐risk postmolar GTN World Health Organization (WHO) risk score ≤ 4 Age ≤ 60 years, female, Chinese Initial treatment performance status: Karnofsky score ≥ 60; laboratory tests: WBC ≥ 3.5×10(9)/L, ANC ≥ 1.5×10(9)/L, PLT ≥ 80×10(9)/L, serum bilirubin ≤ 1.5 times the upper limit of normal, transaminase ≤ 1.5 times the upper limit of normal, blood urea nitrogen, Cr ≤ normal Provide written informed consent Exclusion criteria Unconfirmed diagnosis of GTN Placental‐site trophoblastic tumor (PSTT) or epithelioid trophoblastic tumor (ETT) WHO risk score ≥ 5 Diameter of a single metastatic lesion in the lung was ≥ 2 cm Number of lung CT metastases was ≥ 5 With severe or uncontrolled internal disease, unable to receive chemotherapy Concurrently participating in other clinical trials Unable or unwilling to sign informed consents Unable or unwilling to abide by protocol Interventions Arm 1: hysteroscopic repeat curettage Arm 2: chemotherapy; methotrexate Outcomes Primary Complete remission rate in first‐line treatment Secondary Complications of hysteroscopic repeat curettage surgery (time frame 2 years) Severity of adverse events as assessed by the WHO Overall survival rate Ovarian functional evaluation Pregnancy rate Menstrual cycle resuming rate Starting date 1 March 2019 Contact information Weiguo Lv, MD, 13588193832@163.com Notes ClinicalTrials.gov identifier: NCT03703271 Study sponsor: Women''s Hospital School Of Medicine Zhejiang University Other identifier: ZJHGTN1212 Open in a new tab ReCure . Study name Impact of second uterine evacuation in women with non‐metastatic, low‐risk gestational trophoblastic neoplasia: a phase III trial Methods Study design: randomised trial Setting: multicentre Country: Brazil Planned enrolment: 150 Study duration: 11 February 2021 to 31 December 2025 Recruitment status: recruiting Participants Inclusion criteria Histopathological diagnosis of molar pregnancy according to Sebire morphological criteria, and meeting the FIGO 2000 diagnostic criteria for LR‐NMGTN Exclusion criteria High‐risk GTN (FIGO risk score ≥ 7) or metastatic disease at diagnosis of GTN (stage II, III or IV) Histopathological diagnosis of choriocarcinoma, placental site trophoblastic or epithelioid trophoblastic tumour at the second curettage Previous chemotherapy treatment Level of hCG at time of GTN diagnosis less than 20 IU/L (to minimise risk of including people with false‐positive hCG, either by cross‐reaction with pituitary hormones or by the presence of circulating heterophilic antibodies) Relapsed GTN Incomplete medical records Loss to follow‐up Voluntary desire to stop participating in the study Interventions Arm 1: manual or electronic vacuum aspiration under ultrasound guidance Arm 2: chemotherapy; methotrexate Outcomes Primary outcome Remission rate from primary therapy Secondary outcomes Cycles to remission Time to remission Need for multi‐agent chemotherapy Relapse Death Starting date 11 February 2021 Contact information Marcio Barcellos, MD, mbezerrab@yahoo.com.br Notes ClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT04756713 Study sponsors: Brigham and Women''s Hospital Open in a new tab ANC: absolute neutrophil count; Cr: creatinine; CT: computed tomography; FIGO: International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics; GTN: gestational trophoblastic neoplasia; hCG: human chorionic gonadotropin; IU/L: international units per litre; LR‐NMGTN: low‐risk, non‐metastatic gestational trophoblastic neoplsia; PLT: platelet; RCT: randomised controlled trial; WBC: white blood count; WHO: World Health Organization Differences between protocol and review We made the following changes to the published protocol ( Boonyapipat 2022 ). We added "number of chemotherapy cycles to remission" as a secondary outcome for comparison 1, in addition to the prespecified outcomes, for a more comprehensive analysis. We included "any adverse events" as secondary outcomes in both comparison groups, in addition to the prespecified outcomes, for a more detailed analysis. We included "emergency hysterectomy in planned fertility‐sparing treatments" as a secondary outcome for both comparisons to provide a comprehensive analysis. We expanded the definitions of the following outcomes for a more comprehensive analysis: "Time from diagnosis to remission" now includes "time from randomisation, initiation of treatment, or diagnosis to remission." "Relapse" is defined as experiencing an elevation of hCG levels after achieving complete remission, measured by event rate or time‐to‐event (progression‐free survival ‐ PFS). "Disease‐specific mortality" is defined as events or time‐to‐event (disease‐specific survival or overall survival ‐ OS). "Any adverse events" is defined as any negative outcomes resulting from surgical intervention or chemotherapy. We considered any adverse events or severity grades 3 and 4, or both, as appropriate. We adjusted the summary of findings table to include the same outcomes for both comparisons, including treatment success rate, relapse, disease‐specific mortality, death due to treatment, pregnancy rate, quality of life, and any adverse events, as we considered these the most important outcomes. We provided more details and modified the risk of bias assessment to ensure consistency with the RoB2 guidance. In the Objectives section and elsewhere, we replaced the term ''efficacy and safety'' with ''benefits and harms'' to provide a more comprehensive and patient‐centred perspective when evaluating interventions. Contributions of authors SB conceived the idea, registered the title and wrote the first draft of the protocol. KN, NS, and TL helped to write the protocol. SB and KN searched for relevant trials and performed data extraction and risk of bias assessment. Disagreements were resolved by discussion with NS. SB and KN performed data analysis and GRADE assessment, and resolved any disagreements by discussion with TL. SB and KN drafted the full review and TL read and commented on the full review. All review authors checked the final version of the review. Sources of support Internal sources Faculty of Medicine, Prince of Songkla Univerity, Tha