INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1665,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12449903','Longitudinal Study of the Vascular Glycocalyx and Urinary Podocyte Markers During Pregnancy.','Garcia Valencia O, Suvakov S, Tu H, Dilmaghani D, Milic NM, Weissgerber TL, Dokic V, Gavrilovici P, Hatamova J, Miao J, White WM, Jayachandran M, Nath K, Garovic VD.','2025','proteoglycan podocin nephrin darkfield syndecan syndecan predate nephrin podocin syndecan nonstandard acronyms podocin nephrin pbr podocin nephrin darkfield syndecan podocin nephrin proteoglycans glycosaminoglycans podocytes nephrin podocin podocin nephrin podocytes preeclamptic flowing capiscope capillaroscopy devon diodes glycocheck rrid scr_018254 permeable pbr penetrated pbr pbr pbr venipuncture anticoagulated vacutainers syndecan heparan proteoglycan elisas diaclone besancon uscn echelon syndecan heparan proteoglycan milliliter unspun thawed nephrin podocin podocin nephrin cytometer oliguria pbr pbr syndecan heparan proteoglycan rrid scr_002865 wks gravidity wks pbr microns microns pbr microns microns microns micron pbr microns microns pbr microns pbr microns pbr pbr pbr pbr pbr pbr microns microns pbr microns microns pbr pbr syndecan syndecan heparan syndecan heparan podocin nephrin preeclamptic hdp podocin nephrin syndecan preeclamptic darkfield laminar hormonally syndecan preeclamptic midpregnancy pbr laminar pbr adaptative procoagulatory syndecan syndecan syndecan proteoglycan counteracted reparative syndecan endotheliosis preeclamptic syndecan darkfield syndecan r01hl tochukwu okwuosa referees rolnik nicolaides s1108 s1119 e237 e260 shennan hyett hadar divakar mcauliffe dadelszen eiland nzerue garovic saiki parashuram weissgerber milic phipps prasanna brima kuklina 0b013e3181a45b25 carlberg cluver hesse gandley damen fphys wiles bramham kurlak mistry piercy lightstone chappell weissgerber milic codsi cubro nath nath garovic e010647 brinkkoetter ising benzing nrneph garovic petrovic kalluri nephrin synaptopodin podocin gfl711 gilani jayachandran weissgerber zand milic vallapureddy naav weissgerber glycocheck rrid scr_018254 protocolsio be5ejg3e dane boels teeffelen mutsert heijer rosendaal vlag zonneveld e96477 vink oostenbrugge staals mulders nieuwdorp stroes vink sietsma vlahu lemkes struijk koopman krediet vink donati damiani domizi adrario pelaia microvasc mvr dane boels tiemeier avramut zonneveld vlag vink rabelink f956 f966 ajprenal dane khairoun eskens boels teeffelen rops vlag zonneveld lipowsky solutes microvasc mvr eskens mooij cleutjens cobelens vink vanteeffelen e55399 astapenko ticha tomasova hyspler zadak cerny hemorheol microcirc jayachandran lugo heiling lieske s13293 fillyaw pri kostidis tiemeier wmpj giera carmeliet rabelink glucobiosynthesis bentzer s13049 kiefer knabl martinoff schiessl conzen chappell gandley althouse jeyabalan bregand mcgonigal myerski gallaher hubel syndecan e0157608 bruegger conzen chappell stoeckelhuber welsch reichart mundel shankland strevens swensson ingemarsson willner endotheliosis','Abstract Background We aimed to assess the longitudinal changes of glycocalyx, a proteoglycan/glycoprotein layer that lines and protects endothelial cells in normotensive pregnancies (NPs); to correlate glycocalyx parameters with kidney injury markers (podocin‐positive to nephrin‐positive [P/N] ratio); and to explore changes in P/N ratio in preeclampsia. Methods Plasma and urine samples and glycocalyx measurements using side‐stream darkfield imaging were collected at each trimester from pregnant women (n=31). Plasma glycocalyx components were measured by ELISA, and urinary P/N‐positive extracellular vesicles by flow cytometry. Results In NPs (n=26), glycocalyx degradation increased from the first to the second trimester ( P =0.008) and returned to first‐trimester values in the third trimester ( P =0.008). Microvascular perfusion decreased from the first to the second trimester ( P =0.006) and increased back to first‐trimester values by the third trimester ( P =0.039). Plasma syndecan 1 level showed a steady increase from the first to the second trimester ( P <0.001) and further in the third trimester ( P <0.001); hyaluronic acid increased from the first to the second trimester ( P =0.016) without further changes. Glycocalyx degradation was associated with an increase in P/N ratio from the first to the second trimester of NPs and remained stable through the third trimester. P/N ratio strongly correlated with elevated syndecan 1 and hyaluronic acid levels in the third trimester. Comparison of P/N ratios between NP and preeclampsia (n=5) showed higher values in preeclampsia across all trimesters ( P =0.028). Conclusions Glycocalyx changes during NPs are associated with elevated urinary podocyte markers. Increase in P/N ratio across NPs, and further in preeclampsia, suggests that podocyte injury in preeclampsia is extension of physiological changes in pregnancy, which may predate its clinical presentation. Keywords: microcirculation, nephrin, perfused boundary region, podocin, preeclampsia, syndecan 1, vascular glycocalyx Subject Categories: Vascular Disease, Preeclampsia Nonstandard Abbreviations and Acronyms EV extracellular vesicle HA hyaluronic acid HELLP hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count NP normotensive pregnancy P/N podocin‐positive to nephrin‐positive PBR perfused boundary region Clinical Perspective. What Is New? We provide evidence of a correlation between glycocalyx damage and increased urinary podocyte markers during pregnancy. We provide an exploratory approach to the changes in podocin/nephrin urinary extracellular vesicle ratio in preeclampsia. What Are the Clinical Implications? The measurement of glycocalyx components and urinary podocyte markers could serve as valuable tools for monitoring endothelial and renal health during pregnancy. The use of noninvasive side‐stream darkfield imaging for glycocalyx assessment, along with biomarkers such as syndecan 1, hyaluronic acid, and the podocin/nephrin urinary extracellular vesicle ratio, should be further investigated for the early detection of endothelial dysfunction and kidney injury, as these measures may help identify women at risk for developing preeclampsia before the clinical syndrome develops. Preeclampsia, a pregnancy‐specific, complex, multisystem disorder, is defined as the development of hypertension after 20 weeks of gestational age, with either proteinuria or signs of end‐organ damage. 1 , 2 It affects 2% to 5% of pregnancies and remains one of the leading causes of maternal and perinatal morbidity and death worldwide. 3 , 4 Furthermore, a history of preeclampsia confers an increased risk for cardiovascular and renal complications later in life. 5 , 6 Despite ongoing research, there is an incomplete understanding of its underlying pathogenesis, and the incidence of preeclampsia has been increasing over the past few decades. 7 Endothelial dysfunction and endothelial cell activation have been increasingly recognized as key players in the development of preeclampsia. 8 , 9 The endothelial glycocalyx, a complex layer composed of proteoglycans, glycosaminoglycans, and glycoproteins lining the luminal surface of all endothelial cells, serves as a regulator of the interactions between the vascular endothelium and blood cells, preventing endothelial activation, i.e., leukocyte adhesion and platelet aggregation and resultant damage. We have previously shown that the degradation of the glycocalyx is associated with endothelial dysfunction and vascular injury in preeclampsia; 10 however, limited evidence exists regarding its changes during normal pregnancy and its relationship with kidney structure and function. An intact glycocalyx is necessary for the function and structure of podocytes, specialized epithelial cells that cover the glomerular capillaries and play a crucial role in maintaining the glomerular filtration barrier function. 11 , 12 We initially demonstrated that renal histologic expression of the podocyte‐specific protein, nephrin, but not podocin, is reduced in preeclampsia compared with normotensive pregnancies (NPs). 13 We subsequently demonstrated that renal expression of podocyte‐specific proteins is reflected in urinary extracellular vesicles (EVs), as urine from women with preeclampsia contained a higher ratio of podocin‐positive to nephrin‐positive (P/N) urinary EVs compared with NPs. 14 While it is well established that an intact glycocalyx is necessary for the function and structure of podocytes, their relationship has not been studied in the context of pregnancy and preeclampsia. This study assessed the longitudinal changes in the glycocalyx across trimesters in NP; correlated glycocalyx parameters with urinary podocyte markers, P/N ratio urinary EVs; and compared the longitudinal dynamics of the P/N ratio between NPs and preeclamptic pregnancies. Methods The authors declare that all supporting data are available within the article. Participants A prospective sample of nulliparous pregnant women was recruited through the Mayo Clinic Department of Obstetrics and Gynecology during their first prenatal appointments. Women were not eligible to participate in this study if they had smoked during pregnancy; had chronic health problems, such as hypertension, cardiovascular disease, or diabetes; or were regularly taking medications. The Mayo Clinic Institutional Review Board approved the study (No. 2104‐05). All participants provided written, informed consent before participation. Study Visits Participants attended 1 study visit per trimester (10–14, 22–26, and 34–38 weeks). Visits were scheduled in the morning and participants were asked to avoid caffeine on the morning of the test. Each visit included noninvasive glycocalyx measurements, a blood draw, and urine sample collection. Noninvasive Glycocalyx Measurements Measurements were performed as described previously 10 , 15 after a 10‐minute rest period. Briefly, images of red blood cells (RBCs) flowing through sublingual vessels were obtained by side‐stream dark field imaging (CapiScope Handheld Video Capillaroscopy System, KK Technologies, Devon, UK). The camera is placed under the tongue and uses green light‐emitting diodes to detect hemoglobin in RBCs. Participants were asked not to apply pressure to the tongue when holding the camera to maintain normal blood flow. Automated software (Glycocheck BV, Maastricht, the Netherlands; RRID:SCR_018254) recorded video clips when the image was stable and in focus. As described previously, 16 the software identified all measurable micro vessels <30 μm in diameter. The participant held the camera in 1 position while frames were recorded; then the camera was moved to a new location, and this process was repeated until data on 3000 vascular segments were obtained (1–5 minutes). Measurements were made every 10 μm along each vessel. For each vascular segment that was in focus and had sufficient blood flow for analysis, the program calculated the vessel diameter (median width of the RBC column) and the width of the portion of the glycocalyx that was permeable to RBCs (perfused boundary region [PBR]). When the glycocalyx was degraded, RBCs penetrated further into the glycocalyx leading to an increased PBR. PBR was calculated using validated methods. 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 Measurements were accurate to 0.05 μm. 16 This technique has been validated in humans and animals. 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 , 21 , 22 , 23 , 24 , 25 Day‐to‐day measurements were reproducible in pregnant women during the third trimester. 10 Glycocalyx measurements were obtained for 22 vessel size categories (median RBC column width, 3–25 μm). Based on our previous studies, we included a composite PBR measure for pregnant women that included vessels with a median RBC column width of 10 to 16 μm, which was shown to have the best day‐to‐day reliability among pregnant women. The program also assessed perfusion by calculating the percentage of vessels in each size category that were filled with RBCs. Each woman completed 3 trials, which then were averaged. Participants provided information on food and caffeine intake on the morning of the test and medication use for the preceding 24 hours. Blood Samples Blood samples obtained by venipuncture were drawn into EDTA anticoagulated vacutainers and then centrifuged at 2500 g for 15 minutes to obtain plasma. Plasma was stored at −80 °C. Concentrations of 3 vascular glycocalyx components, syndecan 1, heparan sulfate proteoglycan, and hyaluronic acid (HA), were measured in duplicate using commercially available ELISAs (Diaclone SAS, Besançon, France; USCN Life Science, Wuhan, China; Echelon Biosciences, Salt Lake City, UT). The averages of duplicate values of syndecan 1 (ng/mL), heparan sulfate proteoglycan (ng/mL), and HA (pg/mL) were used for analysis. Urine Samples Two‐milliliter aliquots of unspun urine samples from each participant were received on dry ice and immediately stored at −80 °C until analysis. All clinical identifiers were removed to ensure blinded analysis. Frozen samples were thawed in a 37 °C water bath for 5 minutes at the time of processing. Samples were stained with a predetermined set of antibodies for flow cytometric analysis. Each urine sample was stained for annexin‐V for an EV concentration check, and for podocyte‐specific proteins, including nephrin and podocin. Urinary podocin–nephrin‐positive EVs were measured using a flow cytometer and expressed as a P/N ratio. Detailed information on chemicals, reagents, and antibodies previously have been published. 26 Medical Record Review Participant characteristics and pregnancy outcomes were abstracted from medical records. Records were reviewed by 2 observers to confirm pregnancy diagnoses (V.D.G. and T.L.W.). Women were defined as normotensive if they were not diagnosed with any form of hypertensive pregnancy disorder. Diagnoses were based on established criteria. 3 Gestational hypertension was defined as new‐onset systolic blood pressure of ≥140 mm Hg or diastolic blood pressure of ≥90 mm Hg on at least 2 occasions, measured 4 hours apart, at or after 20 weeks'' gestation. Preeclampsia was diagnosed as gestational hypertension accompanied by ≥1 of the following conditions: proteinuria, defined as ≥300 mg of protein in a 24‐hour urine specimen or protein/creatinine ratio of 0.3, or 1+ (30 mg/L) dipstick urinalysis in the absence of urinary tract infection; evidence of other organ dysfunction, such as acute kidney injury (increase in serum creatinine by ≥0.3 mg/dL within 48 hours, 50% increase in serum creatinine or oliguria); thrombocytopenia <100 000/μL; and elevated liver function tests, aspartate aminotransferase and alanine transaminase (>2× upper limits of normal) or in the presence of pulmonary edema or cerebral or visual symptoms. Hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count (HELLP) syndrome was diagnosed according to the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists diagnostic criteria including lactate dehydrogenase ≥600 IU/L, aspartate aminotransferase and alanine transaminase elevated more than twice the upper limit of normal, and platelet count <100 000 cells/μL. Power Calculation We analyzed the data using a repeated‐measures ANOVA with time points (12–16, 24–28, and 34–38 weeks) and vessel size as within‐subject factors. We determined a minimum sample of 26 women with NPs to assess the trajectory of PBR changes in these women with an estimated effect size of f=0.26, α=0.05, and 80% power. Allowing for a 20% dropout rate, we sought to recruit 32 women. Statistical Analysis Summary statistics were calculated for patient demographics. Noninvasive glycocalyx measurements (RBC and PBR) in vessels with a median RBC column width of 5 to 25 μm were compared using repeated‐measures ANOVA, with vessel size (5–25 μm), and trimester as within‐subjects factors, adjusted for caffeine intake. Blood pressure levels were also compared across trimesters by repeated measures ANOVA. Concentrations of syndecan 1, heparan sulfate proteoglycan, and HA, and the P/N ratio across trimesters were compared using the Friedman test. 27 For post hoc analysis, the Wilcoxon test was used. Correlations between P/N ratio and other variables were identified using Pearson or Spearman correlation coefficients, according to the data distribution. Analyses were performed using SPSS for Windows (RRID:SCR_002865, version 26.0, IBM SPSS, Chicago, IL). Results Participant Characteristics Twenty‐six nulliparous women with normotensive pregnancies completed study visits in the first (12.0±1.2 weeks), second (24.0±1.3 weeks), and third (35.9±1.4 weeks) trimesters (Table 1 ). Five additional women who completed visits in each trimester subsequently developed preeclampsia (n=4) or HELLP syndrome (n=1). Most were White women, and all had never smoked (Table 1 ). Pregnancy outcomes in normotensive and hypertensive pregnancies are shown in Table 2 . Caffeine use was prevalent before all or some glycocalyx measurements in 42.3% of those with NPs and in 20% of women with hypertensive pregnancies (Table 1 ). Table 1. Baseline Clinical Characteristics of Study Participants Characteristic Normotensive pregnancy (n=26) Hypertensive pregnancy (n=5) Maternal age, y, mean±SD 30.2±3.6 28.7±1.6 Systolic blood pressure at delivery, mm Hg, mean±SD 113±13 145±9 Urinary protein: creatinine ratio at delivery, mg/g, median (IQR) N/A 2.21 (0.4–6.6) Gestational age, wks, mean±SD First trimester 12.0±1.2 11.8±1.3 Second trimester 24.0±1.3 23.4±0.9 Third trimester 35.9±1.4 35.4±1.1 Race, n (%) White 24 (92.3) 4 (80.0) Missing/not disclosed 2 (7.7) 1 (20.0) Smoking, n (%) Never 24 (92.3) 5 (100.0) Former 2 (7.7) 0 Gravidity, n (%) 1 25 (96.2) 5 (100.0) 2 1 (3.8) 0 Parity, n (%) Nulliparous 26 (100) 5 (100.0) Caffeine use, n (%) In all measurements 2 (7.7) 0 (0) In some measurements 9 (34.6) 3 (20) Open in a new tab IQR indicates interquartile range; and N/A, not applicable. Table 2. Pregnancy Outcomes Characteristic Normotensive pregnancy (n=26) Hypertensive pregnancy (n=5) Gestational age at delivery, wks 40±1 39±1 Fetal sex, * n (%) Female 12 (48.0) 2 (40.0) Male 13 (52.0) 3 (60.0) Birth weight, g, mean±SD 3455±445 3316±541 Open in a new tab * Data missing for 1 participant. Effect of Pregnancy on Blood Pressure Systolic and diastolic blood pressures differed significantly across the trimesters (Table 3 ). Both systolic and diastolic blood pressures increased in the third trimester (F=5.814, P =0.009; and F=5.019, P =0.011 versus second trimester, respectively). Table 3. Changes in Systolic and Diastolic Blood Pressures Across Normotensive Pregnancy Blood pressure, mm Hg Pregnancy trimester F statistic P value First Second Third Systolic 104±11 102±8 108±8 5.814 0.009 Diastolic 64±7 62±7 66±8 5.019 0.011 Open in a new tab Values are presented as mean±SD for 26 participants. Effect of Pregnancy on Glycocalyx Measurements Vessel Size The median width of vessels did not differ significantly across trimesters (median RBC column width [P50], F=1.173, df=2, P =0.318). Glycocalyx Degradation The width of the PBR is reported for vessels of different sizes, with the median width of the perfused region diameter ranging between 5 and 25 microns. The machine automatically reported the median values for vessels in different size categories (5–25, 5–9, 10–19, and 20–25 microns). Values for each trimester in women with normotensive pregnancies are shown in Table 4 . Glycocalyx degradation, measured by PBR width, showed significant differences across some size categories (10–16 microns and 10–19 microns), but not others (5–9, 20–25, 5–25 microns). In the 10‐ to 19‐micron size, the PBR increased in the second trimester (F=8.003, df=1, P =0.009 versus first trimester) and decreased in the third trimester (F=9.600, df=1, P =0.005 versus second trimester) (Table 4 ). In addition to the analyses that are automatically reported by the software, we also calculated the median diameter for vessels with a median perfused diameter of 10 to 16 microns. Our third‐trimester study showed that day‐to‐day reliability of measurements was highest for vessels in this size range, whereas vessels with a median perfused diameter >18 microns were uncommon in pregnant women in the third trimester. A similar pattern as for PBR 10–19 microns was observed for PBR 10 to 16 microns (first versus second trimester: F=8.284, df=1, P =0.008; second versus third trimester: F=8.326, df=1, P =0.008). Table 4. Changes in Glycocalyx Degradation, Microvascular Perfusion, and Vessel Size Across Normotensive Pregnancies Pregnancy trimester F statistic P value First Second Third Glycocalyx degradation Median P50, μm 8.39±0.44 8.45±0.79 8.63±0.80 1.173 0.318 PBR 10–16, μm 2.50±0.22 2.62±0.27 2.47±0.23 5.774 0.006 PBR 5–25, μm 2.42±0.24 2.53±0.24 2.43±0.20 2.942 0.062 PBR 5–9, μm 1.22±0.10 1.24±0.09 1.20±0.08 2.520 0.091 PBR 10–19, μm 2.67±0.26 2.82±0.28 2.66±0.25 6.117 0.004 PBR 20–25, μm 3.08±0.44 3.24±0.52 3.23±0.40 0.175 0.840 Microvascular perfusion RBC filling percentage of 10–16 μm vessels 0.62±0.06 0.58±0.07 0.60±0.06 4.166 0.021 RBC filling percentage of 5–25 μm vessels 0.57±0.06 0.54±0.07 0.56±0.05 3.527 0.037 Open in a new tab Values are presented as mean±SD (standard deviation) for 26 participants. PBR indicates perfused boundary region; and RBC, red blood cell. P50, median RBC width. Microvascular Perfusion Microvascular perfusion was measured as the percentage of vessels that were perfused with RBCs >50% of the time. The proportion of vessels filled with RBCs for vessel sizes of 10 to 16 microns differed significantly across trimesters (Table 4 ). Compared with the first trimester, microvascular perfusion decreased significantly in the second trimester (F=9.096, df=1, P =0.006 versus first trimester) and increased in the third trimester (F=4.786, df=1, P =0.039 versus second trimester). A similar pattern was observed for RBC perfusion for vessel size 5 to 25 microns (first versus second trimester: F=7.606, df=1, P =0.011; second versus third trimester: F=4.482, df=1, P =0.045). Overall, the second‐trimester blood pressure nadir coincided with glycocalyx thinning (increase in PBR, 10–16 microns) and decrease in microvascular perfusion for vessels between 10 and 16 microns (Figure 1 ). Figure 1. The second‐trimester blood pressure nadir coincided with glycocalyx thinning (increase in PBR) and decrease in microvascular perfusion. Open in a new tab PBR indicates perfused boundary region; RBC, red blood cells; and SBP, systolic blood pressure. Effect of Pregnancy on Circulating Glycocalyx Components Circulating glycocalyx components, measured by syndecan 1 and hyaluronic acid, differed significantly across the trimesters (Table 5 ). Syndecan 1 increased in the second trimester (Z=3.680, P <0.001 versus first trimester) and further increased in the third trimester (Z=3.549, P <0.001 versus second trimester). Hyaluronic acid increased in the second trimester (Z=2.417, P =0.016 versus first trimester) without any further increase in the third trimester (Z=0.588, P =0.0557 versus second trimester). There were no changes in the levels of heparan sulfate throughout the course of normotensive pregnancies (Table 5 ). Table 5. Changes in Circulating Glycocalyx Components Across Normotensive Pregnancies Circulating glycocalyx components Pregnancy trimesters χ 2 P value First Second Third Syndecan‐1 ng/mL 135 (100–148) 613 (454–689) 1351 (982–2209) 32.111 <0.001 Hyaluronic acid, ng/mL 79 (46–102) 118 (81–175) 99 (87–134) 6.778 0.034 Heparan sulfate, pg/mL 2386 (1826–3213) 2044 (1760–2592) 2515 (1881–3158) 0.444 0.801 Open in a new tab Values are presented as median with 25th–75th percentile for 18 participants. Effect of Pregnancy on P/N Ratio Urinary P/N ratio differed significantly across the trimesters (Table 6 ). The ratio increased in the second trimester (F=8.557, df=1, P =0.009 versus first trimester) without any further increase in the third trimester (F=0.892, df=1, P =0.358 versus second trimester). Table 6. Changes in Urinary P/N Ratio Across Normotensive Pregnancy Urine Pregnancy trimesters F statistic P value First Second Third P/N ratio 2.39 (1.68–3.10) 3.92 (2.30–5.55) 6.92 (1.56–12.29) 8.567 0.009 Open in a new tab Values are presented as mean with 95% CI for 18 participants. P/N indicates podocin‐positive to nephrin‐positive. Effect of NP vs preeclampsia on P/N ratio Urinary P/N differed significantly by showing an upward trend across the trimesters in preeclamptic pregnancies, in contrast to NPs ( P =0.028; Figure 2 ). Figure 2. Longitudinal trajectories of the P/N ratio across gestational trimesters. Open in a new tab HDP indicates hypertensive disorders of pregnancy (preeclampsia and HELLP [hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelet count]); and P/N, podocin‐positive to nephrin‐positive. Association of P/N Ratio With Noninvasive and Circulating Glycocalyx Components The P/N ratio was significantly correlated with syndecan 1 (rho=0.517, P =0.020) and HA (rho=0.557, P =0.011). The P/N ratio did not correlate with any glycocalyx imaging measurements ( P >0.05) in any trimester. Discussion Our study presents several novel findings regarding the longitudinal changes of the glycocalyx in NP, and urinary podocyte markers in NPs versus preeclamptic pregnancies. First, using noninvasive side‐stream darkfield imaging, we showed increased glycocalyx degradation and decreased microvascular perfusion in the second trimester, in some vessel size categories compared with both first and third trimesters in NPs. These imaging changes coincided with the previously described and expected second‐trimester blood pressure nadir. We postulate that these changes are interrelated and that a drop in blood pressure leads to a decrease in shear stress, and consequent reduction in glycocalyx thickness. Positive effects of the laminar shear stress on the thickness of the glycocalyx have been previously documented. 28 However, additional mechanisms could play a role including hormonally mediated vasodilation. Similarly, a decrease in microvascular perfusion is blood pressure dependent, and the reduction in microvascular perfusion in the second trimester coincides with the blood pressure nadir. Second, glycocalyx degradation was further confirmed by the plasma levels of glycocalyx components (syndecan 1, HA) that were increased in the second compared with the first trimester. Third, the association between glycocalyx degradation and podocyte damage was inferred by the positive correlation between the circulating glycocalyx components and urinary P/N EV ratio. Fourth, in our exploratory analysis we found that the P/N ratio was elevated in preeclamptic pregnancies compared with NPs across gestation, that is, in all 3 trimester measures. Taken together, our results indicate significant degradation of the glycocalyx during the second trimester of NPs. This degradation likely reflects a physiological adaptation to increased blood volume and cardiac output during midpregnancy. The restoration of the glycocalyx, indicated by the normalization of PBR in the third trimester, is associated with a rise in blood pressure and increased laminar shear stress, which may lead to glycocalyx thickening. We have previously shown that, at the time of delivery, women with early‐onset preeclampsia (<34 weeks of gestation) had a higher PBR compared with NPs. 10 This could potentially be explained by a dysregulation of this adaptative mechanism, likely precipitated by preeclampsia‐associated systemic inflammation, which exerts negative effects on glycocalyx homeostasis and thickness. 29 The dynamic change observed in NPs highlights the glycocalyx adaptability in response to physiological stressors and the importance of endothelial health in maintaining vascular stability during pregnancy. Finally, our findings indicate that previously reported podocyte injury in preeclampsia could be an extension of physiological changes in pregnancy. This is similar to other physiological processes that are exaggerated in pregnancy, such as procoagulatory and proinflammatory states and oxidative stress, which are further exacerbated in preeclampsia. The endothelial glycocalyx plays a fundamental role in maintaining vascular homeostasis and protecting the endothelium from mechanical and biochemical stress. During pregnancy, the glycocalyx undergoes dynamic changes to accommodate increased hemodynamic demands and to facilitate nutrient exchange to the fetus. Our finding of a steady syndecan 1 increase across trimesters is consistent with previous reports 30 , 31 of considerably elevated syndecan 1 serum concentrations during NP. Syndecan 1, a major transmembrane protein and a core component of the glycocalyx, serves as a regulator of diverse cell behaviors and response to stress. The shedding of this proteoglycan from the endothelial surface into the bloodstream is considered a marker of glycocalyx degradation. 32 Similarly, the observed increase in plasma HA levels from the first to the second trimester further supports the occurrence of glycocalyx degradation. HA, another critical glycocalyx component, contributes to the gel‐like properties of the endothelial surface layer, modulating vascular permeability and shear stress. The lack of further significant changes in HA levels in the third trimester may suggest that the initial increase reaches a steady state or is counteracted by reparative mechanisms. Podocyte injury, measured by the urinary P/N ratio, has a causal relationship with proteinuria and subsequent kidney injury. 11 , 33 We found an increase in the P/N ratio from the first to the second trimester in NP suggesting a transient podocyte stress or injury that persists through the third trimester. The strong correlation between the P/N ratio and plasma levels of syndecan 1 and HA in the third trimester highlights a potential mechanistic link between endothelial glycocalyx integrity and podocyte health. It is particularly intriguing to postulate that glycocalyx degradation–podocyte subclinical injury may mechanistically contribute to the increase in proteinuria that occurs in NPs compared with the nonpregnant state, as well as to unexpected findings of glomerular endotheliosis, a hallmark of preeclampsia, in the renal biopsies of women with NP. 34 Our findings need to be confirmed in a larger number of patients, while its mechanistic aspects require preclinical studies. We were not able to compare glycocalyx measures between NPs and preeclamptic pregnancies due to a relatively small sample and heterogeneous clinical presentation (both preeclampsia and its subclass, HELLP syndrome) in the latter group. We compared the trajectory of the P/N ratios, as all women in the preeclampsia group demonstrated proteinuria. The elevated P/N ratio in preeclampsia, even in the first trimester, suggests that podocyte injury may precede the clinical onset of preeclampsia. However, adequately powered, confirmatory studies are needed. If confirmed, this early marker could be critical for identifying women at risk of developing preeclampsia, allowing for closer monitoring and early intervention. The association between high P/N ratios and elevated plasma syndecan 1 levels in preeclampsia further supports the role of glycocalyx degradation in preeclampsia pathogenesis. The increased P/N ratio in preeclampsia could represent a more severe podocyte injury and is consistent with the known pathophysiology of preeclampsia, where endothelial dysfunction contributes to the loss of glomerular filtration barrier integrity. Our data indicate that an additive role of glycocalyx dysregulation may be an understudied mechanism that should be explored in future studies. The findings from this study could have important clinical implications if they are confirmed and extended in larger studies. First, the measurement of glycocalyx components and urinary podocyte markers could serve as valuable tools for monitoring endothelial and renal health during pregnancy. Second, the use of noninvasive side‐stream darkfield imaging for glycocalyx assessment, along with biomarkers such as syndecan 1, HA, and the P/N ratio, could help in the early detection of endothelial dysfunction and kidney injury. This is particularly relevant for identifying women at risk of developing preeclampsia, where early intervention could potentially prevent the progression of the disease. The major limitation of our study is its small sample size, particularly with respect to preeclampsia cases. Larger studies are needed to validate the findings of this study and to explore the variability of glycocalyx and podocyte marker responses across different forms of preeclampsia (early versus late; preeclampsia versus HELLP versus eclampsia versus preeclampsia superimposed on chronic hypertension). Such studies could help establish reference ranges for these biomarkers and improve their utility in clinical practice. Future studies also should focus on the underlying mechanisms of glycocalyx degradation. Understanding the molecular pathways involved in glycocalyx shedding and restoration could lead to the development of therapeutic interventions aimed at preserving glycocalyx integrity. Such interventions could include pharmacological agents that stabilize or promote glycocalyx repair, as well as lifestyle and dietary modifications that support endothelial health. In conclusion, this study provides novel insights into the dynamic changes of the glycocalyx during normotensive pregnancies and highlights the potential link between glycocalyx degradation and podocyte injury. Continued research in this area is essential for advancing our understanding of the pathophysiological mechanisms underlying pregnancy‐related vascular and renal complications, and for developing effective strategies to improve maternal and fetal outcomes. Sources of Funding R01HL 136 348 (V.D.G.). Disclosures None. This manuscript was sent to Tochukwu M. Okwuosa, DO, Associate Editor, for review by expert referees, editorial decision, and final disposition. For Sources of Funding and Disclosures, see page 8. References 1. Rolnik DL, Nicolaides KH, Poon LC. Prevention of preeclampsia with aspirin. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2022;226:S1108–S1119. doi: 10.1016/j.ajog.2020.08.045 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists . Gestational hypertension and preeclampsia: ACOG practice bulletin, number 222. Obstet Gynecol. 2020;135:e237–e260. doi: 10.1097/aog.0000000000003891 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Poon LC, Shennan A, Hyett JA, Kapur A, Hadar E, Divakar H, McAuliffe F, da Silva CF, von Dadelszen P, McIntyre HD, et al. The International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) initiative on pre‐eclampsia: a pragmatic guide for first‐trimester screening and prevention. Int J Gynaecol Obstet. 2019;145:1–33. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.12802 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Eiland E, Nzerue C, Faulkner M. Preeclampsia 2012. J Pregnancy. 2012;2012:586578. doi: 10.1155/2012/586578 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Garovic VD, White WM, Vaughan L, Saiki M, Parashuram S, Garcia‐Valencia O, Weissgerber TL, Milic N, Weaver A, Mielke MM. Incidence and long‐term outcomes of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. J Am Coll Cardiol. 2020;75:2323–2334. doi: 10.1016/j.jacc.2020.03.028 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Phipps E, Prasanna D, Brima W, Jim B. Preeclampsia: updates in pathogenesis, definitions, and guidelines. Clin J Am Soc Nephrol. 2016;11:1102–1113. doi: 10.2215/cjn.12081115 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Kuklina EV, Ayala C, Callaghan WM. Hypertensive disorders and severe obstetric morbidity in the United States. Obstet Gynecol. 2009;113:1299–1306. doi: 10.1