INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1671,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12447911','Landscape of genomic alterations and clinical outcomes in low-grade serous ovarian cancer in Korea.','Shim YS, Kim JH, Seo SS, Kang S, Park SY, Lim MC.','2025','lgsoc lgsoc kuroki guntupalli grisham cheasley binimetinib gershenson fader gershenson hohn nextseq 550dx fanca ncc2024 unconfirmed multigene nm_007294 nm_000059 hg19 map2k1 raf1 lpv lpv v600k g464v g12v g12d g12r a146v q61l q948 g12v k541efs lgsoc lgsoc elnaggar grisham gershenson nasioudis geist musacchio gershenson eutrope grisham grisham gershenson gershenson emb gershenson gershenson einaggar elnaggar interrogating nasioudis nasioudis geist geist musacchio musacchio hrr folr1 lgsoc lgsoc map2k1 raf1 milo binimetinib gershenson frα lgsoc lgsoc frα geist frα folr1 lgsoc frα mirvetuximab soravtansine gershenson gershenson yss jhk jhk yss sbk sss syp seon sokbom myong cheol ncc2024 laversanne cheasley kobel hoenisch s41698 elnaggar baca arguello arend fader gershenson s195 gershenson bodurka malpica gershenson westin eyada gershenson carty pothuri grisham s11864 grisham kalbacher milo ov11 binimetinib hohn brambs hiller schmoeckel geburtshilfe frauenheilkd kuroki guntupalli m3773 berchuck backes grisham leath geist gordhandas iasonos geist iasonos selenica stallworth machida grubbs gershenson grisham kalbacher milo ov11 binimetinib musacchio califano bartoletti arenare losito mito mito s41416 ishibashi katagiri ijms17050625 nasioudis westin samec karnezis toboni folr1 capitalizes liabilities aal5148 s13000','Highlights • Molecular profiling of LGSOC in a Korean cohort revealed KRAS (8.7 %) and BRAF (7.5 %) as the most common mutations. • KRAS and BRAF mutation frequencies were lower than those reported in Western cohorts, suggesting regional variation. • BRCA1/2 mutations were detected in 6.4 % of patients, with limited HRD testing available in this cohort. • The 5-year overall survival rate was 85.9 %, with a median PFS of 32.7 months. Keywords: Ovarian cancer, Hormone therapy, KRAS, BRAF Abstract Introduction To investigate the clinical characteristics, molecular findings, and survival outcomes of patients with low-grade serous ovarian cancer in Korea, with a focus on addressing the limited data on genetic profiling and targeted therapies in Asian populations. Methods This study is a retrospective review of patients with pathologically confirmed LGSOC treated at the National Cancer Center Korea between 2010 and 2023. Patients underwent cytoreductive surgery and/or systemic or hormonal therapy. Molecular profiling included next-generation sequencing (NGS) of formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded tumor samples and homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) testing. Survival outcomes were assessed using Kaplan–Meier analysis. Results A total of 62 patients were included, with a median age of 41 years; 61.3 % presented with advanced-stage disease. BRCA mutations were detected in 6.4 % ( BRCA1 : 4.8 %, BRCA2 : 1.6 %). MAPK pathway alterations were identified in KRAS (11.3 %), BRAF (9.7 %), NF1 (3.7 %), and NRAS (1.2 %). Among BRAF mutations, 66.7 % were V600E, while KRAS mutations predominantly involved codon 12. ER and PR were positive in 88.7 % and 66.1 % of cases, respectively. With a median follow-up of 47.5 months, the median progression-free survival (PFS) was 169.3 months (95 % CI: 60.6–NA). Conclusion This study identifies frequent MAPK pathway mutations, including KRAS and BRAF , and a prevalence of BRCA1/2 mutations in low-grade serous ovarian cancer. Compared to Western cohorts, KRAS and BRAF mutations were observed at relatively lower frequencies, highlighting potential regional differences in the molecular landscape of low-grade serous ovarian cancer. 1. Introduction Ovarian cancer is one of the most common and deadly cancers affecting women worldwide. According to GLOBOCAN 2022 data, there were an estimated 324,398 new cases and 206,839 deaths globally, representing 3.4 % of all cancers and 4.8 % of cancer-related deaths in women ( Bray et al., 2024 ). Ovarian cancer is predominantly diagnosed at advanced stages, frequently due to the absence of specific early symptoms, resulting in a relatively low 5-year survival rate of approximately 51 % ( Liu et al., 2024 , Kuroki and Guntupalli, 2020 ). High-grade serous carcinoma is the most common histological subtype of ovarian cancer, while low-grade serous ovarian cancer is a rarer entity, accounting for less than 5 % of epithelial ovarian cancers. Low-grade serous ovarian cancer is often diagnosed in younger women and is characterized by its slow progression and relatively favorable prognosis compared to HGSC ( Grisham and Iyer, 2018 ). Unlike HGSC, which is commonly associated with TP53 mutations, Low-grade serous ovarian cancer exhibits a distinct molecular profile, including frequent mutations in the MAPK pathway genes such as KRAS, BRAF, and, less commonly, NRAS ( Cheasley et al., 2022 ). Targeted therapies, including MEK inhibitors like trametinib and binimetinib, are being investigated to improve outcomes and address chemotherapy resistance ( Gershenson et al., 2022 , Monk et al., 2020 ). Due to its intrinsic resistance to conventional chemotherapy, neoadjuvant chemotherapy is generally not recommended for low-grade serous ovarian cancer. Current treatment strategies often involve maximal cytoreductive surgery followed by maintenance hormonal therapy. Notably, randomized controlled trials are under investigation to evaluate the efficacy of adjuvant hormonal therapy with letrozole compared to platinum-based chemotherapy followed by hormonal maintenance therapy ( Fader and Gershenson, 2023 ). Despite advances in understanding the molecular landscape of low-grade serous ovarian cancer, studies focusing on genetic profiling and targeted therapies in Asian populations remain limited. This study aims to investigate the clinical characteristics, molecular findings, and survival outcomes of low-grade serous ovarian cancer patients in Korea. 2. Methods 2.1. Study design and participants This study is a retrospective, single-center study conducted at National Cancer Center Korea, involving patients diagnosed with low-grade serous ovarian cancer between March 2010 and December 2023. All included cases were independently reviewed to confirm the diagnosis of low-grade serous ovarian carcinoma. This review incorporated the 2020 WHO Classification of Female Genital Tumors criteria, including histological features and immunohistochemical staining patterns ( Hohn et al., 2021 ). Sequencing was conducted on an Illumina NextSeq 550Dx platform (Illumina, San Diego, CA, USA). We utilized a validated in-house NGS panel covering 161 cancer-related genes, encompassing pathways involved in cell signaling (e.g., MAPK , PI3K-AKT ), DNA repair (e.g., BRCA1/2, ATM, FANCA ), chromatin remodeling (e.g., ARID1A, SMARCA4 ), and others. Variant calling and annotation were performed using a validated bioinformatics pipeline, and pathogenicity was interpreted according to ACMG/AMP guidelines. Specifically, all tumors demonstrated WT1 positivity and wild-type p53 staining patterns; cases showing aberrant p53 expression (either diffuse overexpression or complete absence) were excluded to rule out misclassification as high-grade serous carcinoma. In cases diagnosed prior to 2014, pathology was re-evaluated to ensure consistency with current diagnostic standards. Clinical and molecular data were collected from patients’ medical records and institutional databases. This study was approved by the Institutional Review Board of National Cancer Center Korea (NCC2024-0034) and was conducted in accordance with the Declaration of Helsinki. The requirement for informed consent was waived. Patients were included if they: (1) had a confirmed diagnosis of International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO) 2014 stage I-IV low-grade serous ovarian cancer, (2) underwent cytoreductive surgery and/or received systemic or hormonal therapy at our institution, (3) had clinical, surgical, follow-up, and next-generation sequencing (NGS)–based molecular data available. Patients with unconfirmed diagnoses, incomplete clinical records, or those who underwent only BRCA1/2 testing without multigene NGS were excluded. All included tumors showed morphologic and immunophenotypic features consistent with low-grade serous carcinoma, and cases with borderline or ambiguous histology were excluded. 2.2. Data collection Clinicopathologic and treatment data were collected through a detailed review of medical records, including FIGO 2014 stage, serum CA-125 levels at diagnosis, timing and details of cytoreductive surgery, adjuvant and/or maintenance therapy regimens, and previous history of serous borderline tumor. Postoperative residual disease was categorized into three groups: no gross residual disease, less than 1 cm, and greater than or equal to 1 cm. Patients’ genomic and molecular data were collected including the overall BRCA mutation status, annotated at the genomic level using the transcripts NM_007294.3 for BRCA1 and NM_000059.3 for BRCA2 based on the Human Genome hg19 reference. NGS of tumor samples obtained from formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded (FFPE) tissues was performed, with a focus on identifying mutations in genes involved in the MAPK pathway. Additionally, immunohistochemical staining was conducted to evaluate the expression of estrogen receptor (ER), and progesterone receptor (PR). The results of homologous recombination deficiency (HRD) testing were also reviewed to further characterize the molecular profile. 2.3. Statistical analysis Clinical and molecular characteristics were summarized using descriptive statistics to characterize the patient population. Progression-free survival was defined as the time from the first diagnosis to the first observation of disease recurrence or the date of last follow-up or death from any cause. Overall survival was defined as the time from the first diagnosis to the date of last follow-up or death from any cause. The median follow-up time was estimated using the reverse Kaplan-Meier method, and survival curves were generated accordingly. All statistical analyses were conducted using R (version 4.2.1, R Foundation for Statistical Computing, Vienna, Austria). In accordance with the journal’s guidelines, we will provide our data for independent analysis by a selected team by the Editorial Team for the purposes of additional data analysis or for the reproducibility of this study in other centers if such is requested. 3. Results Between March 2010 and December 2023, 62 patients with pathologically confirmed low-grade serous ovarian cancer were identified at the National Cancer Center Korea, accounting for approximately 3.9 % (62/1,581) of all serous ovarian cancer cases diagnosed during the same period. The clinical and demographic characteristics of patients are shown in Table 1 . Table 1. Clinical and demographic characteristics. N = 62 (N, %) Age (years) Median 41 (16–84) CA125 at diagnosis Median 414.5 (6.5–10610) FIGO stage at diagnosis I 10 (16.1) II 14 (22.6) III 25 (40.3) IV 13 (21.0) Timing of surgery Upfront 51 (82.3) Interval 10 (16.1) No surgery 1 (1.6) Postoperative residual disease (missing = 3) No gross residual disease 49 (83.1) <1 cm 9 (14.3) ≥1 cm 1 (1.6) Frontline chemotherapy No adjuvant chemotherapy 5 (8.1) Platinum 57 (91.9) Non-platinum 0 (0) Maintenance therapy (missing = 1) No maintenance therapy 36 (58.1) Bevacizumab 8 (12.9) PARP inhibitor 6 (9.7) Hormonal therapy 12 (19.4) Prior serous borderline tumor No 55 (88.7) Yes 7 (11.3) Open in a new tab The median age at diagnosis was 41 years (range 16–84), and the median CA125 level at diagnosis was 414.5 (range 6.5–10610). Most patients (61.3 %, n = 38) presented with advanced-stage disease with 40.3 % (n = 25) at stage III and 21.0 % (n = 13) at stage IV. Among the 62 patients, 82.3 % (n = 49) underwent upfront surgery, while 16.1 % (n = 10) had surgery following neoadjuvant chemotherapy. Only one patient (1.6 %) did not undergo surgery. Postoperative residual disease was documented as no gross residual disease in 83.1 % (n = 49), residual disease less than 1 cm in 14.3 % (n = 9), and ≥1 cm in 1.6 % (n = 1). Most patients (91.9 %, n = 57) received platinum-based adjuvant chemotherapy, while 8.1 % (n = 5) did not receive adjuvant chemotherapy, and 1.6 % (n = 1) received non-platinum chemotherapy. Hormonal maintenance therapy was administered in 19.4 % (n = 12) of patients, whereas 58.1 % (n = 36) did not receive maintenance therapy. A history of serous borderline tumor was identified in 11.3 % (n = 7) of patients. Molecular profiling results are summarized in Table 2 . BRCA mutations were identified in 6.4 % of patients, with BRCA1 pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants in 4.8 % (n = 3) and BRCA2 pathogenic or likely pathogenic variants in 1.6 % (n = 1). All four patients with BRCA1/2 mutations underwent both germline and somatic testing, and in each case, the same pathogenic variant was identified in both assays, confirming the germline origin of the mutation. MAPK pathway-related mutations included KRAS in 11.3 % (n = 7), BRAF in 9.7 % (n = 6), NF1 in 1.6 % (n = 1), and NRAS in 1.6 % (n = 1). No mutations were detected in MAP2K1 or RAF1 . Immunohistochemistry revealed ER positivity in 88.7 % (n = 55) and PR positivity in 66.1 % (n = 41). HRD testing was performed in 12 patients, with 0 patients (0 %) showing positive results and 12 patients (100 %) testing negative. The median genomic instability score among tested patients was 11 (IQR 8). Table 2. Frequency of BRCA1/2 mutation and MAPK pathway-altered gene mutation, and HRD, ER, PR Immunohistochemistry. N (%) Overall BRCA mutation status Wild-type/VUS 58 (93.6) BRCA1 PV/LPV 3 (4.8) BRCA2 PV/LPV 1 (1.6) Germline 4 (6.4) Somatic 0 (0) MAPK pathway gene mutation KRAS 7 (11.3) NRAS 1 (1.6) BRAF 6 (9.7) NF1 1 (1.6) Immunohistochemistry ER 55 (88.7) PR 41 (66.1) HRD (Not tested = 50) positive 0 (0) negative 12 (100) Genomic Instability Score Median (IQR) 11 (8) Open in a new tab The molecular details of the gene alterations are summarized in Supplementary Table 1. Among BRAF mutations (n = 6), 66.7 % (n = 4/6) were V600E, while the remaining variants included V600K (n = 1) and G464V (n = 1). KRAS mutations were identified in 7 patients, predominantly affecting codon 12: G12V in 42.9 % (n = 3/7), G12D in 28.6 % (n = 2/7), G12R in 14.3 % (n = 1/7), and A146V in 14.3 % (n = 1/7). An NRAS mutation (Q61L) was identified in one patient, and a NF1 mutation (Q948*). Notably, one patient harbored concurrent KRAS (G12V) and PMS2 (K541Efs*3) mutations. The survival analysis was performed on 62 patients ( Fig. 1 ). With a median follow-up of 47.5 months, the median progression-free survival (PFS) was 169.3 months (95 % CI: 60.6–NA), and the median overall survival (OS) was not reached. The 5-year overall survival rate was 85.9 %, as estimated by the Kaplan–Meier method. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Progression-free survival and overall survival. 4. Discussion The current study provides detailed analysis of genetic alterations in 62 patients with low-grade serous ovarian cancer at a single Asian institution, ensuring consistency in genetic testing methods. During the study period, LGSOC accounted for approximately 3.9 % of epithelial ovarian cancers at our center, in line with the 4.66 % incidence reported in a US Surveillance, Epidemiology, and End Results (SEER) database ( Matsuo et al., 2018 ). Given that LGSOC accounted for 3.9 % of all epithelial ovarian cancer cases at our center, the rarity of this subtype remains a challenge for genomic characterization, particularly in Asian populations where national registries often do not differentiate low- and high-grade histology. In our cohort, KRAS mutations (11.3 %) were the most common MAPK pathway alteration, followed by BRAF (9.7 %), NF1 (1.6 %), and NRAS (1.6 %) mutations. The frequency of BRAF mutations was broadly consistent with previous studies (ranging from 1.5 % to 11 %), whereas KRAS mutations, though still the most prevalent alteration, were observed at rates notably lower than those reported in Western cohorts, which range from approximately 12 % to 33 % ( Table 3 ) ( ElNaggar et al., 2022 , Grisham et al., 2023 , Gershenson et al., 2022 , Nasioudis et al., 2023 , Manning-Geist et al., 2022 , Musacchio et al., 2022 , Gershenson et al., 2022 ). Table 3. Summary of previous studies about low-grade serous ovarian cancer. No N Country Authors Year Study design Outcome measurement Proportion of BRAF alteration Proportion of KRAS alteration Proportion of NRAS alteration Proportion of NF1 alteration 1 341 USA, Canada, Australia, Eutrope Grisham et al. ( Grisham et al., 2023 ) 2023 Post-hoc analysis of RCT Response rate by MAPK pathway related gene V600E 5.9 % 33 % 8.10 % N/A 2 215 USA Gershenson et al. ( Gershenson et al., 2022 ) 2009–2020 Retrospective study NGS information ER, PR, PD-L1, EMB 52.60 % 3 260 USA, UK Gershenson et al. ( Gershenson et al., 2022 ) 2014–2018 RCT Progression-free survival, adverse event 1.50 % 12 % 4 % N/A 4 153 USA EINaggar et al. ( ElNaggar et al., 2022 ) 2013–2017 Retrospective study NGS, fusion gene analysis interrogating RNA, fragment analysis, in situ hybridization, IHC (ER, PR, PD-L1), TMB 6.60 % 23.70 % 11.20 % 7.90 % 5 324 USA, Canada Nasioudis et al. ( Nasioudis et al., 2023 ) 2017–2022 Retrospective study NGS database analysis (panel sequencing, MAPK/ERK pathway gene) 8 % 29 % 8 % 3 % 6 119 USA Manning-Geist et al. ( Manning-Geist et al., 2022 ) 2015–2021 Retrospective study NGS, OS of MAPK pathway driven low-grade serous ovarian cancer compared with lacking alteration 11 % 33 % 11 % 2 % 7 128 Italy Musacchio et al. ( Musacchio et al., 2022 ) 1996–2019 Retrospective study NGS (HRR-pathway gene, MAPK pathway gene, Endocrine Resistance pathway gene), MSI, TMB, PFS, OS 11 % 22 % 14 % 13 % 8 281 USA Rushton et al. ( Rushton et al., 2024 ) 2024 Retrospective study NGS panel (592 genes) + WES; FOLR1 IHC 9.3 % 22.4 % 7.1 % N/A 9 10 Japan Nakamura et al. ( Nakamura et al., 2016 ) 2009–2017 Retrospective study KRAS and BRAF sequence analysis 20 % 0 % N/A N/A 10 32 China Xu et al. ( Xu et al., 2017 ) 2006–2015 Retrospective study polymerase chain reaction (PCR)-based direct gene sequencing ( KRAS and BRAF) 6 % 28 % N/A N/A 11 87 Korea Current study 2010–2023 Retrospective study NGS, ER, PR 9.7 % 11.3 % 1.6 % 1.6 % Open in a new tab A systematic comparison of available literature reveals a distinct East-to-West gradient in KRAS mutation prevalence in LGSOC. Japanese populations demonstrate deviation with 0 % KRAS mutations and predominant PIK3CA pathway activation (60 %), while our Korean cohort shows intermediate rates (11.3 %) with mixed pathway involvement. Chinese populations exhibit higher frequencies (28 %), approaching but still below Western ranges. This pattern suggests potential ethnic-specific genetic architecture or environmental influences affecting LGSOC pathogenesis. Further molecular investigations, including analyses of MAP2K1, RAF1 , and other non-MAPK pathway alterations, are warranted to more comprehensively define the genetic landscape of low-grade serous ovarian cancer in Asian populations. Targeting the MAPK pathway has been an area of active investigation for treating low-grade serous ovarian cancer, which frequently exhibits resistance to conventional chemotherapy. Trials such as GOG 281 and the MILO study demonstrated progression-free survival benefits with trametinib and binimetinib in patients with MAPK pathway alterations ( Monk et al., 2020 ). For instance, in the GOG 281 trial, which evaluated trametinib in 260 patients with recurrent low-grade serous ovarian cancer, KRAS mutations were associated with a progression-free survival benefit (HR 0.55; 95 % CI 0.28–1.07) compared to mutation-negative cases ( Gershenson et al., 2022 ). However, only 7 patients (2.7 %) in this trial were from Asian populations, limiting the generalizability of its findings to this demographic. Additionally, as MEK inhibitors and other MAPK pathway targeted therapies are not yet covered by insurance for low-grade serous ovarian cancer patients, their use is still infrequent. These factors highlight the need for regionally tailored access strategies for emerging targeted therapies. In this study, BRCA1 and BRCA2 mutations were identified in 4.8 % and 1.6 % of patients, respectively, consistent with previous reports showing a low prevalence of BRCA alterations in low-grade serous ovarian cancer. Although PARP inhibitors have shown substantial benefit in high-grade serous ovarian cancer, their clinical utility in low-grade serous ovarian cancer remains poorly defined. A preclinical study by Sun et al. demonstrated that MEK or ERK inhibition could reverse PARP inhibitor resistance in RAS-mutated tumors, including ovarian cancer xenografts. This synergy between PARP and MEK inhibition has led to the initiation of early-phase trials combining these agents in ovarian and other solid tumors. Preliminary findings indicate tolerability and potential efficacy, particularly in tumors harboring RAS pathway mutations, providing a rationale for combination therapies in selected low-grade serous ovarian cancer patients ( Sun et al., 2017 ). In the current study, 6 patients in the cohort received PARP inhibitors, suggesting a modest utilization of these agents in low-grade serous ovarian cancer. Given the chemo-resistant nature of low-grade serous ovarian cancer, integrating targeted therapies such as PARP inhibitors—potentially in combination with MEK inhibitors—may offer a new therapeutic avenue. However, the relatively low prevalence of BRCA mutations and RAS pathway mutations in low-grade serous ovarian cancer underlines the importance of biomarker-driven patient selection. Recent evidence suggests that folate receptor alpha (FRα) may represent a promising therapeutic target in molecularly defined subsets of LGSOC. A recent study analyzed 89 LGSOC tumors and reported that approximately 40 % were FRα-high, defined as ≥75 % of tumor cells with strong membranous staining ( Manning-Geist et al., 2024 ). Notably, FRα-high expression was significantly enriched among tumors lacking MAPK pathway alterations (61 % vs. 20 %, p < 0.001), suggesting that FOLR1 overexpression may serve as an alternative therapeutic biomarker specifically in MAPK-wildtype LGSOC. Given the relatively low KRAS mutation rate observed in our Korean cohort, this finding is of particular interest, and highlights the need to explore FRα-directed therapies, such as mirvetuximab soravtansine in Asian patients with MAPK-negative disease. Maintenance hormonal therapy, such as letrozole, is currently recommended by major guidelines due to its demonstrated benefit in prolonging PFS ( Liu et al., 2024 , Gonzalez-Martin et al., 2023 ). Retrospective studies, such as those by Gershenson et al., demonstrated significant improvements in progression-free survival with hormonal therapy compared to surveillance (median 64.9 vs. 26.4 months, p < 0.001) ( Gershenson et al., 2017 ). Maintenance hormonal therapy was not used prior to 2019 in our institution, and its adoption began gradually thereafter, following increased awareness of prior studies. However, in our cohort, only 16 % of patients received maintenance hormonal therapy. This low utilization likely reflects the lack of insurance coverage for letrozole maintenance therapy in Korea, which remains a significant barrier to its widespread adoption. Policy changes to improve access to guideline-recommended therapies are urgently needed to enhance outcomes for low-grade serous ovarian cancer patients. This study has several limitations. First, as a single-center retrospective study, the findings may not be generalizable to other populations. The small sample size, particularly for certain genetic analyses such as HRD testing, may limit the statistical power to detect fewer common alterations or associations. Additionally, while our study provides insights into an Asian cohort, it lacks a direct comparative group from Western populations, which could have better contextualized the observed regional differences. Another limitation is the lack of detailed clinical outcomes related to specific genetic mutations, such as response rates to targeted therapies, which could have strengthened the clinical implications of our findings. Despite these limitations, this study represents one of the few comprehensive analyses of low-grade serous ovarian cancer in an Asian population, offering a critical foundation for understanding population-specific differences and informing regionally tailored treatment strategies. 5. Conclusion In conclusion, this study demonstrated an analysis of the genetic landscape of low-grade serous ovarian cancer in an Asian cohort, with a KRAS mutation frequency at the lower end of previously reported ranges. These findings may reflect regional or methodological differences and underscore the need for further investigation in diverse populations. 6. Author contribution statement Dr. MC Lim had full access to all the data in the study and takes responsibility for the integrity of the data and accuracy of the data analysis. YSS, and JHK played comprehensive roles, including conceptualization, design, data collection, analysis, preparation of tables and figures, initial drafting of the manuscript, manuscript review, and final approval; JHK and YSS participated in analysis, manuscript review, and final approval. SBK, SSS, SYP and MCL participated in manuscript review and final approval. MCL was involved in conceptualization, design, data analysis, manuscript review, and final approval. CRediT authorship contribution statement Yu-Seon Shim: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Validation, Methodology, Investigation, Data curation. Ji Hyun Kim: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Formal analysis, Data curation, Conceptualization. Sang-soo Seo: Writing – review & editing, Investigation. Sokbom Kang: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Methodology, Investigation, Formal analysis, Data curation. Sang-Yoon Park: Writing – review & editing, Supervision. Myong Cheol Lim: Writing – review & editing, Writing – original draft, Validation, Supervision, Methodology, Investigation, Conceptualization. Ethics approval Ethical approval was obtained from the institutional review boards of the three participating centers in Korea: National cancer center (NCC2024-0034). The requirement for informed consent was waived. Funding None. Footnotes Appendix A Supplementary data to this article can be found online at https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gore.2025.101934 . Appendix A. Supplementary material The following are the Supplementary data to this article: Supplementary Table 1 The molecular details of gene alterations. mmc1.docx (21.1KB, docx) References Bray F., Laversanne M., Sung H., Ferlay J., Siegel R.L., Soerjomataram I., et al. Global cancer statistics 2022: GLOBOCAN estimates of incidence and mortality worldwide for 36 cancers in 185 countries. CA Cancer J. Clin. 2024;74:229–263. doi: 10.3322/caac.21834. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Cheasley D., Fernandez M.L., Kobel M., Kim H., Dawson A., Hoenisch J., et al. Molecular characterization of low-grade serous ovarian carcinoma identifies genomic aberrations according to hormone receptor expression. npj Precis. Oncol. 2022;6:47. doi: 10.1038/s41698-022-00288-2. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] ElNaggar A., Robins D., Baca Y., Arguello D., Ulm M., Arend R., et al. Genomic profiling in low grade serous ovarian cancer: Identification of novel markers for disease diagnosis and therapy. Gynecol. Oncol. 2022;167:306–313. doi: 10.1016/j.ygyno.2022.09.022. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Goo