 10.1016/S0140-6736(18)31930-5 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Cahill A, Stamilio DM, Paré E, et al. Vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC) attempt in twin pregnancies: is it safe? Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2005;193:1050‐1055. doi: 10.1016/j.ajog.2005.06.038 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 33. Trojano G, Damiani GR, Olivieri C, et al. VBAC: antenatal predictors of success. Acta Biomed. 2019;90(3):300‐309. doi: 10.23750/abm.v90i3.7623 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Guise J‐M, Denman MA, Emeis C, et al. Vaginal birth after cesarean: new insights on maternal and neonatal outcomes. Obstet Gynecol. 2010;115(6):1267‐1278. doi: 10.1097/AOG.0b013e3181df925f [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Research data are not shared.',5,'2025-10-13 06:49:42','2025-10-13 06:49:42');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1685,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12447695','Success rate and obstetric outcomes of trial of labor after cesarean delivery-Decision-tree analysis.','Houri O, Bercovich O, Berezovsky A, Gruber SD, Pardo A, Werthimer A, Walfisch A, Hadar E.','2025','ercd ercd ercd lessens previa accreta ercd ercd gestations cesareans rabin rmc ercd ercd transcervical amniotomy gravidity ercd ercd chaid ercd ercd ercd ercd ercd gravidity nvd polyhydramnios abruption ercd nvd nonvaginal pregestational polyhydramnios abruption pregestational ivh ttn ivh ttn tachypnea ivh ercd ercd ercd surpasses hysterectomies ercd ercd ercd optibirth notoriously accustomed houri bercovich berezovsky gulmezoglu lumbiganon rhm cesareans placentation gaudet krewski melamed segev hadar peled wiznitzer yogev landon hauth leveno e110 e127 guise emeis guise denman emeis liston pell dobbie e252 lydon rochelle easterling betran gulmezoglu torloni e0148343 uddin allshouse guise jimison multiattribute lyerly bonzon karch grylka baeschlin hashima osterweil nygren guise niino blondel mohangoo peristat peristat lundgren limbeek vehvilainen julkunen devane optibirth kuppermann kaimal blat luther bowes olshan annessi giovane magnani wattar quenby nittur illuzzi grobman gestations lipschuetz guedalia rottenstreich denman','Abstract Background Pregnant women with a previous cesarean delivery (CD) may opt for a trial of labor after cesarean (TOLAC) or elective repeat cesarean delivery (ERCD). This study aimed to evaluate the success rate, and maternal, and perinatal outcomes of TOLAC and to develop a predictive decision‐tree algorithm for successful TOLAC. Methods A retrospective study was conducted in a tertiary medical center and included all women with one prior CD who delivered between 2008 and 2019. Maternal and perinatal outcomes were compared between successful and failed TOLAC and ERCD groups. A decision‐tree algorithm was constructed using the χ 2 automatic interaction detection method. Results Of 10 325 women with one prior CD (out of 103 542 deliveries), the rate of successful TOLAC, defined as vaginal birth after cesarean (VBAC), was 81.92%. Symptomatic uterine rupture occurred in 55 women (0.98%), with no cases of hysterectomy, or maternal or neonatal death. The decision tree identified key predictors of VBAC success, including maternal age, gestational age at delivery, and history of vaginal delivery. Women with a prior vaginal delivery had the highest likelihood of VBAC (90.3%). The overall accuracy of the model was 82.7%. Conclusions This study demonstrated a high rate of TOLAC attempts and VBAC success in a tertiary medical center with experienced staff and close monitoring. Uterine rupture was rare and not associated with severe maternal or neonatal morbidity or mortality. The decision‐tree algorithm provides a practical tool to predict successful TOLAC, supporting individualized care and informed decision making. Keywords: decision tree, trial of labor after cesarean delivery (TOLAC), uterine rupture 1. INTRODUCTION Pregnant women with a single previous cesarean delivery (CD) can opt for a trial of labor after cesarean (TOLAC) or an elective repeat cesarean delivery (ERCD). TOLAC offers the benefits of shorter recovery time and reduced risk of surgical complications, 1 , 2 and it lessens potential risks in future pregnancies, including placenta previa and placenta accreta , and surgical risks associated with multiple CDs and hysterectomy . 1 , 3 It also avoids the potential neonatal complications associated with ERCD, particularly respiratory problems and long‐term childhood morbidities. 4 , 5 Successful TOLAC is defined as achieving a vaginal birth after cesarean delivery (VBAC). The rate of successful TOLAC ranges between 60% and 80%. 6 , 7 , 8 However, although TOLAC presents a viable option for many women, it is associated with some potential complications, particularly uterine rupture, which, although rare, can have serious consequences for both mother and neonate. 9 In addition, failed TOLAC may lead to repeat emergency CD, raising risks of postpartum hemorrhage, infection, and injury to adjacent organs during surgery. 10 , 11 , 12 The rate of TOLAC has been consistently declining in recent decades, concomitant with a rise in the rate of CDs. 13 , 14 , 15 , 16 The specific reasons for this trend remain uncertain; however, several factors appear to play a role, including clinician perspectives, patient preferences, institutional protocols, and a lack of high‐quality evidence to provide reliable patient counseling. 17 , 18 , 19 , 20 One of the strong reasons for choosing ERCD over TOLAC is the desire for a safe birth and a healthy baby 21 , 22 while avoiding the most serious and immediate complication of uterine rupture. The aim of this study was to present our 12‐year experience with TOLAC and to construct a simple decision‐tree algorithm to estimate the likelihood of a successful TOLAC. 2. MATERIALS AND METHODS 2.1. Setting and study population A retrospective study was conducted in a single tertiary medical center from January 2008 through December 2019. The cohort included all pregnant women after 22 weeks of pregnancy with a live fetus who were admitted to the delivery ward with a history of a single prior low‐segment CD. Twin gestations and women with contraindications to TOLAC—including multiple prior cesareans, non‐transverse uterine scars, prior uterine rupture—were excluded. All methods were carried out in accordance with the relevant guidelines and regulations. This study was performed in adherence to the principles of the Declaration of Helsinki and was approved by the Ethics Committee of Rabin Medical Center (approval no. 0132‐22‐RMC), which waived the need for informed consent because of the retrospective design. The data were anonymized before use. 2.2. Institutional protocol All women followed the same institutional protocol. In the third trimester, they received detailed counseling about the risks, benefits, and outcomes of TOLAC and ERCD. A personalized approach considered their medical and obstetrical history, future plans, and preferences. When no contraindications were present, TOLAC was recommended as the preferred option of labor. Women opting for ERCD were evaluated in a preoperative clinic at 32–34 weeks of pregnancy, where obstetricians provided further counseling and obtained informed consent. Women choosing TOLAC underwent maternal and fetal assessments during antenatal care and upon admission, where they provided consent for TOLAC and possible emergency CD. The delivery ward was equipped for immediate emergency CD, with a multidisciplinary team comprising senior obstetricians, anesthesiologists, and neonatologists who were available on‐site. Maternal and neonatal intensive care units, along with immediate access to blood products, were also available. Intravenous access, baseline hemoglobin, and blood typing were obtained for TOLAC admissions. Labor induction methods such as transcervical catheters, prostaglandin E 2 , oxytocin, or amniotomy were used as needed. Fetal heart rate and uterine activity were continuously monitored. 2.3. Data collection Data for the study were retrieved from computerized medical records. Demographic and pregnancy‐related parameters included maternal age, gravidity, parity, onset of labor (spontaneous, induction, or augmentation), methods of induction (oxytocin, extra‐amniotic balloon, or prostaglandins), and rupture of membranes (spontaneous or artificial). The indication for ERCD was also recorded. Cases of uterine rupture were reviewed, and maternal morbidity parameters included hysterectomy, transfusion of blood products, bowel or urinary injury, and maternal death. Neonatal data included gestational age at delivery, birth weight, neonatal intensive care unit admission, 1‐ and 5‐min Apgar scores, and umbilical cord arterial pH. Neonatal adverse outcomes included asphyxia, seizures, respiratory distress syndrome, intraventricular hemorrhage, hypoxic–ischemic encephalopathy, mechanical ventilation, meconium aspiration, and neonatal death. A composite neonatal outcome was defined as the presence of any adverse neonatal event. 2.4. Data analysis Clinical characteristics, delivery mode, and maternal and fetal outcomes were compared among four subgroups: ERCD, successful TOLAC, failed TOLAC, and uterine rupture group. Rates of TOLAC and VBAC were calculated among women who chose TOLAC. The primary outcome for the decision tree was the success rate of TOLAC, i.e. achieving VBAC. 2.5. Statistical analysis and decision tree Student t test or Mann–Whitney U test, as appropriate, was used to compare continuous variables between groups, and the χ 2 test or Fisher exact test was used to compare categorical variables. Univariate analysis was performed to evaluate the crude association between each variable and the primary outcome. Variables found to be statistically significant were included in a stepwise forward multiple logistic regression model to identify those that were independently associated with the primary outcome. In addition, a clinical decision tree was constructed using the CHAID (Chi‐squared Automatic Interaction Detection) algorithm. The purpose of the tree was to visually stratify eligible patients based on key clinical factors associated with VBAC success, providing a simple and interpretable framework to support shared decision making during counseling. To ensure an unbiased evaluation of the model''s performance, the data set was randomly divided into training and test samples: 70% of the data was allocated to training the decision tree, and the remaining 30% to testing its performance. The decision tree was built using three primary stratifying variables: prior vaginal delivery, gestational age at delivery, and maternal age. Patients were first divided based on history of vaginal birth, followed by gestational age (cut‐off at 39 +1 weeks), and then maternal age (cut‐off at 33 years). Each branch of the tree reflects the probability of VBAC based on these clinical factors. All statistical tests were two‐tailed, and P values less than 0.05 were considered statistically significant. All calculations were performed using SPSS version 25.0 (IBM Corp. Inc., Armonk, NY, USA). 3. RESULTS A total of 103 542 deliveries were performed during the 12‐year study period, of which 22 286 (21.52%) were CDs. Of the 10 325 patients with one documented previous CD, 5603 (54.26%) attempted TOLAC and 4722 (45.73%) had an ERCD (Figure 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Flow chart of study population. CD, cesarean delivery; TOLAC, trial of labor after cesarean. The most common indication for ERCD was non‐vertex presentation (20.92%). Within the ERCD group, 1772 women (37.57%) were ineligible for TOLAC because of a contraindication for vaginal delivery or trial of labor (Table S1 ). Following the exclusion of ineligible patients, 6725 women, representing 65.14% of the cohort with one previous CD, were considered eligible for TOLAC, of whom 5611 (83.10%) agreed to attempt it. The rate of successful TOLAC (achievement of VBAC) was 81.92%. Table 1 shows the demographic and obstetrical characteristics of women with successful TOLAC, failed TOLAC, and uterine rupture during TOLAC compared with patients with ERCD. Table 2 compares the demographic and obstetrical characteristics of women with successful and failed TOLAC. The failed TOLAC group had a lower rate of any prior vaginal delivery (26.50% vs. 61.14%, odds ratio [OR] 0.25, 95% confidence interval [CI] 0.21–0.92, P < 0.001) and lower prior VBAC rate (9.9% vs. 39.11%, OR 0.17, 95% CI 0.13–0.21, P < 0.001), in addition to a higher incidence of gestational diabetes (GDM) (11.36% vs. 5.70%, OR 1.51, 95% CI 1.16–1.97, P = 0.002), pre‐GDM (15.62% vs. 6.42%, OR 1.95, 95% CI 1.55–2.46, P < 0.001), and hypertensive disorders (8.42% vs. 1.73%, OR 5.20, 95% CI 3.78–7.15, P < 0.001). TABLE 1. Obstetrical and maternal characteristics of women with one previous cesarean. a Characteristic Successful TOLAC ( n = 4607) Failed TOLAC (no rupture) ( n = 949) Uterine rupture TOLAC ( n = 55) ERCD b ( n = 4722) All TOLAC ( n = 5611) P value Maternal age, years 33.08 ± 4.58 34.15 ± 4.93 32.04 ± 4.46 33.45 ± 6.2 33.45 ± 6.2 <0.01 Gestational age at delivery, weeks 39.57 (38.57–40.14) 38.28 (37.71–39.0) 39.28 (37.85–40.0) 37.16 (37.0–39.0) 37.16 (37.0–39.0) <0.01 BMI at delivery 22.94 (20.50–25.64) 24.22 (21.48–29.22) 23.655 (21.76–26.76) 24.144 (21.37–28.3) 24.144 (21.37–28.30) <0.01 Gravidity 3 (2–4) 3 (2–4) 3 (2–4) 3 (2–4) 3 (2–4) <0.01 Parity 2 (1–3) 1 (1–2) 1 (1–2) 2 (1–3) 2 (1–3) <0.01 Delivery <37 wk. 168 (3.65) 60 (6.33) 15 (14.29) 329 (6.96) 243 (4.33) <0.01 Mode of delivery <0.01 NVD 3903 (84.71) 5 (5) 3908 (69.64) VE 684 (14.84) 10 (10) 694 (12.36) CD 949 (100) 40 (85) 4722 (100) 989 (17.62) Any PVD 2828 (61.14) 271 (28.5) 17 (30.9) — — <0.01 Prior VBAC (≥1) 1802 (39.11) 94 (9.9) 7 (12.7) — — <0.01 Prior VBAC (≥2) 678 (14.71) 20 (2.10) 3 (5.45) — — Hypertensive disorders c 80 (1.73) 80 (8.42) 0 (0) 242 (5.12) 160 (2.85) 0.013 GDM 263 (5.70) 81 (11.36) 4 (7.27) 636 (13.46) 348 (6.20) <0.01 Pre‐GDM 296 (6.42) 112 (15.62) 4 (7.27) 96 (13.20) 412 (7.34) <0.01 Oligohydramnios 87 (1.88) 17 (2.47) 0 (0) 34 (0.72) 104 (1.85) 0.38 Polyhydramnios 50 (1.08) 5 (0.72) 0 (0) 65 (1.37) 55 (0.98) 0.4 Intrapartum fever 53 (1.15) 3 (0.43) 3 (4.762) — 59 0.006 Placental abruption 22 (0.47) 14 (2.02) 4 (6.35) — 40 <0.01 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: BMI, body mass index (calculated as weight in kilograms divided by the square of height in meters); CD, cesarean delivery; ERCD, elective repeat cesarean section; GDM, gestational diabetes mellitus; NVD, nonvaginal delivery; Pre‐GDM, pregestational diabetes mellitus (Types 1 or 2); PVD, prior vaginal delivery; TOLAC, trial of labor after cesarean; VBAC, vaginal birth after cesarean; VE, vacuum extraction. a Data are presented as mean ± standard deviation, median (interquartile range), or number (percentage). b Patients with failed (including uterine rupture) and successful TOLAC were compared for outcome with a reference group of women with an elective repeated cesarean section. c Hypertensive disorder included chronic hypertension, gestational hypertension, and pre‐eclampsia. TABLE 2. Obstetrical and maternal characteristics of women attempting TOLAC. a Characteristic Group OR 95% CI P value Successful TOLAC b ( n = 4607) Failed TOLAC (including rupture) ( n = 1006) Delivery <37 weeks 168 (3.65) 60 (6.33) 1.76 1.31–2.41 0.002 Any PVD 2828 (61.14) 271 (28.5) 0.25 0.21–0.92 <0.01 Prior VBAC (≥1) 1802 (39.11) 94 (9.9) 0.17 0.13–0.21 <0.01 Prior VBAC (≥2) 678 (14.71) 20 (2.10) 0.12 0.07–0.19 <0.01 Hypertensive disorder c 80 (1.73) 80 (8.42) 5.2 3.78–7.15 <0.01 GDM 263 (5.70) 81 (11.36) 1.51 1.16–1.97 0.002 Pre‐GDM 296 (6.42) 112 (15.62) 1.95 1.55–2.46 <0.01 Oligohydramnios 87 (1.88) 17 (2.47) 0.94 0.56–1.6 0.84 Polyhydramnios 50 (1.08) 5 (0.72) 0.48 0.19–1.21 0.12 Meconium staining 559 (14.58) 357 (38.77) 4.36 3.72–5.11 <0.01 Intrapartum fever 53 (1.15) 6 (0.59) 0.5314 0.22–1.22 0.14 Placental abruption 22 (0.47) 18 (1.78) 3.84 2.05–7.18 <0.01 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; GDM, gestational diabetes; OR, odds ratio; Pre‐GDM, pregestational diabetes mellitus (Types 1 or 2); PVD, prior vaginal delivery; TOLAC, trial of labor after cesarean. a Data are presented as number (percentage) unless otherwise stated. b Women with failed TOLAC and uterine rupture were compared with the reference group of women with successful TOLAC. c Hypertensive disorder included chronic hypertension, gestational hypertension, and pre‐eclampsia. A decision tree was constructed to identify the most suitable criteria for selecting patients most likely to achieve VBAC. Factors significantly associated with VBAC on univariate analysis (maternal age, gestational age, history of vaginal deliveries) were incorporated into the model (Figure 2 ). The findings revealed that a history of prior vaginal birth was the factor that best distinguished this patient group. Specifically, women who had previously undergone vaginal delivery, either before or after a prior CD, had the highest (90.3%) likelihood of VBAC. Within this subset, the probability of VBAC was 95.3% at 39 +1 weeks of pregnancy and decreased to 84.5% below that. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Factors incorporated into the model. VBAC, vaginal birth after cesarean. Conversely, for women without a prior vaginal delivery, the overall probability of VBAC was 72.6%. Within this group, the chances of VBAC were 88.3% if pregnancy duration exceeded 39 +1 weeks and dropped significantly to 58.2% below this. Among women without a history of vaginal delivery and gestation less than 39 +1 weeks, those aged more than 33 years had the least favorable outcome, with a VBAC rate of 47.0%. The overall accuracy of this decision‐tree model was 82.7%. Maternal complications associated with TOLAC are presented in Table 3 . The rate of postpartum hemorrhage was similar in women with failed and successful TOLAC (4.38% vs. 3.65%, OR 1.21, 95% CI 0.87–1.77, P = 0.33), but the need for blood product transfusions was higher in those who failed TOLAC (3.98% vs. 0.68%, OR 6.06, 95% CI 3.61–10.16, P = 0.01). There were no cases of hysterectomy or maternal death. TABLE 3. Maternal outcome of attempted TOLAC. a Outcome parameters Group OR 95% CI P value Failed TOLAC (including uterine rupture) ( n = 1006) Successful TOLAC ( n = 4607) Maternal death 0 0 — — — Hysterectomy 0 0 Postpartum hemorrhage 33 (4.38) 156 (3.65) 1.21 0.82–1.77 0.33 Blood products transfusion 30 (3.98) 29 (0.68) 6.06 3.61–10.16 <0.01 Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; OR, odds ratio; TOLAC, trial of labor after cesarean. a Data are presented as number (percentage) unless otherwise stated. Perinatal outcomes of TOLAC are presented in Table 4 . Neonatal HIE occurred in a single case of successful TOLAC and in none of the other groups. Composite neonatal outcomes were higher in the failed TOLAC group (OR 3.09, 95% CI 1.43–4.70, P < 0.001). There were no cases of neonatal death in any group. TABLE 4. Perinatal outcome of attempted TOLAC. a Outcome parameters Group OR 95% CI P value Uterine rupture ( n = 55) Failed TOLAC ( n = 949) Successful TOLAC b ( n = 4607) Neonatal birth weight, g 3160 (2953–3538) 3250 (2942–3533) 3190 (2826.5–3507) <0.01 Umbilical cord pH <7 6 (10) 1 (0.1) 4 (0.187) 6.023 1.80–20.06 <0.01 1‐min Apgar <7 18 (31.66) 32 (3.37) 123 (2.673) 2.053 1.47–2.85 <0.01 5‐min Apgar <7 7 (10) 14 (1.47) 60 (1.304) 1.476 0.87–2.48 0.14 NICU admission 13 (21.67) 93 (9.97) 221 (4.839) 2.353 1.84–2.99 <0.01 HIE 0 0 (0) 1 (0.022) — — — IVH 1 (1.66) 5 (0.527) 15 (0.326) 1.621 0.58–4.47 0.36 TTN 1 (2.12) 12 (1.26) 49 (1.06) 1.22 0.66–1.32 0.45 RDS 2 (3.23) 13 (1.370) 28 (0.608) 2.271 1.17–4.4 0.01 Mechanical ventilation 2 (3.32) 21 (2.213) 46 (0.998) 2.244 1.3–3.77 <0.01 Meconium aspiration 0 1 (0.105) 6 (0.130) 0.809 0.09–6.72 — Composite neonatal outcome c 5 (8.33) 64 (6.74) 96 (2.08) 3.09 1.43–4.7 <0.01 Neonatal death 0 0 0 — — — Open in a new tab Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; HIE, hypoxic–ischemic encephalopathy; IVH, intraventricular hemorrhage; NICU, neonatal intensive care unit; OR, odds ratio; RDS, respiratory distress syndrome; TOLAC, trial of labor after cesarean; TTN, transient tachypnea of newborn. a Data are presented as median (interquartile range) or number (percentage) unless otherwise stated. b Women with failed TOLAC and uterine rupture were compared with the reference group of women with successful TOLAC. c Composite neonatal outcome was defined as any one of the following: asphyxia, seizures, RDS, IVH, HIE, mechanical ventilation, meconium aspiration, and neonatal death. 4. DISCUSSION This retrospective study conducted in a single, high‐volume delivery ward shows a high rate of TOLAC attempts among eligible patients (65.14%) and a high success rate of VBAC deliveries (81.92%), with favorable outcomes for both mothers and neonates. The rate of uterine rupture was 0.98%, and there was no neonatal mortality, maternal mortality, or need for hysterectomy. To enhance the predictability of successful VBAC, we formulated a decision tree based on three straightforward obstetrical parameters: prior vaginal delivery, gestational age at admission, and maternal age. Successful VBAC has been associated with more favorable outcomes than ERCD. A systematic review and meta‐analysis of 203 studies demonstrated that maternal mortality was significantly higher with ERCD compared with planned TOLAC (1.34 vs. 0.38 per 10 000 births). Perinatal mortality was significantly increased with planned TOLAC compared with ERCD (13 vs. 5 per 10 000 births), but the absolute rates were low. 10 The evidence suggests that VBAC is a reasonable and safe option for most women. Consequently, consensus statements from multiple organizations including the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists and the National Institutes of Health, have underscored planned TOLAC as a clinically sound choice for most women with a prior low‐segment CD. 8 , 23 Nevertheless, as the prevalence of CDs increases worldwide, 14 , 24 the rate of TOLAC continues to decline. Cross‐sectional analyses conducted in the USA using national hospital discharge survey data revealed fluctuations in TOLAC rates, with a peak of 51.8% in 1995 and a nadir of 15.9% in 2006. 14 More recent data from national registry studies indicated a modest improvement to 21.7% between 2010 and 2020, 15 although the absolute rates remain comparatively low. In some countries, such as Taiwan, the reported TOLAC rates were markedly low, at less than 5%. 25 Among European countries, reported TOLAC rates varied substantially, from 14.8% to 52.2%. 26 Within our center, the data demonstrated a remarkable incidence of TOLAC, coupled with a notably high success rate, which surpasses most global publications. The reasons for the decline in the success of TOLAC are unclear. Insights from a study in the USA attributed it to concerns of uterine rupture and medicolegal considerations. 7 We found an incidence of 0.98% for uterine rupture, similar to the 0.5%–1.0% reported in a previous study. 8 There were no maternal deaths, hysterectomies, or significant maternal complications in our cohort, and no neonatal mortality. These findings suggest that in an experienced tertiary center, adverse effects of uterine rupture can be effectively managed, with positive maternal and neonatal outcomes, supporting a policy of TOLAC as an acceptable alternative to ERCD. The low rates of TOLAC attempts in earlier studies may also be partly attributable to a failure to optimally refine patient selection for the procedure despite known clinical predictors of success. 21 , 22 Decisions regarding the mode of birth require careful consideration of patient preferences, obstetrical history, available data on TOLAC risks and benefits compared with ERCD, and the feasibility of TOLAC within the chosen birth environment. 27 In our center, obstetrical care is highly accessible and cost‐free, and it is supplemented by comprehensive discussions at 32 weeks of pregnancy with mothers opting for ERCD. The awareness of the team of the advantages of TOLAC might contribute to the high TOLAC rates in our population. However, research has not universally substantiated this trend. The OptiBIRTH trial 28 explored the impact of a woman‐centric intervention on successful TOLAC rates across 15 maternity units in Germany, Ireland, and Italy with VBAC rates below 35%. No statistically significant increase in VBAC rates was observed in the intervention compared with the control sites. Similarly, in the PROCEED trial, 29 the use of a person‐centered decision support tool failed to enhance TOLAC rates over the usual care (43.3% vs. 46.2%). These findings might be explained by the notoriously slow pace of change in clinical practice, with both clinicians and women needing time to become accustomed to new approaches to care. In our center, TOLAC is an everyday experience, which makes it easier for caregivers and patients to universally support and recognize it as routine policy, if not otherwise contraindicated. Multiple calculators have been created to assist patients and obstetricians to better understand their chance of success. 30 , 31 A more individualized approach takes into account the many factors that have been associated with VBAC to determine how one woman''s chances of a successful VBAC may differ from the overall population average. 32 , 33 , 34 , 35 , 36 We try to use a simple decision‐tree analysis to provide optimal multivariable predictive cut‐off points for an increased likelihood of VBAC. Although logistic regression can be interpreted only as a probability for a given outcome, a decision‐tree analysis provides a stepwise prediction and the best‐fit scenario within a clinical situation. The most important parameter of successful TOLAC in our study was previous vaginal delivery. This is supported by a 2010 meta‐analysis wherein the two factors that increased the odds of VBAC by at least threefold were history of VBAC and history of vaginal birth. 37 This study possesses several strengths that enhance its relevance. It included a large cohort, making it one of the most extensive investigations on this topic from a single center, and the data for the study originated from a single tertiary center, ensuring uniformity in labor‐management protocols and meticulous documentation. However, certain limitations warrant acknowledgment. As the focus centered on admission‐related factors, intrapartum variables such as oxytocin use, epidural use, and labor duration were not included, although they might influence the choice of delivery route. The retrospective design might have introduced inherent biases and limitations. In constructing our decision‐tree model, specific variables, namely indication for previous CD and body mass index were not incorporated because of data gaps. This deliberate exclusion was aimed at preserving the model''s integrity and accuracy, despite the possible compromise introduced by incomplete data. In addition, as this study was conducted at a single center, external validation is needed to confirm the decision‐tree model''s generalizability. In conclusion, our study presents a remarkably high rate of attempted TOLAC and VBAC success within a single high‐volume center. Our findings provide reassurance that even if TOLAC fails, or in the event of uterine rupture, the risk of severe maternal and perinatal outcomes remains low, especially in an experienced tertiary care setting. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS All authors contributed to the study conception and design. AB and SDG prepared the material and performed the data collection. AW and AV performed the data analysis. EH interpreted the data for the study. OB and AP drafted and revised the manuscript. OH wrote the first draft of the manuscript. All authors commented on previous versions of the manuscript, and all authors read and approved the final manuscript. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors have no conflicts of interest. Supporting information Table S1. Indication for elective repeat cesarean delivery. IJGO-171-317-s001.docx (13.8KB, docx) Houri O, Bercovich O, Berezovsky A, et al. Success rate and obstetric outcomes of trial of labor after cesarean delivery—Decision‐tree analysis. Int J Gynecol Obstet. 2025;171:317‐325. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.70204 DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT The data sets generated and analyzed during the present study are not publicly available because of ethical committee regulations, but they are available from the corresponding author on reasonable request. REFERENCES 1. Souza JP, Gülmezoglu A, Lumbiganon P. WHO Global Survey on Maternal and Perinatal Health Resea