ty, BMI, gestational age, MVPA 16/20 “yes” Diastolic blood pressure Beta coefficient N/A 0.002 (95% CI –0.02–0.03) Age, smoking, education, marital status, poverty to income ratio, race/ethnicity, BMI, gestational age, MVPA 16/20 “yes” Open in a new tab Abbreviations: BMI, body mass index (calculated as weight in kilograms divided by the square of height in meters); CI, confidence interval; GH, gestational hypertension; LTPA, leisure‐time physical activity; MET, metabolic equivalent of task; MVPA, moderate‐to‐vigorous‐intensity physical activity; PE, pre‐eclampsia; SED, sedentary behavior. a Study quality assessment performed using the Newcastle‐Ottawa Scale for cohort and case–control studies and the Appraisal Tool for Cross‐Sectional Studies (AXIS) for cross‐sectional studies. For the Newcastle‐Ottawa Scale, studies were required to control for both age and BMI to be awarded one star for the “comparability” category; studies were awarded an additional star if they controlled for any additional factor. Studies only controlling for one of the two were awarded only one star for comparability. Quality is summarized as the total number of stars awarded. For AXIS, quality is summarized as the total number of questions awarded a “yes” response. For detailed quality assessments for each included study, refer to Appendix S3 . 4.1. Gestational hypertension The association between sedentary behavior and risk of gestational hypertension was reported in two studies. Saftlas et al. used two assessments of sedentary behavior self‐reported through an interview: (1) number of hours per day spent sitting; and (2) work activity levels derived by calculating the percentage of each participant''s work day spent sitting. 39 No significant association was found between amount of time spent sitting and gestational hypertension risk ( P = 0.73; Figure 2 ). Spracklen et al. also assessed two self‐reported measures of sedentary behavior: (1) amount of time per day spent sitting at work (continuous and categorized); and (2) a composite sitting variable approximating the total time spent sedentary per day, calculated using self‐reported number of hours spent on feet at work and number of hours spent on feet away from work. 40 Similarly, no significant association was found between sedentary behavior and risk of gestational hypertension (Figure 2 ). Chasan‐Taber et al. also investigated the outcome of gestational hypertension; however, due to low incidence ( n = 17, 1.4%), authors were unable to evaluate this outcome separately from other hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. 31 FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Reported adjusted odds ratios and 95% confidence intervals of studies assessing gestational hypertension and pre‐eclampsia. CI, confidence interval; OR, odds ratio. 4.2. Pre‐eclampsia The association between sedentary behavior and risk of pre‐eclampsia was investigated in four studies. In addition to the outcome of gestational hypertension, Saftlas et al. and Spracklen et al. also investigated pre‐eclampsia using the same assessments of sedentary behavior as described earlier; however, no significant associations were found (Figure 2 ). 39 , 40 Chasan‐Taber et al. examined the association between sedentary behavior during pre‐ and early pregnancy self‐reported on the Pregnancy Physical Activity Questionnaire (PPAQ) and risk of developing pre‐eclampsia among Hispanic women and found no significant difference in risk between sedentary behavior tertiles (Figure 2 ). 31 Do et al. also assessed sedentary behavior through self‐report on the PPAQ and found a significant association between weekly energy expenditure (MET‐h/week) in sedentary activities and pre‐eclampsia risk (unadjusted OR [95% CI] 1.04 [1.00–1.08], P = 0.03); however, after adjusting for parity, smoking and diastolic blood pressure, the association was no longer significant (adjusted OR [95% CI] 1.03 [0.99–1.08], P = 0.13). 32 4.3. Total hypertensive disorders of pregnancy Four studies reported on the association between sedentary behavior and total hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. Three studies 31 , 34 , 37 defined total hypertensive disorders of pregnancy as combined pre‐eclampsia and gestational hypertension; however, Liu et al. defined it as combined pre‐eclampsia, gestational hypertension, eclampsia, and either pre‐eclampsia or gestational hypertension in addition to chronic hypertension. Meander et al. assessed sedentary behavior as self‐reported time spent sitting down and reported two analyses, continuous and binary (≥7 vs. <7 h). 37 The continuous exposure was borderline statistically significant (unadjusted OR [95% CI] 1.21 [1.00–1.50]), whereas the binary exposure was statistically significant (unadjusted OR [95% CI] 1.65 [1.03–2.62]). Chasan‐Taber et al. reported effect estimates for sedentary behavior in pre‐pregnancy and early pregnancy, separately, although neither was significantly associated with total hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. 31 Similarly, Lawan et al. assessed sedentary time through self‐report on the PPAQ and found that sedentary behavior did not differ significantly between participants with pre‐eclampsia or gestational hypertension relative to those without ( P = 0.162). 34 Liu et al. defined sedentary behavior as self‐reported time spent using a computer and time spent using a smartphone and/or tablet computer. 35 Comparing computer time between cases and controls, they found that cases spent more time on the computer relative to controls (median [interquartile range, IQR] 0 [0–6.0] vs. 3.0 [0–7.5], P = 0.003). However, time spent on smartphone and/or tablet computers was not significantly different between cases and controls (median [IQR] 5.50 [3.50–7.88] vs. 5.50 [3.50–7.50], P = 0.089). 4.4. Blood pressure The association of sedentary behavior with blood pressure in pregnancy was investigated in five studies. All included studies reported this association for blood pressure measured at one point in time, except for Lane et al., who reported this association for blood pressure measured in each trimester, 33 although we only discuss reports for the second and third trimesters here. Both Lane et al. and Wallace et al. utilized the MoM Health cohort participants; 33 , 41 however, Wallace et al. additionally included participants from a second cohort and, as a result, we retained both studies in our analysis. Lane et al. assessed sedentary behavior as time spent in a sitting or reclining position measured using an activity monitor. 33 Wallace et al. assessed time spent sitting through self‐report on the Sedentary Behavior Two Domain Questionnaire (SB2D). 41 Regarding systolic blood pressure, only one study, Raguema et al., found a statistically significant positive correlation with sedentary behavior self‐reported on the PPAQ (correlation coefficient = 0.25, 0.001 < P < 0.05). 38 However, when the authors restricted their analysis to pre‐eclampsia cases only, they found a negative and not statistically significant correlation (correlation coefficient = −0.05). Regarding diastolic blood pressure, only Lane et al. found an association, specifically between high versus low sedentary behavior and diastolic blood pressure measured in the second trimester at a study visit ( β [95% CI] = 4.9 [1.6–8.2]). 33 This association was not statistically significant with third‐trimester blood pressure or blood pressure measured as part of a routine prenatal clinic visit. Similarly, none of the other studies found an association between sedentary behavior and diastolic blood pressure. 4.5. Quality assessment To summarize quality assessments, the following thresholds were used to convert Newcastle‐Ottawa Scale assessments to Agency for Health Research and Quality (AHRQ) standards: good quality, 3 or 4 stars in selection domain AND 1 or 2 stars in comparability domain AND 2 or 3 stars in outcome/exposure domain; fair quality, 2 stars in selection domain AND 1 or 2 stars in comparability domain AND 2 or 3 stars in outcome/exposure domain; poor quality, 0 or 1 star in selection domain OR 0 stars in comparability domain OR 0 or 1 stars in outcome/exposure domain. Cross‐sectional studies assessed using the AXIS tool were converted into poor‐, fair‐, and good‐quality categories based on the proportion of ‘yes’ responses: poor quality, <12/20 (60%); fair quality, 12–15/20 (60%–80%); good quality >15/20 (80%). Detailed quality assessments for each extracted study result can be found in Appendix S3 . All extracted study results from the four case–control studies were determined to be of poor quality, most commonly due to a lack of comment regarding non‐response rate, interviews not being blinded to case–control status, and inadequate control of important confounding variables based on pre‐defined confounding domains (age and body mass index [BMI, calculated as weight in kilograms divided by the square of height in meters]). 35 , 38 , 39 , 40 Four of the five included cohort studies had results of good quality. 31 , 32 , 33 , 41 Results from Meander et al. were assigned poor to fair quality due to methodology involving participant recall of sedentary time during pregnancy (exposure) at 32–34 weeks gestation after the time of diagnosis of pre‐eclampsia/gestational hypertension (outcome) which may have introduced potential for recall bias on the exposure depending on factors associated with the outcome. 37 Two of the three included cross‐sectional studies were assigned good quality. 34 , 36 Results from Aburezq et al. were assigned fair quality due to a lack of sample size justification, self‐reported blood pressure ascertainment, no description of non‐responders, and no discussion of study limitations. 30 Additionally, participants were recruited postpartum, and therefore report of sedentary behavior during pregnancy is subject to recall bias and may be altered based on experiences during pregnancy, labor, or delivery. 5. DISCUSSION This systematic review based on 12 observational studies found limited evidence of an association between sedentary behavior and pre‐eclampsia, gestational hypertension, total hypertensive disorders of pregnancy, or gestational blood pressure. The limited number of studies assessing this association as well as heterogeneity in the methods used to estimate sedentary behavior was a barrier to quantifying this relationship, indicating a need for continued investigation on this topic. Of the included studies, only two used an objective measure of sedentary behavior 33 , 36 and some of the exposure measures have not previously been validated. Although four studies found a statistically significant association between sedentary behavior and a hypertensive disorder of pregnancy in their most‐adjusted results, 33 , 35 , 37 , 38 three of these studies were rated as either poor or fair quality using the Newcastle‐Ottawa Scale. We are aware of only one other systematic review that examined the relationship between sedentary behavior and gestational hypertensive disorders. In 2017, Fazzi et al. synthesized three studies on sedentary behavior during pregnancy and hypertensive disorders during gestation. 42 Two of these studies were included in our review, neither of which found a statistically significant association. 31 , 36 Fazzi et al. also included a study which found more gestational hypertension among women who worked in environments requiring sitting (e.g., sewing operators) as compared with women working in environments which were also sedentary but wherein they could move during working time (e.g., secretaries). 43 We excluded this study at the full‐text stage as occupation was used as a marker for sedentary behavior and no quantitative estimate of sedentariness or time spent engaging in sedentary behavior was provided. Our findings build upon those by Fazzi et al. to suggest that there is little evidence of an association between sedentary behavior and pre‐eclampsia or other hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. There is a large body of evidence on the association between physical activity immediately prior to or during pregnancy and pre‐eclampsia, demonstrating, for the most part, a protective effect of physical activity against pre‐eclampsia. 15 , 44 Conversely, far less is known about sedentary behavior as a risk factor for pre‐eclampsia. The scientific literature in this field has expanded only in recent years and only two of our included studies were published more than 10 years ago. 36 , 39 The disparity in results may be based on differences in the methods for estimating participants'' sedentary behavior, as most included studies relied on self‐reported behavior. In the case of highly sedentary individuals incorrectly classified as being less sedentary, the effect of sedentary behavior may have been underestimated in studies. Alternatively, decreasing sedentary behavior may be less important than increasing physical activity, in relation to reducing the risk of hypertensive disorders of pregnancy. Larger prospective studies are needed to better evaluate this association, and randomized trials evaluating the effect of decreasing sedentary behavior may also be useful. The reliance on self‐reported measures of sedentary behavior is a limitation in this field. Although there is no single best measure for capturing sedentary behavior and even objective measures such as accelerometers have problems, objective measures are considered more accurate and reliable relative to subjective measures. 45 , 46 , 47 Subjective measures may be affected by recall bias and social desirability bias, 45 , 48 which may substantially affect results. Conversely, objective measures cannot separate sedentary behavior according to domain (e.g., leisure, work, transportation) or type (e.g., television watching, driving, computer work). Future research should use objective measures of sedentary behavior when possible, either on its own or in combination with subjective measures. Additionally, the definition of sedentary behavior varied across studies, with broader definitions encompassing all activity below two METs and more limited definitions covering time spent sitting at work. We excluded many studies that also conceptualized sedentary behavior as low levels of physical activity, despite evidence that sedentary behavior and physical activity are independent risk factors for many disease outcomes. 24 This misclassification in the literature makes assessing relationships between sedentary behavior and outcomes more challenging. Of the studies that conducted multivariable analysis, there was wide variety in the covariates included for adjustment. The variable most frequently adjusted for in analyses was BMI, but otherwise, confounder selection differed across studies. In terms of demographic and socioeconomic variables, age was adjusted for in six studies, and only two studies 33 , 36 adjusted for race and one 36 for an income variable (poverty‐to‐income ratio). Physical activity was taken into account in only one study. 39 Given that sedentary behavior is emerging as an independent risk factor from physical activity for many chronic diseases, adjustment for physical activity in future studies may be beneficial. None of the studies adjusted for comorbidities known to be risk factors for pre‐eclampsia and other gestational disorders of pregnancy, such as chronic (non‐gestational) hypertension and type 2 diabetes. As with other systematic reviews, the present review has limitations due to the availability, quality, and heterogeneity of the identified primary studies. First, the methods and units for quantifying sedentary behavior varied across studies and many relied on self‐reported sedentary behavior. Second, we identified few studies that met our inclusion criteria. Further research on this topic may provide further detail about the relationship of interest. Additionally, most studies included in the present systematic review were conducted in western counties. The generalizability of the findings may not apply to other settings with varying socioeconomic and lifestyle patterns. Finally, all included studies were observational as no experimental evidence was found. Future high‐quality randomized trial evidence may provide a higher level of certainty regarding the relationship of interest. 6. CONCLUSIONS Overall, we found little evidence in the present systematic review to suggest a relationship between sedentary behavior and pre‐eclampsia. Additional high‐quality observational studies or randomized trials are warranted to reach more definitive conclusions. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS LXP and SM contributed to the conception and design of the systematic review, registered the protocol in PROSPERO, conducted the literature search, extracted and interpreted the data, conducted quality assessment, and drafted the manuscript. All authors reviewed, revised, and approved the final manuscript. FUNDING INFORMATION There was no dedicated funding for the present review. LXP and SM are independently supported by Canadian Institutes of Health Research Doctoral Awards. Funding agencies had no role in the conceptualization, design, data extraction, interpretation, or writing of the manuscript. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors have no conflicts of interest. Supporting information Data S1. IJGO-171-106-s002.pdf (329.9KB, pdf) Appendix S1. IJGO-171-106-s001.docx (48.2KB, docx) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS We thank Ursula Ellis, reference librarian, University of British Columbia, for her assistance with the literature search strategy and Lorri Puil, editor, Cochrane Hypertension Group, for her teaching on systematic review methods in health research during the graduate coursework of LXP and SM. Pei LX, Marozoff S. Sedentary behavior during pregnancy and risk of pre‐eclampsia and/or gestational hypertension: A systematic review. Int J Gynecol Obstet. 2025;171:106‐123. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.70193 DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT Data sharing is no