INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1689,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12447692','Comparison of the effectiveness and pregnancy outcomes of the "incise and suture" versus "fold and suture" methods in hysteroscopic-assisted laparoscopic treatment of previous cesarean scar defect: A retrospective cohort study.','Wu S, Hu W, Zeng X, Yi S, Wang X, Zhang Q, Wang H, Li Y, Jiang J, Xiao S.','2025','incised pcsd pcsd xiangya incised incised incised pcsd climb pcsd pcsd pcsd pcsd pcsd pcsd pcsd diverticula levonorgestrel lng ius electrocautery electrocoagulation heteroperitoneal pcsd pcsd incised pcsd xiangya hysterolaparoscopic hysterolaparoscopic xiangya pcsd xiangya pcsd dissect peritonealize incised excise electrocoagulation reinstating incised incised pcsd pcsd incised incised amenorrheic incised incised incised incised incised incised pcsd pcsd pcsd shortens incised incised diverticula incised incised incised incised diverticula reformation incised incised deepest incise shiying weiwei betran e005671 boza gorgen laparoscopically minim allornuvor nobuta hanada e12532 antila langsjo maenpaa huhtala frishman underrecognized minim bij vaate voet naji levonorgestrel intermenstrual isthmoplasty dosedla calda vervoort vissers hehenkamp brolmann huirne maetzold voorhis jordans ipm stegwee nobuta e12431 donnez donnez donnez orellana dolmans karampelas birbarah nisolle mashiach minim','Abstract Objective To investigate the clinical efficacy of the hysteroscopic‐assisted laparoscopic incised‐suture versus folded‐suture methods at the cesarean incision site for the treatment of previous cesarean scar defects (PCSD) and to evaluate pregnancy outcomes. Methods This was a retrospective analysis of 95 patients with PCSD who underwent hysteroscopic‐assisted laparoscopic surgery between June 2021 and September 2024 at the Third Xiangya Hospital of Central South University. Results There were no significant differences in population characteristics such as age and number of cesarean sections ( P > 0.05). The folded‐suture group had shorter operation time (39.03 ± 1.17 vs. 60.28 ± 1.14 min, P < 0.001) and volume of intraoperative hemorrhage (5.00, 2.00 vs. 20.00, 10.00 mL, P < 0.001). There were no statistically significant variations in the cure or effective rates regarding menstrual abnormalities between the two groups at 3 months, 6 months, 1‐year post‐surgery and the terms of overall clinical efficacy ( P > 0.05). The overall effective rates for the incised‐ and folded‐suture groups were 76.4% and 75.0%, respectively. In comparison to the incised‐suture group, the desire to maintain postoperative fertility in the folded‐suture group was significantly greater (odds ratio [OR] 8.33, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 2.27–30.58, P < 0.001), and the average time after surgery to pregnancy was shorter (5.60 ± 0.72 vs. 11.00 ± 2.70 months, P < 0.05). No substantial difference was observed in the pregnancy outcomes between the two groups. Conclusion Hysteroscopic‐assisted laparoscopic cesarean incised and folded suturing are both effective, with folded suturing being more safe and more suitable for patients requiring short‐term fertility. Keywords: clinical effectiveness, hysteroscopy, laparoscopy, previous cesarean scar defect (PCSD), surgical approach 1. INTRODUCTION In recent years, the number of cesarean sections performed each year has been rising, which has led to a serious global public health issue. China has implemented a variety of interventions to address the high incidence of cesarean section, such as promoting natural childbirth through the implementation of comprehensive measures such as nonpharmacologic analgesia, standardizing the use of oxytocin 1 and so on. These measures have been effective to a certain extent; however, since China''s “three‐child” policy came into effect, the number of cesarean sections performed has continued to climb, and the phenomena of old age and pregnancies among women who have previously had one have become more noticeable. 2 As such, physicians have been paying more attention to previous cesarean scar defect (PCSD), a prevalent long‐term consequence of cesarean sections. PCSD refers to the formation of a depression or diverticulum at the uterine incision that interacts with the uterine cavity following a surgical section in the lower part of the anterior wall of the uterus. 3 , 4 , 5 PCSD may lead to a range of symptoms, such as prolonged menstruation, chronic pelvic pain, and infertility, which affects the patient''s quality of life. 6 The etiology and risk factors for PCSD remain ambiguous. 8 Nevertheless, factors such as the frequency of cesarean sections, incision site, uterine orientation, past labor and delivery before cesarean section, and surgical technique may significantly influence the development of PCSD. 7 , 8 Transvaginal ultrasound, magnetic resonance imaging, hysteroscopy, and ultrasound combined with hysteroscopy can assist in diagnosing PCSD. 5 The treatment of PCSD is categorized into conservative management, medication and surgery. Patients with small cesarean incision diverticula, mild or asymptomatic symptoms, and no fertility requirements usually receive conservative management or undergo treatment with oral contraceptive pills or a levonorgestrel intrauterine extended‐release system (LNG‐IUS). 9 For patients who fail to respond to drug treatment or have fertility requirements, it is more suitable to choose surgical treatment. Prevalent surgical techniques encompass hysteroscopic electrocautery of the incision scar and electrocoagulation of the ectopic endometrium at the incision location, 10 laparoscopic or hybrid heteroperitoneal laparoscopic surgery for uterine repair and scar resection from the incision 11 , 12 and transvaginal surgical excision of the incision scar and uterus reconstruction. 13 There are no clear guidelines for the treatment of PCSD. As a result, the purpose of this study was to examine the clinical efficacy of two surgical techniques for the treatment of PCSD, namely the incised‐ and folded‐suture methods, and to assess the pregnancy outcomes of these patients. 2. MATERIALS AND METHODS 2.1. Patient details This was a retrospective cohort study of individuals ( n = 95) with PCSD who underwent laparoscopic surgery at the Gynecology Department of the Third Xiangya Hospital of Central South University from June 2021 to September 2024. Among them, 60 patients underwent combined hysterolaparoscopic incision and suturing, and 35 patients underwent combined hysterolaparoscopic folding and suturing. Data on each patient''s medical history, symptoms, auxiliary examinations, and intraoperative indicate were obtained, and the postoperative recovery of each patient was monitored over a follow‐up time of 1–24 months through outpatient visits and by telephone. This study was approved by the Ethics Committee of the Third Xiangya Hospital of Central South University. 2.2. Inclusion and exclusion criteria The inclusion criteria were as follows: (1) PCSD patients who underwent laparoscopic surgery at the Third Xiangya Hospital from June 2021 to September 2024, and (2) the patients had a confirmed diagnosis of PCSD by ultrasonography documented as part of the patient''s medical history. 14 , 15 The following were the exclusion criteria: (1) Abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB) patients with other established etiologies, (2) patients with acute systemic inflammation or severe postoperative infection, (3) patients suffering uncontrolled systemic diseases, (4) patients with malignant tumors or precancerous lesions, and (5) patients that were lost to follow‐up. A detailed patient selection flow chart is shown in Figure 1 . FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Patient selection flow chart. 2.3. Surgical methods Initially, hysteroscopy was conducted for both procedures to assess the nature and severity of the depression at the cesarean incision site, the presence of a viable flap at the inferior margin of the depression, and the retention of menstrual blood. Laparoscopic exploration of the pelvis was performed to dissect the adhesions, open the peritoneal reflection and mobilize downward the bladder. The location and extent of the cesarean incision diverticulum were observed using a hysteroscopic light source. For the folded‐suture method, successive folding sutures with a No. 2 knot‐free thread were used to close the myometrium and peritonealize the uterus. For another method, an ultrasonic knife was employed in the incised‐suture technique to excise the inferior section of the anterior uterine wall at the cesarean scar, execute electrocoagulation of the residual endothelium at the cesarean incision site, and conduct continuous suturing of the myometrial wound edges using a No. 2 knot‐free suture. After the completion of either method of suture closure, the uterine position was adjusted to an anterior tilt and anterior flexion, and the cesarean section incision site was basically flat on repeated hysteroscopic examination. 2.4. Observation indicators The following observation indices were used: (1) Basic clinical data, including age, number of cesarean deliveries, duration from symptom onset to the most recent cesarean section, preoperative transvaginal ultrasonographic incision diverticulum width, depth, thickness, and peripheral normal myometrial thickness; (2) perioperative circumstances, encompassing intraoperative hemorrhage, operative time (the amount of time the surgeon spends performing surgery on the uterus), and operative complications; (3) postoperative effectiveness metrics, including the duration of the menstrual cycle following surgery, postoperative transvaginal ultrasonographic incision diverticulum width, depth, thickness, and thickness of the surrounding normal myometrium; (4) postoperative fertility needs; and (5) postoperative pregnancy, such as the average time from surgery to pregnancy, mode of conception, and pregnancy outcome. For the evaluation of efficacy, the patient was considered (1) cured if her menstrual cycle returned to normal; (2) enhanced if the duration of monthly bleeding was markedly reduced by over 3 days relative to the preoperative period, although without reinstating the total number of menstruation days to the prior count; and (3) ineffective if there was no shortening of the menstrual time compared with the preoperative period or if the duration of shortening was less than 3 days. The effective rate was defined as the aggregate of the cure and improvement rates. 16 2.5. Statistical analysis Categorical data are represented as frequencies and percentages within each group and were examined using the chi‐square test. Data that follow a normal distribution are represented as the mean ± standard deviation (SD) and were evaluated using the student''s t ‐test. Data that do not follow a normal distribution are represented by medians and ranges and were evaluated using the Mann–Whitney U test. The Statistical Analysis System 9.4 (SAS Institute, Cary, NC, USA) was utilized for statistical analysis. A statistically significant difference was indicated by a cutoff value of P less than 0.05. 3. RESULTS 3.1. Population characteristics This study comprised 95 patients in total, with a follow‐up time ranging from 1 month to 2 years. Among them, 60 patients were treated with laparoscopic incised‐suturing of cesarean scar diverticulum combined with hysteroscopy, and 35 patients were treated with laparoscopic folded‐suturing of cesarean scar diverticulum combined with hysteroscopy. The preoperative characteristics of the 95 patients are summarized in Table 1 . TABLE 1. Preoperative characteristics of the participants. Variable Incised‐suture group ( n = 60) Folded‐suture group ( n = 35) P value Age (years) 35.12 ± 4.46 35.17 ± 4.61 0.959 Number of cesarean sections 2.00 (1.00) 1.00 (1.00) 0.140 Menstrual period before the prolongation (days) 6.26 ± 0.21 6.25 (2.00) 0.647 Menstrual period before surgery (days) 13.03 ± 0.57 13.17 ± 1.15 0.911 Time since last cesarean section (months) 5.00 (30.75) 12.00 (57.00) 0.236 Preoperative PCSD parameters using vaginal B ultrasound Residual myometrium (mm) 2.10 (1.00) 2.00 (1.10) 0.902 Peripheral myometrium (mm) 10.40 ± 3.34 9.69 ± 3.18 0.444 Width (mm) 9.50 (7.25) 7.50 (4.50) 0.848 Depth (mm) 7.50 (5.00) 7.91 ± 3.31 0.586 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: PCSD, previous cesarean scar defect. There was no significant difference in age, number of cesarean sections, premenstrual period before the prolongation of menstruation, preoperative menstrual period, or time from the last cesarean section to the onset of symptoms between the two groups. The parameters of the defect measured by vaginal B‐ultrasound prior to surgery are displayed in Table 1 . No significant differences in the width, depth, thickness or thickness of the peripheral normal myometrium were found between the incised‐ and folded‐suture groups. 3.2. Surgical conditions and clinical effectiveness of postoperative menstruation Table 2 displays the surgical circumstances as well as the clinical effectiveness of postoperative menstruation. Significant differences were displayed in the operative times (60.28 ± 1.14 vs. 39.03 ± 1.17 min, P < 0.001) and the volume of intraoperative hemorrhage (20.00, 10.00 vs. 5.00, 2.00 mL, P < 0.001) between the two groups, but no significant differences in the two groups'' postoperative menstrual period were detected ( P > 0.05). None of the patients experienced intra‐ or postoperative complications. TABLE 2. Surgical conditions and outcomes. Variable Incised‐suture group ( n = 60) Folded‐suture group ( n = 35) P value Surgical outcomes Operative times (min) a 60.28 ± 1.14 39.03 ± 1.17 <0.001 Intraoperative blood loss (mL) 20.00 (10.00) 5.00 (2.00) <0.001 Menstrual period after surgery (days) 8.50 (5.25) 9.0 (5.00) 0.902 Clinical efficacy after surgery ( n , %) 3 months Cure 14 (26.4) 8 (30.8) 0.685 Efficacy 42 (79.2) 18 (75.0) 0.677 Six months Cure 13 (29.5) 7 (36.8) 0.568 Efficacy 35 (79.5) 13 (81.3) 1.000 One year Cure 13 (37.1) 4 (33.3) 1.000 Efficacy 27 (77.1) 8 (80.0) 1.000 Total efficacy 42 (76.4) 19 (76.0) 0.972 Open in a new tab a The amount of time the surgeon spends performing surgery on the uterus. Postoperatively, three patients were amenorrheic and received a pharmacologic amenorrhea regimen. The cure rates of abnormal menstruation in the two groups at 3 months, 6 months and 1 year after the operation were 26.4% versus 30.8%, 29.5% versus 36.8% and 37.1% versus 33.3%, respectively. The effective rates of abnormal menstruation in the two groups at 3 months, 6 months and 1 year after the operation were 79.2% versus 75.0%, 79.5% versus 81.3%, and 77.1% versus 80.0%, respectively, which were not significantly different. In terms of overall clinical efficacy, the effective rate in the incision and suture group was slightly higher than that in the other group (76.4% vs. 76.0%), but there was no significant difference (odds ratio [OR] 0.98, 95% confidence interval [CI]: 0.32–2.97, P = 1.000). 3.3. Postoperative fertility needs The patients'' postoperative fertility needs and intended conception approaches are shown in Table 3 . There were more patients with fertility needs in the folded‐suture group than in the incised‐suture group (OR 8.33, 95% CI: 2.27–30.58, P < 0.001). More women preferred natural conception (OR 11.28, 95% CI: 1.28–99.39, P < 0.05). TABLE 3. Postoperative fertility needs. Variable Incised‐suture group ( n = 60) Folded‐suture group ( n = 35) P value Have fertility requirements ( n , %) 30 (50) 25 (89.3) <0.001 Intended conception approach ( n , %) Natural 29 (96.7) 18 (72.0) 0.028 Assisted (IVF‐ET) 1 (3.3) 7 (28.0) 0.028 Open in a new tab 3.4. Postoperative pregnancy outcomes Five patients in the incised‐suture group and 10 patients in the folded‐suture group experienced subsequent pregnancies, and their outcomes are shown in Table 4 . The mean time from surgery to pregnancy was shorter in the folded‐suture group (5.60 ± 0.72 vs. 11.00 ± 2.70 months, P < 0.05). TABLE 4. Postoperative pregnancy outcomes. Variable Incised‐suture group ( n = 5) Folded‐suture group ( n = 10) P value Average time to pregnancy (months) 11.00 ± 2.70 5.60 ± 0.72 0.024 Conception approach ( n , %) Natural 5 (100) 6 (60.0) 0.302 Assisted (IVF‐ET) 0 4 (40.0) 0.302 Outcomes of pregnancy ( n , %) Spontaneous abortion 0 1 (10) 0.667 Legally induced abortion 2 (40.0) 0 0.095 Delivery 2 (66.7) 1 (10) 0.242 Open in a new tab In the incised‐suture group, all five patients had spontaneous pregnancies, and in the folded suture group, four of 10 patients conceived through assisted reproduction. In the incised‐suture group, two patients successfully delivered at >37 weeks of gestation, and two patients underwent a legally induced abortion. In the folded‐suture group, there was only one successful delivery, and only one patient underwent curettage for a missed abortion. The remaining eight patients were still pregnant. There was no significant difference in the two groups'' conception approach or pregnancy outcome ( P > 0.05). 4. DISCUSSION PCSD is a long‐term cesarean section complication that may present with a variety of symptoms, including abnormal uterine hemorrhage, postmenstrual bleeding, dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic pain, and secondary infertility and other symptoms. 5 , 8 , 17 , 18 Surgical treatment for PCSD is advocated in principle because PCSD can seriously affect the quality of life of patients. The current surgical treatment methods include hysteroscopy, hysteroscopy combined with laparoscopy, and transvaginal repair surgery. 5 , 17 The residual myometrial thickness at the incision site is a major factor in determining the choice of surgical method, according to the consensus statement of the Chinese Medical Association''s Section on Family Planning. Hysteroscopic surgical treatment is a feasible and safe method with less surgical trauma and faster postoperative recovery when the thickness of the residual myometrium at the incision site is greater than 3 mm. However, laparoscopic surgery should be preferred when the thickness of the myometrium is less than 3 mm because of the risk of uterine perforation and bladder injury, and resection of the cesarean section scar diverticulum and suturing are frequently utilized in clinical practice at present. Some studies 18 , 20 , 21 have confirmed that this method of scar reconstruction can effectively increase the residual myometrial thickness, remove abnormal blood vessels, and reduce the risk of uterine rupture in subsequent pregnancy. At present, a new type of hysteroscopy‐combined laparoscopy is being used to increase the thickness of the residual muscle layer by splicing and suturing the scar''s upper and lower edges. 22 , 23 This approach makes surgery less difficult, shortens the operation time, reduces intraoperative bleeding and surgical trauma, reduces the risk of surgical infection, decreases the risk of surgical complications, prevents the formation of a new scar, and preserves the integrity of the uterus. Some studies 23 , 24 have shown that this surgical method can increase the thickness of the residual myometrium within a short period after surgery, improve menstrual abnormalities such as prolonged periods and provide shorter postoperative contraception. The 2019 expert consensus of the Chinese Medical Association proposed that pregnancy can be planned as appropriate 6 months after folded‐suture surgery, while contraception is recommended for 1–2 years after incised‐suture surgery. 20 This is an advantage for patients with fertility needs that are more urgent. In this study, two surgical styles, incised‐ and folded‐suture, were adopted according to the indications and needs of the patients. The results showed that there were no significant differences in age, time from the onset of symptoms to the last cesarean section, or the thickness of the residual myometrium and the surrounding normal myometrium, which were not influencing factors for the choice of surgical method. When the degree of separation of the upper and lower edges of the uterine diverticula is small and the residual muscular layer is thick, considering the reduction in uterine trauma, the avoidance of the formation of new scars and the reduction in the risk of repeated scarring defects, 24 and the patient''s strong desire to conceive again due to a low number of births, folded‐suture surgery seems to be more appropriate. For diverticulum with a large degree of separation of the upper and lower edges and a thin residual muscular layer, if the folding suture is insufficient in suture strength or poor tissue healing, it may lead to recurrence of diverticulum. In this case, incised suture is more effective in increasing the thickness of the residual muscular layer. In terms of surgical safety, our study found the operation time for the folded‐suture group was approximately 20 min shorter than that of the incised‐suture group, where there was less bleeding in during the operation, and neither group had intra‐ or postoperative complications. Overall, the folded suture technique was found to be safer than the incision suture technique; however, more research data are needed to explore the safety of the two methods. Moreover, in response to the efficiency of the surgery, the overall improvement rates of postoperative menstruation were 76.4% and 76.0% in the incised‐suture and folded‐suture groups, respectively, and the postoperative contraceptive time in the folded‐suture group was about half a year shorter than that in the incised‐suture group. Most of the patients benefited after surgery; however, since folded‐suture surgery has been practiced in our hospital only for the last 3 years and is only performed by an experienced chief physician in our hospital, fewer patients underwent folded‐suture surgery, and the symptoms of some of the patients were not improved; additionally, some were lost to follow‐up or currently pregnant. Therefore, it was not possible to compare pregnancy outcomes between the two groups, so further studies are needed. Therefore, how can the appropriate operation be chosen for the patient''s own situation? According to the existing studies, for patients who need laparoscopic surgery, if the patient has short‐term fertility requirements, a strong desire to have children, a small uterine diverticulum or thick residual muscular layer of the uterine diverticula, the folded suture that preserves the integrity of the uterus, reduces uterine trauma, and prevents reformation of the scar is recommended. If the patient has no desire for fertility or no short‐term desire to have children and the diverticulum is large or the residual muscular layer is thin, the incised suture can completely remove the scar tissue and effectively increase the myometrium''s thickness of the lower part of the uterus; however, before the operation, the patient needs to be informed of the risk of recurrence of the scar diverticulum, and there may be little improvement in the menstrual period or even further prolongation of menstruation after the operation. In conclusion, the hysteroscopic‐assisted laparoscopic cesarean incised‐ and folded‐suture methods are both effective, and folded‐suture surgery is more safe and more appropriate for patients with short‐term fertility needs and stronger desire to conceive. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS All authors have accepted responsibility for the entire content of this manuscript and approved its submission. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT The authors state no conflict of interest. ACKNOWLEDGMENTS Our clinical research would not have been possible without the help of all the doctors who helped to collect the original data, along with permission from the participants for ongoing research. The authors would like to express the deepest gratitude to participants who participated in this study by providing their valuable information and informed consent. Wu S, Hu W, Zeng X, et al. Comparison of the effectiveness and pregnancy outcomes of the “incise and suture” versus “fold and suture” methods in hysteroscopic‐assisted laparoscopic treatment of previous cesarean scar defect: A retrospective cohort study. Int J Gynecol Obstet. 2025;171:453‐459. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.70191 Shiying Wu and Weiwei Hu contributed equally to the study. DATA AVAILABILITY STATEMENT Data is available from the authors upon reasonable request. REFERENCES 1. Cheng X, Sun Z. The influence of fertility policy adjustment on the mode of cesarean section and the management strategy under the three‐child policy. Chin J Eugenics Genet. 2022;30(8):1296‐1299. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Betran AP, Ye J, Moller AB, Souza JP, Zhang J. Trends and projections of caesarean section rates: global and regional estimates. BMJ Glob Health. 2021;6(6):e005671. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Api M, Boza A, Gorgen H, Api O. Should cesarean scar defect Be treated laparoscopically? A case report and review of the literature. J Minim Invasive Gynecol. 2015;22(7):1145‐1152. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. Allornuvor GF, Xue M, Zhu X, Xu D. The definition, aetiology, presentation, diagnosis and management of previous caesarean scar defects. J Obstet Gynaecol. 2013;33(8):759‐763. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Tsuji S, Nobuta Y, Hanada T, et al. Prevalence, definition, and etiology of cesarean scar defect and treatment of cesarean scar disorder: a narrative review. Reprod Med Biol. 2023;22(1):e12532. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 6. Antila‐Långsjö RM, Mäenpää JU, Huhtala HS, Tomás EI, Staff SM. Cesarean scar defect: a prospective study on risk factors. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2018;219(5):458.e1‐458.e8. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 7. Tower AM, Frishman GN. Cesarean scar defects: an underrecognized cause of abnormal uterine bleeding and other gynecologic complications. J Minim 