INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1690,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12447669','Can shoulder dystocia be predicted before operative vaginal delivery using a score that includes ultrasonographic head-perineum distance measurement?','Renon P, Drumez E, Sanchez M, Labreuche J, Garabedian C.','2025','perineum ovd unicentric lille ovd cephalic multiclass penalized imputations ovd perineum multiparous perineum ovd perineum perineum oasis ovd clavicle humerus oasis ovd perineum ovd tsur kasbaoui ovd lille ovd ovd perineum ovd spatulas ovd hm70a perineum labia majora fourchette intruding perineum mcroberts suprapubic nonoccipito ovd ovd vaginally multiparity cerog obst penalized imputations chained imputations resamples overoptimism overoptimism deciles ovd nulliparity nonoccipito ovd nonoccipito spatulas ovd perineum ovd nonlog ovd discriminator oasis multiparity perineum overoptimism multiparous ovd multiparity kasbaoui perineum nulliparity nonoccipito heretofore øverland multiparity palatnik heinonen seif duewel circumferences antenatally cephalic tsur splits ovd ovd ovd unicentric ovd ovd prelabor ovd ovd ovd ovd renon drumez labreuche garabedian perineum rcog rcog poujade azria ceccaldi ouzounian usman hanidu kovalenko sonopartogram s997 s1016 eggebø sonopartogram sonopartogram akmal tsoi kametas nicolaides eggebø gjessing heien prelabor lanzarone kahrs usman ghi tsur batsry toussia kasbaoui severac aissi ghi eggebø isuog fws bauman mohsin heinze pine forge pine forge buuren groothuis oudshoorn chained softw jstatsoft v45 i03 royston kgm vergouwe b604 royston massaro kgm jan07 g7594 youssefzadeh tavakoli panchal mandelbaum ouzounian dhiman e074819 øverland vatten eskild palatnik grobman hellendag janetos gossett heinonen saisto gissler kaijomaa sarvilinna seif tadbiri crimmins s261 s262 duewel doehmen dittkrist hockley impey','Abstract Objective The main study objective was to develop a novel shoulder dystocia (SD) prediction score using ultrasound‐based head–perineum distance measured before an operative vaginal delivery (OVD). Methods This retrospective unicentric study (Lille, France) included all cases of OVD of singleton pregnancies from March 2019 to October 2020, with cephalic presentation and > 37 weeks of gestation, for which intrapartum sonography was performed. A multiclass‐penalized logistic regression model was used to develop the SD prognostic score, with missing values imputed by multiple imputations. Results Among the 1708 patients with OVD, 773 who underwent ultrasound for head–perineum distance were included. SD occurred in 99 cases (12.8%). The SD''s predicting factors (and their weights) included the following: maternal age younger than 28 years (3 points); multiparous (4 points); induced labor (4 points); gestational diabetes (3 points); and head–perineum distance without pressure (≤20 mm [−2 points], using 21–30 mm as reference, 31–40 mm [2 points], 41–50 mm [4 points], 51–60 mm [6 points], and >60 mm [8 points]). Three patient risk subgroups were categorized as score range (occurrence percentage) as low risk: < 3 (< 10%), high risk: 3–8 (10%–20%), and very high risk: > 8 (> 20%). Conclusion The developed scoring system may help predict SD occurrence during OVD using five delivery room parameters. Replication with other populations and prospective cohorts will be needed for validation. Keywords: childbirth, forceps, head–perineum distance, intrapartum sonography, macrosomia labor dystocia, operative vaginal delivery, shoulder dystocia, ultrasound, vacuum Abbreviations AUC area under curve BMI body mass index HPD head‐perineum distance IQR inter‐quartile range OASIS obstetrical anal sphincter injuries OR odds‐ratio OVD operative vaginal delivery ROC receiver operating characteristics SD shoulder dystocia US ultrasonography 1. INTRODUCTION Shoulder dystocia (SD) can cause significant obstetrical complications, including both fetal and maternal morbidities. Fetal morbidities from SD may include transient or permanent Erb palsy, clavicle or humerus fracture, perinatal asphyxia, hypoxic–ischemic encephalopathy, and neonatal death. 1 , 2 Maternal morbidities may include obstetrical anal sphincter injuries (OASIS), postpartum hemorrhage, and psychological distress, including acute stress and posttraumatic stress disorder. Thus, several SD risk factors have been identified and evaluated, with the three primary factors being a history of SD, fetal macrosomia, and maternal diabetes. 3 Other factors have been highlighted, including excessive maternal weight or weight gain, operative vaginal delivery (OVD), and induced labor; however, none have been rigorously evaluated for their causal link with SD. Intrapartum ultrasonography (US) can help predict complications during labor for vaginal delivery. 4 , 5 For instance, it can be used to accurately assess fetal head position, in contrast to digital vaginal examination, which is incorrect 51% of the time. 6 Furthermore, transperineal US can improve decisions concerning delivery type, including measuring the head–perineum distance (HPD). 7 , 8 A prospective European study determined that transperineal sonography could predict vacuum extraction outcomes among a cohort of women with slow progress in second‐stage labor. 9 Determining SD risk factors in patients with OVD is challenging. Several studies have established that some risk factors can predict SD occurrence during operative or surgical delivery with high accuracy. For instance, Tsur et al. 10 developed a machine learning model based on fetal and maternal characteristics that can predict SD during vaginal delivery with high accuracy (ie, specificity: 0.92, sensitivity: 0.52; area under the receiver operating characteristic [ROC] curve [AUC], 0.866). Measurement of fetal head station by transperineal US during second‐stage labor may be another effective tool for predicting SD occurrence. Intrapartum US has already become relevant for predicting vaginal delivery outcomes. For example, Kasbaoui et al. 11 showed that HPD >40 mm is significantly associated with the occurrence of a difficult extraction, based on a composite criterion and after adjusting for parity, head position, and fetal macrosomia. However, HPD measurement has not yet been specifically evaluated for predicting SD. Thus, the study aim was to develop a novel score‐based system to predict SD occurrence during OVD using HPD measurement. 2. MATERIALS AND METHODS 2.1. Study design This monocentric, retrospective study was conducted from March 2019 to October 2020 at Lille (France). The study''s protocol follows the STROBE (Strengthening the Reporting of Observational Studies in Epidemiology) guidelines 12 (see Table S1 ). We included data from all patients with singleton pregnancies and an OVD at ≥37 weeks of gestation who underwent US‐measured HPD to evaluate fetal station. 2.2. Research methods The birth attendant was a resident supervised by a senior obstetrician. Before every OVD, the fetal position and fetal head station (ie, low, medium, or high level) were assessed by both vaginal examination and transperineal US. Intrapartum US is routine in our center, and all medical staff are trained in its performance. Transperineal US was used to measure HPD without placing pressure on the perineum. Appropriate OVD (eg, vacuum, forceps, or spatulas) choice relies on the attendant''s evaluation of the obstetrical situation, which combines the context (parity, labor, fetal heart rate), fetal head station, and fetal position. All these data are routinely reported in patient records. Only vaginal deliveries were included herein (thus, all cesarean sections associated with failed OVD were excluded). US was performed using a portable machine always available within the birth center (Samsung HM70A). Fetal position was determined by abdominal approach, to identify head and spine positions. 6 Then, the abdominal transducer was covered with a sterile glove and positioned horizontally on the perineum, between the labia majora in the posterior fourchette, to achieve a coronal view without intruding into the genital tract. 13 HPD was measured in a frontal transperineal scan as the shortest distance from the outer bony limit of the fetal skull to the perineum. The clinical relevance of this examination is assessed at the discretion of the birth attendant and thus not realized as a systematic practice. The primary outcome was the occurrence of SD, defined as at least one additional obstetrical maneuver, other than gentle downward head traction, for shoulder delivery. Adopting the McRoberts position or suprapubic pressure were considered additional maneuvers. SD was diagnosed and a maneuver was only applied after the head had been delivered, no spontaneous rotation of the head had occurred during the following contractions, and at least 1 min had passed. 1 Candidate predictors for inclusion in a point‐based SD prediction scoring system were maternal characteristics (age, parity, obesity, previous cesarean section, and gestational diabetes); delivery characteristics (labor induction, nonoccipito‐anterior fetal position, fetal head station [mid 0 to +1; low +2 to +3; outlet +4 to +5], OVD instrumentation, and sequential OVD); and US‐measured HPD without pressure. Parity is defined as the number of previous deliveries after 22 weeks of amenorrhea, 14 whether occurring vaginally or surgically. In this study, multiparity was assessed when at least one previous delivery occurred after 22 weeks. Written informed consent was obtained from every participant and the study and database were approved by the National Committee of Research in Obstetrics and Gynecology (CEROG #2020‐OBST‐0301, 01/05/2020). 2.3. Statistical analysis Categorical variables are expressed as frequency (percentage). Quantitative variables are expressed as mean ± standard deviation or, when not normally distributed, median (25th–75th percentile). Normality of distribution was checked graphically and by applying the Shapiro–Wilk test. Main maternal and fetal characteristics were compared according to SD status (presence versus absence) in bivariate analyses, using χ 2 test (or Fisher exact test when expected cell frequency was < 5) for categorical variables and Student t test (or Mann–Whitney U test for non‐Gaussian distribution) for quantitative variables. To develop the prognostic score of SD, all prespecified candidate predictors (irrespective of bivariate analysis results) were considered for inclusion in multivariable penalized logistic regression model using the Firth method. 15 Before developing multivariate analysis, the log‐linearity assumption was checked for all quantitative candidate predictors using restricted cubic spline functions. 16 Since the log‐linearity assumption was not established for maternal age, it was treated as a binary variable using a cutoff value that maximized the Youden index from ROC curve analysis. We also assessed the absence of collinearity between candidate predictors by calculating the variance inflation factors. 17 The full model was simplified using a stepwise backward selection procedure with a removal criterion of P > 0.05. To avoid case deletion in multivariate analysis due to missing data for candidate predictors (range, 0–6.9%, yielding a case deletion of 10.9%), missing data were imputed by multiple imputations using the regression switching approach (chained equation, m = 25 imputations). 18 The imputation procedure was performed with the missing‐at‐random assumption using SD status and all candidate predictors, with the predictive mean‐matching method for quantitative variables and logistic regression (binary, ordinal, or multinomial) models for categorical variables. Rubin rules were used to combine estimates derived from multiple imputed data sets. 19 The performance of the selected prognostic model was examined by assessing discrimination and calibration in each imputed data set. Discrimination was assessed using the C statistic, which corresponds to the AUC. The C statistic for new scores should be in the 0.60 to 0.85 range. 20 To address the problem of overestimation in the selected model, 16 we performed a bootstrap internal validation (2000 bootstrap resamples) to correct the C statistic for overoptimism and calculate the shrinkage factor. We reported the median (range) C statistic, corrected for overoptimism among the 25 imputed data sets. 21 Calibration was assessed in each imputed data set by applying the Hosmer–Lemeshow goodness‐of‐fit test 22 and plotting observed probability against predicted probability in deciles. For clinical purposes, a point‐based scoring system was determined using regression coefficients of the selected prognostic model; the number of points was determined using the Framingham risk development method (see Supporting Information Appendix A for detailed calculations). 23 The development of the prediction model follows the TRIPOD (Transparent Reporting of a Multivariable Prediction Model for Individual Prognosis or Diagnosis) guidelines 24 (Table S2 ). All tests were two‐tailed and the threshold for statistical significance was P < 0.05. All analyses were performed using SAS software version 9.4 (SAS Institute Inc.). 3. RESULTS Among the 1708 OVD cases, SD occurred in 167 patients (9.7%). However, 935 patients were excluded because the HPD measurement was not performed or missing from the patient record. A final cohort of 773 patients (45.3%) met the inclusion criteria and underwent analyses; among these, SD occurred in 99 patients (12.8%) (Figure 1 ). FIGURE 1. Open in a new tab Flowchart. Maternal and neonatal characteristics of the two groups are shown in Table 1 . Differences between the SD and non‐SD groups were nulliparity (68.7% versus 82.5%, P = 0.001), obesity (26.3% versus 11.9%, P < 0.001), gestational diabetes (26.3% versus 16.2%, P = 0.015), labor induction (55.7% versus 35.1%, P < 0.001), nonoccipito‐anterior fetal position (28.3% versus 19.4%, P = 0.042), and mid fetal station (34.3% versus 18%, P < 0.001). TABLE 1. Maternal and fetal characteristics in the groups. Shoulder dystocia ( n = 99) No shoulder dystocia ( n = 674) P value Maternal characteristics Age, y Mean ± standard deviation 29.4 ± 5.7 29.7 ± 4.8 0.53 < 28 a 38/99 (38.4) 199/653 (30.5) 0.11 Nulliparous 68/99 (68.7) 556/674 (82.5) 0.001 BMI > 30 kg/m 2 26/99 (26.3) 80/674 (11.9) < 0.001 Previous cesarean section 15/99 (15.2) 68/674 (10.1) 0.13 History of shoulder dystocia 2/88 (2.0) 2/674 (0.3) NA Gestational diabetes 25/95 (26.3) 101/625 (16.2) 0.015 Delivery characteristics Induction labor 49/88 (55.7) 222/632 (35.1) < 0.001 Epidural 99/99 (100.0) 666/671 (99.3) NA Indication of OVD 0.011 Fetal heart rate abnormalities 62/98 (63.3) 506/672 (75.3) Arrest disorder 36/98 (36.7) 166/672 (24.7) Nonoccipito‐anterior fetal position 28/99 (28.3) 131/674 (19.4) 0.042 Fetal head station < 0.001 Outlet station 6/99 (6.1) 72/674 (10.7) Low station 59/99 (59.6) 481/674 (71.4) Mid station 34/99 (34.3) 121/674 (18.0) HPD without pressure, mm 44.3 ± 12.3 38.5 ± 11.8 < 0.001 Operative delivery instrument 0.60 Vacuum 52/99 (52.5) 367/674 (54.5) Forceps 45/99 (45.5) 282/674 (41.8) Spatulas 2/99 (2.0) 25/674 (3.7) Sequential 24/99 (24.2) 113/672 (16.8) 0.071 Duration of OVD, min 9.0 (6.0–13.0) 7.0 (5.0–11.0) 0.076 Obstetrical anal sphincter injury III or IV 21/98 (21.4) 86/673 (12.8) 0.021 Postpartum hemorrhage > 500 mL 7/96 (7.3) 62/632 (9.5) 0.48 Neonatal characteristics Gestational age (weeks of amenorrhea) b 40.0 (39.0–40.0) 40.0 (39.0–41.0) 0.91 Apgar score < 7 (5 min) 3/98 (3.1) 7/673 (1.0) 0.12 Arterial pH < 7.1 24/96 (25.0) 125/666 (18.8) 0.15 Weight, g Mean ± standard deviation 3755 ± 383.3 3356 ± 440.5 < 0.001 Birth weight > 4000 22/80 (27.5) 34/510 (6.7) < 0.001 Open in a new tab Note : Values are described as number/total number (percentage) or median (25th to 75th percentile) unless otherwise indicated. Abbreviations: BMI, body mass index; HPD, head–perineum distance; NA, not available; OVD, operative vaginal delivery. a To accommodate the nonlog‐linear relationship with shoulder dystocia. b A total of 95 missing values ( n = 11 in shoulder dystocia group). Arrest disorder in OVD indication was the most significant discriminator between the SD and non‐SD groups (36.7% vs. 24.7%, P = 0.011). HPD without pressure was higher in the SD group compared with the non‐SD group (44.3 ± 12.3 cm versus 38.5 ± 11.8 cm, P < 0.001). More OASIS were observed in the SD group than in the non‐SD group (21.4% versus 12.8%, P = 0.021). Birth weight was higher in the SD group than in the non‐SD group (3755 ± 383.3 g versus 3356 ± 440.5 g, P < 0.001). Results of the multivariate analysis of candidate SD predictors to build a prediction scoring system are summarized in Table 2 , after handling missing values by multiple imputation. Maternal age younger than 28 years, multiparity, induced labor, HPD without pressure, and gestational diabetes were selected as independent predictors of SD, with good discrimination ability (median statistic among the 25 imputed data sets, 0.723; range, 0.705–0.731). TABLE 2. Predictive factors of shoulder dystocia. Outcomes OR (95% CI) P value Points b Age, y 0.023 < 28 1.71 (1.07–2.72) 3 ≥ 28 1.00 (reference) – 0 Nulliparous 0.007 No 1.98 (1.20–3.27) 4 Yes 1.00 (reference) – 0 Induction labor 0.002 No 1.00 (reference) – 0 Yes 2.07 (1.29–3.31) 4 HPD without pressure, mm 1.43 (1.18–1.73) a <0.001 – ≤ 20 −2 21–30 0 31–40 2 41–50 4 51–60 6 > 60 8 Gestational diabetes 0.043 No 1.00 (reference) – 0 Yes 1.72 (1.01 − 2.91) 3 Open in a new tab Note : Odds ratios (ORs), P values, and estimates were calculated after handling missing values (m = 25). Abbreviations: CI, confidence interval; HPD, head–perineum distance. a Calculated for a 10‐mm increase. b See Supporting Information for derivation of points from regression coefficients using the Framingham scoring system approach. The formula used to calculate the predicted probability of SD with the selected prognostic model (after shrinking the regression coefficients to improve predictions when applied to future patients) is presented in the Supporting Information Appendix S1 . In an internal validation by bootstrapping sampling, the discrimination ability of prognostic model remained good, with a median C statistic corrected for overoptimism of 0.685 (range, 0.675–0.695 from the imputed data sets). Calibration plots of the prognostic model of SD in each imputed data set are shown in Figure 2 . The predicted probability of SD was similar to the observed SD rate, indicating that the prognostic model was well calibrated. FIGURE 2. Open in a new tab Calibration plots for the prognostic model. A point‐based scoring system was built from the continuous prognostic model (Table 2 ), allocating values for each factor from −2 to 8 points, for a total score range from −2 to 22 points. The allocation was made as follows: maternal age younger than 28 years (3 points); multiparous (4 points); induced labor (4 points); gestational diabetes (3 points); and HPD without pressure (≤ 20 mm [−2 points], 21–30 mm [reference], 31–40 mm [2 points], 41–50 mm [4 points], 51–60 mm [6 points], and > 60 mm [8 points]). Table 3 presents the predicted probabilities for each item in this point‐based scoring system. We then categorized patient risk as low risk (< 3 points; <10%), high risk (3–8 points; 10%–20%), and very high risk (>8 points; >20%). TABLE 3. Predicted probability of shoulder dystocia by point scoring system. Points No. Predicted probabilities (%) Risk group –2 12 5.1 Low risk 0 41 6.7 1 10 7.7 2 79 8.8 3 30 10.0 High risk 4 96 11.4 5 58 13.0 6 78 14.7 7 72 16.6 8 67 18.7 9 67 21.0 Very high risk 10 43 23.6 11 31 26.3 12 22 29.2 13 21 32.3 14 16 35.6 15 11 39.0 16 8 42.5 17 9 46.1 18 2 49.7 Open in a new tab Note : Numbers were calculated as mean values of the 25 imputed data sets. Predicted probabilities are reported for the observed scores in the derivation cohort, estimated from regression coefficients (after applying the shrinkage factor, see Supporting Information Appendix A ) according to the approach described in Framingham. 23 Note : points = sum of risk factors. No. = number of cases that met the corresponding sum of risk factors 4. DISCUSSION 4.1. Main findings Our point‐based tool for predicting SD during OVD uses readily available intrapartum risk factors, supplemented by US measurement of HPD. The resulting point‐based risk score uses the presence or absence of maternal age younger than 28 years, multiparity, induced labor, gestational diabetes, and HPD without pressure. Three derived SD risk categories accounted for < 10% in the lowest score and 20% in the highest score. 4.2. Clinical relevance Few previous reports have described US measurement of engagement and SD occurrence. SD occurrence was considered by Kasbaoui et al. to be an “extraction difficulty”. 11 They found a mean HPD of 49 mm when SD occurred during vacuum delivery. Their composite criterion comprised US perineum–skull distance, nulliparity, nonoccipito‐anterior fetal position, and estimated birth weight > 4000 g. In multivariate analysis, they showed that higher HPD values had greater odds ratios (ORs) for SD, where HPD > 40 mm (OR, 2.38 [95% confidence interval (CI), 1.51–3.74], P = 0.0002) and HPD > 60 mm (OR, 3.02 [95% CI, 1.68–5.43], P = 0.0002). The link between SD and intrapartum sonography has been understudied, and such measurements have heretofore never been included in a published SD prediction score. The candidate predictive factors of this study are consistent with those found in the literature. For instance, in a Norwegian study, 28 Øverland et al. looked specifically at birth weight and parity, as SD risk factors. They found that multiparity was an independent risk factor, even for a second delivery (para 1: adjusted OR, 1.19 [95% CI, 1.13–1.24]; para ≥ 2: adjusted OR, 1.06 [95% CI, 1.0–1.12]). Furthermore, the induction of labor is also an independent risk factor in their analysis (adjusted OR, 1.15 [95% CI, 1.09–1.20]). Maternal age as a candidate risk factor is more controversial in the literature. Indeed, two previous reports 29 , 30 highlight that maternal age is a relevant candidate factor for SD. Palatnik et al. found in univariate analysis that the mean age of the patients statistically differed between an SD group and a non‐SD group (30.7 ± 4.8 years and 31.6 ± 5.1 years, respectively) ( P = 0.042). Nonetheless, in the multivariate analysis, maternal age was used to adjust the OR of others risk factors found in their population. Heinonen et al. showed that most of the SD cases were seen among women aged 20 to 39 years. They used maternal age as one of the five predictive factors to create their predictive point‐scoring system, among body mass index, diabetes, mode of delivery, and gestational age. Despite their findings, no multivariate analysis was performed in the statistical analysis. Finally, Seif et al. 31 reported that maternal age was not a predictive factor because no statistical difference was found in the median maternal age between their SD and non‐SD groups (26 years [IQR, 21–32 years] and 25 years [IQR, 21–30 years], respectively) ( P = 0.2). Despite these discrepancies, many scores have been proposed. Those including US measurements use only antenatal US, not labor measurements. In a multicentric retrospective German cohort of 15 361 vaginal deliveries, Duewel et al. 32 built a score with three continuous factors: estimated fetal weight ≥ 4250 g, the difference between abdominal and head circumferences ≥2.5 cm, and the presence of maternal diabetes. The risk score applied to the derivation cohort had an AUC of 0.685 (95% CI, 0.61–0.75; P < 0.001) and the validation cohort had an AUC of 0.707 (95% CI, 0.63–0.77; P < 0.001). The authors concluded that an easily applied tool using routine US measurements is required to antenatally evaluate SD risk. Other authors have proposed different scores based on maternal, obstetrical, and fetal parameters. For example, Gupta et al. 33 conducted a retrospective cohort study of singleton vaginal cephalic deliveries at ≥ 36 weeks of gestation. Their sample included 40 284 births, with 240 SD occurrences (risk 0.6%). They compared four risk prediction models: (A) estimated birth weight alone; (B) estimated birth weight plus all significant independent antenatal risk factors (maternal height); (C) estimated birth weight plus all significant independent antenatal and intrapartum variables (maternal height and instrumental delivery); and (D) all significant independent antenatal and intrapartum factors when estimated birth weight was excluded (maternal height, body mass index, parity, gestation, fetal sex, length of second‐stage labor, and instrumental delivery). These data were randomly split into training and testing data sets. In the former, SD risk increased markedly as estimated birth weight increased, from 0.1% when estimated birth weight ≤ 3500 g to 10% when estimated birth weight > 4500 g. They showed that model C (maternal height, instrumental delivery, estimated birth weight) had the best AUC (0.8954) for predicting SD occurrence. The use of artificial intelligence has also been tested. In a monocentric retrospective study analyzing 2584 singleton vaginal deliveries, Tsur et al. 10 pooled 18 potential SD risk modifiers in a machine learning model; 500 random splits of training and test sets showed an AUC of the machine learning model of 0.793 ± 0.041, outperforming both adjusted estimated fetal weight alone and adjusted estimated fetal weight plus maternal diabetes in 95.2% of repeats. Despite the accurate predictive model developed, the 18 candidate predictive factors mentioned were less clinically relevant for a daily practice of OVD. Our results indicate that intrapartum US is a valuable tool that can help obstetricians predict SD occurrence during OVD. US‐measured HPD is a reliable, reproducible, rapid, and simple assessment during OVD. We observed that a higher HPD was related to a higher rate of SD, corroborating that HPD is an independent SD risk factor. An app is currently in development to assist clinicians in assessing the score with the different items in daily clinical situations. Because these analyses were based on a unicentric retrospective cohort, external validation should be conducted in a different population. 27 4.3. Strengths and limitations Few studies have previously addressed intrapartum sonography and SD risk; a key feature herein was that it is the first use of HPD to increase SD risk score accuracy. We were able to analyze HPD measurements before OVD and maternal antenatal risk factors within a relatively large population‐based sample. We were also able to develop a score based on a standard US measurement, supporting its feasibility for use in other clinical settings. However, the study was not without limitations. It used a retrospective design. The SD incidence (12.8%) was higher than in other recent reports (ie, 0.2%–3.0%). 1 , 25 This may have been because the sample was exclusively OVD cases, which is an independent factor in SD. Moreover, we included all cases of SD, not only “severe” ones requiring several maneuvers or with shoulder‐to‐head delivery interval (information not available in our database). In addition, our data may not be generalizable to populations with other characteristics (eg, ethnicities) and when other definitions of SD are used. 26 Another limitation is the lack of a validation cohort to compare the estimated and actual occurrences of SD. Nonetheless, recent literature 27 —including Collins et al. (even in studies with large sample sizes)—encourages developing the model using the available data set, performing an internal validation by bootstrapping and conducting an external validation in subsequent research. Moreover, although statistically significant, birth weight cannot be a candidate predictive factor because estimated fetal weight is not routinely assessed in every labor ward or in prelabor consultation. Furthermore, arrest disorder is considered as a subjective criterion. In addition, there is a lack of a clear definition in the literature. Thus, it has been decided that it should not be implemented in the scoring system. Finally, instrumentation choice during OVD is unstandardized, although there was not a significant difference herein between the SD and non‐SD groups regarding OVD instrumentation. Operators were also not blinded to the sonographic measurement, which is a usual limit of retrospective studies on sonographic evaluation before OVD. 5. CONCLUSIONS Although SD remains difficult to predict, this scoring tool, which includes both maternal factors and US HPD measurement, facilitates assigning individual risk categories in OVD. This may improve the management of delivery mode, especially for high‐ and very high‐risk groups. External validation in other settings will be needed to confirm the discriminative ability of the score. Clinicians should exercise caution, as this point‐scoring system is not yet ready for clinical application until such validation takes place. AUTHOR CONTRIBUTIONS P.R. wrote the manuscript. P.R. and M.S. created and filled in the database. J.L. and E.D. managed statistical analysis and wrote the Statistical analysis section of the manuscript. C.G. supervised the writing of the manuscript and the statistical analysis. FUNDING INFORMATION None. CONFLICT OF INTEREST STATEMENT C.G. made conferences for General Electric. The remaining authors report no conflicts of interest. Supporting information Appendix S1: Supporting information. IJGO-171-272-s001.docx (30.7KB, docx) Data S1: Supporting information. IJGO-171-272-s002.docx (141.7KB, docx) Renon P, Drumez E, Sanchez M, Labreuche J, Garabedian C. Can shoulder dystocia be predicted before operative vaginal delivery using a score 