article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Weston MR, Martins SL, Neustadt AB, et al. Factors influencing uptake of intrauterine devices among postpartum adolescents: a qualitative study. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 2012;206(1):40.e1-7–40.e7. doi: 10.1016/j.ajog.2011.06.094. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Nahas G, Magalhães C, Bueloni-Dias F, et al. Immediate postpartum insertion of copper intrauterine device in a Brazilian university hospital: expulsion and continuation rates. Rev Bras Ginecol Obstet. 2023;45(1):31–37. doi: 10.1055/s-0042-1759628. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Ezugwu EC, Achara JI, Ezugwu OC, et al. Acceptance of postpartum intrauterine contraceptive device among women attending antenatal care in a low-resource setting in Nigeria. Int J Gynaecol Obstet. 2020;148(2):181–186. doi: 10.1002/ijgo.13027. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Karra M, Canning D, Foster S, et al. Location and content of counselling and acceptance of postpartum IUD in Sri Lanka. Reprod Health. 2017;14(1):42. doi: 10.1186/s12978-017-0304-7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Laporte M, Marangoni M, Surita F, et al. Postplacental placement of intrauterine devices: a randomized clinical trial. Contraception. 2020;101(3):153–158. doi: 10.1016/j.contraception.2019.12.006. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Obstetrics IFoGa . Technical Brief: implementing postpartum intrauterine device (PPIUD) services in healthcare facilities; 2020. p. 7. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Data Availability Statement Data is available through reasonable request to the corresponding author.',8,'2025-10-13 06:55:35','2025-10-13 06:55:35');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1712,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12444180','A clinical study on the efficacy of concurrent chemoradiotherapy combined with targeted therapy and hyperthermia in patients with locally advanced cervical cancer.','Jia Y, Zhang F, Zou K, Zou L.','2025','crr efficacies crr crr crr thermotherapy crr crr thermotherapy ib3 nimotuzumab sensitizing radioresistance dalian dalian crr ①age ②pathologically ③maximum ④karnofsky ①age ③kps dalian ky2025 parametria iridium nedaplatin nimotuzumab nimotuzumab synchronize synchronization crr glutamyl karnofsky crr iiic1 iiic2 crr crr crr crr diverged converged crr crr converged crr crr crr radiosensitization radiosensitization nedaplatin nephro igrt vmat hepatoprotection gastroprotection thermoprotective crr crr crr crr yuhan kun lijuan yuhan kun zoukunmd dmu lijuan zoulijuan1963 dmu waggoner devasia mariotto yabroff roane leath s40265 nimotuzumab santivasi antioxid oei vriend crezee franken krawczyk s13014 roti roti hyperth hyperth oei oei horsman stalpers franken crezee chemosensitizer addr lutgens pijls johannesma kock buijsen mastrigt lammering ruysscher lambin cd006377 2010cd006377 radiochemotherapy zhichen jingcheng thermoradiochemotherapy haaren kamer wiersma crezee franckena hyperth hyperth franckena lutgens koper kleynen banasik jobsen leer creutzberg dielwart norden canters rhoon westermann mella banasik koper uitterhoeve velden schem wilt prosnitz tinteren hyperth hyperth vasanthan mitsumori oliynychenko tatsuzaki hiraoka pectasides kamposioras papaxoinis pectasides ctrv seam pradier rave schmidberger bomecke meden zmk caski s004320050237 cecco aliberti baldo giacomin torbe falco torbe ciepiela kurzawa radiochemotherapy s12032 marth landoni mahner iv72 iv83 mdx220 lainakis x230 x232 mdl265 latorre lena catino crucitta sambiasi guida misino kirchheiner tanderup lindegaard haie meder petric mahantshetty jurgenliemk dorr nout enriquez aceves galicia coronel calderillo cetina ric hepatotoxicities suc hsp27 ubbink bruggenwirth nijsten porte g43 g50 ajpgi hyperth hyperth toraya fiering hyperth hyperth girardin philpott suman fornace biocel dokladny moseley autophagic japplphysiol cyclohydrolase sawai kurimoto suganuma kuzuya koide koide kamiya ajcr','Highlights • Hyperthermia combined with targeted therapy enhances treatment efficacy in LACC. • Hyperthermia reduces radiotherapy and chemotherapy toxicity. • Clinical evidence confirms hyperthermia-targeted therapy synergy in cervical cancer. • Provides evidence for personalized cervical cancer therapy. Keywords: Local advanced cervical cancer, Hyperthermia, Chemoradiotherapy, Targeted therapy Abstract Objective To explore novel therapeutic approaches for locally advanced cervical cancer (LACC), we evaluated the efficacy and safety of hyperthermia (HT) and/or targeted therapy combined with cisplatin-based concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT). Methods This retrospective study analyzed 119 LACC patients (tumor diameter ≥4 cm) treated at our institution (Jan 2021–Oct 2024), stratified into: CCRT ( n = 48), targeted therapy + CCRT (T-CCRT, n = 44), and HT + targeted therapy + CCRT (HT-T-CCRT, n = 27). Complete/objective response rates (CRR/ORR) at 3/12 months were assessed. Statistical analyses were performed using standard software to compare efficacies and evaluate adverse events (AEs). Results 1. Efficacy: At 3 months, HT-T-CCRT achieved the highest CRR (85.19 % vs. CCRT 58.33 %, P < 0.05) and ORR (96.30 %). T-CCRT showed significantly higher ORR (95.46 % vs. CCRT 81.25 %, P < 0.05). At 12 months, no significant intergroup differences existed in CRR (58.33 %/61.36 %/77.78 %) or ORR (70.83 %/75.00 %/77.78 %). 2. Safety: CCRT had lower diarrhea (52.08 % vs. 81.82 %) and vomiting (50.00 % vs. 86.36 %) rates than T-CCRT (both P < 0.05). HT-T-CCRT further reduced vomiting (55.56 % vs. 86.36 %), diarrhea (55.56 % vs. 81.82 %), and liver impairment (22.22 % vs. 47.73 %) versus T-CCRT (all P < 0.05), with comparable safety to CCRT. Conclusions Efficacy: The targeted combination group had higher ORR and CRR at 3/12 months than chemoradiation, with a significant 3-month ORR difference. The thermotherapy-targeted group showed the highest CRR/ORR, with significantly better 3-month CRR vs chemoradiation but not vs targeted alone. Safety: Diarrhea/vomiting were lower with chemoradiation than targeted; thermotherapy-targeted had lower diarrhea/vomiting/liver injury than targeted alone, with comparable overall AEs to chemoradiation. Graphical abstract Open in a new tab Introduction Cervical cancer ranks as the fourth leading cause of cancer-related deaths among women and it remains the second leading cause of cancer deaths in women aged 20 to 39 [ 1 ]. According to global statistics, there are approximately 500,000 new cases of cervical cancer each year. According to the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics (FIGO 2018) staging system, stages IB3 to IVA are defined as locally advanced cervical cancer (LACC). Five large-scale randomized clinical trials have established cisplatin-based concurrent chemoradiotherapy as the standard treatment [ 2 ]. Even with continuous advancements in modern medicine, the five-year survival rate for patients with LACC remains only 60 % to 70 % [ 3 ]. Thus, the exploration and optimization of treatment strategies to enhance local control rates, has emerged as a central focus in contemporary medical research. Bevacizumab functions by neutralizing circulating VEGF-A (vascular endothelial growth factor-A). In 2014, the phase III GOG-0240 trial [ 4 ] demonstrated a significant improvement in overall survival (OS) with a median OS of 17 months compared to 13.3 months for chemotherapy alone, reducing the risk of death by 29 % (HR=0.71; P = 0.004). Based on these findings, bevacizumab was approved for use in combination with chemotherapy for the treatment of cervical cancer. In LACC, nimotuzumab combined with cisplatin-based concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT) achieved a superior objective response rate (ORR) of 98.3 % vs. 87.4 % for CCRT alone [ 5 ]. This regimen proved safe and effective, significantly improving local tumor control and disease-free survival (DFS). Hyperthermia (HT) treatment—locally heating tumors to 42 °C for 60 min—significantly enhances cancer therapy by directly killing tumor cells and sensitizing them to radiotherapy and chemotherapy. Mechanistically, HT inhibits DNA double-strand break repair (via homologous recombination and non-homologous end joining), leading to lethal accumulation of DNA damage [ [6] , [7] , [8] ]; increases tumor membrane permeability to boost chemotherapeutic drug uptake; and improves tumor oxygenation to overcome hypoxia-induced radioresistance [ 9 ]. Clinically, in LACC, a retrospective study ( n = 487) confirmed that HT combined with radiotherapy improves complete response rates, reduces recurrence, and enhances long-term survival [ 10 ]. A randomized trial ( n = 435) demonstrated superior 5-year overall survival (82 % vs. 72 %) for chemoradiotherapy plus HT versus chemoradiotherapy alone, without significantly increased toxicity [ 11 ]. This study conducted a retrospective analysis of 119 patients with LACC treated in the Department of Radiation Oncology at the Second Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University between January 2021 and October 2024. This study compares three treatments for LACC—CCRT, T-CCRT, and HT-T-CCRT—to evaluate clinical efficacy and adverse reactions and to seek improved therapeutic approaches. Materials and methods General data Research object This study gathered data from 119 patients diagnosed with bulky locally advanced cervical cancer (characterized by a maximum cervical tumor diameter of ≥4 cm) who received treatment at the Department of Radiation Oncology, the Second Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University from January 2021 to October 2024. The patients were categorized into three treatment groups: - Concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT) group: 48 cases - Targeted therapy combined with CCRT (T - CCRT) group: 44 cases - Hyperthermia plus targeted therapy combined with CCRT (HT - T - CCRT) group: 27 cases A minimum sample of 25 per group provided 80 % power (α=0.05) to detect 30 % CRR difference based on prior LACC trials (GOG protocols 165/219). Inclusion and exclusion criteria Inclusion Criteria: ①Age ≥ 18 years; ②Pathologically confirmed cervical cancer with FIGO (International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics) stage IIB to IVA; ③Maximum tumor diameter ≥ 4 cm; ④Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) score > 60. Exclusion Criteria: ①Age < 18 years; ② Presence of distant metastasis at initial diagnosis; ③KPS score < 60. Data collection This study enrolled a cohort of 119 patients with bulky LACC treated with CCRT between January 2021 and October 2024. Data collection encompassed patient demographics (age), clinical characteristics (FIGO stage, histopathology, HPV status, initial tumor size, comorbidities), treatment specifics (CCRT details, radiation therapy [RT] dose), and treatment-related adverse events (AEs). Treatment response was evaluated at 3 months and 12 months after RT completion. Progression-free survival and overall survival (OS) analyses were conducted with a data cutoff date of October 2024. The study protocol received approval from the Ethics Committee of the Second Affiliated Hospital of Dalian Medical University (Approval No KY2025–054–01), ensuring adherence to ethical standards for human research. Methods Radiotherapy Radiotherapy comprised two sequential components: external beam radiotherapy (EBRT) followed by brachytherapy. EBRT was delivered using intensity-modulated radiotherapy with 6 MV X-rays. The clinical target volume routinely encompassed the cervix, uterus, parametria, upper vagina, and regional lymph nodes. Extended coverage was applied based on disease extent: para-aortic nodes were included if common iliac or para-aortic nodal involvement was present, and the entire vagina plus inguinal nodes were targeted in cases of lower vaginal involvement. EBRT dosing was 45.0–50.4 Gy administered in 25–28 fractions at 1.8–2.0 Gy per fraction, delivered five times per week. Subsequently, high-dose-rate brachytherapy using Iridium-192 was performed, targeting the cervix and any residual macroscopic disease. The brachytherapy dose was 28–30 Gy delivered in 4–5 fractions at 6–7 Gy per fraction and was typically administered twice weekly. Chemotherapy Prior to treatment initiation, all patients underwent comprehensive pretreatment screening, including standard blood tests, liver function tests, and renal function assessments, to exclude contraindications for aggressive therapy. Concurrent chemotherapy consisted of paclitaxel (135–175 mg/m²) combined with cisplatin (50–75 mg/m²), administered in 1–2 cycles at 21-day intervals. For patients exhibiting cisplatin intolerance, alternative platinum agents (carboplatin or nedaplatin) were substituted according to institutional guidelines. Supportive care protocols mandated premedication with dexamethasone for allergy prophylaxis alongside organ-protective agents. Chemotherapy was suspended for any patient developing Grade 3–4 toxicity according to CTCAE criteria, with treatment resumption contingent upon adequate recovery. Targeted therapy Two targeted therapy agents were concurrently administered with chemoradiotherapy: bevacizumab and nimotuzumab. Bevacizumab was delivered at 15 mg/kg per dose on a 21-day cycle schedule, with patients receiving 1–2 total cycles. Nimotuzumab was administered at 100–200 mg per dose on a weekly schedule (every 7 days), totaling 6 cycles throughout treatment. Both agents were timed to synchronize with chemoradiotherapy phases as per institutional protocols. The selection between the two targeted therapeutic agents was primarily determined by the patient''s socioeconomic status. Dosage regimens were individualized based on age and overall physical condition, with elderly patients typically receiving reduced doses. Hyperthermia Deep pelvic hyperthermia was administered concurrently with CCRT using an external high-frequency hyperthermia system. Treatments were delivered twice weekly over the course of CCRT, with a minimum of eight sessions per patient protocol. Hyperthermia sessions were initiated following commencement of EBRT to ensure therapeutic synchronization with radiation sensitization phases. Follow-up All patients who met the inclusion criteria were followed up at a frequency of 3 to 6 months after the completion of all treatments. The follow-up period ended in October 2024. Evaluation criteria Efficacy Evaluation: Based on the WHO Response Evaluation Criteria in Solid Tumors (RECIST). Objective response indicators are defined as follows: • CR (Complete Response): Complete disappearance of all target lesions. • PR (Partial Response): >50 % reduction in tumor burden. • SD (Stable Disease): ≤50 % reduction or ≤25 % increase in tumor burden. • PD (Progressive Disease): >25 % increase in tumor burden or appearance of new lesions. Derived Efficacy Metrics: • CRR: Percentage of patients achieving CR within the cohort. • ORR: Percentage of patients achieving CR or PR within the cohort. Adverse Event (AE) Assessment: • Standard: NCI-CTCAE v5.0 • Monitored parameters: Myelosuppression/peripheral neuropathy/cancer pain intensity/diarrhea/nausea/vomiting • Timeframe: During treatment + 30-day post-treatment Liver Function Monitoring: Parameters: Alanine aminotransferase, aspartate aminotransferase, alkaline phosphatase, gamma-glutamyl transferase, total bilirubin, direct bilirubin. Impairment assessment: CTCAE v5.0 criteria based on serial transaminase elevations (pre- vs. post-treatment). Statistical methods Using statistical software, the count data were expressed as percentages ( %), and the measurement data were expressed as mean ± standard deviation. The differences between groups were statistically analyzed using the chi-square test, and the rank data were analyzed using the rank sum test. P < 0.05 meant the difference was statistically significant. Results General clinical data Comprehensive analysis of baseline characteristics—including age, hypertension prevalence, diabetes incidence, Karnofsky Performance Status (KPS) scores, tumor size (≤5 cm vs. >5 cm), HPV status, pathological type (predominantly squamous cell carcinoma), and FIGO stage—revealed no statistically significant differences among the groups (all P > 0.05), demonstrating comparable baseline characteristics across cohorts ( Table 3.1 ). We Added sensitivity analyses (multivariable regression adjusting for diabetes status) confirming robust treatment effect on CRR (adjusted OR: 3.21, 95 % CI: 1.18–8.74; P = 0.022). Table 3.1. General clinical data. Characteristic CCRT ( n = 48) H-CCRT ( n = 44) HT-T-CCRT ( n = 27) χ² value P value Age 0.541 0.763 ≤60 34 (70.83) 29 (65.91) 17 (62.96) >60 14 (29.17) 15 (34.09) 10 (37.04) Hypertension 2.127 0.345 No 37 (77.08) 39 (88.64) 22 (81.48) Yes 11 (22.92) 5 (11.36) 5 (18.52) Diabetes 5.938 0.068 No 44 (91.67) 42 (95.45) 21 (77.78) Yes 4 (8.33) 2 (4.55) 6 (22.22) KPS score 5.123 0.077 <80 34 (70.83) 39 (88.64) 23 (85.19) ≥80 14 (29.17) 5 (11.36) 4 (14.81) Maximum cervical tumor diameter 0.054 0.974 ≤5 cm 26 (54.17) 24 (54.55) 14 (51.85) >5 cm 22 (45.83) 20 (45.45) 13 (48.15) HPV 1.926 0.749 Deficiency 25 (52.08) 21 (47.73) 13 (48.15) Positive 21 (43.75) 21 (47.73) 11 (40.74) Negative 2 (4.17) 2 (4.55) 3 (11.11) Pathological type 3.467 0.177 Squamous cell carcinoma 48 (100.00) 42 (95.45) 27 (100.00) Adenocarcinoma 0 (0.00) 2 (4.55) 0 (0.00) FIGO stage 3.185 0.203 IIB-IIIC1 42 (87.50) 36 (81.82) 26 (96.30) IIIC2-IVA 6 (12.50) 8 (18.18) 1 (3.70) Open in a new tab Clinical efficacy 3-month Assessment HT-T-CCRT had significantly higher CRR vs. CCRT alone ( P = 0.048), HT-T-CCRT vs. T-CCRT showed no significant CRR difference ( P = 0.343), overall intergroup compari