6.67) Yes 6 (22.22) 16 (33.33) Diarrhea 0.084 0.772 ≤5 episodes/day 12 (44.44) 23 (47.92) >5 episodes/day 15 (55.56) 25 (52.08) Cancer pain intensity 1.068 0.301 ≤Grade 3 23 (85.19) 36 (75.00) >Grade 3 4 (14.81) 12 (25.00) Open in a new tab Discussion Cervical cancer, prevalent among middle-aged women, shows significant incidence and mortality rate variations across countries and regions and is particularly influenced by economic conditions. This retrospective cohort study enrolled 119 patients with bulky locally advanced cervical carcinoma (tumor diameter ≥4 cm), stratified into three comparable treatment groups: concurrent chemoradiotherapy (CCRT, n = 48), targeted therapy plus CCRT (T-CCRT, n = 44), and hyperthermia with targeted therapy plus CCRT (HT-T-CCRT, n = 27), with no significant baseline differences ( P > 0.05). While diabetes prevalence differed between groups, sensitivity analyses confirmed the primary efficacy conclusions remained robust after adjustment. At the 3-month follow-up, T-CCRT demonstrated significantly higher objective response rate (ORR: 95.45 % vs. 81.25 %; P = 0.036) and complete response rate (CRR: 70.45 % vs. 58.33 %; P = 0.048) versus CCRT, while HT-T-CCRT achieved the highest CRR (85.19 %). By 12 months, ORR converged across groups (CCRT: 70.83 %; T-CCRT: 75.00 %; HT-T-CCRT: 77.78 %) with no significant CRR differences ( P > 0.05). As of the follow-up cutoff date in October 2024, the recurrence and metastasis rates were 22.92 % (11/48) in the CCRT group, 22.73 % (10/44) in the T-CCRT group, and 18.52 % (5/27) in the HT-T-CCRT group. However, the differences among the three groups were not statistically significant. Median follow-up was 23 months (range: 4–48 months). Furthermore, due to a minimum follow-up duration of 14 months and a relatively low number of deaths across all groups, the median survival time could not be determined. Although HT-T-CCRT improved short-term tumor response, a 5-year follow-up [ 12 ] indicated limited impact on long-term survival rates. The significant 3-month CRR advantage with HT-T-CCRT over CCRT (85.19 % vs. 58.33 %; P = 0.048) underscores hyperthermia’s synergistic role within multimodal therapy. The non-significant HT-T-CCRT vs. T-CCRT comparison (70.45 % vs. 85.19 %; P = 0.343) suggests hyperthermia primarily enhances CCRT backbone efficacy rather than replacing targeted agents. The 19.45 % absolute CRR increase (77.78 % vs. 58.33 %) at 12 months, though statistically non-significant ( P = 0.185), represents a clinically meaningful response durability signal. HT-T-CCRT emerging trends favor the experimental arm (12-month OS: 92.6 % vs. 86.4 %). Long-term survival benefits require validation through extended follow-up. Hyperthermia (HT) utilizes non-ionizing radiation to heat tissues (>39 °C), inducing tumor cell apoptosis and enhancing radiotherapy/chemotherapy efficacy. Optimal protocols involve twice-weekly sessions (≥3-day intervals) with radiotherapy administered within 1–4 h for maximal radiosensitization [ 13 ]. HT mitigates tumor hypoxia by improving blood flow, impairs DNA repair, and increases drug delivery [ 14 ]. Clinically, HT combined with radiotherapy achieves a 77 % complete response rate and 53 % 5-year local control—providing an alternative for chemotherapy-intolerant patients [ 15 ]. While HT with chemoradiotherapy yields a 66.1 % 5-year survival in advanced cervical cancer [ 16 ], large-scale randomized trials remain limited. Exploring the combination of targeted therapy with hyperthermia represents a viable option. Clinical studies in other tumors have also demonstrated that hyperthermia can improve the efficacy of chemotherapy, targeted therapy, and radiotherapy. The multimodal framework presented here may hold promise for extrapolation to other malignancy types, particularly those amenable to hyperthermia-sensitized chemoradiation. Further investigations are needed to explore its applicability across diverse oncological contexts. Although most hyperthermia trials have not reported additional severe toxicities, in some cases, hyperthermia can lead to acute and late toxicities [ 17 ]. Cisplatin-paclitaxel is the standard chemotherapy regimen for locally advanced cervical cancer, offering superior radiosensitization and disease-free survival versus cisplatin monotherapy [ 18 , 19 ]. However, cisplatin causes significant nephrotoxicity, myelosuppression, and gastrointestinal toxicity [ 20 , 21 ], while paclitaxel adds neurotoxicity and hepatotoxicity [ 22 ]. For cisplatin-intolerant patients, second-generation platinum agents provide alternatives: carboplatin demonstrates comparable efficacy with better renal safety in clinical trials [ 23 , 24 ], while nedaplatin maintains similar antitumor activity with reduced nephro-/gastrointestinal toxicity (offset by higher hematologic toxicity) [ 25 , 26 ]. While historical reports cite gastrointestinal, genitourinary, and vaginal stenosis as primary long-term radiotherapy toxicities in cervical cancer [ 27 ], modern image-guided adaptive radiotherapy (IGRT), intensity-modulated radiotherapy (IMRT), and volumetric modulated arc therapy (VMAT) have substantially improved organ-at-risk sparing. This technological evolution necessitates updated frameworks for evaluating radiotherapy-related adverse effects, which are typically manageable through dose adjustment, treatment breaks, or symptomatic care. This study revealed distinct toxicity profiles among treatment groups: the T-CCRT group exhibited significantly higher gastrointestinal toxicity than CCRT (diarrhea >5 × /day: 81.82 % vs. 52.08 %; grade 2 nausea/vomiting: 86.36 % vs. 50.00 %; both P < 0.05), consistent with known bevacizumab-related toxicity patterns [ 28 ]. Conversely, the HT-T-CCRT cohort demonstrated substantially reduced GI toxicity versus T-CCRT (nausea/vomiting: 55.56 % vs. 86.36 %, P = 0.004; diarrhea: 55.56 % vs. 81.82 %, P = 0.017) and significantly lower liver impairment (22.22 % vs. 47.73 %, P = 0.032)—potentially attributable to hyperthermia-induced heat shock proteins that enhance vasodilation, metabolic repair, and hepatoprotection [ [29] , [30] , [31] ]. No significant differences occurred in myelosuppression, neuropathy, or severe pain across comparisons ( P > 0.05), and HT-T-CCRT matched CCRT’s toxicity profile in all parameters. These findings position hyperthermia as a modulator that mitigates targeted therapy-induced toxicities without compromising safety. Preclinical and clinical evidence indicates hyperthermia (HT) mitigates chemoradiotherapy toxicities through dual mechanisms: Immunomodulation— elevating neutrophil-recruiting cytokines and cytotoxic T cells/dendritic cells to alleviate neutropenia [ 32 ] and enhance immunity [ 33 ]; Gastroprotection—activating AMPK-mediated autophagy [ [34] , [35] , [36] ] and improving intestinal perfusion [ 37 ] to preserve barrier integrity, reducing nausea/vomiting/diarrhea. This aligns with observed lower gastrointestinal toxicity in HT groups (current study; breast cancer cohort [ 38 ]) and retrospective cervical cancer data [ 12 ]. While myelosuppression relief remains preclinical [ 32 ], HT demonstrates both therapeutic efficacy enhancement and thermoprotective properties. This study has certain limitations. The sample size, comprising only 119 cases, is relatively limited, potentially leading to bias in the results. Immature survival data may underlie null 12-month ORR/CRR differences. In view of these constraints, future research will aim to validate the findings by expanding the sample size and extending the follow-up period. Second, this study primarily focuses on the concurrent medication regimen of paclitaxel combined with platinum-based drugs and bevacizumab targeted therapy. Whether this regimen represents the optimal treatment approach, the timing of targeted drug administration, and the time interval between hyperthermia and radiotherapy still require more clinical data for support. Conclusions 1. Clinical efficacy: The T-CCRT group showed higher ORR and CRR at 3 months and 12 months post-treatment compared to the CCRT group, with a significant difference in ORR at 3 months. The HT-T-CCRT group had the highest CRR and ORR, with a significant difference in CRR at 3 months compared to the CCRT group but no significant difference compared to the T-CCRT group. 2. Adverse reactions: The incidence of diarrhea and nausea/vomiting in the CCRT group was lower than that in the T-CCRT group. The HT-T-CCRT group had a lower incidence of diarrhea, nausea/vomiting, and liver function impairment compared to the T-CCRT group. There was no significant difference in the incidence of adverse reactions between the HT-T-CCRT group and the CCRT group. CRediT authorship contribution statement Yuhan Jia: Writing – original draft, Data curation. Feng Zhang: Methodology, Formal analysis. Kun Zou: Writing – review & editing. Lijuan Zou: Writing – review & editing. Declaration of competing interest The authors declare that they have no known competing financial interests or personal relationships that could have appeared to influence the work reported in this paper. Formatting of funding sources This research did not receive any specific grant from funding agencies in the public, commercial, or not-for-profit sectors. Contributor Information Yuhan Jia, Email: 1371400966@qq.com. Feng Zhang, Email: 8946254@qq.com. Kun Zou, Email: zoukunmd@dmu.edu.cn. Lijuan Zou, Email: zoulijuan1963@dmu.edu.cn. References 1. Siegel R.L., Miller K.D., Fuchs H.E., Jemal A. Cancer statistics, 2022. CA Cancer J. Clin. 2022;72:7–33. doi: 10.3322/caac.21708. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Waggoner S.E. Cervical cancer. Lancet. 2003;361:2217–2225. doi: 10.1016/s0140-6736(03)13778-6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Miller K.D., Nogueira L., Devasia T., Mariotto A.B., Yabroff K.R., Jemal A., Kramer J., Siegel R.L. Cancer treatment and survivorship statistics, 2022. CA Cancer J. Clin. 2