INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(1715,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12443183','Barriers to improving preterm newborn outcomes through effective antenatal corticosteroid use in Ethiopia.','Yang WC, Getachew T, Smith ER, Bekele D, Haile S, Metaferia G, Tilahun ZB, Arsenault C.','2025','underused underused immaturity betamethasone agrarian pastoral unofficial livelihood lbw betamethasone betamethasone betamethasone betamethasone chorioamnionitis prereferral prereferral betamethasone betamethasone intramuscularly prereferral chorioamnionitis somali harari afar sidama sidama sidama nationalities agrarian pastoral pastoral somali benishangul gumuz dawa harari agrarian amhara oromiya sidama pastoral afar benishangul gumuz gambella somali tigray agrarian pastoral unofficial livelihood agrarian pastoral agrarian pastoral snnp nationalities lbw girma legesse tigray oromia girma legesse metaferia cemonc bemonc neonatologist 2kg bemonc cemonc prereferral severities cadres amhara oromia fundal holistically unsustainable godlee ohuma assefa lakew belay neonatol s40748 tessema berheto e0001471 pgph wolde gonete akalu edhs s13104 muhe nigussie e1130 nmrt mcgoldrick cd004454 cd004454 shennan jacobsson aeb gyamfi bannerma e102 hofmeyr rogozinska reprodutive usaid dhsprogram arsenault jogh woldekidan arja worku e0002640 pgph girma berhe mekonnen metaferia abayneh aynalem 2333794x21990344 2333794x21990344 legesse abera alemu oromia e0289050 ataguba e1003843 mitiku s12905 basha woya tekile v20i2 tamir asmamaw negash e001897 bmjpo roder dewan nimako twum danso nay e002539 nimako benski kakamega ghsp zahroh hazfiarini e1004074 levison nanthuru chiudzu antony levison ijwh s185199 kruk girma e0208898 masatu abawollo argaw tsegaye e0281626 teshale tesema edhs e0235538 shewaye cherie molla e0288922 arsenault mfeka nkabinde e1004446 arsenault cemonc e0003989 pgph arsenault degnan','Abstract Ethiopia has prioritised high-impact interventions to reduce neonatal deaths, including antenatal corticosteroids (ACS) utilisation. However, effective ACS use has faced various challenges. We used multiple data sources to examine the current landscape of ACS use in Ethiopia and to elucidate barriers to effective ACS utilisation, including a review of national obstetric guidelines over the past decade, a review of literature, and a descriptive analysis of health facility data. National obstetric protocols recommend administering ACS in both hospitals and health centres. However, ACS remains substantially underused. The 2016 Ethiopian Emergency Obstetric and Newborn Care Assessment reported that only 5% of preterm infants were born to women who had received corticosteroids before delivery. At the health facility level, the 2021 Ethiopian Service Provision Assessment survey showed that only 22.1% of facilities providing antenatal care and delivery services had administered ACS in the past 3 months, and 44.7% of facilities had injectable corticosteroids in stock at the time of the survey. Notably, private clinics had both the lowest corticosteroid availability (16.9%) and utilisation rate (2.8%). We identified several barriers to effective ACS use, including healthcare service delivery organisation, gaps in healthcare providers’ knowledge and skills (particularly at the primary healthcare level and in private facilities), challenges in accurate gestational age assessment resulting from limited access to early ultrasound and late initiation of antenatal care, and insufficient corticosteroid availability. Increasing ACS uptake alone is unlikely to have the desired population benefits without considering health service delivery redesign and integration with other life-saving maternal and newborn health interventions. Keywords: Health systems, Global Health, Maternal health Summary Box. Ethiopia’s national protocols recommend administering antenatal corticosteroids (ACS) to all eligible women at hospitals and in health centres, and urgent referrals to a higher level of care are required if ACS is provided in health centres. However, effective ACS provision faces a number of challenges. ACS is substantially underused, and the availability of corticosteroids is suboptimal in Ethiopia. Significant gaps exist between corticosteroid availability and ACS utilisation across subnational regions and facility types, particularly in lower levels of care such as primary hospitals, health centres and private clinics. The country’s health service delivery structure affects the effectiveness of ACS use. A substantial proportion of pregnant women give birth at health centres, including those who deliver prematurely. However, health centres might not be sufficiently equipped and staffed to ensure the survival of preterm babies that require specialised care. Insufficient corticosteroid availability and gaps in provider knowledge—both in correct administration of ACS and recognising preterm labour—are also key barriers to effective ACS use. To ensure the population benefits of ACS, this intervention must be delivered to eligible women in healthcare settings capable of managing preterm labour and caring for preterm newborns, with healthcare providers following clinical guidelines and supported by national policies. Introduction Ethiopia faces a substantial burden of preterm infants. The estimated prevalence of preterm birth in the country was 12.9% in 2020—higher than the global average of 9.9% and the prevalence in sub-Saharan Africa of 10.1%. 1 This prevalence corresponded to an estimated 495 900 preterm infants in 2020, placing Ethiopia fifth among the top eight countries that accounted for more than half of preterm births globally. 1 As a leading cause of neonatal death, preterm birth is a serious public health issue. 2 3 A study assessing risk factors of neonatal mortality in Ethiopia found that preterm infants had five times the odds of death compared with term infants. 4 An observational study in the country reported that respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), a common respiratory complication due to fetal lung immaturity, contributed to nearly half of preterm infant deaths. 5 While Ethiopia has made progress in reducing the neonatal mortality rate from 59 per 1000 live births in 1990 to 29 per 1000 live births in 2021, 6 the country is off track to meet the Sustainable Development Goal of ending preventable newborn death by 2030. 3 It is clear that prioritising interventions for preterm infants is crucial for reducing neonatal mortality in Ethiopia. Antenatal corticosteroids (ACS) use among pregnant women at risk of imminent preterm labour has been proven to significantly improve preterm health outcomes by accelerating fetal lung maturity and reducing respiratory complications. A 2020 Cochrane review, including 27 trials of more than 10 000 infants, found that ACS reduces the risk of RDS and neonatal death by 30% and 22%, respectively. 7 The WHO, the International Federation of Gynecology and Obstetrics, and the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists recommend administering ACS to pregnant women at risk of imminent preterm labour between gestational age (GA) of 24 and 34 weeks. 8 , 10 As the majority of preterm births globally occur in low-income and middle-income countries (LMICs), ACS use has been strongly advocated in these countries. A recent Lancet series on ‘small vulnerable newborns’ identified a package of antenatal interventions for the health of this vast population, highlighting the population benefits of ACS in reducing complications of preterm births. 11 Similarly, Ethiopia’s national strategies have prioritised high-impact interventions to improve newborn outcomes, including promoting ACS use. 12 , 14 However, there is scarce evidence on the current status of ACS utilisation in Ethiopia, despite the country’s burden of preterm births and the population benefits of ACS. This study aimed to assess the landscape of ACS utilisation in Ethiopia and to identify the barriers to its effective implementation. Data sources This study drew data from multiple sources, including (1) the 2021–2022 Ethiopia Service Provision Assessment (SPA) survey, (2) the 2016 Ethiopian Emergency Obstetric and Newborn Care (EmONC) assessment, (3) Ethiopian national obstetric and antenatal care (ANC) guidelines and (4) a literature review on ACS utilisation in Ethiopia. First, SPA surveys are health facility surveys designed by the Demographic and Health Survey Programme (DHS) to measure service availability and quality of care in LMICs. 15 The 2021–2022 Ethiopia SPA is the most recent assessment for Ethiopia. 16 Using the SPA data, we assessed ACS availability and recent utilisation. The SPA surveys have five core instruments, one of which is the inventory instrument that has 21 questionnaire sections to assess infrastructure, staffing and the availability of drugs and equipment for various healthcare services (including infectious diseases management, child care and vaccination, ANC, delivery and newborn care, and non-communicable diseases (NCDs), among others). While the questionnaire for delivery and newborn care does not assess corticosteroid availability, this information is available in the questionnaire section on NCD medicines. We used corticosteroid availability, which is recorded in the NCD medicines; we believe it should reflect the drug’s availability in the facility’s central pharmacy. This approach was adopted to be consistent with our previous study and is consistent with our understanding of how drugs are stored and managed. 17 We restricted our analyses to facilities that provided ANC or delivery services (normal spontaneous delivery or caesarean sections) and excluded health posts. We presented the weighted proportions of facilities with injectable corticosteroids (either dexamethasone or betamethasone) available at the time of the survey and those that had administered ACS within the 3 months prior to the survey. The results were stratified by (1) subnational regions and facility types (referral hospitals, general hospitals, primary hospitals, private clinics and health centres) and (2) by region type: urban, agrarian and pastoral regions (an unofficial regional classification reflecting the predominant livelihood system in the region). Second, the 2016 Ethiopian EmONC assessment is a cross-sectional census of all facilities that provided delivery services in 2015. 18 This assessment comprised 14 modules that collected data on facility readiness and healthcare quality, including physical and human resources, service statistics, provider knowledge and referrals. One module collected data on newborn complications at the individual level, including newborns with difficulties in breathing, young infants with infections and preterm and low birth weight (LBW) babies (birth weight <2 kg). The information was collected by reviewing the medical records of the last two cases admitted in the last 12 months in each facility, if available. After excluding term and post-term newborns with birth weight less than 2 kg, we described the characteristics of facilities by babies’ status of ACS receipt. 18 Third, we worked closely with Ethiopian obstetricians and policymakers, relying on their expertise to identify national guidelines and protocols on maternal and newborn care issued over the past decade, including those related to ACS administration. 19 , 27 Fourth, we searched PubMed to identify previous studies about ACS utilisation in Ethiopia and reviewed peer-reviewed literature on the topic. National guidelines on ACS use Box 1 summarises the country’s national guidelines on ACS use. First, like the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines, injectable dexamethasone is included among the obstetric medicines in Ethiopia’s most recent Essential Medicines List, which also lists betamethasone as an alternative, while the WHO list does not include betamethasone. 19 28 We reviewed two versions of national obstetric guidelines: the 2014 Standard Treatment Guidelines (including separate guidelines for general hospitals, primary hospitals and health centres) and the 2021 Obstetrics Management Protocols (including separate protocols for hospitals and for health centres). 2021 25 , 27 Both recommend administering ACS for imminent preterm labour at hospitals and health centres. ACS use was covered as early as 2013 in the Basic Emergency Obstetric and Newborn Care Manual. 24 Box 1. Summary of Ethiopian national guidelines on antenatal corticosteroid use. 2020 Ethiopian National Essential Medicine List sixth edition 19 Dexamethasone is listed in the section for essential obstetrics and gynaecological medications. Betamethasone can be used alternatively. 2020 Management Protocol on Selected Obstetrics Topics for Hospitals 23 2021 Obstetric Management Protocol for Hospitals 20 Administer antenatal corticosteroids (betamethasone 12 mg IM, 24 hours apart for two doses or dexamethasone 6 mg IM, 12 hours apart for four doses) for lung maturity. If preterm birth is considered imminent, treatment for short duration still improves fetal lung maturity and chances of neonatal survival. Therefore, the first dose of corticosteroids should be administered even if the ability to give the second dose is thought to be unlikely. Antenatal corticosteroid therapy should not be administered in women with chorioamnionitis. 2021 Obstetric Management Protocol for Health Centres 21 Prereferral management includes dexamethasone 6 mg IM stat (document the time given on referral paper). Urgent referrals after prereferral care are required. 2014 Standard Treatment Guidelines for General Hospitals 27 2014 Standard Treatment Guidelines for Primary Hospitals 26 Steroid therapy for stimulation of surfactant production to be given at 28–34 weeks of gestation. The first line drug is betamethasone (12 mg IV, 24 hours apart for two doses). The second line drug is dexamethasone (6 mg PO/IM, 6 hours apart for two doses). After 48 hours from the first dose, the full effect on maturing the surfactant has been obtained. If the patient does not deliver within 1 week, the treatment should be repeated if the fetus is less than 34 weeks of gestation. Use of corticosteroids in the presence of infection is contraindicated. 2014 Standard Treatment Guidelines for Health Centres 25 The recommendations are the same as the 2014 Standard Treatment Guidelines for General Hospitals and Primary Hospitals. Patients with preterm labour should be treated in a centre where there is a neonatal intensive care unit and should be referred as early as possible. 2013 Basic Emergency Obstetric and Newborn Care Training Manual 24 Give corticosteroids to the mother to improve fetal lung maturity (betamethasone 12 mg IM, two doses 12 hours apart or dexamethasone 6 mg IM, four doses 6 hours apart). Corticosteroids should not be used in the presence of frank infection. Consider transferring to the most appropriate service for the care of the newborn, if possible. IM, intramuscularly; IV, intravenous; PO, oral. Ethiopian guidelines recommend ACS use in both hospitals and health centres. In health centres, ACS should be given as part of prereferral management, followed by urgent referrals to a higher level of facility. Regarding eligibility, the Ethiopian guidelines specify the eligible period as between 28 and 34 weeks of gestation; this information is found in the 2014 version but not clearly described in the 2021 protocol. While it is slightly different from the international recommendations of 24–34 weeks of gestation, this difference might be attributed to the viable gestational age in the context. Ethiopian guidelines also mention the contraindications of ACS use, advising against ACS administration in cases of infections, such as chorioamnionitis. The dose and administration route in the country’s guidelines generally aligns with international recommendations. A repeated course of ACS is recommended 1 week after the first course if the pregnant woman is still under 34 weeks of gestation. ACS availability and utilisation: evidence from facility assessments and literature Analyses of the 2021–2022 SPA data revealed that among facilities providing ANC or delivery services (n=710), 44.7% had injectable corticosteroids available at the time of the survey, but only 22.1% had administered ACS in the past 3 months prior to the survey ( table 1 ). Among public facilities, ACS availability was highest in primary hospitals (81.3%), followed by general hospitals (77.5%) and referral hospitals (60.0%), and lowest in health centres (49.4%). ACS utilisation increased by the level of care, ranging from 20.8% in health centres to 78.9%, 82.5% and 86.7% in primary, general and referral hospitals, respectively. Notably, private clinics had the lowest availability (16.9%) and utilisation (2.8%). Across subnational regions, Somali (53.7%) and Harari (50.0%) had the highest proportion of facilities that had used ACS recently, and Afar had the lowest (10.6%). The region of Sidama had the lowest availability (22.4%). Low availability in Sidama may be due to the mix of healthcare facility types in this region. In Sidama, only 39% of the 64 facilities assessed were hospitals (referral, general or primary) compared with 61% of facilities in Southern Nations, Nationalities and Peoples, for example. Across the subnational region types, availability was highest in urban regions (53.2%), followed by agrarian (43.4%) and pastoral (48.7%), which coincides with stronger health system infrastructure and more hospitals in urban areas. In contrast, pastoral regions had the highest proportion of facilities that used ACS recently (35.1%), which might reflect a higher need due to a higher rate of preterm births in these regions where maternal undernutrition is prevalent. 29 For example, Somali and Benishangul-Gumuz had the highest rate of neonatal mortality according to the latest DHS, with respectively 45 and 55 deaths per 1000 live births compared with the national average of 33 per 1000. 30 Nonetheless, it remains unclear why utilisation is higher in some regions. It may be due to greater need (higher prevalence of preterm labour) or inappropriate overuse. Future research should explore the reasons for differences in recent use across regions. We also found gaps between corticosteroid availability and recent ACS utilisation, especially in primary hospitals, health centres and private clinics. For example, while half of health centres at the national level had ACS in stock, only one-fifth had recently administered them. Table 1. Proportion of facilities that had injectable corticosteroids available and had provided antenatal corticosteroids in the past 3 months by subnational regions and facility types. Facility types Subnational regions Referral hospitals n=30 General hospitals n=120 Primary hospitals n=218 Private clinics (Including MCH specialised clinics) n=75 Health centres n=267 Availability Recent use * Availability Recent use * Availability Recent use * Availability Recent use * Availability Recent use * Availability Recent use * National (n=710) † 44.7% 22.1% 18 (60.0%) 26(86.7%) 93 (77.5%) 99 (82.5%) 171 (81.3%) 165 (78.9%) 19 (16.9%) 2 (2.8%) 128 (49.4%) 65 (20.8%) Urban regions ‡ (n=105) 70/105 (53.2%) 41/105 (16.2%) 3/9 (33.3%) 5/9(55.6%) 29/32 (90.6%) 23/32 (71.9%) 4/4 (100%) 2/4 (50%) 4/15 (33.4%) 0/15 (0%) 30/45 (75.8%) 11/45 (23.4%) Addis Ababa (n=64) 47/64 (53.5%) 29/64 (14.7%) 2/7 (28.6%) 4/7(57.1%) 22/25 (88.0%) 18/25 (72.0%) 3/3 (100%) 2/3 (66.7%) 2/7 (34.4%) 0/7 (0%) 18/22 (81.8%) 5/22 (22.7%) Dire Dawa (n=27) 16/27 (51.7%) 5/27 (15.2%) 1/1 (100%) 0/1 (0%) 5/5 (100%) 3/5 (60.0%) NA NA 1/6 (16.9%) 0/6 (0%) 9/15 (60.0%) 2/15 (13.3%) Harari (n=14) 7/14 (50%) 7/14 (50%) 0/1 (0%) 1/1 (100%) 2/2 (100%) 2/2 (100%) 1/1 (100%) 0/1 (0%) 1/2 (50.0%) 0/2 (0%) 3/8 (37.5%) 4/8 (50.0%) Agrarian regions ‡ (n=477) 287/477 (43.4%) 267/477 (21.2%) 13/19 (68.4%) 19/19 (100%) 57/79 (72.2%