 care or delivery services (normal spontaneous deliveries or Caesarean sections). Sampling weights were taken into account when reporting proportions. The Tigray region was excluded from the 2021–2022 Ethiopian SPA survey. ‡ Urban, agrarian and pastoral regions are unofficial regional classifications based on the dominant livelihood system in a region. It should be noted that agrarian and pastoral regions may also include urban, agrarian and pastoral areas. MCH, maternal and child health; NA, not applicable; SNNP, Southern Nations Nationalities, and Peoples; SPA, Service Provision Assessment. Evidence on ACS coverage rates at the individual level is limited. The 2016 EmONC assessment reviewed the medical records of the last two preterm babies born in facilities providing delivery care in Ethiopia. They found that among 1150 preterm and LBW babies (<2 kg), only 4.5% were born to women who received ACS. 18 We also found differences in facility characteristics between babies who had and had not received ACS ( table 2 ). Among newborns who received ACS, the majority (71.2%) were born in hospitals; in contrast, only 26.0% of those who did not receive ACS were found in hospitals. Few studies have investigated ACS coverage in Ethiopia, most of which found low coverage, even in higher-level facilities. 31 , 33 Two studies (Girma et al and Legesse et al ) retrospectively reviewed medical records in the highest level of hospitals to assess predictors of neonatal mortality; one study was conducted in two hospitals in Tigray, and the other was done among five hospitals in Oromia. 31 33 In the study by Girma et al , 14.4% of preterm babies (GA<37 weeks) were born to mothers who received ACS. Similarly, another study by Legesse et al revealed a similar coverage rate of 15.0%, but among neonates who were diagnosed with RDS and admitted to neonatal intensive care units. The former found that ACS exposure was not significantly associated with neonatal survival, 31 while the latter showed that ACS administration protected against neonatal death. 33 Another study by Metaferia et al described ACS utilisation in Ethiopia, using data from five teaching hospitals in different regions of the country. 32 They found that among preterm infants (GA<35 weeks) admitted to these five hospitals, one-third (37.5%) were delivered by women who received ACS. Almost all those who received ACS were delivered at hospitals, whereas a substantial proportion (19.1%) of those who did not receive ACS were born in health centres. 32 Table 2. Characteristics of health facilities according to antenatal corticosteroid exposure status of preterm and low birth weight babies (birth weight <2000 g). Characteristics Preterm and low birth weight babies (n=1150) With ACS exposure n=52 (4.5%) Without ACS exposure n=1098 (95.5%) P value * Frequency ( % ) Frequency ( % ) Urban (ref. rural) 41 (78.8%) 608 (55.4%) 0.001 Managing authority types Public 43 (82.7%) 1042 (94.9%) <0.001 Private for-profit 8 (15.4%) 33 (3.0%) Not-for-profit or faith-based/mission-based 1 (1.9%) 23 (2.1%) Facility type Referral/specialised hospitals 7 (13.5%) 36 (3.3%) <0.001 General hospitals 19 (36.5%) 108 (9.8%) Primary hospitals 11 (21.2%) 142 (12.9%) Private clinics † 3 (5.8%) 19 (1.7%) Health centres 12 (23.1%) 793 (72.2%) EmONC status Designated CEmONC facilities 37 (74.0%) 251 (24.4%) <0.001 Designated BEmONC facilities 12 (24.0%) 652 (63.5%) Not designated EmONC facilities 1 (2.0%) 124 (12.1%) Equipment Number of beds for obstetric care (mean, SD) 21.6 (22.3) 10.2 (13.0) <0.001 Have a functioning NICU 29 (55.8%) 213 (19.4%) <0.001 Have a functioning newborn corner 34 (65.4%) 403 (36.7%) <0.001 Have a functioning space for managing maternal complications 27 (51.9%) 263 (24.0%) <0.001 Have a functioning space for OB/GYN operations 27 (51.9%) 180 (16.4%) <0.001 Staffing Have at least one doctor 40 (76.9%) 296 (27.0%) <0.001 Have at least one paediatrician/neonatologist 14 (26.9%) 93 (8.5%) <0.001 Have a