icosteroids were given before birth to the pregnant woman, a preterm baby born at a small gestational age in a health centre lacking necessary equipment and medications is very unlikely to survive. These babies need immediate intensive care, such as respiratory support, which is usually unavailable in health centres. Referrals of preterm babies are then required, but often challenging because of distance and transportation constraints, as well as limited medical information continuity. Previous studies have examined the association between institutional deliveries and neonatal death in Ethiopia 35 , 37 ; however, evidence on differences in newborn mortality, specifically, preterm baby mortality, by facility type is sparse. Nonetheless, one ecological study that used data from several LMICs found that regions with a greater share of hospital deliveries (as opposed to lower-level clinics) had lower neonatal mortality rates after controlling for regional confounders. 34 Fundamentally, it is crucial to revisit the country’s structure of maternal care service delivery. One outstanding question is determining the appropriate facility level for labour and delivery services. Since 15% of pregnancies end with complications and the majority of these complications cannot be predicted, 38 it is reasonable to question whether health centres are an appropriate setting for childbirth care. Previous studies have examined the effect of service delivery redesign and suggested that shifting deliveries to or close to hospitals could improve maternal and neonatal survival, whereas the primary level of care, such as health centres, should be reserved for antenatal and postpartum care. 39 40 Pregnant women with preterm labour or obstetric danger signs should be advised to go to hospitals directly rather than presenting first at health centres. Policies regarding which level of care to seek delivery services based on risk stratification need to be tailored to the local context, considering feasibility and facility capability. Simply increasing ACS uptake is unlikely to improve preterm newborn survival without consideration of how service delivery structure affects the effectiveness of ACS. Scaling up ACS use requires attention to where both healthy and high-risk pregnant women should deliver, how well facilities are equipped and staffed to manage deliveries and maternal and newborn complications of varying severities, and how referral systems function across levels of care when preterm labour occurs. Knowledge gaps and know-do gap A systematic review found that varied levels of knowledge on ACS use among healthcare providers affected its appropriate use. 41 In Ethiopia, provider knowledge gaps have been frequently mentioned as critical challenges to implementing ACS effectively. These gaps are twofold. First, there is a lack of or insufficient understanding of ACS use and its benefits. Second, there are difficulties in identifying eligible pregnant women at risk of imminent preterm labour. Knowledge gaps may be particularly important in primary-level facilities staffed with midwives and health officers and in private facilities (as private facilities operate separately from the public system and might lack relevant training updates). Low utilisation of ACS at the primary level and in private facilities was confirmed in table 1 . Two qualitative studies in Malawi similarly showed low awareness of ACS and its purpose for accelerating fetal lung maturity among midwives and clinical officers. 42 43 Not only do these cadres have less training in preterm labour management, but they also see fewer cases each month than those working in larger hospitals. For these reasons, maintaining skills in appropriate ACS administration is challenging at the primary care level. The findings in the 2016 EmONC assessment reflected these two layers of knowledge gaps: among the facilities that had not used ACS in the past 3 months, about one-fourth (23%) reported a lack of training, and nearly two-thirds (63%) reported not having seen any case with indication for ACS use. 18 The latter is worth further investigation, as not seeing cases with the indication could be attributed to low service volume, inability to identify such cases or both. Beyond the knowledge gaps in ACS use, the know-do gap among providers is another challenge. Studies have shown that know-do gaps—the gap between having the knowledge and practising based on the knowledge—are associated with poor quality of care. 44 45 In the case of ACS use, studies found that perceived risks and benefits by providers also determine the administration of ACS. 41 43 The perceived risks, especially the fear of infection, and uncertainties regarding its benefits could partly explain the know-do gap. Accurate GA assessment An accurate gestational age assessment is the first and foremost requirement for determining eligibility for ACS use. However, there are practical challenges about GA assessment in Ethiopia. First, access to ultrasound in the first trimester—the gold standard to assess GA—is still limited, while the government plans to scale up obstetric ultrasound services in health centres. 46 47 Our previous study using the 2021–2022 SPA data found that only 4.5% of facilities that provided ANC and/or delivery services had a functional ultrasound, with 1.2% among facilities that provided ANC, 6.1% among those performing normal deliveries, and 84.9% among those doing caesarean sections. 17 In addition, many pregnant women initiate ANC visits late, resulting in missed opportunities for accurate GA dating in early pregnancy. 48 Although ANC coverage is 74%, the 2019 Ethiopian Mini DHS showed that only one-third (28%) of these women had their first ANC visit in the first trimester. 30 Second, the date of the last menstrual period (LMP) is often used to determine GA. However, a study in the Amhara region of Ethiopia showed that one-fourth of pregnant women did not know their LMP. 49 A recent study in the Oromia region found that only 2% of pregnant women were given an estimated due date during their first ANC visit. 50 Healthcare providers often need to rely on proxies such as fundal height or LMP, making it challenging to diagnose preterm labour and ascertain the GA period eligible for ACS. Drug availability Drug availability also remains a barrier to ACS use. The availability of injectable corticosteroids across different facility types is suboptimal, with only 50% of health centres and 60%–80% of hospitals at various levels having corticosteroids in stock at the time of the SPA survey ( table 1 ). Since national protocols recommend ACS use, 20 21 and corticosteroids are listed in the national essential medicine list, 19 there needs to be a stable stock of injectable corticosteroids at all levels of care. Injectable corticosteroids are low-cost and do not require a specific temperature for storage. These two features should make it feasible to maintain a consistent stock. Strengths and limitations To our knowledge, this is the first study that comprehensively examined the status of ACS utilisation and identified key implementation barriers to ACS use in Ethiopia, a country with a high burden of preterm births. A major strength of our study is that we pulled evidence from multiple sources to holistically investigate barriers to effective ACS use from multiple perspectives. However, our study has some limitations. First, we used a proxy to measure injectable corticosteroid availability. While we believe the corticosteroid availability in the questionnaire section for NCD medicines should reflect its availability in the facility, we do recognise the importance of this limitation and caution for careful interpretations; meanwhile, we also argue there is value in using well-established survey data as a starting point. Second, we did not perform a standard systematic review to identify Ethiopian national guidelines and prior studies on this topic because first, the literature on ACS use in Ethiopia is sparse, and second, most of these national guidelines are stored on government websites, which are unavailable to the public and could not be captured by standard systematic review approach. Lastly, we included the four most prominent barriers, although there might be patient-level barriers that are not captured in our study. Conclusions In this study, we described ACS utilisation in Ethiopia and identified several barriers to its effective use. Our findings confirm the complexity of these barriers, as exemplified in a framework for safe and effective ACS use proposed in our previous work. 51 We found that stable corticosteroid availability remains an issue across all levels of care. Even in settings with corticosteroids available, gaps in providers’ knowledge of ACS use and difficulties in identifying preterm labour, particularly in primary healthcare facilities, remain critical barriers to effective ACS use. Meanwhile, an accurate GA assessment to identify women who are eligible to receive the drug is challenging in the country. From an overall implementation standpoint, the country’s healthcare service delivery structure appears to shape the effectiveness of ACS administration at the primary level. Addressing these barriers requires a strong policy foundation, with investments in facility infrastructure, a stable drug supply chain, workforce capacity and a redesigned service delivery structure that can maximise the benefits of ACS. Efforts to narrow knowledge gaps should go beyond providing in-service training and workshops because these approaches often generate suboptimal and unsustainable impacts, 52 particularly in health centres with low service volumes. ACS is a promising intervention for improving preterm newborn survival, but it cannot achieve population benefits on its own. ACS utilisation must be integrated into a well-functioning health system with competent healthcare providers who follow clinical guidelines, a service delivery structure that can ensure its effectiveness, and a fundamental backup from the national guidelines. Simply increasing ACS uptake without strengthening health system and considering service delivery redesign is unlikely to improve neonatal health at the population level. Efforts to promote ACS uptake should prioritise solutions that address existing barriers and be integrated into a systematic approach to enhance maternal and newborn health. Supplementary material online supplemental file 1 bmjgh-10-9-s001.docx (25.8KB, docx) DOI: 10.1136/bmjgh-2025-019102 Footnotes Funding: The authors have not declared a specific grant for this research from any funding agency in the public, commercial or not-for-profit sectors. Provenance and peer review: Not commissioned; externally peer reviewed. Handling editor: Fi Godlee Patient consent for publication: Not applicable. Data availability free text: 2021–2022 Ethiopian DHS SPA data were available on the DHS website and the 2016 Ethiopian EmONC data were available from the Ethiopian Public Health Institute upon request. Author note: The reflexivity statement for this paper is linked as an online supplemental file 1. Data availability statement Data may be obtained from a third party and are not publicly available. References 1. Ohuma EO, Moller A-B, Bradley E, et al. National, regional, and global estimates of preterm birth in 2020, with trends from 2010: a systematic analysis. The Lancet. 2023;402:1261–71. doi: 10.1016/S0140-6736(23)00878-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Assefa N, Lakew Y, Belay B, et al. Neonatal mortality and causes of death in Kersa Health and Demographic Surveillance System (Kersa HDSS), Ethiopia, 2008-2013. Matern Health Neonatol Perinatol. 2016;2:7. doi: 10.1186/s40748-016-0035-8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Tessema GA, Berheto TM, Pereira G, et al. National and subnational burden of under-5, infant, and neonatal mortality in Ethiopi