knowledge transfer regarding preterm birth in Malawi following the implementation of targeted health messages over 3 years. Int J Womens Health . 2019;11:75–95. doi: 10.2147/IJWH.S185199. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 44. Gage AD, Kruk ME, Girma T, et al. The know-do gap in sick child care in Ethiopia. PLoS One. 2018;13:e0208898. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0208898. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 45. Leonard KL, Masatu MC. Professionalism and the know-do gap: exploring intrinsic motivation among health workers in Tanzania. Health Econ. 2010;19:1461–77. doi: 10.1002/hec.1564. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 46. Abawollo HS, Argaw MD, Tsegaye ZT, et al. Institutionalization of limited obstetric ultrasound leading to increased antenatal, skilled delivery, and postnatal service utilization in three regions of Ethiopia: A pre-post study. PLoS One. 2023;18:e0281626. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0281626. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 47. The Federal Democratic Republic of Ethiopia MoH Health Sector Transformation Plan II, 2020/21-2024/25. 2021 48. Teshale AB, Tesema GA. Prevalence and associated factors of delayed first antenatal care booking among reproductive age women in Ethiopia; a multilevel analysis of EDHS 2016 data. PLoS One. 2020;15:e0235538. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0235538. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 49. Shewaye M, Cherie N, Molla A, et al. A mixed-method study examined the reasons why pregnant women late initiate antenatal care in Northeast Ethiopia. PLoS One. 2023;18:e0288922. doi: 10.1371/journal.pone.0288922. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 50. Arsenault C, Mfeka-Nkabinde NG, Chaudhry M, et al. Antenatal care quality and detection of risk among pregnant women: An observational study in Ethiopia, India, Kenya, and South Africa. PLoS Med. 2024;21:e1004446. doi: 10.1371/journal.pmed.1004446. [ DOI ] [ PMC free articl